     "text": [
      "{'llm': 'openai/gpt-oss-120b', 'langs': ['en', 'fr', 'ru', 'zh', 'ar', 'he'], 'datetime': '2025-08-12T14:14:43.875501', 'data': [{'seed': {'name': 'Sino-Soviet War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Soviet_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Soviet_War', 'name': 'Sino-Soviet War', 'years': '1929, 1969', 'short_description': 'The term \"Sino‑Soviet War\" refers to two separate armed conflicts between China and the Soviet Union: a brief 1929 railway clash and the larger 1969 border conflict over disputed territory.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'disambiguation list'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The term \"Sino‑Soviet War\" refers to separate clashes: the 1929 railway dispute and the 1969 border conflict on Zhenbao/Damansky Island, each documented on Wikipedia with links to detailed articles.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry presents a disambiguation, emphasizing neutrality and navigation. The Chinese article frames the 1969 clash as a Chinese victory, highlights nuclear‑war risks, US mediation, and its diplomatic fallout, reflecting a nationalistic and strategic perspective. These differences illustrate how language and audience shape emphasis and interpretation.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Soviet_War', 'key_points': ['\\u202f“**Sino‑Soviet War**” isn’t a single historic event; it’s a catch‑all name applied to several distinct clashes between China and the Soviet Union.', '\\u202fThe most frequently cited usage refers to the **Sino‑Soviet conflict of 1929**, a short‑lived armed dispute centered on control of a railway line.', '\\u202fIt also encompasses the **Sino‑Soviet border conflict of 1969**, a more substantial military confrontation along the two nations’ frontier.', '\\u202fFor related but broader China‑Russia hostilities, the term “**Sino‑Russian War**” is used, with its own disambiguation page.', '\\u202fThis Wikipedia entry functions as a navigation aid, directing readers to the specific articles for each conflict.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8F%8D%E5%AE%9D%E5%B2%9B%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'key_points': ['**中方胜利并夺得实际控制权**：1969 年 3 月的珍宝岛冲突，以中国人民解放军击退苏联部队、缴获并修复 T‑62 坦克告终，随后在岛上驻军并长期占领，标志着中苏边界争议的实际转折。', '**核危机与冷战升级的警示**：苏联军方策划对华“外科手术式”核打击，甚至准备部署中程弹道导弹；美国政府在幕后斡旋防止冲突升级，凸显此事是冷战期间继古巴导弹危机后又一次可能引发全球核战争的重大危机。', '**促成中美关系破冰**：珍宝岛事件让中国领导层认识到中苏矛盾已超越中美矛盾，推动毛泽东等人主动向美国示好，随后尼克松访华、基辛格秘密会谈等外交突破迅速展开，标志着中国外交路线的根本性转向。', '**对中苏军备和边境政策的深远影响**：冲突暴露了中国反坦克武器的不足，直接催生 73 式、86 式等新型反装甲装备；同时也迫使苏联放缓对华的军事挑衅，最终在 1990‑1991 年的中俄边界协定中正式承认珍宝岛属于中国。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Sino-Soviet border conflict', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenbao_Island_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenbao_Island_incident', 'name': 'Sino-Soviet border conflict', 'years': '1969', 'short_description': 'A seven‑month undeclared military clash between the Soviet Union and China in 1969, centered on Zhenbao (Damansky) Island on the Ussuri River, with additional skirmishes in Xinjiang, ending with a Soviet‑declared victory and a return to the status‑quo ante bellum.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Battle_of_Zhenbao_(Damansky)_Island'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1969 Zhenbao (Damansky) Island clash was an armed confrontation between Soviet and Chinese forces over the disputed border island, resulting in casualties on both sides and a temporary cease‑fire; the dispute persisted until later border treaties transferred the island to China.', 'bias_comment': 'English accounts stress Soviet victory and Chinese premeditation, portraying the event as a Soviet‑won, nuclear‑brink flashpoint. Russian sources depict a defensive Soviet triumph, highlighting Chinese aggression and Soviet heroism. Chinese narratives frame the clash as a defensive response to Soviet intrusion, stressing strategic realignment. These variations reflect each nation’s political legacy and historiographic aims, influencing emphasis on aggression, victory, and broader Cold‑War implications.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenbao_Island_incident', 'key_points': ['**A Soviet‑won, undeclared war sparked by China’s pre‑planned aggression** – After the 1964 border talks collapsed, Chinese forces deliberately ambushed Soviet border guards on Zhenbao (Damansky) Island in March\\u202f1969; Soviet archival evidence shows the Chinese acted as the initiator, while both sides later claimed victory.', '**The clash brought the world’s two biggest communist powers to the brink of nuclear war** – Massive troop buildups (Soviet 375\\u202f000, Chinese 1.5\\u202fmillion) and the threat of Soviet nuclear use versus China’s “people’s war” deterrence turned the border dispute into a high‑stakes Cold‑War flashpoint.', '**It reshaped global alignments: China turned to the United States** – To counterbalance the Soviet threat, Beijing opened secret contacts that led to Henry\\u202fKissinger’s 1971 visit and Nixon’s historic 1972 trip, hastening the Sino‑U.S. rapprochement while Sino‑Soviet relations stayed icy.', '**The 1969 confrontation ended only with a cease‑fire and a return to the status quo**, but the border remained unresolved for decades; it was finally settled in a series of China‑Russia treaties (1991, 2003, 2008), granting China control of Zhenbao Island and most disputed islands.', '**Historians view the conflict as a mix of strategic brinkmanship and Mao’s domestic political calculus** – Soviet scholars stress the danger of a Chinese mass invasion, while Chinese analysts emphasize the need to demonstrate strength and deter Soviet “imperialism,” underscoring how internal politics drove an otherwise avoidable border war.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC', 'key_points': ['**Силовой эпизод советско‑китайского раскола** – в марте\\u202f1969\\u202fгода на спорный остров\\u202fДаманский (по‑китайски\\u202fЧжэньбао) произошли две крупные стычки (2\\u202fи\\u202f15\\u202fмарта), в ходе которых советские пограничники, подкреплённые батальонами армии и артиллерией «Град», отразили атаки китайского контингента в\\u202fоколо\\u202f2\\u202fтыс.\\u202fчеловек, нанеся ему существенные потери.', '**Разные интерпретации конфликта** – советская официальная позиция обвиняла китайцев в провокациях, «вводя» свои силы на советскую территорию, тогда как китайские источники отмечали, что советские пограничники сами нарушили границу и агрессивно вытеснили мирных китайских рыбаков; обе стороны используют эпизод как доказательство имперского (по‑советски – ревизионистского) «нападения» противника.', '**Крупные человеческие и материальные потери** – у СССР погибло 58\\u202fчеловек (ещё 94 ранено), в том числе несколько офицеров; Китай оценивает свои потери от\\u202f100 до\\u202f300 человек (по официальным китайским памятникам – 68 захороненных). Станки, БТР‑ы и танки Т‑62 были уничтожены, а советские танки и БТР‑ы существенно повреждены.', '**Политический итог** – несмотря на тактическую победу СССР, конфликт усилил общий советско‑китайский раскол и привёл к длительным переговорам; в\\u202f2004\\u202fгоду остров окончательно передали КНР, а в советском памятнике подчёркнуты героизм советских пограничников (пять Героев Советского Союза).', '**Символическое значение** – Даманский стал ярким примером того, как локальная пограничная битва может стать политическим индикатором холодной войны, демонстрируя одновременно военную готовность СССР к защите границ и готовность к дипломатическому компромиссу после обострения отношений.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8F%8D%E5%AE%9D%E5%B2%9B%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'key_points': ['**1969\\u202fZhenbao (Damansky) Island clash – a brief but fierce Sino‑Soviet border fight** in which Chinese PLA forces (≈100 troops) repelled a larger Soviet contingent (≈300 troops), captured a Soviet\\u202fT‑62 tank and secured de‑facto control of the disputed island.', '**A turning point in the Cold War:** the incident amplified Soviet nuclear‑threat posturing toward China, prompting Beijing to portray the Soviet Union as the primary aggressor and to shift its strategic calculus toward rapprochement with the United\\u202fStates and the West.', '**Domestic military and political fallout:** the battle exposed the PLA’s shortcomings against modern Soviet armor, driving an accelerated programme of indigenous anti‑tank weapons (e.g., 73‑type AT\\u202fgun,\\u202f红箭‑73 missiles) and a nationwide “deep‑dig, stock‑grain, no‑domination” war‑readiness campaign orchestrated by Mao Zedong.', '**Long‑term diplomatic resolution:** despite the 1969‑1970s tensions, the two sides eventually signed the 1991 China‑Russia border agreement (later upheld by Russia after the USSR’s collapse), formally recognizing Zhenbao Island as Chinese territory and easing the broader Sino‑Soviet border dispute.', '**Overall stance of the article:** it foregrounds the Chinese narrative of a defensive, victorious response to Soviet aggression, underscores the incident’s role in reshaping China’s foreign policy, and treats the Soviet nuclear threat as a catalyst for China’s strategic realignment during the late‑Cold‑War era.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of proxy wars', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_proxy_wars'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_proxy_wars', 'name': 'List of proxy wars', 'years': '415\\u202fBC–1990s', 'short_description': 'A chronological list of wars in which major powers backed opposing sides to pursue their interests, ranging from the 5th‑century\\u202fBC Sicilian Expedition to Cold‑War conflicts of the 1990s.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Introductory definition paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Proxy wars involve smaller states fighting on behalf of more powerful nations, receiving political, financial, or military support. They have occurred from antiquity (e.g., the Sicilian Expedition) to the Cold War era, shaping regional outcomes and often allowing great powers to advance rival interests without direct confrontation.', 'bias_comment': 'Western narratives often stress great‑power manipulation, whereas Russian and Chinese sources may frame conflicts as anti‑imperialist or liberation struggles. Language‑specific articles can differ in labeling parties as \"aggressors\" versus \"freedom fighters\" and in emphasizing geopolitical versus ideological motives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_proxy_wars', 'key_points': ['**Proxy wars are conflicts where smaller states fight on behalf of larger powers**, receiving political, financial or military backing that lets the great powers pursue rival interests without direct confrontation.', '**They have occurred throughout history, from ancient battles such as the Sicilian Expedition (415‑413\\u202fBC) to modern Cold‑War confrontations**, showing that the practice predates the 20th‑century ideological split.', '**Key 20th‑century examples illustrate the Cold War’s “East‑West” rivalry:** the Greek, Korean, and Vietnam wars, as well as numerous insurgencies in Africa, Asia and Latin America, were fought by local factions supported respectively by the United States and its allies or by the Soviet Union, China and their allies.', '**Outcomes vary, but many proxy wars ended with the victor’s backer consolidating influence** (e.g., Nationalist victory in the Finnish Civil War backed by Germany, Turkish victory in its War of Independence backed by nationalist forces, North Vietnamese victory supported by communist bloc).  In several cases the war produced stalemates or compromises that reshaped regional borders and political orders.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Tielieketi', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tielieketi'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tielieketi', 'name': 'Tieliekoti incident', 'years': '1969', 'short_description': 'The Tieliekoti incident was a border clash on August 13, 1969 between Soviet and Chinese troops near Tieliekoti in Xinjiang, where Soviet forces eliminated about 30 Chinese soldiers and captured four.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Tieliekoti_incident'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 13\\u202fAugust\\u202f1969 near Lake Zhalanashkol, Soviet border troops clashed with a Chinese unit, killing roughly 30 Chinese soldiers, capturing four, and losing two of their own. The area later became part of a China‑Kazakhstan border settlement.', 'bias_comment': 'Russian sources stress Soviet heroism, precise casualty figures, and award honors, framing the event as a defensive triumph. French accounts add Soviet casualties, unit details, and emphasize the joint 1999 border declaration. English text is a brief, citation‑poor stub, omitting Soviet losses and awards, and flags later territorial claims as unsourced. These differences reflect national narratives and varying source reliability.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tielieketi', 'key_points': ['**Location & name** – Tielieketi (also called Terekty) sits in Yumin County, Xinjiang, China, right on the China‑Kazakhstan border; its name derives from the intermittent Terekty River that flows across the frontier.', '**1969 border clash** – On\\u202f13\\u202fAugust\\u202f1969, during the Sino‑Soviet border conflict, Soviet border troops eliminated a Chinese unit of roughly 30 soldiers and captured four. Soviet sources frame the incident as a response to a night‑long Chinese “border violation” and even note that the Chinese detachment was equipped with professional video‑recording gear.', '**Post‑Soviet territorial claims** – The article asserts that after the USSR’s dissolution the area was administered by Kazakhstan and was handed back to China in a 1999 China‑Kazakhstan border agreement, but these statements are uncited and flagged as needing verification.', '**Reliability note** – The page is a stub, marked for lacking sufficient citations, and many of its key claims—especially regarding the later border settlement—remain unsourced.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tielieketi', 'key_points': ['**Localisation\\u202f:** Terekti (ou Tielieketi) est un hameau du Xinjiang, Chine, situé dans le comté de Yumin, à la frontière avec le Kazakhstan, nommé d’après la rivière intermittente Terekti.', '**Conflit de 1969\\u202f:** Le 13\\u202faoût\\u202f1969, dans le cadre de la rupture sino‑soviétique, des troupes frontalières soviétiques ont affronté une unité chinoise près du lac Zhalanashkol\\u202f; les Soviétiques ont éliminé≈30 soldats chinois, en ont capturé quatre, et ont perdu deux hommes. Les sources soviétiques soulignent que les Chinois violaient régulièrement la frontière la nuit précédente et étaient équipés d’appareils photographiques et d’une caméra.', '**Unités impliquées\\u202f:** Le combat a opposé les forces de la 78ᵉ division de chars et de la 155ᵉ division de fusiliers motorisés soviétiques à la détachement chinois.', '**Conséquences territoriales\\u202f:** Après l’effondrement de l’URSS, la zone a été administrée par le Kazakhstan ; en 1999, la Chine et le Kazakhstan ont signé une déclaration conjointe réglant la frontière, et Tielieketi a été cédé à la Chine.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82_%D1%83_%D0%BE%D0%B7%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%96%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%8C', 'key_points': ['**Soviet‑Chinese skirmish\\u202f–\\u202f13\\u202fAugust\\u202f1969** – Chinese troops crossed the USSR‑China frontier near Lake\\u202fZhalanashkol (Semipalatinsk region, Kazakh\\u202fSSR). Soviet border guards, after failed negotiations, met the incursion with armored personnel carriers, entrenched positions and a counter‑offensive that pushed the intruders back onto Chinese soil.', '**Casualties and claims of heroism** – The Soviet side suffered 2 dead and 10 wounded; Chinese losses were far heavier (≈19 killed, 3 captured, many wounded). The action was portrayed in Soviet sources as a disciplined defence that “saved the border,” and more than thirty border‑guards received high state awards (Order of Lenin, Red Banner, etc.).', '**Political fallout** – The clash was the largest post‑Damansky confrontation and intensified Soviet‑Chinese tensions, prompting high‑level talks in September\\u202f1969 that led to a cease‑fire. The dispute over the exact border line remained until a 1998 agreement (Shanghaï‑5 summit) transferred the contested sector to China; a Chinese monument was erected there in 2008.', '**Dual naming and perspective** – Russian accounts call it the “border conflict at Lake\\u202fZhalanashkol,” while Chinese sources refer to the “Teretki incident,” underscoring how each side frames the episode as a defensive action against a provocation.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Category:International incidents - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:International_incidents'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:International_incidents', 'name': 'Category:International incidents', 'years': 'Various', 'short_description': 'A Wikipedia category that lists 29 pages covering a wide range of international incidents, including diplomatic, border, combat, and maritime events from the 19th to 21st centuries.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Pages in category \"International incidents\"'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia category \"International incidents\" compiles events involving diplomatic, military, or legal disputes between nations, organized into Border, Combat, Diplomatic, and Maritime sub‑categories, covering incidents from the 18th century to the present.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language coverage tends to prioritize incidents affecting Western or English‑speaking states, often using terms like \"affair\" or \"crisis\" that highlight singular events. Other language Wikipedias may list additional regional disputes, use alternative naming conventions, or frame events within different geopolitical narratives, leading to variations in perceived importance, scope, and interpretation across linguistic editions.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:International_incidents', 'key_points': ['**Broad umbrella for cross‑border crises** – the “International incidents” category aggregates events that have drawn diplomatic, military, or legal attention between nations, ranging from border skirmishes to high‑profile diplomatic disputes.', '**Four distinct sub‑themes** – the category is organized into **Border incidents**, **Combat incidents**, **Diplomatic incidents**, and **International maritime incidents**, each with dozens of pages that illustrate the different ways states can clash.', '**Diverse historical span** – the 29 listed articles cover everything from 18th‑century naval seizures (e.g., *Capture of Port\\u202fEgmont*) to contemporary flashpoints (*Ryanair Flight\\u202f4978*, *Assange v Swedish Prosecution Authority*), showing that international tensions are a long‑standing, recurring phenomenon.', '**Emphasis on the impact of singular events** – many entries are named “affairs,” “incidents,” or “crises,” underscoring how a single episode—whether a hijacking, a shooting, or a diplomatic remark—can shape broader international relations and become a focal point for scholarly and public discussion.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '珍宝岛事件', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8F%8D%E5%AE%9D%E5%B2%9B%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8F%8D%E5%AE%9D%E5%B2%9B%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'name': '珍宝岛事件', 'years': '1969', 'short_description': '1969年3月，中国人民解放军与苏联军队在黑龙江的珍宝岛（达曼斯基岛）发生武装冲突，争夺岛屿主权，冲突以中国获胜并实际控制该岛告终。', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#背景'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"In March 1969, Chinese and Soviet forces clashed on Zhenbao (Damansky) Island, a disputed river island on the Ussuri, resulting in casualties on both sides; the conflict heightened Cold‑War tensions, led to later border agreements recognizing Chinese sovereignty, and remains a symbol in both nations' histories.\", 'bias_comment': 'Chinese (zh) sources stress defensive self‑defence, mass mobilization and the incident’s role in the Cultural Revolution, often using state‑controlled propaganda language. Russian (ru) accounts highlight Soviet heroism, label the clash a Soviet victory, and portray Chinese actions as provocation, sometimes inflating Soviet casualties. English (en) articles aim for a balanced narrative but rely heavily on secondary academic works and may under‑represent primary nationalist narratives, leading to a more diplomatic framing. Across languages, casualty figures, the characterization of the conflict (defensive vs aggressive), and the strategic interpretation (turning point toward the US vs Soviet victory) differ, reflecting each nation’s historiographical agenda.', 'languages_used': ['zh', 'ru', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8F%8D%E5%AE%9D%E5%B2%9B%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'key_points': ['**A brief but decisive Sino‑Soviet border war** – In March\\u202f1969 the People’s Liberation Army fought a 15‑day clash on Zhenbao (Daman) Island, seized a Soviet T‑62 tank and established de‑facto Chinese control of the disputed territory.', '**A Cold‑War turning point** – Soviet “surgical” nuclear threats and diplomatic pressure convinced Chinese leaders that the USSR now posed a greater danger than the United States, prompting a strategic shift toward rapprochement with Washington that opened the road to the 1970s\\u202f–\\u202fUS‑China détente.', '**Domestic political mobilisation** – The incident was portrayed in China as a defensive “self‑defence counter‑attack,” fueling massive propaganda, nationwide “deep‑digging‑and‑stockpiling” campaigns and the evacuation of senior Party and military leaders, underscoring its symbolic weight within the Cultural Revolution.', '**Long‑term diplomatic resolution** – Subsequent border agreements (1979, 1990, 2008) formally recognised Chinese sovereignty over Zhenbao and related islands, ending the territorial dispute and laying the groundwork for the normalisation of Sino‑Russian relations.', '**Source bias warning** – The article relies heavily on first‑hand (primary) accounts, signaling the need for more secondary scholarly sources to provide a balanced, historiographical perspective.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC', 'key_points': ['**Силовой конфликт\\u202f— советская победа, но с тяжкими потерями** – В марте\\u202f1969\\u202fгода советские пограничники, подкреплённые танками, БТР и артиллерией, отразили нападения китайских войск на остров Даманский, потеряв при этом 58 убитых и 94 раненых, тогда как китайская сторона понесла от\\u202f100\\u202fдо\\u202f300\\u202fпотерь.', '**Корни спора в территориальных притязаниях и политическом расколе** – Китай требовал пересмотра границ, указывая на несправедливость советских‑китайских договоров XIX\\u202fв. и обвиняя Советский Союз в «социалистическом империализме», тогда как советская позиция трактовала конфликт как защиту суверенитета и реакцию на китайские провокации.', '**События использовались в пропаганде обеих сторон** – Советские и китайские СМИ выступали с обвинениями в адрес друг друга (китайские издания «долой новых царей», советская «Позор провокаторам!»), а в США подчёркивали, что инцидент был подстроен для внутренней политической мобилизации Китая.', '**Последующее урегулирование и передача острова** – После прекращения огня и переговоров в 1969\\u202fгоду советские пограничники покинули остров; в 2004\\u202fгоду Даманский официально перешёл под юрисдикцию КНР.', '**Разные исторические оценки** – Российские источники подчёркивают спланированную китайскую провокацию и героизм советских бойцов (пять героев СССР), тогда как китайские публикации описывают советскую агрессию как нарушение международного права и вынуждают к самообороне.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zenbao_Island', 'key_points': ['**Geography & Status:** Zhenbao (Damansky) Island is a tiny 0.74\\u202fkm² wetland on the Ussuri River, officially part of China’s Heilongjiang Province and designated a Ramsar‑protected nature reserve since 2011.', '**Cold‑War Flashpoint:** The island became the focal point of a Sino‑Soviet border dispute. China argued the thalweg principle gave it rights to most river islands, while the USSR claimed the island; fierce battles in 1969 caused heavy casualties and even raised fears of a World‑War‑III escalation.', '**Diplomatic Resolution:** After decades of tension, the 1991 Sino‑Soviet Border Agreement recognized the island as Chinese territory and Soviet troops withdrew, ending the militarized dispute.', '**Cultural Memory:** The 1969 clash remains a touchstone in Chinese and Russian narratives—evidenced by a 2004 Russian documentary and anecdotes from U.S. President Nixon’s memoirs about the “hot‑line” breakdown.', '**Strategic Symbolism:** While the island is g