ризованный батальон) развернула крупный огонь, отбросив китайские силы.', '**Разные версии начала конфликта:** советские источники считают, что китайские пограничники провоцировали и вторглись на советскую территорию; китайская сторона – что советские пограничники сами устраивали провокации, а его действия 2\\u202fмартa были «самообороной».', '**Политический итог:** несмотря на тактическую победу СССР, 2004\\u202fг. остров официально передан КНР в рамках договорённостей по урегулированию пограничных разногласий.', '**Наследие и память:** пять советских военнослужащих получили звание Героя Советского Союза; событие стало предметом пропагандистских статей, публичных демонстраций и музейных экспозиций, подчёркивающих как советскую стойкость, так и китайскую решимость защищать «свои» территории.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E7%8F%8D%E5%AE%9D%E5%B2%9B%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'key_points': ['**1969 年 3 月的珍宝岛冲突**是中苏边界争端的核心战役之一。中国人民解放军在短期激烈交火后取得胜利，实际控制了岛屿；双方死亡人数在官方统计上出现巨大差异，凸显了冲突的激烈程度。', '**冲突几乎演变为核危机**：苏联军方高层曾公开讨论对中国进行“外科手术式”核打击的计划，甚至动用中程弹道导弹威慑。美国在尼克松政府的斡旋下向苏方施压，帮助中方避免了核战争的爆发。', '**政治层面的深远影响**：珍宝岛事件让中共高层认识到中苏矛盾已超出普通边境摩擦，促使毛泽东把对美、对西方的关系放在更重要位置，随后实现了中美关系破冰、在联合国取代中华民国合法席位等外交突破。', '**军事与后续边界解决**：冲突暴露了中国装甲和反坦克装备的不足，直接推动了 73、86 型反坦克炮和红箭‑73 导弹等新型武器的研发。事件后期，中苏通过谈判逐步缓和，1991 年俄方正式承认珍宝岛归属中国，标志着长达半世纪的领土争议基本结束。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Talk:Albanian–Chinese split - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Sino-Albanian_split'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Sino-Albanian_split', 'name': 'Albanian–Chinese split', 'years': '1976-1978', 'short_description': 'The Albanian–Chinese split was the ideological and diplomatic rupture between Albania and the People’s Republic of China, culminating in the termination of Chinese aid and assistance to Albania in 1978 after disputes following Mao Zedong’s death and criticism of the Three Worlds Theory.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': \"Removed section quoting “Soon after Mao's death in 1976… invite Tito to Beijing in 1977… end of assistance programs for Albania in 1978.”\"}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"Between 1976‑1978 Albania and China ended their political alliance. Talk‑page edits addressed source verification, Tito's historical role, stylistic consistency, reduction of ideological bias, and administrative decisions like article naming and hyphen usage.\", 'bias_comment': 'English edits aim to temper Hoxha‑centric rhetoric and add Chinese viewpoints, showing a bias toward balanced narratives. Albanian and Chinese language pages may foreground national perspectives, altering emphasis on Tito, Mao, or Soviet relations.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Sino-Albanian_split', 'key_points': ['**Source quality matters** – Editors removed an unreferenced “anonymous comment” link, stressing that only historically‑scientific, verifiable citations belong on the page.', '**Clarify historical context** – A brief debate highlighted the relevance of Tito to the post‑Mao split, with contributors agreeing that his role as an “enemy of international socialism” helps explain Albania’s earlier break with the USSR.', '**Stylistic consistency** – Multiple threads tackled punctuation (en‑dash vs. hyphen for “Sino‑Albanian”), transliteration of names, acronym introduction, and passive‑voice usage, all aimed at aligning the article with Wikipedia’s style guides.', '**Reduce ideological bias** – A major suggestion was to trim the over‑reliance on Enver\\u202fHoxha’s quotations, paraphrase excessive rhetoric, and balance the narrative with more historian commentary and Chinese perspectives.', '**Administrative outcomes** – A 2014 move request to rename the article was rejected; the dash‑usage discussion ended with consensus favoring a hyphen, reflecting the community’s commitment to editorial standards.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Tutung', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tutung'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tutung', 'name': 'Battle of Tutung', 'years': '1934', 'short_description': 'In 1934, Soviet Red Army attacked Chinese Muslim 36th Division led by Ma Zhongyang on the frozen Tutung River in Xinjiang, using bombers and mustard gas; Chinese troops wore sheepskins and fought with swords, inflicting heavy casualties before retreating.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1934, during the Soviet‑backed Xinjiang campaign, General Ma Zhongying’s Chinese Muslim 36th Division fought Soviet forces near Tutung. The clash involved tanks, artillery, bombers and, according to some reports, chemical weapons, resulting in heavy casualties and a Soviet tactical victory.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language accounts often cite Soviet archives and Western secondary sources, which may understate Chinese perspectives or overemphasize Soviet use of chemical weapons. Chinese-language histories tend to highlight Ma’s bravery and unconventional tactics, while Russian sources may downplay controversial weaponry. These disparities can skew perceived causality and moral judgments across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tutung', 'key_points': ['**A brutal clash in the Soviet‑run Xinjiang campaign (1934):** The Battle of Tutung pitted General\\u202fMa\\u202fZhongying’s Chinese Muslim 36th Division against two Soviet brigades equipped with tanks, artillery and bomber aircraft.', '**Soviet use of chemical warfare:** Red‑Army bombers reportedly dropped mustard‑gas shells, underscoring the Soviet reliance on overwhelming firepower and illicit weapons to force a breakthrough.', '**Chinese troops’ desperate, unconventional counter‑attack:** Frozen‑river defenders camouflaged themselves in sheepskins, charged Soviet machine‑gun nests with curved swords at close range, and temporarily stalled a Soviet pincer movement.', '**Heavy losses on both sides and a forced retreat:** Casualties were described as “heavy” for the Chinese and Soviet forces alike; ultimately Ma ordered a withdrawal, handing the Soviets a tactical victory.', '**Article status:** The entry is marked as a stub, indicating that the episode is still under‑documented in Western scholarship.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '64th Fighter Aviation Corps', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/64th_Fighter_Aviation_Corps'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/64th_Fighter_Aviation_Corps', 'name': '64th Fighter Aviation Corps', 'years': '1950-1956', 'short_description': 'The 64th Fighter Aviation Corps was a Soviet Air Forces unit that operated MiG-15 fighters in the Korean War, providing air cover for Chinese and North Korean forces and claiming a 3.4:1 kill ratio, before withdrawing after the 1953 cease‑fire and disbanding in 1956.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 64th Fighter Aviation Corps, a Soviet unit stationed in Manchuria, covertly joined the Korean War, flying MiG‑15s and claiming high kill ratios; the 63rd Guard Fighter Aviation Regiment, a WWII Soviet unit, fought on the Eastern Front, later modernizing and serving until 2001.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources stress covert involvement and contested kill ratios, reflecting Western scrutiny, while Russian sources emphasize heroic lineage and honors, typical of national military historiography. Reported figures differ, and the two distinct units risk conflation.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/64th_Fighter_Aviation_Corps', 'key_points': ['**Soviet air power was a decisive, though covert, factor in the Korean War** – the 64th Fighter Aviation Corps, based in Manchuria, supplied MiG‑15s that flew over 64,000 sorties and engaged in 1,872 air