st heavily influenced—by the ideological clash between the Western and Eastern blocs.', '**Proxy wars as the dominant pattern:**\\u202fIn most cases the two superpowers supplied weapons, funding, advisers or troops to local forces, turning local disputes (e.g., Greek Civil War, Korean War, Congo Crisis, Angolan War of Independence) into indirect battles between capitalism and communism.', '**Mixed outcomes, but frequent bloc victories:**\\u202fThe “Result” column records a roughly even split: a sizable number of conflicts ended with a **Western‑bloc victory**, a comparable number with an **Eastern‑bloc victory**, and several were **inconclusive** or settled by negotiated settlements, highlighting that neither side achieved an outright global dominance.', '**Cold‑War conflicts spanned the entire era:**\\u202fThe table covers the whole Cold‑War timeline (1944\\u202f–\\u202f1996), beginning with immediate post‑World‑War unrest and ending with the lingering insurgencies of the 1990s, underscoring how the superpower competition shaped world politics for nearly five decades.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of American spies', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_American_spies'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_American_spies', 'name': 'List of American spies', 'years': 'c.1775–2020s', 'short_description': 'A comprehensive list of American individuals and groups involved in espionage, spanning from the Revolutionary War era through the Civil War, World Wars, the Cold War, and modern intelligence cases up to the 21st‑century.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#American_Revolution_era_spies'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia page lists American spies from the Revolutionary era to the present, categorizing individuals by the side they served and noting notable figures, while stating the list is incomplete and open to contributions.', 'bias_comment': \"The English article aims for a balanced ledger, but other