aders toward institutional inclusivity or power‑politics interpretations.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_88', 'key_points': ['**Resolution\\u202f88 (8\\u202fNov\\u202f1950) formally invited a\\u202fPeople’s Republic of China delegate to attend the Security Council’s review of the UN Command’s Korean‑War report**, invoking rule\\u202f39 of the provisional procedures.', '**The motion passed with a solid majority – 8 votes in favour, 2 against (Republic of China and Cuba) and one abstention (Egypt)**, showing broad Council support for involving the PRC despite the ongoing diplomatic split over “China’s representation.”', '**The resolution underscored the UN’s willingness to broaden participation in deliberations on the Korean conflict**, reinforcing the legitimacy of the UN Command’s actions against North Korean aggression.', '**Opposition came mainly from the Republic of\\u202fChina, which still claimed the Chinese seat, and from Cuba, reflecting Cold‑War alignments**, while Egypt’s abstention signaled a cautious neutrality.', '**As a short‑stub entry, the article notes the resolution’s place within the larger Korean‑War and early‑Cold‑War UN security‑council actions**, linking to related resolutions and the broader list of 1950 decisions.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'United Nations Security Council Resolution 345', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_345'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_345', 'name': 'United Nations Security Council Resolution 345', 'years': '1974', 'short_description': \"UN Security Council Resolution 345, adopted on 17 January 1974, expanded the Council's working languages to include Chinese alongside English, French, Russian and Spanish.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_wor