on is incomplete and inviting further contributions.', 'bias_comment': 'The English version provides more granular detail for UN/ROK forces than for NKPA/Chinese units, potentially reflecting source availability or editorial focus. Korean and Chinese language pages tend to emphasize their own forces, offering richer unit breakdowns for the North, while Russian sources may highlight Soviet involvement. These variations illustrate how national narratives shape the depth and emphasis of unit listings.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War_order_of_battle', 'key_points': ['**A massive, multinational “order of battle”** – the article catalogues every major ground, naval and air unit that fought in the Korean War, from the ROK’s pre‑war divisions and the UN Command’s U.S. corps and allied battalions to the NKPA’s infantry divisions, the Chinese People’s Volunteer Army’s army groups, and even Soviet aviators.', '**Pro‑Southern (UN) forces dominate the listing** – detailed break‑downs are provided for the Republic of Korea Army, the U.S. Eighth Army and its I, IX and X Corps, plus the long roster of Commonwealth, Asian, African and Latin‑American contingents (e.g., French, Dutch, Turkish, Thai, Philippine, Ethiopian “Kagnew” Battalion, etc.).', '**Pro‑Northern forces are summarized more compactly** – the North Korean People’s Army divisions and Chinese Volunteer Army army groups are enumerated, but with less unit‑by‑unit detail than the UN side, reflecting the article’s focus on the coalition’s composition.', '**Non‑combat medical and support units are highlighted** – the page notes the extensive humanitarian contribution of neutral nations (Swedish Red Cross, Danish hospital ship, Indian parachute field ambulance, Italian Red Cross, etc.), underscoring the war’s global humanitarian footprint.', '**The list is acknowledged as incomplete** – a prominent notice warns that the compilation is still missing entries (as of August\\u202f2008) and invites readers to help fill the gaps, emphasizing the collaborative, still‑evolving nature of the resource.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Luo Guibo', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luo_Guibo'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luo_Guibo', 'name': 'Luo Guibo', 'years': '1954-1957', 'short_description': \"Luo Guibo became the People's Republic of China's first ambassador to North Vietnam in September 1954, heading the Chinese Political Advisory Group and serving in the post until 1957.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"Luo Guibo was a Chinese Communist Party official who served as liaison to Ho Chi Minh, led the Chinese Political Advisory Group, and became the first PRC ambassador to North Vietnam (1954‑1957), influencing Vietnam's military strategies, land‑reform policies, and Sino‑Vietnamese relations.\", 'bias_comment': 'Chinese-language histories portray Luo as a key architect of revolutionary solidarity, while Vietnamese sources may credit domestic agency and downplay external direction. Western academic works often frame his actions within Cold‑War narratives, emphasizing Chinese intervention. These linguistic and historiographical lenses can shift perceived agency and motive.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luo_Guibo', 'key_points': ['**Key conduit of Chinese‑Vietnamese cooperation:**\\u202fLuo\\u202fGuibo was dispatched in early\\u202f1950 as the CCP’s liaison to Hồ\\u202fChí\\u202fMinh, later heading the Chinese Political Advisory Group and serving as the first PRC ambassador to North Vietnam (1954‑1957). His presence anchored Beijing’s political, military and economic assistance throughout the First Indochina War.', '**Strategic architect of Vietnam’s war‑play:**\\u202fHe helped organize the Chinese Military Advisory Group, supplied weapons and supplies, and authored the “north‑west strategy” that steered Viet\\u202fMinh offensives away from the costly Tonkin delta toward the weak French positions in Sơn\\u202fLa, Lai\\u202fChâu, Nghĩa\\u202fLộ and Upper\\u202fLaos—plans that underpinned the decisive Điện\\u202fBiên\\u202fPhủ campaign.', '**Driver of socio‑political reforms:**\\u202fDrawing on his own experience with land‑reform in Jiangxi, Luo drafted a six‑point programme (Sept‑Oct\\u202f1952) urging the Vietnamese Workers’ Party to launch full‑scale land redistribution, mass mobilisation and cadre training. His proposals sparked scholarly debate over whether Chinese pressure or Vietnamese initiative ultimately triggered the DRV’s 1953‑54 land‑reform drive.', '**High‑ranking PRC official and outspoken diplomat:**\\u202fFrom 1957 to 1970 he served as Vice‑Minister of Foreign Affairs, later governing Shanxi province, and even issued a formal protest to the British chargé d’affaires over the 1967 Hong\\u202fKong riots, denouncing “atrocities” and “collusion with American imperialism.”', '**Legacy as memoirist and emblem of revolutionary internationalism:**\\u202fLuo authored *Revolutionary Recollections*, presenting his career as a model of proletarian internationalism and cementing his reputation as one of China’s most influential advisors in the early Cold‑War Asian theater.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Sino-Third World relations', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Third_World_relations'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Third_World_relations', 'name': 'Sino-Third World relations', 'years': '1950s–present', 'short_description': \"Sino-Third World relations describe the diplomatic, economic, and ideological interactions between the People's Republic of China, the Republic of China (Taiwan), and developing nations across Asia, Africa, and Latin America from the 1950s to the present, encompassing phases such as the Bandung Line, support for liberation movements, the Three Worlds Theory, and modern South‑South cooperation.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#History'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since the 1950s, China has portrayed the developing world as a unified ‘Third World’ bloc to counter super‑power influence. Its policy alternated between revolutionary support and pragmatic trade, using diplomatic, military, and economic aid to expand influence, while the PRC and ROC competed for recognition.', 'bias_comment': 'Chinese sources often frame the partnership as solidarity and anti‑imperialism, downplaying strategic self‑interest, whereas Western accounts may stress geopolitical motives. Language differences can affect emphasis: Mandarin narratives highlight “mutual development,” while English analyses may focus on power projection, leading to divergent portrayals of the same actions.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Third_World_relations', 'key_points': ['**China frames the developing world as a pivotal “Third World” bloc** – from the 1950s onward Chinese leaders have portrayed Asia, Africa and Latin America as a collective force that can counterbalance super‑power hegemony and legitimize China’s own rise on the global stage.', '**Policy has swung between revolutionary activism and pragmatic partnership** – early PRC outreach combined ideological support for liberation movements with aggressive border actions; by the 1970s the “Three‑World Theory” recast the Third World as a strategic ally against the USSR and US, while the 1980s shifted toward “peaceful coexistence,” trade, joint ventures and “South‑South cooperation.”', '**China leverages the Third World for political and economic leverage** – it consistently backs the Group of\\u202f77, supplies military aid, technical assistance and low‑interest loans, and uses multilateral platforms (e.g., UN vetoes, BRICS, BRI) to expand influence while presenting itself as a “development partner” rather than a hegemon.', '**Both Chinese states (PRC and ROC) have competed for legitimacy in the Third World** – the PRC has cultivated diplomatic ties and development projects across Africa, Asia and Latin America, whereas the ROC has historically pursued limited military and political support in selected conflicts to maintain its own international recognition.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'September 11 Great Armed Clan Feud of Matian', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_Great_Armed_Clan_Feud_of_Matian'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_Great_Armed_Clan_Feud_of_Matian', 'name': 'September 11 Great Armed Clan Feud of Matian', 'years': '1993', 'short_description': 'The September\\u202f11\\u202fGreat Armed Clan Feud of Matian was a 34‑hour armed conflict on 11\\u202fSeptember\\u202f1993 between villages of Matian and Jinggang in Chenzhou, Hunan, China, involving thousands of peasants using dynamite, homemade guns and cannons; the fighting halted railway service, caused five deaths, and required dispatch of over 1,000 police.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph describing the feud'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 11\\u202fSeptember 1993, a violent clan conflict erupted in Matian, Hunan, involving six townships. Using dynamite, homemade weapons, and cannons, the fighting lasted 34\\u202fhours, disrupted the Beijing–Guangzhou railway, caused five deaths, dozens of injuries, and prompted a large police response.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize the scale and the state’s heavy‑handed response, while Chinese language accounts may frame the event as a local dispute resolved by government mediation, potentially downplaying the violence. Cross‑language differences can affect portrayal of causes, victim counts, and the role of authorities.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_Great_Armed_Clan_Feud_of_Matian', 'key_points': ['**A violent, large‑scale clan war erupted on 11\\u202fSept\\u202f1993 in Matian, Hunan, pitting villagers from six townships against each other with dynamite, homemade guns and cannons, turning a two‑square‑kilometre area into a battlefield for 34\\u202fhours.**', '**The feud had deep historical roots: a 1928 killing of Jinggang peasants by a Matian warlord and a regional pattern of clan clashes (over 1,500 incidents in 1987‑88 alone) set the stage for the 1993 bloodshed.**', '**The conflict disrupted a major national artery—the Beijing–Guangzhou railway was halted for five hours, five people died, dozens were injured, and more than 1,000 police were deployed, ultimately using tear‑gas and mass arrests to restore order.**', '**The aftermath revealed the extent of the villagers’ armament (dozens of homemade cannons, hundreds of kilograms of dynamite) and underscored the Chinese authorities’ heavy‑handed, law‑enforcement‑focused response.**', '**Even years later the rivalry lingered; a 2009 attempt at renewed fighting was only averted through successful government mediation, highlighting ongoing tensions and the importance of state‑led conflict resolution in rural China.**']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '2007 Chinese anti-satellite missile test', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2007_Chinese_anti-satellite_missile_test'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2007_Chinese_anti-satellite_missile_test', 'name': '2007 Chinese anti-satellite missile test', 'years': '2007', 'short_description': 'On 11\\u202fJanuary\\u202f2007, China launched a solid-fuel missile from Xichang that destroyed the FY‑1C weather satellite in a head‑on kinetic intercept at 865\\u202fkm altitude, creating thousands of debris pieces.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#lead'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"On 11\\u202fJanuary\\u202f2007, China used a DF‑21‑derived SC‑19 missile to destroy the FY‑1C weather satellite at 865\\u202fkm, creating thousands of debris 