d chapters (origins, economic programmes, peasants, famines, bureaucracy, culture, labor camps, and the dictators themselves), the work zeros in on the most salient parallels, intentionally avoiding a comprehensive histories of either revolution.', '**Critical, polemical tone** –\\u202fReviewers describe the volume as a “solid diatribe” and a “powerful reminder of the system of privilege and power” of the Stalin‑Mao regimes, emphasizing the author’s strong stance against both dictators rather than a neutral synthesis.', '**Scholarly but not introductory** –\\u202fThe book is praised for its meticulous detail and “big‑picture” accuracy, yet scholars warn it presumes prior knowledge of the Russian and Chinese revolutions and is aimed at an academic audience.', '**Title and translation politics** –\\u202fThe English title “Stalin and Mao” replaces the French “La récidive” (the recurrence) and the original chapter label “monstres” with “Dictators,” a change noted by critics as a concession to the political sensitivities of the Chinese University Press.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%BC%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%BC%D0%B0', 'key_points': ['**Надёжный академический консенсус:** большинство историков и политологов рассматривают нацизм и сталинизм как два ярких примера тоталитарных режимов\\u202f—\\u202fсо схожими механизмами контроля (культ личности, массовая пропаганда, терор, концентрационные лагеря), но с разными идеологическими целями (расовая империя\\u202f—\\u202fГитлер,\\u202fмировая пролетарская революция\\u202f—\\u202fСталин).', '**Ключевые теоретики и их акценты:**', '*Ханна Арендт* ввела понятие тоталитаризма, проследила его корни в антисемитизме и «новом империализме», подчеркнула роль беспрекословного набора последователей, двойную природу пропаганды (индиктринация\\u202fvs\\u202fпропаганда) и особую функцию лагерей как инструмента легитимации режима.', '*Карл Фридрих* и *Збигнев Бжезинский* систематизировали «пять столпов» тоталитарных систем (идеология, партийный монополизм, контроль над вооружением, контроль над средствами массовой информации, террористический полицейский надзор) и разместили нацизм и сталинизм в более широкой традиции автократий, адаптированных к индустриальному обществу.', '**Научные разногласия:** некоторые исследователи (Фицпатрик, Гейер) подчеркивают фундаментальные различия между режимами, а другие (Генри Руссо, Буррен, Верт) защищают полезность сравнения как описательного инструмента. Споры о «приравнивании» сталинизма к нацизму привели к политическим конфликтам (Historikerstreit, запрет сравнения в России).']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Spaceflight before 1951', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceflight_before_1951'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceflight_before_1951', 'name': 'Spaceflight before 1951', 'years': '1942-1950', 'short_description': \"Practical spaceflight began during WWII with the German V‑2 rocket; post‑war the U.S. and Soviet programs adapted V‑2s into sounding rockets such as Aerobee and Viking, and by 1950 had conducted 46 Aerobee launches, gathering the first direct measurements of Earth's upper atmosphere.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Between 1942‑1950, WWII‑era rockets like the German V‑2 enabled early high‑altitude research. The US and Soviet Union adapted V‑2 technology, creating sounding rockets and conducting scientific and biological experiments, establishing the technological groundwork for the later space race.', 'bias_comment': 'English article emphasizes the German V‑2’s role and the US‑Soviet rivalry, possibly reflecting Western historiography. Other language versions (e.g., Russian, German) may highlight national achievements, downplay rival nations, and differ in the amount of detail on Soviet programs or German wartime contributions, leading to varied emphasis.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceflight_before_1951', 'key_points': ['**World‑War‑II rockets jump‑started the space age** – The German V‑2 was the first vehicle to breach the 100\\u202fkm “space” threshold (174\\u202fkm in June\\u202f1944), and its capture after the war gave the United States and the Soviet Union a practical, liquid‑fuel platform for post‑war research and military development.', '**Rapid post‑war rocket proliferation turned weapons into scientific tools** – U.S. and Soviet programs rapidly built on V‑2 technology (U.S. Viking and R‑1/R‑2 missiles), while purpose‑built sounding rockets such as the Aerobee (first launched 1947) and Bumper (V‑2 + WAC\\u202fCorporal) turned the former missile into a reliable, high‑altitude research vehicle.', '**Pioneering scientific achievements** – By 1950, 46 Aerobee flights and a series of V‑2 launches collected the first direct measurements of atmospheric density, temperature, ionospheric and magnetic properties, and delivered the first biological payloads (seeds, fruit flies, mice, monkeys). Viking\\u202f4 (May\\u202f1950) became the first sounding rocket launched from a ship and the first to exceed 100\\u202fkm, while V‑2 missions produced the first photographs of large Earth‑surface regions and the first animal‑in‑space flights.', '**Super‑power rivalry sets the stage for the space race** – By the late‑1940s both the United States (V‑2, Aerobee, Viking) and the Soviet Union (R‑1, R‑2, later R‑3 plans) had entered the post‑V‑2 era, establishing parallel national first‑s‑in‑space milestones and creating the technological foundation that would culminate in the launch of the first artificial satellite in 1957.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Foreign interventions by China', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_interventions_by_China'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_interventions_by_China', 'name': 'Foreign interventions by China', 'years': '1950–present', 'short_description': 'China’s foreign interventions began with the Korean War (1950–1953) and have continued through the Vietnam War, Cold‑War conflicts, and into the 21st‑century, including economic aid, military support, and occasional direct military actions up to the present.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#History'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since 1950 China has engaged in foreign interventions ranging from large‑scale troop deployments in the Korean and Vietnam wars to contemporary financial aid, loans, infrastructure projects, and limited military support. These actions are presented as non‑interference but are also described as influencing partner nations’ policies.', 'bias_comment': 'Chinese sources emphasize a \"peaceful, non‑interventionist\" stance, framing aid as mutual development, while Western analyses often highlight debt‑trap and geopolitical motives. Translation choices (e.g., \"strategic support\" vs. \"intervention\") can shift perception, and local language coverage may stress sovereignty concerns differently than English narratives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_interventions_by_China', 'key_points': ['**From “non‑intervention” rhetoric to active power‑projection:**  While China long proclaimed a “peaceful, non‑interventionist” foreign policy, the rise of Beijing as a global economic and military power has turned that motto into a dual strategy—maintaining the façade of non‑interference while using aid, loans, and military support to shape foreign regimes in its favor.', '**Economic leverages as the primary tool of intervention:**  Contemporary Chinese interventions rely heavily on finance rather than troops.  The Export‑Import Bank of China and China Development Bank now lend more to the developing world than the World Bank, funneling billions of dollars, infrastructure projects, and debt‑laden loans to over‑100 nations—especially those sanctioned by the West—to secure political influence and access to resources.', '**Cold‑War‑era military interventions turned “strategic support”:**  In the Korean War China deployed over 200\\u202f000 troops under the People’s Volunteer Army, framing the war as “Resist U.S. Aggression, Aid Korea.”  During the Vietnam War Beijing supplied the North Vietnamese with tens of thousands of weapons, millions of rounds of ammunition, and hundreds of thousands of troops for engineering and air‑defence duties, as well as massive economic aid, cementing its role as a major communist ally.', '**Post‑Cold‑War “soft‑power” coercion in Asia and Latin America:**  China now uses trade, investment, and debt‑relief to keep strategic partners—most notably North Korea, Venezuela, Bolivia, Cuba and others—politically pliant.  Critics warn that this “debt‑trap” diplomacy can lead to outright control of ports, railways and even political decisions, echoing the region‑wide pattern of turning financial dependence into geopolitical leverage.', '**Growing acceptance of overt interventionist doctrine among Chinese elites:**  Academic and policy circles within China increasingly argue that protecting national interests abroad justifies direct or indirect intervention—whether through military aid, large‑scale infrastructure financing, or supplying riot‑control equipment to authoritarian regimes—signalling a shift from the historic “Four‑Nos” (no arms, no alliances, no colonialism, no hegemony) toward a more assertive, interests‑driven foreign policy.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Talk:Amur Annexation - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Amur_Annexation'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Amur_Annexation', 'name': 'Talk:Amur Annexation - Wikipedia', 'years': '1858-1860', 'short_description': 'The Amur Annexation refers to the Russian acquisition of th