u incident was part of a wider campaign to curb “leftist” factionalism and restore order to a destabilised provincial administration.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%8E%A7%E6%B1%9F%E8%B7%AF%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'key_points': ['1979年9月9日，上海控江路因一起抢蟹纠纷升级为警民冲突，警员先行动粗导致围观人群暴力围攻，甚至对女职工吴某进行严重性侵——事件被描绘为警方失职与群众失控的双重暴行。', '现场冲突迅速失控，约2000人聚集，出现抢劫、砸毁摊位、辱骂妇女等群体性犯罪，导致多名警员受伤、无死亡但伤情不明，凸显当时城市治安的脆弱。', '该事件被视为中国大陆“死刑复核权下放”及随后“1983年严打”政策的直接导火索，文章强调其在推动《刑法》《刑事诉讼法》修订及最高人民法院授权省级法院审查死刑案件方面的历史意义。', '法院对涉案的流氓犯人作出有期徒刑判决，体现了事后司法对暴力与性侵行为的严惩，同时也暗示该案在舆论与政策层面引发的强硬治安措施的合法化倾向。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'The Great Gamble', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Gamble'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Gamble', 'name': 'The Great Gamble', 'years': '2009', 'short_description': 'The Great Gamble: The Soviet War in Afghanistan is a 2009 non‑fiction book by Gregory Feifer that examines the Soviet‑Afghan War (1979‑1989) and its impact on both Afghanistan and the Cold War.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Great Gamble is a 2009 non‑fiction book by Gregory Feifer about the 1979‑1989 Soviet‑Afghan war, detailing its origins, battles, and political outcomes, and reviewed by major media outlets.', 'bias_comment': 'The title frames the conflict as a strategic \"great gamble,\" emphasizing Soviet errors and drawing parallels to recent Russian actions, reflecting a Western analytical lens. English reviews stress strategic miscalculations, Russian‑language commentary highlights ideological motives, while Afghan‑language sources focus on humanitarian impacts, showing cross‑language perspective gaps.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Gamble', 'key_points': ['**A 2009 non‑fiction account** of the 1979‑1989 Soviet‑Afghan war written by journalist Gregory\\u202fFeifer, positioned as a detailed narrative of the conflict’s origins, battles, and political fallout.', '**Emphasizes the war as a “great gamble”** for the Soviet Union, drawing parallels to contemporary Russian military interventions and highlighting strategic miscalculations that led to a costly defeat.', '**Received strong coverage from major media**—NPR, *The New Yorker*, *The New York Times*, and Kirkus Reviews—praising its thorough research, engaging storytelling, and balanced appraisal of both Soviet and Afghan perspectives.', '**Classified as a key work in Cold‑War and Afghan‑history literature**, even though the Wikipedia entry remains a stub, underscoring its relevance for scholars and readers interested in the geopolitical lessons of the Soviet‑Afghan war.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Korean Air Lines Flight 902', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902', 'name': 'Korean Air Lines Flight 902', 'years': '1978', 'short_description': 'On 20 April 1978, Korean Air Lines Flight 902, a Boeing 707, was intercepted and shot down by Soviet air defense after entering Soviet airspace, killing two and forcing an emergency landing on the frozen Korpijärvi Lake.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Soviet_air_defence'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 20\\u202fApril\\u202f1978 a Korean Air Lines Boeing\\u202f707 entered Soviet airspace, was hit by a Soviet interceptor missile, and made an emergency landing on frozen Lake\\u202fKorpiarvi; two passengers died, 107 survived, and the incident sparked diplomatic tension during the Cold War.', 'bias_comment': \"Arabic sources stress Soviet aggression and Cold‑War provocation, English accounts present both Soviet and Korean narratives about navigation errors and intercept orders, while Russian texts focus on technical details and dispute over the plane's deviation, reflecting each language’s geopolitical perspective.\", 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War‑Era tragedy:** On\\u202f20\\u202fApril\\u202f1978 a Korean Air Lines\\u202f902 (a Boeing\\u202f707‑321B) was struck by a Soviet‑launched missile after it entered the USSR’s prohibited airspace, underscoring the heightened tensions and dangerous brinkmanship of the period.', '**Human cost:** The attack killed two passengers and left the remaining 107 occupants—109 passengers and 12 crew members—alive, turning a routine international flight into a stark reminder of the civilian toll of superpower confrontations.', '**Soviet aggression narrative:** The incident is presented as a clear example of Soviet military overreach, with the Su‑17’s “missile strike on the plane’s eastern wing” framed as an unprovoked act against a non‑military aircraft.', '**Political fallout:** The event amplified international criticism of Soviet air‑space policies, feeding into broader Cold‑War debates over freedom of navigation, the legitimacy of “restricted zones,” and the need for stronger diplomatic safeguards for civilian aviation.', '**Legacy:** Korean Air Lines\\u202f902 remains a cautionary case study in aviation‑security and Cold‑War history, illustrating how a single air‑space violation could spiral into a lethal, geopolitically charged confrontation.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War‑era shoot‑down:** On 20\\u202fApril\\u202f1978 a Korean Air Lines Boeing\\u202f707 strayed into Soviet airspace and was intercepted by a Su‑15; the Soviet pilot was ordered to fire, striking the plane and forcing an emergency landing on the frozen Korpijärvi Lake in Karelia.', '**Human cost and survival:** Of the 109 people aboard, two passengers were killed by the missile strike and a third died after the crash; the remaining 107 survived, were rescued by Soviet helicopters and eventually repatriated via Finland and Aeroflot.', '**Competing narratives:** Soviet authorities asserted the airliner ignored repeated commands to follow the interceptor, justifying the shoot‑down as a defensive act; the Korean crew and many witnesses claimed navigational equipment failures (magnetic‑pole‑induced gyro errors) caused the off‑course deviation and that the interceptor failed to follow ICAO signaling procedures.', '**Political fallout:** The incident strained East‑West relations, with the USSR refusing international investigators’ access to the wreckage or flight‑recorders, demanding a $100\\u202f000 “caretaking” fee from South Korea (never paid), and later using the episode to reinforce its strict air‑space enforcement policies.', '**Legacy:** The tragedy foreshadowed the deadlier Korean Air Lines Flight\\u202f007 shoot‑down five years later; Korean Air no longer operates the Paris‑Anchorage‑Seoul routing, and the 902 flight number has been reassigned to a modern Boeing\\u202f777 service between Paris‑Incheon.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%98%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82_%D1%81_%D1%8E%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BC_%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BC_(1978)?uselang=en', 'key_points': ['**Soviet interception and forced landing** – After veering off its Paris‑Anchorage‑Seoul route and violating USSR airspace, a Korean Air\\u202fLines Boeing\\u202f707 (KE‑902) was intercepted by Su‑15 fighters, hit by an R‑8 air‑to‑air missile, and compelled to make an emergency landing on the frozen Lake\\u202fKorpiarvi in the Karelian\\u202fASSR (23\\u202f:05\\u202fMSK,\\u202f20\\u202fApr\\u202f1978).', '**Casualties and damage** – The missile strike tore a large section of the left wing and penetrated the cabin, killing two passengers (a Seoul businessman and a Japanese‑Korean café owner) and wounding 13 others with shrapnel, barotrauma and hypothermia; the remaining 107