ines Boeing\\u202f707 entered Soviet airspace and was shot down, killing two passengers; in 1977 an Aeroflot Il‑18 crashed during a go‑around in poor weather, killing 77. Both incidents resulted in numerous injuries and repatriations.', 'bias_comment': 'The Russian article stresses Soviet territorial defense and frames the shoot‑down as a justified response, reflecting Cold‑War political narratives, while the English article highlights pilot error and weather, focusing on technical causes. This illustrates how language and national perspective shape accident reporting.', 'languages_used': ['ru', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%98%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82_%D1%81_%D1%8E%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BC_%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BC_(1978)?uselang=en', 'key_points': ['**Soviet‑U.S. confrontation:** The Korean Air Lines Boeing\\u202f707 (flight\\u202f902) strayed far off its Paris‑Anchorage‑Seoul route, entered Soviet airspace over the Kola Peninsula, and was intercepted by Soviet Su‑15 fighters that, after failed visual‑signal attempts, launched an air‑to‑air missile at the airliner.', '**Forced ice landing:** The missile strike crippled the left wing and ruptured the fuselage, forcing the crew to make an emergency landing on the frozen surface of Lake\\u202fKor\\xadpijarvi near the village of\\u202fLoykhi in the Karelian\\u202fASSR.', '**Human cost and aftermath:** Two passengers were killed (one by a missile fragment, the other later from injuries), 13 more were injured, and the surviving 107 occupants were rescued and taken to Soviet hospitals before being repatriated.', '**Political narrative clash:** Soviet authorities framed the incident as a breach of their territorial sovereignty and a justified defensive action, while Korean officials and the crew blamed faulty navigation equipment and stressed that the aircraft was a civilian passenger plane, not a reconnaissance aircraft.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeroflot_Flight_5003_(1977)', 'key_points': ['**Pilot error was the primary cause** – the crew’s distraction, delayed recognition of a drift off‑course, and an improper go‑around maneuver caused an excessive angle of attack, stall, and loss of control.', '**Adverse weather compounded the mistake** – low clouds, fog, ice and visibility under 1\\u202fkm at Mineralnye Vody forced a difficult approach and contributed to the crew’s disorientation.', '**A catastrophic missed‑approach** – after aborting the first landing the aircraft turned sharply at only ~80\\u202fm altitude, suffered structural damage to its control surfaces, and crashed within seconds, killing 77 of the 98 aboard.', '**Investigation’s verdict** – the official report classified the accident under “airliner accidents caused by pilot error,” citing the crew’s poor handling of the go‑around as the decisive factor.', '**One of the deadliest Soviet‑era Il‑18 crashes** – the accident remains a stark example of how human error, amplified by poor weather, can turn a routine approach into a fatal disaster.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '2023 Black Sea drone incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Black_Sea_drone_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Black_Sea_drone_incident', 'name': '2023 Black Sea drone incident', 'years': '2023', 'short_description': 'On 14 March 2023, Russian Su‑27 fighter jets intercepted a U.S. MQ‑9 Reaper drone over the Black Sea near Crimea, damaging its propeller and forcing it to crash into the sea, marking the first direct contact between Russian and U.S. forces since the Cold War.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Incident'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 14\\u202fMarch\\u202f2023 a Russian Su‑27 intercepted a US MQ‑9 Reaper over the Black Sea, causing the drone’s crash. The U.S. called the act reckless, while Russia said it was defensive. The incident heightened U.S‑Russia tensions amid the Ukraine war.', 'bias_comment': 'English coverage emphasizes both U.S. condemnation and Russian defensive framing, potentially balancing but still reflecting Western perspectives; Russian-language articles often stress the protective nature of the interception and highlight Russian heroism. Ukrainian-language sources may stress Russian aggression and broader war context, possibly omitting U.S. statements. These divergences illustrate national narratives influencing portrayal of the incident.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Black_Sea_drone_incident', 'key_points': ['**First direct US‑Russia air encounter since the Cold War:** On\\u202f14\\u202fMarch\\u202f2023 a Russian Su‑27 fighter intercepted a US MQ‑9 Reaper over the Black Sea, damaging its propeller and forcing it to crash into international waters.', '**US condemns the “reckless, unprofessional” action:** The Pentagon and White House labeled the incident a provocation and warned of escalation, briefing President\\u202fBiden and NATO allies while releasing video showing fuel‑dumping and propeller damage.', '**Russia denies using weapons, frames it as a protective maneuver:** The Russian defence ministry claimed the drone flew without a transponder and that the aircraft merely performed “harsh manoeuvring,” later rewarding the pilots with state medals for “preventing” a violation of Russian airspace.', '**Strategic implications and recovery worries:** U.S. officials said the drone’s software was wiped to prevent intelligence capture; deep‑water conditions and limited naval assets make recovery difficult, while Russia reportedly moved ships to the crash site.', '**Broader context of heightened US‑Russia tension:** The incident occurred amid intensified sanctions on Russia, ongoing US support for Ukraine, and a series of “spill‑over” confrontations linked to the Russo‑Ukrainian War.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of the Cold War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War_Timeline'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War_Timeline', 'name': 'Timeline of the Cold War', 'years': '1945-1991', 'short_description': 'Chronological list of key political and military events marking the Cold War, the post‑World War\\u202fII rivalry between the Western Bloc (US, NATO allies) and the Eastern Bloc (USSR, Warsaw Pact allies).', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph after lead'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From 1945 to 1991 former WWII allies shifted into a bipolar rivalry, forming NATO and the Warsaw Pact, competing in nuclear and technological arms races, supporting opposite sides in regional conflicts, and influencing domestic politics through anti‑communist and anti‑imperialist measures.', 'bias_comment': 'English narratives often emphasize U.S. initiatives (Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan) and portray Soviet moves as expansionist, while Russian sources stress security concerns and depict the West as aggressive. German and French accounts tend to balance both sides, and non‑Western languages highlight decolonization and third‑world perspectives, exposing historiographic biases.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War_Timeline', 'key_points': ['**Post‑World War\\u202fII power vacuum turned into a bipolar clash:**  From the Yalta and Potsdam conferences through the Truman Doctrine (1947) and the Marshall Plan, the United\\u202fStates and the Soviet Union moved from wartime allies to rivals, each seeking to shape the political order in Europe, Asia and the Middle East.', '**Institutionalization of the rivalry:**  NATO (1949) and the Warsaw Pact (1955) formalized opposing military blocs, while multilateral bodies (UN, Comecon, SEATO, the Non‑Aligned Movement) became arenas for competing diplomatic and economic influence.', '**Escalation of the nuclear and technological arms race:**  The U.S. monopoly on atomic bombs ended with the Soviet test (1949), followed by the development of hydrogen bombs, intercontinental missiles, Sputnik, and nuclear‑powered submarines—each side using “strategic surprise” to intimidate the other.', '**Proxy wars and decolonization as flashpoints:**  Civil wars and insurgencies in Greece, Korea, Vietnam, Indonesia, India/Pakistan, the Suez Crisis, the Hungarian Revolution, and the Cuban Revolution illustrate how the superpowers backed opposing sides, turning regional struggles into Cold‑War battlegrounds.', '**Domestic backlash and ideological crackdowns:**  McCarthyism in the U.S., the purges and uprisings in Eastern Europe, and the cultural clash over “containment” versus “expansion” reveal how the global standoff permeated internal politics, shaping public opinion and policy on both sides.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Comparison of US and Chinese Military Armed Forces - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_US_and_Chinese_Military_Armed_Forces'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_US_and_Chinese_Military_Armed_Forces', 'name': 'Comparison of US and Chinese Military Armed Forces', 'years': '2022', 'short_description': 'A Wikipedia article providing a side‑by‑side table that compares the United States Armed Forces and China’s People’s Liberation Army in terms of official name, service branches, command structure, leadership, personnel numbers, defense spending, GDP share, nuclear arsenals, and listed major wars and events.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Table_comparison_between_the_US_and_Chinese_Military'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The United States and China maintain distinct military profiles: the U.S. has a smaller force with higher spending and a larger nuclear arsenal, while China fields more personnel with lower spending and a smaller nuclear stock. Both have multi‑