u202fيونيو\\u202f1950، أقرَّ مجلس الأمن للأمم المتحدة قرارًا (رقم\\u202f82) يسمح بتشكيل قوة متعددة الجنسيات (معظِمها أميركي) لحماية كوريا الجنوبية، ما أعطى للغربيين مبرراً للغزو اللاحق إلى شمال الجزيرة.', '**دور الصين وتغيّر موازين القوة** – مع تراجع القوات الأممية إلى شبه جزيرة بوسان، أطلقت الصين حملة “حرب مقاومة أمريكا ومساعدة كوريا” في أكتوبر\\u202f1950، مما أدّى إلى توسيع نطاق القتال إلى شمال كوريا وخلق جبهة جديدة أدت إلى استقرار الخط الأمامي حول خط العرض\\u202f38.', '**نتيجة الصراع وتبعاته** – انتهت الحرب باتفاق هدنة في 27\\u202fيوليو\\u202f1953، أسفر عن إنشاء منطقة منزوعة السلاح وتثبيت تقسيم شبه الجزيرة الكورية إلى دولتين مستقلتين، مع استمرار التوترات الجيوسياسية والذكريات المتباينة عن “حرب مَنسيّة” في الوعي العالمي.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'key_points': ['**A Cold‑War proxy that ended in a stalemate:** The Korean War (June\\u202f1950\\u202f–\\u202fJuly\\u202f1953) was the first major “proxy war” of the Cold War, pitting a Soviet‑ and Chinese‑backed North Korea against a U.S.‑led United Nations force supporting South Korea.  Despite massive offensives by both sides, the fighting concluded with an armistice, the creation of the Demilitarized Zone, and no formal peace treaty—leaving the peninsula technically still at war.', '**Massive human and material cost with disputed outcomes:** The conflict caused 1‑3\\u202fmillion civilian deaths and over 5\\u202fmillion military casualties on all sides, while North Korea suffered extensive strategic bombing and South Korea endured widespread displacement.  Territorial changes were minor (North Korea kept Kaesong but lost about 3,900\\u202fkm²), and the result is widely described as “inconclusive.”', '**Origins in post‑World‑War division and super‑power rivalries:** After Japan’s 1945 surrender, the 38th parallel split Korea into Soviet and U.S. occupation zones.  Stalin’s and Mao’s calculations—combined with Kim\\u202fIl‑Sung’s belief that the South was vulnerable—led to the June\\u202f1950 invasion, while the United States intervened partly to protect its emerging security perimeter around Japan and to contain Soviet expansion.', '**Competing narratives and terminology:** South Korea refers to the conflict as the “6·25 War,” the United States once called it a “police action” and later the “Forgotten/Unknown War,” while North Korea frames it as the “Fatherland Liberation War” and China labels the Chinese‑involved phase the “Great Movement to Resist America and Assist Korea.”  These divergent names reflect each side’s political stance on legitimacy and victimhood.', '**Enduring legacy of a “frozen conflict”:** The armistice solidified a heavily fortified border and a 2½‑mile wide DMZ, but the absence of a peace treaty has kept the Korean Peninsula in a perpetual state of tension, shaping regional security, diplomatic relations, and domestic politics in both Koreas and their allies to this day.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War proxy showdown:**\\u202fThe Korean War (1950‑1953) was framed as the first major East‑West clash of the Cold War – North Korea, backed by the Soviet Union and later the Chinese People’s Volunteer Army, attacked the South, prompting a UN‑led (predominantly US) military response that turned the peninsula into a battlefield for competing super‑power ideologies.', '**Multinational involvement & massive human toll:**\\u202fSixteen nations contributed troops, equipment, or medical aid, yet the conflict cost an estimated 2–3\\u202fmillion lives (including 200–300\\u202fkilled civilians) and left hundreds of thousands wounded or missing, making its casualty ratio even higher than World\\u202fII or the Vietnam War.', '**Naming reveals political bias:**\\u202fDifferent sides label the war according to their stance – “6·25 War / Korean War” (neutral/Western), “祖國解放戰爭” (North Korean) and “抗美援朝戰爭” (Chinese) – underscoring how the conflict is interpreted as liberation, aggression, or a “forgotten war.”', '**Broader geopolitical ripple effects:**\\u202fThe war cemented the division of the Korean Peninsula, spurred the creation of the Demilitarized Zone, accelerated US naval presence in the Taiwan Strait, revived Japan’s post‑war economy, and entrenched the enduring South‑North Korean and Taiwan‑China tensions that persist today.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of conflicts in the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conflicts_in_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conflicts_in_the_United_States', 'name': 'List of conflicts in the United States', 'years': '1775–present', 'short_description': 'A chronological compilation of wars, battles, riots, massacres, and other violent conflicts that have taken place within the United States and its territories from the American Revolutionary War to contemporary times.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The article lists US armed conflicts from 1775 onward, including wars, rebellions, riots, and territorial disputes, covering both external wars and internal unrest, and noting Indigenous resistance and expansion-driven violence.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry frames U.S. conflicts broadly, emphasizing domestic unrest, Indigenous resistance, and expansion-driven violence. Other language versions (e.g., Spanish, French) may downplay internal rebellions or emphasize external wars, reflecting national narratives and varying historiographic emphases. These differences affect perceived prevalence and significance of each conflict.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conflicts_in_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**A sweeping chronicle of American warfare:**\\u202fThe list tracks every major and minor armed clash from the Revolutionary War through the late‑19th‑century Indian wars, the Civil War, and the Spanish‑American War, underscoring how conflict has been a constant thread in U.S. history.', '**Native‑American resistance as a persistent theme:**\\u202fRepeated entries—Cherokee–American wars, Northwest Indian War, Apache, Comanche, Sioux, and many other tribal campaigns—highlight the long, often brutal, struggle between expanding U.S. settlements and Indigenous peoples.', '**Domestic unrest and “bloodless” wars:**\\u202fBeyond conventional battles, the catalog includes rebellions (Shays’, Whiskey, Nat Turner), riots, massacres, labor‑strike violence (Haymarket, Homestead, Pullman), and even “wars” over land or resources (Toledo, Range, Railroad wars), portraying the United States as repeatedly torn by internal social and political conflict.', '**Broad definition of conflict:**\\u202fThe article deliberately folds together formal wars, skirmishes, territorial disputes, terrorist attacks, and episodes of genocide, massacres, and human sacrifice, signaling that violence—whether state‑sanctioned or grassroots—forms an integral part of the nation’s narrative.', '**A pattern of expansion‑driven violence:**\\u202fFrom early colonial wars and the Mexican‑American War to the annexation of Hawaii and the frontier Indian campaigns, the list underscores how territorial ambition and the drive for resources have repeatedly sparked both external and internal battles.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'User:Jackyd101/British colonial wars - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Jackyd101/British_colonial_wars'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Jackyd101/British_colonial_wars', 'name': 'British colonial wars (1815-1939)', 'years': '1815-1939', 'short_description': 'User project listing British colonial wars from the end of the Napoleonic Wars in 1815 to the start of World\\u202fII in 1939, aiming to create articles for lesser-known conflicts.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#intro'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_