nally Commonwealth, British and Canadian personnel predominated at the operational level, giving the force a truly integrated, multinational combat capability throughout the war.', '**Extensive naval contribution that kept the seas contested:**\\u202fThe Royal Navy kept at least one carrier on station for most of the conflict (Glory, Ocean, Theseus, Triumph, Unicorn) and the Royal Australian Navy added HMAS\\u202fSydney.  Together with ships from Canada and New\\u202fZealand, these fleets provided carrier‑based air cover, shore bombardment and blockades.  A notable achievement was Lt\\u202fPeter\\u202fCarmichael’s propeller‑driven Sea\\u202fFury shooting down a MiG‑15 in 1952 – one of the few jet kills by a piston‑engine fighter.', '**Limited but symbolically important air involvement:**\\u202fThe only front‑line Commonwealth air unit was the RAAF’s No.\\u202f77 Squadron, which flew P‑51 Mustangs and later Gloster Meteors.  British and Canadian crews served alongside them, while the RAF contributed a wing of Short Sunderland flying boats based in Japan.  No other Commonwealth air force fielded a dedicated combat squadron under the BCFK banner.', '**A lasting legacy of shared Commonwealth sacrifice:**\\u202fBCFK’s forces remained active until 1956, and 1,589 Commonwealth dead are buried at the United Nations Memorial Cemetery in Busan.  Memorials in London and Korea commemorate a multinational effort that showcased the Commonwealth’s rapid‑reaction capability and its willingness to fight side‑by‑side with the United Nations during the Korean War.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Passamaquoddy Bay', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passamaquoddy_Bay'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passamaquoddy_Bay', 'name': 'Passamaquoddy Bay', 'years': '1814, 1910', 'short_description': 'Passamaquoddy Bay is an inlet of the Bay of Fundy on the US‑Canada border; its boundary was contested and clarified by the Treaty of Ghent (1814) and the Passamaquoddy Bay Treaty of 1910, establishing the international line through the bay.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Overview'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Passamaquoddy Bay is a tidal inlet of the Bay of Fundy on the U.S.–Canada border, with most waters in Canada. The 1814 Treaty of Ghent addressed boundary issues; the 1910 treaty defined the border line. The bay has strategic passages and a history of smuggling.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes border disputes, historical smuggling, and modern navigation claims, reflecting North‑American geopolitical focus. French or other language versions may emphasize ecological importance, Indigenous Mi\\'kmaq heritage, or tourism, thus downplaying contention. The \"needs additional citations\" tag indicates reliance on uncited material.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passamaquoddy_Bay', 'key_points': ['**Geography & Jurisdiction:** Passamaquoddy Bay is a tidal inlet of the Bay of Fundy straddling the\\u202fU.S.–Canada border (Maine\\u202f↔\\u202fNew\\u202fBrunswick), with most of its waters lying in Canada and key coastal points ranging from West\\u202fQuoddy\\u202fHead (Maine) to L’Etete (New\\u202fBrunswick).', '**Border‑line Legacy:** The exact overseas boundary has long been contentious; even the 1814\\u202fTreaty of\\u202fGhent called for “commissioners to divide the islands,” yet ambiguities persisted until the 1910\\u202fPassamaquoddy\\u202fBay Treaty formally fixed a line of seven straight segments from Treat\\u202fIsland to Friar\\u202fHead.', '**Strategic Passages & Modern Dispute:** Three main entrances—Letete Passage, Head\\u202fHarbour Passage, and Quoddy\\u202fNarrows—also carry the international boundary. Canada claims Head\\u202fHarbour Passage as internal waters, while the United\\u202fStates asserts a right of innocent passage, especially to block proposed super‑tanker traffic to LNG facilities.', '**Historical Smuggling Hub:** During the early‑19th\\u202fcentury (post‑Revolution, War of\\u202f1812, and Jefferson’s embargo) the bay was a thriving smuggling corridor for flour, British goods and later Nova\\u202fScotia gypsum, largely tolerated by local authorities.', '*Note: The article carries a “needs additional citations” tag, indicating that many of these details lack verifiable sources.*']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Talk:Gunboat diplomacy - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Gunboat_diplomacy'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Gunboat_diplomacy', 'name': 'Discussion on the origin of the term “gunboat diplomacy” (Matthew Perry) on the Talk page', 'years': '2006-2007', 'short_description': 'Wikipedia editors debated in 2006‑2007 whether the phrase “gunboat diplomacy” originated with Commodore Matthew C. Perry’s 1850s fleet that forced Japan to open to trade, or if it predates his actions.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Diplomacy'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"Talk page editors debated whether the term “gun‑boat diplomacy” originated with Commodore Matthew Perry's 1853 Japan expedition or earlier, discussed updating modern examples and neutrality, flagged a potentially biased distinction between “gun‑boat diplomacy” and “power projection,” and addressed technical and scope concerns.\", 'bias_comment': 'The English discussion reflects a typical Western historiographic focus on US/Western actions, possibly overlooking non‑Western perspectives on early gun‑boat tactics. Anglophone sources may dominate the origin debate, while non‑English articles could emphasize earlier European or Asian examples, shaping perceived origins.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Gunboat_diplomacy', 'key_points': ['**Origins and terminology** – Editors argue the article should clarify whether “gunboat diplomacy” began with Commodore\\u202fMatthew\\u202fPerry’s 1853 expedition to Japan, noting the term may pre‑date his actions and urging research into earlier examples.', '**Modern relevance and neutrality** – Several contributors request a 21st‑century update (e.g., Iran, recent U.S. interventions) and flag language in the “Modern contexts” section as potentially biased, especially the distinction between “gunboat diplomacy” and the newer term “power projection.”', '**Technical housekeeping** – Notices address a broken image/HTML tag on the page and document the InternetArchiveBot’s recent archiving of dead external links to preserve source material.', '**Content scope concerns** – A brief mention of the 2018 APEC Papua\\u202fNew\\u202fGuinea meeting was challenged as off‑topic and possibly original research, suggesting its removal or better integration.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Competition between Airbus and Boeing', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition_between_Airbus_and_Boeing'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition_between_Airbus_and_Boeing', 'name': 'Competition between Airbus and Boeing', 'years': '1990s–present', 'short_description': 'Since the 1990s, Airbus and Boeing have engaged in a duopolistic rivalry over large jet airliners, influencing orders, deliveries, technology, and subsidy disputes, with each periodically claiming unfair state aid and alternating market leadership.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#lead'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since the 1990s Airbus and Boeing have formed a global commercial aircraft duopoly, competing over orders, deliveries, and technology, while both accuse each other of illegal subsidies, leading to WTO disputes, market shifts, and ongoing innovations.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic entry emphasizes the historical formation of a duopoly, the role of failed mergers and the Eastern Bloc’s collapse, framing the rivalry as a \"dual‑hold\" and highlighting subsidy disputes as \"wars\". The English article focuses on recent market trends, Airbus’s recent lead, detailed product comparisons, and economic factors like currency and backlog. These differences reflect varied regional perspectives: Arabic highlights historical and political dimensions, while English stresses quantitative data and recent competitive shifts.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition_between_Airbus_and_Boeing', 'key_points': ['**A duopoly forged by history and failed mergers** – Since the early 1990s Airbus (born from a European economic consortium) and Boeing (bolstered by the 1997 McDonnell\\u202fDouglas takeover) have become the sole players in the global commercial‑aircraft market, a “dual‑hold” that emerged after numerous abortive consolidation attempts and the collapse of the Eastern Bloc’s aerospace sector.', '**Intense rivalry over orders and deliveries** – Between 1997‑2006 each firm secured roughly 5\\u202f200 orders (Airbus 5\\u202f276, Boeing 5\\u202f214) and chased yearly sales leads, with Airbus overtaking Boeing in deliveries for the first time in 2003 and repeatedly swapping the top‑spot thereafter.', '**Subsidy wars and WTO disputes** – Both sides accuse the other of unfair government aid: Airbus claims the U.S. gives Boeing illicit military/NASA contracts and tax breaks, while Boeing argues Europe provides Airbus with up‑to‑33\\u202f% of program costs as “official” loans. The clash has sparked multiple WTO cases and threatened broader trade wars.', '**Product overlap but niche differentiation** – Airbus’s family‑wide strategy (A320‑A380) now covers essentially every Boeing segment (737‑777‑747), yet each company retains comparative advantages—Airbus dominates the ultra‑wide‑body market with the A380, while Boeing leverages twin‑engine efficiency (787) and lower‑fuel‑burn designs (777‑200\\u202fLR) to out‑sell in certain ranges.', '**Market impact and future outlook** – With about 4\\u202f900 Airbus and 4\\u202f500 Boeing jets still in service (≈13\\u202f000 total) and both aiming for ~50\\u202f% of new‑aircraft orders, the duopoly remains entrenched, but the competition continues to drive rapid technological innovation, aggressive pricing, and ongoing legal battles over state assistance.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition_between_Airbus_and_Boeing', 'key_points': ['**A duopoly that’s increasingly tilted toward Airbus** – Since the 1990s, Airbus and Boeing have been the only players in the large‑jet market, but recent data (2015‑2024) shows Airbus pulling ahead in both orders (8,950 vs\\u202f5,012 net) and deliveries (7,043 vs\\u202f5,312), eclipsing Boeing as the world’s largest aerospace firm in 2019 and finally surpassing it in aircraft‑in‑service numbers in 2023.', '**Product battles are driving the shift** – Airbus’s A320 family has out‑sold the 737‑NG/‑MAX in the single‑aisle segment, while the A350‑900/ULR and A380 have contested Boeing’s 777‑X/747‑8 on the wide‑body front. The A320‑neo’s engine‑choice flexibility and the A220 acquisition cemented Airbus’s dominance in the low‑cost market, leaving Boeing scrambling to respond (e.g., the 737\\u202fMAX program).', '**State‑aid accusations dominate the rivalry** – Both firms regularly allege illegal subsidies from each other’s governments. The WTO dispute that began in 2005 has seen rulings that European launch aid to Airbus was “unfair” (later partially reversed) and that Boeing received $5‑$7\\u202fbn in prohibited U.S. subsidies, leading to reciprocal tariffs and a 2021 five‑year truce.', '**Strategic outsourcing versus European integration** – Boeing’s heavy reliance on global supply‑chain partners (e.g., Japanese firms, U.S. state incentives) contrasts with Airbus’s more region‑focused consortium model, though Airbus has opened final‑assembly lines in China and the U.S. to gain political footholds.', '**Currency, market backlogs and production capacity matter** – Dollar‑euro exchange swings affect relative costs, while Airbus now commands roughly 65\\u202f% of the single‑aisle backlog (2021) and plans higher annual output (A320neo\\u202f≈\\u202f900\\u202fyr⁻¹, A220\\u202f≈\\u202f168\\u202fyr⁻¹) versus Boeing’s 600\\u202fyr⁻¹ for the 737\\u202fMAX, reinforcing its market‑share advantage.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of the British Army', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_British_Army'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_British_Army', 'name': 'Timeline of the British Army', 'years': '1537-present', 'short_description': 'A chronological list of the British Army’s major wars, battles, and related events from its earliest formation in 1537 through to the present day.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Intro'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The timeline charts the British Army from its 1537 origins to present, noting its early militia roots, unification after 1661, global engagements, continuous organizational and technological changes, and recent reforms such as the Future Soldier programme and specialist units.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes continuity, institutional achievements, and modern reforms, reflecting a British‑centric viewpoint. Non‑English versions may foreground colonial repercussions, war‑time controversies, or differing national perspectives, leading to divergent emphases on imperialism, resistance, and the Army’s role in global conflicts.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_British_Army', 'key_points': ['**A continuity of service stretching back to the 16th\\u202fcentury** – the timeline begins with the 1537 charter of the Honourable Artillery Company (the oldest regiment in continuous British‑Army service) and traces the evolution of early militia units into the modern force.', '**From fragmented English, Scottish and Irish forces to a unified British Army** – the 1661 Charles\\u202fII warrant and the 1707 Acts of Union merged separate national armies, establishing a single regimental system that would later be standardized by the 1751 numerical designations and the Cardwell‑Childers reforms of the late‑19th\\u202fcentury.', '**A global combat record across empire‑building, world wars and post‑colonial conflicts** – the list highlights the Army’s participation in the Seven\\u202fYears’ War, American War of Independence, Napoleonic campaigns, colonial wars (Afghanistan, Zulu, Boer), both World Wars, the Cold‑War decolonisation era, and the 21st‑century operations in the Gulf, Iraq, Afghanistan, the Balkans, Africa and Ukraine.', '**Constant organisational and technological adaptation** – new branches such as the Royal Artillery (1722), Royal Engineers, Royal Corps of Signals (1920), Royal Armoured Corps (1939), Parachute Regiment (1942), SAS (1941) and the Ranger Regiment (2021) were created, while weaponry progressed from muskets and hand‑gonnes to machine guns, tanks, and today’s modern armoured and digital systems.', '**Recent reform and forward‑looking capability** – the “Future Soldier” programme, formation of specialist units (Special Reconnaissance Regiment, Ranger Regiment), and the Army’s role in training Ukrainian forces, NATO deployments, and high‑profile operations such as Operation Pitting (Afghanistan) illustrate a continuing emphasis on agility, expeditionary reach, and alignment with contemporary security challenges.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of wars involving the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_Involving_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_Involving_the_United_States', 'name': 'List of wars involving the United States', 'years': '1609–present', 'short_description': 'A comprehensive list of 125 military conflicts involving the United States, spanning from early colonial wars in the early 17th century to ongoing engagements in the 21st century, covering major wars, interventions, and periods of conflict.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph before sections'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The article lists 125 U.S. military engagements from 1609 to present, defining any armed action by U.S. forces as a \"war\", with only five formally declared wars, and categorizes them chronologically and by domestic versus foreign operations.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry adopts a broad U.S.-centric definition of \"war,\" potentially inflating conflict counts and downplaying non‑military interventions. Non‑English Wikipedia versions may emphasize different episodes, use alternative terminology, or omit certain interventions, reflecting local historiographical biases and varied political perspectives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_Involving_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**Scope of U.S. combat history:**\\u202fThe article tallies **125\\u202fmilitary conflicts** involving the United States—only **five** of which were formally declared wars (Revolution, War of\\u202f1812, Spanish‑American War, WW\\u202fI, WW\\u202fII)—yet it treats every armed engagement by any U.S. service as a “war” for comprehensiveness.', '**Breadth of coverage:**\\u202fIt lists **major wars** (e.g., Revolutionary War, Civil War, World Wars, Gulf War) alongside **extended periods of conflict** (Indian Wars, Cold War, War on Terror) and notes **five ongoing interventions** (Yemen, Somalia, Syria, Gaza, and related operations).', '**Inclusion criteria & exclusions:**\\u202fOnly conflicts where the U.S. military (Army, Navy, Marine Corps, Air Force, Space Force, Coast Guard, intelligence agencies, PMCs, etc.) acted in a **military capacity** are counted; **rebellions, isolated terrorist attacks, riots, labor disputes, feuds, cyber‑warfare, coups, and single‑battle actions** are deliberately omitted unless they constitute a distinct punitive operation.', '**Organizational structure:**\\u202fThe list is divided chronologically (colonial\\u202f1609‑1774, 1775‑1900, 1901‑2000, 2001‑present) and further split into **domestic vs. foreign** engagements, with extensive cross‑references to related timelines, deployment lists, and auxiliary military resources.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of United States military operations', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_United_States_military_operations'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_United_States_military_operations', 'name': 'Timeline of United States military operations', 'years': '1775–present', 'short_description': 'Chronological list of U.S. armed forces engagements abroad and major domestic deployments, covering wars, interventions, occupations, and other operations from the American Revolutionary War to the present.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Extraterritorial_and_major_domestic_deployments'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The article lists every U.S. ground, naval or Marine deployment from 1775 onward, including wars, minor expeditions, anti‑piracy raids, and protection missions, while excluding diplomatic aid, covert CIA actions, and non‑regular forces, focusing on officially authorized operations.', 'bias_comment': \"The article’s focus on 'boots‑on‑the‑ground' actions, using CRS sources, presents a US‑centric, official‑legitimacy narrative, omitting covert, diplomatic, or Guard‑only missions. Non‑English versions may include broader definitions, altering perceived frequency and nature of U.S. power projection. This selection frames U.S. interventions as lawful, downplaying unofficial or contested actions.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_United_States_military_operations', 'key_points': ['**A\\u202fchronological catalogue of every U.S. “boots‑on‑the‑ground” operation** – from the Revolutionary War and early Indian wars to the Spanish‑American War, the list records every deployment of active‑duty troops, Marines, or sailors, even when the mission was a brief “show of force,” anti‑piracy raid, or protection detail for American citizens and property abroad.', '**Major wars are highlighted in bold, but the bulk of the record consists of low‑intensity actions** – engagements such as the Quasi‑War with France, the Barbary Wars, the many “punitive expeditions” in the Caribbean, Pacific islands, and Central\\u202fAmerica, and the numerous occupations of “unstable” foreign ports dominate the timeline, underscoring how the U.S. frequently used small‑scale force to safeguard commercial interests and assert influence.', '**Strict inclusion criteria shape the narrative** – the timeline omits any operation that involved only diplomatic aid, covert CIA missions, or state‑guard/National‑Guard units that were not fully integrated into the regular armed forces, thereby presenting a picture of overt, congressional‑authorized military involvement rather than the full spectrum of American power projection.', '**The source (Congressional Research Service) and the framing emphasize official legitimacy** – by grounding the list in CRS reports, the article stresses that each entry reflects an authorized use of force (often backed by congressional statutes or presidential orders), reinforcing the view that U.S. military actions have historically been justified as lawful, officially sanctioned responses to perceived threats to national interests.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Dixon Entrance', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixon_Entrance'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixon_Entrance', 'name': 'Dixon Entrance', 'years': '1787', 'short_description': 'The Dixon Entrance is an 80‑km strait at the Canada‑United States border, named for Captain George Dixon, who surveyed the area in 1787.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Etymology'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Dixon Entrance is an ~80\\u202fkm strait between Alaska (USA) and British Columbia (Canada), named for Captain George Dixon (1787). A 1903 arbitration set the A‑B line; the U.S. sees it as a land demarcation, Canada as a maritime boundary, creating overlapping claims and ongoing fishing/resource tensions.', 'bias_comment': 'English text emphasizes the legal‑technical dispute and U.S./Canada jurisdictional claims, while French includes the indigenous Haida name and broader diplomatic/economic angles, reflecting broader cultural context. Both prioritize the British explorer naming, underrepresenting indigenous toponymy.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dixon_Entrance', 'key_points': ['**Geography & significance** – Dixon\\u202fEntrance is an 80\\u202fkm‑long strait on the Pacific coast that separates Alaska (U.S.) from British Columbia (Canada) and forms a key segment of the Inside Passage shipping route.', '**Name origin** – The waterway was named by Joseph\\u202fBanks for Royal‑Navy officer and explorer Captain\\u202fGeorge\\u202fDixon, who surveyed the area in 1787.', '**Core of the dispute** – The 1903 “A‑B line” drawn by an arbitration court marks the northern limit of the entrance. Canada treats this line as the **maritime boundary**, while the United States maintains it only delineates land ownership and therefore does **not** recognize it for sea‑area jurisdiction (e.g., fishing rights, seabed resources).', '**Resulting boundary mosaic** – Because the two nations use different lines, the entrance contains four distinct zones: two overlapping claim areas south of the A‑B line, two unclaimed pockets north of it, and a low‑tide feature (Nunez Rocks) that the U.S. uses as a basepoint despite Canada’s objections.', '**Ongoing tensions** – The failure of the U.S. to depict the A‑B line on official maps and the differing interpretations under 