ates', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Indies_Anti-Piracy_Operations_of_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Indies_Anti-Piracy_Operations_of_the_United_States', 'name': 'West Indies anti-piracy operations of the United States', 'years': '1814–1825', 'short_description': 'A series of United States Navy and Revenue Marine actions from 1814 to 1825 that suppressed Caribbean piracy, with the West Indies Squadron hunting pirate vessels around the Antilles, Gulf of Mexico and Caribbean Sea, culminating in the capture of pirate Roberto Cofresi in 1825.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Introductory summary (first paragraph)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From 1814 to 1825 the U.S. Navy and Revenue Marine operated a West Indies Squadron to suppress Caribbean piracy, conducting naval engagements, boarding actions, and cooperating with British forces, despite diplomatic, health, and legal challenges; the campaign ended with the capture of pirate Roberto Cofresí.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize the campaign’s decisive, government‑backed nature and punitive tactics, while other language versions (e.g., Spanish, French) may highlight local socio‑political impacts, portray the pirates more sympathetically, or stress diplomatic friction with Spanish colonies. These differences reflect national historiographic biases and varying source availability.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Indies_Anti-Piracy_Operations_of_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**A decisive, government‑backed campaign:**  From 1814‑1825 the U.S.\\u202fNavy and Revenue Marine assembled a permanent “West\\u202fIndies Squadron” (≈1,300 men, 2\\u202ffrigates, 2\\u202fcorvettes, 2\\u202fsloops, 2\\u202fbrigs, 4\\u202fschooners, 2\\u202fgunboats) to hunt Caribbean pirates after a wave of attacks on American merchant ships.', '**Heavy combat and harsh tactics:**  The squadron’s actions ranged from board‑and‑blade raids and boat assaults to full‑scale naval battles (e.g.,\\u202fUSS\\u202fGrampus vs.\\u202fPalyrma, the Funda\\u202fBay fight, the March\\u202f1823 action at Cape\\u202fCruz).  Captured pirates were often turned over to Spanish authorities, executed, or sent to the gallows—illustrating a punitive, “no‑mercy” approach.', '**Political and logistical constraints:**  Operations were hampered by diplomatic limits (Spanish and Cuban governors repeatedly denied landing rights), disease outbreaks that forced ships back to the United States, and occasional court‑martial (Commodore\\u202fPorter’s unauthorized incursion at Fajardo).  Nonetheless, the squadron was permitted to land in remote areas and cooperate with British forces.', '**Outcome and legacy:**  The capture and execution of the famed pirate Roberto\\u202fCofresí in 1825 is presented as the symbolic end of West‑Indies piracy, with the campaign declared a “victory” despite lingering incidents.  The anti‑piracy experience laid the groundwork for later U.S. maritime law‑enforcement missions that continue in the modern Coast Guard.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Aegean Sea anti-piracy operations of the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegean_Sea_Anti-Piracy_Operations_of_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegean_Sea_Anti-Piracy_Operations_of_the_United_States', 'name': 'Aegean Sea anti-piracy operations of the United States', 'years': '1825-1828', 'short_description': 'U.S. anti-piracy operations in the Aegean Sea (May 1825–December 1828) were conducted by the Mediterranean Squadron to protect American merchant shipping from Greek pirates during the Greek War of Independence, resulting in operational success and the cessation of piracy by 1828.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Intro'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"From 1825 to 1828 the U.S. Navy deployed a squadron to the Aegean Sea t