ey-Pauncefote_Treaty', 'key_points': ['**A groundbreaking arbitration proposal:** In 1897 the United\\u202fStates and Britain negotiated the Olney–Pauncefote Treaty, which would have required both nations to submit major disputes to neutral arbitration rather than resort to war.', '**Broad but uneven support:** President Grover\\u202fCleveland (and later William\\u202fMcKinley), most leading newspapers, academics, and U.S. opinion leaders backed the treaty; in Britain it was championed by Liberal pacifist Randal\\u202fCremer.', '**Irish‑American and Senate opposition:** Irish‑American groups, still hostile to Britain over its treatment of Ireland, vehemently opposed the treaty. The U.S. Senate, eager to retain its constitutional control over treaty making, added amendments that stripped the agreement of its substantive content and imposed a two‑thirds Senate‑approval hurdle before any arbitration could proceed.', '**Failure to pass:** The heavily weakened treaty fell three votes short of the required two‑thirds majority (43‑26), reflecting Senate “Anglophobia” and a reluctance to surrender authority over foreign policy.', '**Historical significance:** Although never ratified, the episode highlighted early 20th‑century tensions between the desire for peaceful conflict resolution and domestic political resistance to ceding sovereign discretion. (See Nelson\\u202fM.\\u202fBlake, “The Olney–Pauncefote Treaty of 1897,” *American Historical Review* 1945.)']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Hay–Pauncefote Treaty', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hay%E2%80%93Pauncefote_Treaty'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hay%E2%80%93Pauncefote_Treaty', 'name': 'Hay–Pauncefote Treaty', 'years': '1901', 'short_description': 'The Hay–Pauncefote Treaty, signed on 18\\u202fNovember\\u202f1901 between the United States and Great Britain, nullified the Clayton–Bulwer Treaty and gave the United States the right to build and control a canal across the Central American isthmus, while guaranteeing neutral access for all nations.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Final_treaty'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1901 Hay–Pauncefote Treaty nullified the 1850 Clayton‑Bulwer Treaty, granting the United States exclusive rights to build and control a Central American canal, while Britain acquiesced. Later U.S. toll exemptions sparked British protests, leading to a 1914 repeal restoring equal tolls.', 'bias_comment': 'English accounts often foreground U.S. strategic motives and portray Britain as a passive concessionist, downplaying Panamanian agency and local impact. Spanish or French sources may stress colonial competition and regional sovereignty, while Latin American narratives can highlight the treaty’s long‑term effects on Panama’s autonomy. Such variations reveal national biases in framing the treaty’s significance.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hay%E2%80%93Pauncefote_Treaty', 'key_points': ['**Legal green‑light for the Panama Canal:** The 1901 Hay–Pauncefote Treaty cancelled the 1850 Clayton‑Bulwer Treaty and officially granted the United States the exclusive right to construct and control a canal across Central America.', '**U.S.‑driven amendments:** While the original draft called for a permanently neutral canal (mirroring the Suez model) and invited other powers to guarantee that neutrality, the U.S. Senate excised the multilateral guarantee and inserted a defense‑clause that allowed the United States to fortify and, in wartime, restrict access to the waterway.', '**British concession, American dominance:** Britain, eager to counter German influence and recognizing its waning regional power, accepted a compromise that left the United States free to build and protect the canal, effectively ceding the strategic project to American hands.', '**Post‑opening toll dispute:** The 1912 Panama Canal Act exempted U.S. coast‑to‑coast vessels from tolls, prompting a sharp British protest that the treaty’s neutral‑access principle had been violated. The controversy culminated in President Wilson’s 1914 push to repeal the exemption, forcing all ships—including American ones—to pay the same tolls.', '**Long‑term impact:** By abolishing the Clayton‑Bulwer restrictions and allowing U.S. fortifications and wartime control, the treaty paved the way for the Panama Canal’s construction, shaped Anglo‑American relations in the early 20th\\u202fcentury, and set a precedent for U.S. dominance over a major global trade route.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'American Revolutionary War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Revolutionary_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Revolutionary_War', 'name': 'American Revolutionary War', 'years': '1775-1783', 'short_description': 'A war of American independence fought between the Patriots and Great Britain from April\\u202f19\\u202f1775 to September\\u202f3\\u202f1783, ending with the Treaty of Paris that recognized United States sovereignty.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Introduction'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The American Revolutionary War (1775‑1783) saw colonists, aided by French and Spanish support, fight British forces across multiple theaters, ending with Britain recognizing U.S. independence in the 1783 Treaty of Paris.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes the decisive American victory, the pivotal role of French and Spanish aid, and the war’s global scale, reflecting a U.S‑centric viewpoint. Articles in French, Spanish, German, etc., may highlight their own contributions, stress British losses, or stress Indigenous experiences, showing linguistic and national biases.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Revolutionary_War', 'key_points': ['**A decisive American victory (1775‑1783) that secured U.S. independence** – The Continental Army, led by George\\u202fWashington, defeated Britain’s far larger forces, culminating in Cornwall\\u202fII’s surrender at Yorktown and the 1783 Treaty of Paris, which forced Britain to recognize the United States as a sovereign nation.', '**Foreign alliances were the war‑changing factor** – French military and naval aid (after the 1778 Treaty of Alliance) and Spanish support turned the conflict from a colonial rebellion into a global war, overwhelming British resources and enabling key victories at Saratoga and Yorktown.', '**A protracted, multi‑theater struggle with huge human cost** – Fighting spanned the northern, middle, southern, western, Caribbean, and Atlantic fronts; combined American, British, German (Hessian), and Native‑American forces suffered tens of thousands of combat and disease deaths, illustrating the war’s breadth and brutality.', '**Political upheaval and enduring legacy** – The war emerged from escalating British taxation and legislative overreach (e.g., Stamp, Townshend, and Intolerable Acts) and was propelled by revolutionary ideas (Common Sense, Declaration of Independence). Its outcome reshaped global geopolitics, ending the first British Empire’s American holdings and inspiring subsequent independence movements worldwide.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'North Rock', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Rock'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Rock', 'name': 'North Rock', 'years': 'present', 'short_description': 'North Rock is an offshore rock near the Gulf of Maine and Bay of Fundy whose ownership is disputed between Canada (New Brunswick) and the U.S. (Maine), forming part of the maritime boundary dispute known as the \"Grey Zone\".', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'First paragraph after title'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'North Rock is an uninhabited offshore rock at the Canada–US maritime border. Both New\\u202fBrunswick (Canada) and Maine (USA) claim sovereignty, making it a disputed “grey zone.” The roc