ould have been the world’s first long‑range turbofan‑powered airliner and a true strategic transport, blending the V‑bombers’ speed with a six‑ab‑rear, 100‑plus‑seat cabin.', '**Technological ripple effect** – Its advanced wing, high‑lift devices and use of the Rolls‑Royce Conway turbofan forced Boeing and Douglas to redesign the 707 and DC‑8, proving the V‑1000’s design was ahead of its time.', '**Cancellation driven by politics and shifting priorities** – By 1955 the Ministry of Supply, pressured by Treasury cuts, a waning need for a bomber‑compatible transport, and the decision to buy Bristol\\u202fBritannias, pulled the plug while the prototype was 80\\u202f% complete and £4\\u202fmillion had already been spent.', '**Consequences for the British aerospace sector** – The termination handed the emerging big‑jet market “without a struggle” to the United States, a point repeatedly stressed in parliamentary debates and by Vickers MD George Edwards, who lamented the loss of a potential export‑lead.', '**Legacy despite failure** – The V‑1000’s concepts lived on in the later Vickers\\u202fVC10 and helped shape the first generation of commercial jets, underscoring how its cancellation is still viewed as a missed industrial opportunity.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Alaska boundary dispute', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaska_boundary_dispute'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaska_boundary_dispute', 'name': 'Alaska boundary dispute', 'years': '1867–1903', 'short_description': 'A territorial dispute between the United States and the United Kingdom (for Canada) over the Alaska panhandle, inherited from the Russian Empire and settled by an international arbitration commission in 1903 that largely favored the American claim.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Arbitration'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Alaska Boundary dispute (1867‑1903) involved a border dispute between U.S. Alaska and British‑controlled Canada, caused by