ts are a major criticism** – sanctions have been linked to shortages of medicine, food and basic services in Cuba, Venezuela, Iran and other jurisdictions, prompting calls from U.S. lawmakers and NGOs to suspend or ease regimes during crises such as the COVID‑19 pandemic.', '**The policy fuels geopolitical backlash** – secondary sanctions alienate allies (particularly the EU) and accelerate “de‑dollarisation” efforts, prompting rivals like China and Russia to develop alternative payment systems and to deepen trade ties that evade U.S. restrictions.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BA%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%B2_%D1%81%D0%B2%D1%8F%D0%B7%D0%B8_%D1%81%D0%BE_%D0%B2%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B6%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%A0%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%A3%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%83', 'key_points': ['**Massive, multilateral pressure:**\\u202fSince February\\u202f2022, dozens of countries and international bodies have imposed sweeping economic, financial and diplomatic sanctions on Russia, Belarus and dozens of affiliated individuals and firms—freezing Central Bank assets, cutting off major banks, banning air traffic, blocking ports and sanctioning top officials and their families. The sheer breadth of the measures underscores a unified global condemnation of the invasion.', '**Escalating “sanctions packages”:**\\u202fThe sanctions regime has been rolled out in a series of\\u202f“packages” (first, second, … up to the seventh and beyond), each expanding restrictions to new sectors—energy, defense, technology, finance, culture, science and even personal travel. The article emphasizes how rapidly the scope widened, turning Russia into the world’s most sanctioned state by early\\u202f2022.', '**Political messaging and deterrence:**\\u202fWestern leaders frame the sanctions as a non‑military tool to punish aggression and prevent a wider war, with the U.S. president warning that “the alternative would be