ure. The unanimous decision was authored by Chief Justice Ellsworth.', 'bias_comment': \"The English article emphasizes the case's legal significance, focusing on admiralty jurisdiction and judicial notice. Non‑English sources (e.g., French, Spanish) may stress the historical context of U.S. maritime law or the geopolitical implications of the arms export, potentially reflecting national narratives about early American law. These variations can introduce subtle bias by highlighting different aspects of the case.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_v._La_Vengeance', 'key_points': ['**Clear affirmation of admiralty jurisdiction:** The Supreme Court ruled that a federal action to forfeit a vessel—here for illegal export of arms—constitutes a civil cause of admiralty and maritime jurisdiction.', '**Judicial notice of geography recognized:** The Court declared that courts may take judicial notice of factual geographic information without needing formal proof, underscoring a pragmatic approach to maritime cases.', '**Unanimous, Ellsworth‑led decision:** Chief Justice Oliver\\u202fEllsworth authored the opinion, which was joined by all other justices, signaling strong, collective endorsement of the holding.', '**Early precedent for maritime forfeiture:** As an 1796 decision, *United States v. La Vengeance* set an important early foundation for how the United States courts handle forfeiture proceedings involving ships, influencing later admiralty jurisprudence.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Territorial claims in the Arctic', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_claims_in_the_Arctic'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_claims_in_the_Arctic', 'name': 'Territorial claims in the Arctic', 'years': '1925–present', 'short_description': 'Eight Arctic coastal states (Canada, Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Norway, Russia, Sweden, United States) claim jurisdiction over land, internal waters, territorial seas, EEZs, and disputed continental shelf extensions in the Arctic, with overlapping claims and ongoing disputes under UNCLOS.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#National_sectors:_1925–2005'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since 1925, Arctic territorial claims have been governed by UNCLOS, limiting coastal states to 12‑nm territorial seas and 200‑nm EEZs. Overlap arises from extended continental‑shelf submissions, while disputes persist over the North\\u202fPole basin, Northwest Passage, Beaufort Sea, and former Hans Island.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources stress legal frameworks and scientific filing processes, while Russian, Danish, and Canadian language pages often foreground national sovereignty narratives and symbolic actions. Some non‑English articles may underplay the \"Arctic rush\" or portray the U.S. position differently due to its non‑ratification of UNCLOS.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_claims_in_the_Arctic', 'key_points': ['**Legal framework dominates the Arctic** – Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) the North\\u202fPole and the surrounding ocean floor are international “heritage of all mankind.”\\u202fCoastal states only control a 12‑nm territorial sea, a 24‑nm contiguous zone and a 200‑nm exclusive economic zone (EEZ); Article\\u202f234 (“Arctic exception”) lets ice‑covered EEZs impose extra environmental rules, but only on a non‑discriminatory scientific basis.', '**Extended continental‑shelf races are the main source of overlap** – Canada, Denmark (via Greenland), Norway and Russia have all filed, or are filing, scientific claims to extend their continental shelves far beyond the 200‑nm limit, potentially giving them exclusive rights to seabed resources.\\u202fThe United\\u202fStates, although a major Arctic actor, has not ratified UNCLOS and therefore has no formal filing, but 