ement unenforceable on public‑policy grounds that such payments could jeopardize ship safety and contractual stability.', 'bias_comment': 'The summary relies solely on the English Wikipedia entry, potentially reflecting a British legal perspective and omitting non‑English or contemporary scholarly critiques of the decision’s impact on maritime labor rights.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_v_Watson', 'key_points': ['**Context & Claim:** In 1791, a seaman on the *Alexander* was promised an extra five guineas for performing additional navigation work during a dangerous voyage, and he sued to enforce that promise.', '**Court’s Ruling:** The King’s\\u202fBench held the supplemental‑wage agreement unenforceable, citing a strong public‑policy objection to rewarding opportunistic claims made under duress at sea.', '**Policy Rationale (Lord\\u202fKenyon):** Enforcing such promises would “materially affect the navigation of this kingdom,” risking ship safety by encouraging sailors to exploit emergencies for extra pay.', '**Legal Principle Reinforced:** Regular wages for sailors cannot be expanded by ad‑hoc, peril‑driven demands, thereby preserving maritime safety and the stability of contractual obligations on board ships.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Atlantic Sounding Co. v. Townsend', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Sounding_Co._v._Townsend'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Sounding_Co._v._Townsend', 'name': 'Atlantic Sounding Co. v. Townsend', 'years': '2009', 'short_description': \"2009 US Supreme Court case holding that a seaman may recover punitive damages under general maritime law for a shipowner's failure to provide maintenance and cure, overturning prior Fifth Circuit restrictions.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph (summary)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Supreme Court held that seamen may recover punitive 