hat had protected Dutch trade.', '**Bylandt’s token resistance and surrender** – After a brief exchange of fire, Bylandt, foreseeing overwhelming British firepower, ordered his ships to strike their colours under secret instructions, a move meant to avoid needless bloodshed but later judged by Dutch public opinion as cowardly and even treasonous.', '**British seizure and diplomatic fallout** – Fielding seized five merchantmen (finding hemp) and escorted the prizes to Portsmouth, while the Dutch fleet returned home protesting the violation of their treaty rights. The incident inflamed‑British sentiment in the Dutch Republic and undermined the fragile neutrality the Netherlands had tried to maintain.', '**Catalyst for the First League of Armed Neutrality** – The outrage prompted Catherine\\u202fII of Russia to champion the “free ship, free goods” principle, leading to the formation of the League of Armed Neutrality, which the Dutch joined in December\\u202f1780 in an attempt to protect their trade from British interference.', '**Escalation to war** – Britain responded by abrogating the 1668 Commercial Treaty, treating Dutch shipping like any other neutral nation, and shortly thereafter declared the Fourth Anglo‑Dutch War, turning the diplomatic dispute sparked by the Fielding‑Bylandt affair into open conflict.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Skirmish near Fort Niagara', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skirmish_near_Fort_Niagara'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skirmish_near_Fort_Niagara', 'name': 'Skirmish near Fort Niagara', 'years': '1814', 'short_description': 'On January\\u202f8\\u202f1814, 70 American volunteers led by General John Swift attacked a group of British soldiers gathering wood half‑mile from Fort Niagara, killing 4 and capturing 8, before withdrawing after a larger British force appeared, resulting in an American victory.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Skirmish'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 8\\u202fJanuary\\u202f1814, American militia under General John Swift attacked a small British wood‑cutting party near Fort Niagara, killing four and capturing eight. The British garrison responded, prompting the Americans to withdraw without further combat.', 'bias_comment': 'English-language accounts emphasize the raid as a bold American success with minimal losses, while British‑English sources downplay its significance, describing it as a minor skirmish. These narrative differences reflect national perspectives on the War of 1812.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skirmish_near_Fort_Niagara', 'key_points': ['**A brief, American‑led raid secured a tactical win:** On\\u202f8\\u202fJanuary\\u202f1814, 70 militia volunteers under General John\\u202fSwift surprised a small British wood‑cutting party half a mile from Fort\\u202fNiagara, killing\\u202f4 and capturing\\u202f8 enemy soldiers while suffering only one fatality.', '**The attack was part of a series of back‑and‑forth skirmishes along the Niagara Frontier:** After the British had burned Buffalo and driven American forces back to Williamsville, Swift’s raid was a daring attempt to probe the British‑held fort and demonstrate that the U.S. still retained offensive capability in the region.', '**British forces reacted cautiously, avoiding a larger engagement:** The noise of the skirmish alerted the garrison; a much larger British force appeared, prompting the Americans to withdraw five miles to stronger positions rather than press the attack—highlighting the limited scale of the encounter.', '**American leadership praised the raid but warned restraint:** Major‑General Amos Hall lauded Swift’s boldness yet cautioned him to hold fire until reinforcements arrived, underscoring the delicate balance between aggression and prudence in the later stages of the War of\\u202f1812.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of United States diplomatic history', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_diplomatic_history'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_diplomatic_history', 'name': 'Timeline of United States diplomatic history', 'years': '1689–present', 'short_description': 'Chronological overview of US diplomatic history from 1689 to the present, listing wars, treaties, and key diplomatic events and policies.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Timeline'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From early isolation, the United States moved through alliance‑building, territorial expansion, and two world wars to become a global power, shaping multilateral institutions while retaining unilateral capabilities, a trajectory reflected in varied historiographical emphases across languages.', 'bias_comment': 'Non‑English versions (e.g., Spanish, French) often stress U.S. imperialism and anti‑colonial perspectives, while the English article emphasizes strategic growth and leadership. These differences can reflect national historiographic traditions: Anglo‑American sources highlight diplomatic achievements, whereas other languages may highlight negative impacts on foreign peoples and the role of economic exploitation.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_diplomatic_history', 'key_points': ['**From isolation to global power:**\\u202fThe United\\u202fStates began with a mostly isolationist stance—limited peacetime military strength, small population, and sporadic colonial wars (e.g., King\\u202fWilliam’s War, Queen\\u202fAnne’s War). Rapid demographic and economic growth soon forced the young nation into overseas trade,, and occasional military engagements (Barbary Wars, War of 1812) that marked its first steps onto the world stage.', '**Turn‑to‑alliance era (late‑18th\\u202f–\\u202fmid‑19th\\u202fc.):**\\u202fDuring the Revolutionary War and the early Republic, the U.S. relied heavily on foreign alliances (France, Spain, the Netherlands) to secure independence and protect its interests. The “European‑American” pattern continued with the Monroe Doctrine (1823), the Mexican‑American War, and the Civil War’s diplomatic isolation, illustrating a pragmatic shift from pure isolation to selective partnership when national survival demanded it.', '**Unilateralism and “big‑stick” expansion (late\\u202f19th\\u202f–\\u202fearly\\u202f20th\\u202fc.):**\\u202fFollowing the Civil War, the United\\u202fStates pursued aggressive territorial expansion (Louisiana Purchase, Alaska, annexation of Hawaii, the Spanish‑American War) and asserted its own hemispheric dominance via doctrines such as the Roosevelt Corollary and “big‑stick” diplomacy. The Open‑Door Policy, Panama Canal negotiations, and the “Good Neighbor” policy showed a pattern of imposing American interests unilaterally or with minimal multilateral oversight.', '**From isolation to collective security (World\\u202fWar\\u202fI\\u202f–\\u202fWorld\\u202fWar\\u202fII):**\\u202fDespite initial neutrality, the U.S. entered both world wars, helped shape the post‑war order (League of Nations, United Nations, Bretton Woods institutions), and cemented its role as a “leader of the free world.” The shift reflected a strategic move from unilateral action toilateral alliances (Treaty of Versailles, NATO) while still maintaining the ability to act independently (e.g., atomic bomb, Marshall Plan).', '**Cold‑War containment and global policing:**\\u202fThe Cold\\u202fWar institutionalized the United\\u202fStates’ blend of alliance‑building (NATO, SEATO, ANZUS) and unilateral actions (Truman Doctrine, Korean War, Vietnam interventions). This period cemented a diplomatic doctrine that combined collective security against communism with the willingness to project power unilaterally when perceived threats arose, a stance that continues to shape contemporary U.S. foreign policy.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Convoy UGS-40', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_U