e primary, heavily‑used channel linking the Strait of Juan\\u202fde\\u202fFuca with the Strait of Georgia, forming the main maritime route to Vancouver’s ports.', '**Crucial border marker** – The strait defines part of the Canada‑U.S. boundary; it was selected by the 1870 arbitrator (Kaiser Wilhelm\\u202fI) in the San\\u202fJuan Islands (Pig\\u202fWar) dispute, cementing the modern border.', '**Historic and cultural hub** – Named by Spanish explorers in 1790, the strait has long served Indigenous Salish peoples for trade and raiding, and later became a focal point of 19th‑century diplomatic conflict.', '**Strategic navigation challenges** – While the shortest route to the Gulf of\\u202fGeorgia, Haro Strait presents dangerous rip tides near Turn\\u202fPoint and Boundary\\u202fPass, demanding careful piloting for commercial traffic.', '**Rich marine ecosystem** – The waters host prolific salmon runs (Fraser River), abundant whale‑watching opportunities, and diverse fauna such as orcas, sea lions, seals, and seabirds, underscoring its environmental and tourism value.']}]}}], 'source': 'USA_UK_discovered.json', 'embedding_model': 'sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2', 'clusters': 138, 'start_year': 1900, 'end_year': 2005, 'countries': ['USA', 'UK'], 'max_events': 200, 'search_engine': 'exa'}\n",
      "{'llm': 'openai/gpt-oss-120b', 'langs': ['en', 'fr', 'ru', 'zh', 'ar', 'he'], 'datetime': '2025-08-12T13:46:41.520458', 'data': [{'seed': {'name': 'Korean Air Lines Flight 007', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_007'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_007', 'name': 'Korean Air Lines Flight 007', 'years': '1983', 'short_description': 'A Korean Air Lines Boeing 747 was shot down by a Soviet Su‑15 interceptor after unintentionally entering prohibited Soviet airspace on September\\u202f1\\u202f1983, resulting in the deaths of all 269 passengers and crew.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Shoot-down'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 1 September 1983, Korean Air Lines Flight 007, a Boeing 747, deviated from its planned route and entered prohibited Soviet airspace over Sakhalin, where Soviet interceptors shot it down, killing all 269 aboard.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic source frames the incident as a Cold‑War flashpoint, stressing Soviet aggression, U.S. anti‑communist reaction, and lasting political impact, while the English source highlights a navigational error, misidentification, and subsequent aviation safety reforms, reflecting differing national narratives and emphasis on political versus technical aspects.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_007', 'key_points': ['**A tragic Cold‑War flashpoint:** On\\u202f1\\u202fSeptember\\u202f1983 a Korean Air Lines\\u202f747 (flight\\u202f007) strayed into a restricted Soviet airspace over the Sakhalin islands and was shot down, killing all\\u202f269 passengers and crew—including U.S. Congressman Larry\\u202fMcDonald.', '**Soviet denial turned accusation:** The USSR first pretended it did not know about the crash, then admitted the intercept, claiming the aircraft was on a “spy mission” and portraying the shoot‑down as a deliberate 