: {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902', 'name': 'Korean Air Lines Flight 902', 'years': '1978', 'short_description': 'On 20\\u202fApril\\u202f1978, a Korean Air Lines Boeing 707 en route from Paris to Seoul was intercepted after deviating into Soviet airspace and was shot down by a Soviet Su‑15, killing two of the 109 occupants; the aircraft later made an emergency landing on the frozen Korpijärvi Lake in the Soviet Union.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph (summary)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 20 April 1978, Korean Air Lines Flight 902, a Boeing 707, unintentionally entered Soviet restricted airspace over the Kola Peninsula. Soviet interceptors fired a missile, disabling the aircraft, which crashed on a frozen lake. Of 121 aboard, 2 died; survivors were rescued.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic entry frames the incident as a Cold‑War flashpoint, highlighting Soviet aggression and propaganda. The English version stresses a defensive response and diplomatic friction, portraying the missile strike as a justified reaction to a perceived threat. The Russian article presents the Soviet view as lawful, focusing on procedural correctness and differing cause explanations, reflecting national narratives and political agendas.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902', 'key_points': ['**KAL\\u202f902\\u202f–\\u202fCold‑War flashpoint:** On\\u202f20\\u202fApril\\u202f1978 a Korean Air Lines Boeing\\u202f707-321B (flight\\u202f902) was struck by a Soviet‑launched SA‑17 missile after unintentionally entering the USSR’s restricted airspace, underscoring the high‑tension atmosphere of the Cold War.', '**Human cost:** Of the 121 people on board (109 passengers\\u202f+\\u202f12 crew), only two died; the remaining 107 survivors were rescued after a dramatic emergency landing in the Soviet Arctic region (Lukchi).', '**Political resonance:** The episode was widely cited alongside other Cold‑War incidents (e.g., KAL\\u202f007, the Korean Peninsula standoff, Soviet air‑space violations) as evidence of Soviet brinkmanship and the perils of navigating contested air corridors during that era.', '**Broader Cold‑War backdrop:** The article is embedded in an extensive catalogue of Cold‑War conflicts, frozen disputes, diplomatic doctrines, and international organisations—highlighting how the KAL\\u202f902 incident fits into a larger narrative of superpower rivalry, proxy wars, and the global race for influence from the 1940s through the 1990s.', '**Legacy & documentation:** The crash is documented by aviation‑safety databases, contemporary Soviet and Western news reports, and scholarly works that argue the event exemplifies the “cover‑up” tendencies and propaganda battles characteristic of Cold‑War-era aviation accidents.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Air_Lines_Flight_902', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War “defensive” action:** The Soviet air defence, convinced the wayward Boeing\\u202f707 was a threat after it breached Soviet airspace, ordered an interceptor to engage and ultimately fired missiles that tore off a wing, causing rapid decompression and forcing the aircraft down.', '**Navigational error and limited loss of life:** The crew’s navigation system was confused by the North Magnetic Pole, leading the plane off its planned Paris‑Anchorage‑Seoul route; only two of the 109 occupants were killed, the rest surviving the crash‑landing on the frozen Korpijärvi\\u202fLake.', '**Soviet rescue mixed with diplomatic friction:** Soviet helicopters rescued the survivors and escorted them to Kem, but the USSR later billed South Korea US\\u202f$100,000 for “caretaking” of the passengers—a charge that was never paid, underscoring the strained East‑West relations.', '**Aftermath and legacy:** The incident spurred international outcry over Soviet shoot‑downs of civilian aircraft, foreshadowing the even deadlier KAL\\u202f007 shoot‑down five years later, while Korean Air continues to operate the Paris‑Seoul route (now non‑stop to Incheon) without the Anchorage stop.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%98%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82_%D1%81_%D1%8E%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BC_%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BC_(1978)?uselang=en', 'key_points': ['**Гражданская авиация в чужом воздушном пространстве** – 20\\u202fапреля\\u202f1978\\u202fгода южнокорейский Boeing\\u202f707 (рейс\\u202fKE‑902) отклонился от курса и, пролетев над Кольским полуостровом, нарушил советскую границу. Советские ПВО не получили от него сигналов распознавания и признали самолёт «нарушителем», несмотря на попытки согласовать принудительную посадку.', '**Военный перехват и ракетный удар** – истребитель Су‑15 431‑го авиационного полка, под командованием капитана Александра\\u202fБосова, совершил несколько попыток «показать» сигналы посадки, на что экипаж «Боинга» не отреагировал. По приказу генерала‑майора Царькова Су‑15 выпустил авиационную ракету\\u202fR‑8, пробив правый двигатель и часть крыла, после чего самолет потерял управляемость и вынужденно сёл на лёд озера Корпиярви.', '**Последствия для экипажа и пассажиров** – из 12 членов экипажа и 97 пассажиров (в том числе 5 детей) погибли двое (один мгновенно от осколка ракеты, второй скончался в госпитале) и 13 получили медицинскую помощь от травм, баротравмы и переохлаждения. Выжившие были эвакуированы в Кемь и возвращены в Южную Корею.', '**Политический и юридический резонанс** – Советский правительственный ком‑комитет признал действия ПВО «правильными и решительными», а советская прокуратура вынесла решение о недопустимости уголовного преследования пилотов, ограничившись их выдворением. СССР потребовал от Южной Кореи возмещение 100\\u202fтыс.\\u202fдолл. США, однако международные авиационные организации (ИКАО) официальных претензий к СССР не выдвинули.', '**Ретроспективные оценки** – официальные отчёты советских и западных источников расходятся в трактовке причины отклонения курса (неисправный полукруговой компас vs. человеческая ошибка). Несмотря на разные версии, инцидент остаётся примером холодной войны, когда нарушение границы было воспринято как потенциальная разведывательная миссия, а реакция ПВО – как законный ответ.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Proxy war', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proxy_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proxy_War', 'name': 'Proxy war', 'years': '1947-1991', 'short_description': 'A proxy war is an armed conflict where two opposing powers support third‑party belligerents—often non‑state actors—to fight on their behalf, allowing major powers to pursue strategic goals without direct combat.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#History'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Proxy war describes a conflict where major powers support opposing non‑state or client forces, supplying resources and political backing while avoiding direct combat. Historically used from medieval times through the Cold War and into modern disputes, such wars often prolong fighting, increase casualties, and create long‑term instability.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources foreground strategic motives and contemporary relevance, framing proxies as a \"least‑bad\" tool amid nuclear deterrence. Russian texts stress hidden costs, blow‑back, and long‑term instability, highlighting dependency and the rise of private military companies. These emphases reflect differing national narratives on great‑power responsibility and the moral evaluation of proxy warfare.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proxy_War', 'key_points': ['**Definition & core dynamic** – A proxy war is a conflict in which two principal powers back opposing non‑state or client forces, letting the “proxy” do the fighting while the sponsors supply money, weapons, training, or political support.', '**Historical continuity** – From medieval privateers and Barbary pirates to the Cold‑War contests (Korea, Vietnam, Afghanistan) and today’s Iran‑Saudi, US‑Russia, and China‑North‑Korea contests, great powers have long used proxies to advance strategic goals without direct confrontation.', '**Key motivations** – Sponsors turn to proxies to (a) avoid the political cost of a declared war and domestic war‑weariness, (b) circumvent treaties or international backlash, (c) reduce financial and casualty burdens, and (d) manage security‑dilemma dynamics by shaping outcomes through cheaper, deniable agents.', '**Impact on conflict** – External backing often escalates wars: battles become longer, more lethal, and less likely to yield diplomatic settlements, with civilian death tolls running into the hundreds of thousands in Iraq, Syria, Yemen, Afghanistan, etc.', '**Contemporary relevance** – Proxy warfare remains a “least‑bad” option for powerful states in an era of nuclear deterrence and media‑scrutinized conflicts, underpinning today’s “grey‑zone” confrontations and shaping the strategic calculus of both liberal democracies and authoritarian regimes.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B8-%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0', 'key_points': ['**Proxy wars are a deliberate “by‑proxy” strategy:** scholars (Mumford, Bar‑Siman‑Tov) stress that a conflict is a proxy war only when external powers intervene *indirectly*—they supply aid, weapons, or political backing while avoiding direct combat.', '**They create hidden costs and long‑term instability:** the “dependency” of client states on great‑power sponsors, the tendency to **prolong and intensify** fighting, and the frequent “blow‑back” (unintended, often hostile, consequences) are highlighted as the chief drawbacks of proxy warfare.', '**Great powers use proxy wars to sidestep nuclear or direct escalation:** during the Cold War the USSR and the USA relied on proxy conflicts (e.g., Korea, Vietnam, Angola, Afghanistan) as the cheapest way to pursue geopolitical aims without risking a full‑scale war.', '**The model is expanding in the 21st\\u202fcentury:** shrinking conventional armies, soaring weapon costs, and the rise of **private military companies** (cheaper, no public casualties) make proxy wars an increasingly attractive tool for future conflicts.', '**Historical examples illustrate the pattern:** from the Spanish Civil War and Angolan war to the U.S.‑Iran‑Hezbollah nexus in the 2000s, each case shows how major powers manipulate local actors to achieve strategic objectives while keeping their own forces largely “hands‑free.”']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Flight 7', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_007'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_007', 'name': 'Flight 7', 'years': '1937–2008', 'short_description': 'Flight 7 (or Flight 007) is a disambiguation term covering several aviation accidents, notably crashes in 1937, 1957, 1962, 1980, 1983, and 2008.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Disambiguation list of flights numbered 7'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The flight number 7/007 has been assigned to several unrelated aviation accidents from 1937 to 2008, involving various airlines, aircraft types, and causes such as mechanical failure, structural issues, and a 1983 shoot‑down that attracted Cold‑War attention.', 'bias_comment': 'English Wikipedia emphasizes the 1983 Korean Air Lines shoot‑down and Cold‑War tension, potentially overshadowing other incidents. Non‑English versions may give more weight to regional accidents (e.g., the 2008 Kata\\u202fAir Transport crash in Korean) or present the pattern of tragedy differently, reflecting local media focus and national narratives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_007', 'key_points': ['**Multiple historic incidents** – The designation “Flight\\u202f7” (or “Flight\\u202f007”) has been used for a series of notable aviation accidents spanning from the 1930s to the 2000s.', '**Varied causes and aircraft** – These events involve different airlines, aircraft types, and failure modes: crashes due to mechanical failure (Western\\u202fAir\\u202fExpress\\u202f1937, Kata\\u202fAir\\u202fTransport\\u202f2008), structural or operational issues (Pan\\u202fAm\\u202f1957, Air\\u202fFrance\\u202f1962, LOT\\u202fPolish\\u202f1980), and even a deliberate shoot‑down (Korean\\u202fAir\\u202fLines\\u202f1983).', '**Geographic and political significance** – While most accidents occurred in the United States or Europe, the 1983 Korean\\u202fAir\\u202fLines incident gained worldwide attention because a civilian Boeing\\u202f747 was downed by a Soviet interceptor, heightening Cold‑War tensions.', '**Pattern of tragedy** – The repeated appearance of the same flight number in high‑profile disasters underscores how a simple identifier can become associated with multiple, distinct aviation tragedies over time.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Strategic Arms Limitation Talks', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Arms_Limitation_Talks'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Arms_Limitation_Talks', 'name': 'Strategic Arms Limitation Talks', 'years': '1969-1979', 'short_description': 'Bilateral U.S.–Soviet negotiations that produced two arms control agreements—SALT\\u202fI (1972), freezing strategic ballistic missile launchers, and SALT\\u202fII (1979), limiting strategic nuclear forces—aimed at curbing the nuclear arms race during the Cold War.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#SALT_I_Treaty'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Strategic Arms Limitation Talks (SALT) were two rounds of U.S.–Soviet negotiations (1969‑1979) that produced SALT I (1972), freezing limits on strategic launchers and ABM sites, and SALT II (1979), setting broader caps but never ratified, influencing later arms‑control treaties.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources stress bilateral Cold‑War dynamics and U.S. policy “linkage” failures; French texts frame SALT I as symbolic and SALT II as overly ambitious, linking it to broader détente and non‑proliferation goals; Russian accounts highlight the high‑stakes nature and strategic breakthrough, downplaying domestic political obstacles. These emphases reflect each language’s national historiographical lenses, potentially biasing interpretations of success, motives, and legacy.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Arms_Limitation_Talks', 'key_points': ['**Bilateral Cold‑War arms control initiative:**\\u202fSALT comprised two rounds of U.S.–Soviet talks (SALT\\u202fI\\u202f1969‑72, SALT\\u202fII\\u202f1972‑79) aimed at halting the strategic nuclear arms race and establishing a framework for future reductions.', '**SALT\\u202fI’s concrete, limited success:**\\u202fThe 1972 agreement froze the number of ICBM/SLBM launchers, imposed a one‑site limit on anti‑ballistic‑missile (ABM) systems, and produced the separate ABM Treaty—seen as a diplomatic breakthrough that “froze” rather than cut arsenals.', '**SALT\\u202fII’s unrealised promise:**\\u202fNegotiated in Vienna and signed in 1979, it would have capped MIRV‑equipped launchers and banned new missile programs, but the U.S. Senate refused ratification after the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan; nevertheless both sides informally observed its limits until 1986.', '**Linkage and its failure:**\\u202fU.S. policy under Nixon/Kissinger linked arms control to other geopolitical issues (Middle East, Berlin, Vietnam).  Historians cite this “linkage” as a flawed assumption that ultimately undermined the effectiveness of the SALT process.', '**Legacy for later treaties:**\\u202fSALT set the diplomatic precedent and technical groundwork for subsequent agreements—START\\u202fI, START\\u202fII, and New\\u202fSTART—shifting the Cold‑War focus from limiting to actually reducing strategic nuclear forces.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Arms_Limitation_Talks', 'key_points': ['**SALT\\u202fI\\u202f(1972)\\u202f–\\u202fpremier cadre limité mais symbolique** – signé à Moscou par Nixon et Brejnev, le traité ne réduit pas les arsenaux\\u202f; il fixe uniquement des plafonds pour les ICBM et les missiles balistiques lancés depuis sous‑marins, marquant la première fois que les deux superpuissances s’accordent sur des limites concrètes pour leurs armes stratégiques offensives.', '**SALT\\u202fII\\u202f(1979)\\u202f–\\u202fun accord trop ambitieux pour le climat géopolitique** – bien que signé à Vienne, le traité n’a jamais été ratifié (refus du Sénat américain après l’invasion soviétique de l’Afghanistan). Malgré l’absence de ratification, les parties ont lorsqu‑même respecté ses dispositions, soulignant que la stabilité stratégique était jugée plus importante que les rivalités idéologiques.', '**Contexte de détente post‑crise de Cuba** – les négociations s’inscrivent dans le mouvement de «\\u202fcoopération pacifique\\u202f» initié par Kennedy et Khrouchtchev, recherchant une parité militaire plutôt qu’une supériorité, afin d’éviter une course aux armements coûteuse et le risque d’une guerre nucléaire accidentelle.', '**Lien avec le Traité de non‑prolifération (TNP) et la politique de maîtrise des armements** – SALT\\u202fI s’appuie sur les obligations du TNP (article\\u202fVI) et constitue la première étape d’une série d’accords ultérieurs (INF, START, New\\u202fSTART) qui visent à encadrer progressivement la réduction et la vérification des arsenaux nucléaires.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Arms_Limitation_Talks', 'key_points': ['**Two‑round, superpower‑level arms‑control process** – From 1969 to 1979 the USSR and the USA conducted a series of high‑stakes negotiations that culminated in two landmark treaties: **SALT‑I (May\\u202f1972)** and **SALT‑II (June\\u202f1979)**.', '**SALT‑I froze and limited the existing strategic arsenals** – It capped the numbers of land‑based ICBMs, submarine‑launched ballistic missiles and launch sites, imposed a single‑site limit on anti‑ballistic‑missile (ABM) systems, and tied the introduction of new sea‑based missiles to the removal of older land‑based ones.', '**SALT‑II expanded and tightened the constraints** – Building on SALT‑I, it added limits on heavy bombers, introduced a ceiling on the total number of strategic delivery vehicles (including a cap on those equipped with multiple independently‑targetable reentry vehicles), and prohibited the deployment of space‑based nuclear weapons; although the U.S. Senate never ratified it, both sides continued to observe its provisions.', '**Strategic and diplomatic significance** – The SALT agreements marked a breakthrough in Cold‑War détente, curbing the costly nuclear arms race, paving the way for later arms‑control frameworks (e.g., the START series, New START) and demonstrating that the two rival superpowers could negotiate verifiable limits even amid deep geopolitical rivalry.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '1960 U-2 incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1960_U-2_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1960_U-2_incident', 'name': '1960 U-2 incident', 'years': '1960', 'short_description': 'On 1\\u202fMay\\u202f1960 a US Lockheed U‑2 spy plane piloted by Francis Gary Powers was shot down over Soviet territory near Aramil, Sverdlovsk Oblast by a Soviet S‑75 Dvina surface‑to‑air missile; Powers parachuted and was captured.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Event'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 1 May 1960 a U.S. U‑2 spy plane piloted by Francis Gary Powers was shot down over Soviet airspace, exposing the U.S. aerial reconnaissance program and triggering a diplomatic crisis that cancelled the Paris summit and led to a prisoner exchange.', 'bias_comment': 'Arabic sources stress Cold‑War ideological framing, portraying the U.S. mission as necessary intelligence and the Soviet reaction as a defense of sovereignty, reflecting regional narratives of superpower rivalry. English sources highlight the cover‑up, political scandal, and technological shifts, focusing on U.S. policy and NATO allies. Chinese sources stress the denial, Soviet exposure, the episode as a low point in U.S.–Soviet relations, and regional impacts on Pakistan and India, adding legal/judicial angles. These differences illustrate how language communities prioritize ideological, political, or regional perspectives, potentially biasing emphasis on blame, consequences, and broader Cold‑War context.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/1960_U-2_incident', 'key_points': ['The 1960 U‑2 incident was a U.S. high‑altitude reconnaissance mission that was downed by Soviet air defenses, leading to the capture of pilot Francis\\u202fGary\\u202fPowers and a dramatic diplomatic showdown between President Dwight\\u202fEisenhower and Soviet Premier Nikita\\u202fKhrushchev.', 'The shoot‑down exposed the United States’ covert over‑flight program, intensified Cold‑War mistrust, and forced the two superpowers into a rapid “prisoner‑exchange” deal that temporarily de‑escalated the crisis but underscored the fragility of the nuclear‑age standoff.', 'Eisenhower’s administration, backed by the CIA, framed the mission as a necessary intelligence‑gathering effort to monitor Soviet military installations, while Soviet leaders portrayed the incident as a blatant violation of their sovereignty and a proof of Western aggression.', 'The episode reinforced prevailing Cold‑War doctrines—such as the “containment” and “domino” theories—highlighting the high‑stakes competition for strategic information and the ever‑present risk of escalation into open conflict.', 'In the broader historical narrative, the U‑2 incident is presented as a pivotal flashpoint that both exemplified and accelerated the escalating tension of the post‑World‑War\\u202fII geopolitical rivalry.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1960_U-2_incident', 'key_points': ['**A daring U‑2 spy mission turned diplomatic disaster:** On\\u202f1\\u202fMay\\u202f1960 a CIA‑run Lockheed U‑2 piloted by Francis\\u202fGary\\u202fPowers was shot down by a Soviet S‑75 (SA‑2) missile deep inside Soviet airspace, exposing the United\\u202fStates’ high‑altitude aerial‑reconnaissance program.', '**The United\\u202fStates’ cover‑up back‑fired spectacularly:** Washington initially claimed the aircraft was a civilian NASA weather plane, but the Soviets paraded the captured pilot, wreckage and surveillance film, forcing a public admission of espionage and igniting a domestic political scandal.', '**Immediate diplomatic fallout crippled the Paris summit:** The incident shattered the “Spirit of Camp\\u202fDavid,” led Khrushchev to cancel the Four‑Power summit, and embarrassed Eisenhower’s administration, prompting intense congressional hearings and a rare presidential admission of responsibility.', '**Strategic consequences reshaped intelligence tactics:** The vulnerability of high‑altitude platforms spurred the U.S. to develop low‑level, high‑speed bombers, accelerate the Corona satellite program, and pursue next‑generation spy aircraft (A‑12, D‑21), while the USSR reverse‑engineered a U‑2 copy and tightened its air‑defence posture.', '**Long‑term political ripples affected allies and the Cold War trajectory:** Pakistan’s security was jeopardised and its U.S. relationship strained; Norway’s temporary U‑2 base was scrutinised; the incident heightened anti‑U.S.ened East‑West mistrust, foreshadowing the intensifying arms race that culminated in the Cuban Missile Crisis.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/1960_U-2_incident', 'key_points': ['**美方先行否认、俄方公开揭露**：美国政府在事件初期坚称U‑2只是气象飞机，试图掩盖其间谍任务；而苏联在赫鲁晓夫的指挥下公开展示残骸与飞行员，加剧了双方的信任危机。', '**美苏关系跌至冷战低点**：U‑2被击落直接导致巴黎四国首脑会议破裂，美国与苏联的紧张局势升至自冷战以来最激烈的阶段。', '**情报与技术转向**：事件暴露高空侦察的局限，促使美国加速研发间谍卫星（科罗娜计划）及超音速侦察机（A‑12/SR‑71），标志着情报手段由飞机转向太空与高超音速平台。', '**外交与地区影响**：美方对巴基斯坦的秘密基地曝光，使美巴关系恶化，苏联则借此加深与印度的合作，进一步撕裂南亚的安全格局。', '**后续司法与舆论**：加利·鲍尔斯被判间谍罪并入狱，后以交换获释；美国的官方掩饰被广泛批评为“总统谎言”，成为冷战时期外交欺瞒的典型案例。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'United States grain embargo against the Soviet Union', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_grain_embargo_against_the_Soviet_Union'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_grain_embargo_against_the_Soviet_Union', 'name': 'United States grain embargo against the Soviet Union', 'years': '1980-1981', 'short_description': 'The United States grain embargo against the Soviet Union was imposed by President Jimmy Carter in January 1980 in response to the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan and was lifted by President Ronald Reagan on April 24, 1981; it aimed to pressure the USSR by cutting off grain exports, affecting American farmers more than the Soviets.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1980 President Carter imposed a grain embargo on the Soviet Union to protest its invasion of Afghanistan. The measure had limited effect on Soviet agriculture, hurt U.S. grain prices, and faced domestic opposition. It was lifted by President Reagan in April 1981.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources emphasize the embargo's symbolism, domestic political fallout, and alleged KGB influence, possibly overstating its strategic significance. Russian-language accounts focus more on Soviet adaptation and minimal economic impact, downplaying U.S. political motives. These divergences reflect national narratives and the Cold‑War lens.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_grain_embargo_against_the_Soviet_Union', 'key_points': ['**Carter’s “food‑as‑weapon” gamble:** The grain embargo was presented by President Jimmy Carter as a way to pressure the Soviet Union over its Afghan invasion, but analysts and contemporaneous observers judged it a dubious, largely symbolic move that failed to cripple Soviet agriculture.', '**Back‑fire on American farmers:** The sudden ban plunged U.S. grain prices, triggered protest strikes by farm groups, and eroded support for Carter among the Rural constituency—making the embargo a political liability in the 1980 presidential race.', '**Limited Soviet impact & quick adaptation:** Deprived of American phosphate fertilizer, the USSR shifted imports to Argentina, Brazil, Venezuela and other South‑American sources, and continued to receive grain under the 1973 agreement, underscoring the embargo’s negligible strategic effect.', '**Cold‑War espionage and political intrigue:** KGB operatives, according to former officers Boris Yuzhin and Oleg\\u202fGordievsky, were directed to discredit Carter and back Ronald\\u202fReagan during the 1980 election, highlighting how the sanction became a covert battlefield.', '**Reagan’s reversal and restored “fertilizer détente”:** After winning the election with farm‑bureau support, Reagan lifted the embargo on 24\\u202fApril\\u202f1981, reviving grain and fertilizer trade—including the lucrative Armand\\u202fHammer‑Occidental Petroleum arrangements—thereby signaling the embargo’s ultimate abandonment as an ineffective policy tool.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Корейская война', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0', 'name': 'Корейская война', 'years': '1950-1953', 'short_description': 'Военный конфликт между Северной и Южной Кореей, начавшийся 25\\u202fиюня\\u202f1950\\u202fгода вторжением КНДР в южный лагерь и завершившийся перемирием 27\\u202fиюля\\u202f1953\\u202fгода, при участии ООН, США, Китая и СССР.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Дата'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Korean War (1950‑53) was a Cold‑War proxy conflict in which North Korean forces, supported by China and the USSR, fought UN troops led by the United States against South Korea, ending in an armistice and a still‑divided peninsula with massive human casualties.', 'bias_comment': 'The Russian article frames the war as a geopolitical flashpoint emphasizing super‑power rivalry and distinct national narratives (e.g., DPRK’s \"Fatherland Liberation War\"). The English version highlights U.S. dominance within the UN coalition and calls the war \"Forgotten\" in the West, reflecting Western‑centric perspectives. Both versions differ in terminology, casualty emphasis, and portrayal of agency, illustrating how national historiographies shape interpretation.', 'languages_used': ['ru', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War flashpoint with competing super‑powers** – The Korean War is portrayed as a classic proxy conflict of the Cold War, where the Soviet Union and China backed the DPRK while the United States led a UN coalition supporting the ROK. The text stresses the geopolitical stakes, the reluctance of Stalin to trigger a wider war, and the way both blocs turned the Korean peninsula into a battlefield for their ideological rivalry.', '**North‑Korean ambition vs. South‑Korean resistance** – The war’s trigger is framed as Kim\\u202fIl‑sung’s deliberate attempt to “unify” Korea under communist rule, opposed by a South led by Syngman\\u202fRhee that claimed legitimacy over the whole peninsula. The narrative underscores the rapid early success of the DPRK’s forces, the desperate defence at the Pusan Perimeter, and the later UN counter‑offensive that pushed the front back to roughly the 38th parallel.', '**Massive human and material loss, with lasting division** – Casualty figures run into millions (civilian deaths exceeding those of WWII in proportion), and the text highlights the extensive destruction of cities, the bombing of the North, and the loss of thousands of aircraft, tanks, ships, and other equipment. The war’s “final” outcome is the permanent split of Korea, the creation of a 4‑km‑wide Demilitarized Zone, and the fact that a formal peace treaty was never signed, leaving the conflict technically ongoing.', '**Divergent national narratives and terminology** – The article notes how each side labels the conflict to reflect its own stance: the ROK calls it the “June\\u202f25 Incident,” the DPRK calls it the “Fatherland Liberation War,” while the PRC frames it as a “War of Resistance against American Aggression.” These differing names illustrate the competing historical interpretations and political legitimation each government continues to promote.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War proxy war with an inconclusive outcome** – The Korean War (1950‑53) was one of the first major Cold‑War proxy conflicts, pitting Communist‑backed North Korea (with China and the USSR) against U.N. forces led by the United\\u202fStates supporting South Korea; the fighting ended in an armistice and a still‑divided peninsula.', '**Massive human cost and devastation** – Over 1\\u202fmillion military deaths and 1.6–3\\u202fmillion civilian casualties occurred, with widespread destruction of Korea’s cities; North Korea became one of the most heavily bombed nations in history.', '**U.S. dominance within the UN coalition** – Although 21 nations contributed troops, the United\\u202fStates supplied roughly 90\\u202f% of personnel and dictated the strategic direction, framing its intervention as a “police action” to contain Communism and protect the security of Japan.', '**Territorial swing and stalemate** – Initial North Korean advances captured Seoul, but the Inchon landing and subsequent U.N. offensives pushed to the Yalu River; Chinese intervention forced a retreat, and after two years of back‑and‑forth battles the front settled near the 38th parallel, leading to the creation of the Demilitarized Zone.', '**Legacy of a “forgotten” yet ongoing conflict** – Despite the armistice, no peace treaty was signed, leaving the Korean War a “frozen conflict” that still shapes regional politics, military postures, and collective memory (often called the “Forgotten War” in the West).']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Холодная война', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A5%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A5%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0', 'name': 'Холодная война', 'years': '1946-1991', 'short_description': 'Глобальное геополитическое, военное, технологическое, экономическое и идеологическое противостояние между блоком во главе с СССР (социалистический лагерь) и блоком во главе с США (капиталистический мир) в период 1946–1991 годов.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph after title (definition)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Cold War (c.\\u202f1946‑1991) was a prolonged geopolitical rivalry between the United States and its allies and the Soviet Union and its allies, featuring ideological competition, proxy wars, arms and space races, diplomatic crises, and ending with the Soviet collapse and U.S. dominance.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources tend to emphasize the United States' perspective, framing the rivalry as a struggle for freedom against communism, while Russian sources highlight Soviet security concerns and portray Western actions as imperialist. Both narratives downplay internal dissent and overstate the binary nature of global alignment.\", 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A5%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0', 'key_points': ['**A bipolar, ideologically‑driven showdown:**\\u202fFrom 1947‑1991 the United\\u202fStates and the Soviet Union (with their capitalist‑Western and communist‑Eastern blocs) locked into a global struggle for political, economic and cultural dominance, even though the two superpowers never fought each other directly.', '**Proxy wars and “hot” flashpoints:**\\u202fThe rivalry manifested in regional conflicts—Korea, Vietnam, the Cuban Missile Crisis, the Afghan war, and countless Third‑World coups—where each side supplied arms, money and advisers to opposing factions, turning local disputes into extensions of the super‑power contest.', '**Arms, technology and psychological warfare:**\\u202fA relentless nuclear and conventional arms race, the Space Race, extensive espionage, disinformation campaigns, and massive propaganda (e.g., Radio Free Europe, Soviet “active measures”) were used to demonstrate superiority and intimidate the opponent while avoiding outright war.', '**From détente to collapse and US hegemony:**\\u202fPeriods of easing (détente) gave way to renewed tensions in the 1970s‑80s; Mikhail\\u202fGorbachev’s reforms and the 1989 revolutions shattered Soviet control, leading to the USSR’s 1991 dissolution and leaving the United\\u202fStates as the sole superpower.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A5%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0', 'key_points': ['**Идеологическое противостояние сверхдержав** – Холодная война (≈\\u202f1946‑1991) воспринималась как глобальная битва между социалистическим блоком во главе с СССР (ОВД\\u202f+\\u202fСЭВ) и капиталистическим Западом, возглавляемым США, НАТО и ЕЭС, где каждый блок пытался доминировать в политической, экономической и культурной сферах.', '**Ключевые кризисы и «гонка вооружений»** – Наиболее опасными моментами стали блокада Берлина, Карибский кризис 1962\\u202fг., Корейская война, а также стремительное развитие ядерного потенциала обеих сторон (первый советский ядерный взрыв\\u202f—\\u202f1949\\u202fг.) и планирование взаимных ударов, которые почти привели к Третьей мировой войне.', '**Политика сдерживания и дипломатический отклик** – США в ответ на советскую экспансию развернули доктрину Трумэна, план NSC‑68, создание НАТО и масштабные экономические программы (План Маршалла); СССР ответил формированием Варшавского договора, усилением репрессий внутри и внешними провокациями.', '**Разложение социалистического блока и «победа» США** – Советско‑китайский раскол, реформы Горбачёва (перестройка\\u202f+\\u202fгласность) и массовые демократические движения в Восточной Европе привели к распаду ОВД, СЭВ и к окончательному краху СССР в 1991\\u202fг.; США и их союзники позиционировали эти события как окончание конфронтации и переход к однополярному миру.', '**Разные интерпретации причин конфликта** – Как советские, так и западные исследователи спорят о вине: советские источники обвиняют империализм США, а западные – агрессивную политику СССР; обе стороны признают, что взаимные недоверия и стремление к геополитическому доминированию подпитывали десятилетия напряжённости.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Война во Вьетнаме', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%92%D1%8C%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%92%D1%8C%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5', 'name': 'Война во Вьетнаме', 'years': '1955–1975', 'short_description': 'Вьетнамская война — вооружённый конфликт в Вьетнаме, Лаосе и Камбодже между 1\\u202fноября\\u202f1955 (или 1960) и падением Сайгона 30\\u202fапреля\\u202f1975\\u202fгода, являвшийся частью второй Индокитайской войны и Холодной войны.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph (before first heading)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Vietnam War (1955‑1975) was a Cold‑War proxy conflict between communist North Vietnam, backed by the Soviet bloc and China, and South Vietnam, supported by the United States and its allies, resulting in massive casualties, regional spillover, and the 1975 reunification of Vietnam under communist rule.', 'bias_comment': 'The Russian source emphasizes the war’s Cold‑War context, the involvement of over 40 states, and portrays it as a grand geopolitical struggle. The English source stresses U.S. troop escalation, domestic backlash, environmental damage, and regional fallout, highlighting American and humanitarian perspectives. These differences reflect each language’s national historiographical focus.', 'languages_used': ['ru', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%92%D1%8C%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5', 'key_points': ['**Продолжительность и контекст** – Война во Вьетнаме (вторая Индокитайская война) охватила период с\\u202f1955/1960\\u202fпо\\u202f1975\\u202fгод и стала ключевым сценарием холодной войны, где Восток и Запад сражались через местные силы.', '**Стороны конфликта** – Противоборствовали Южный Вьетнам с поддержкой США, Южной Кореи, Австралии, Новой\\u202fЗеландии, Таиланда, Филиппин и прочих союзников против Северного Вьетнама, Национ фронта освобождения (Вьетконг) и их советских, китайских, корейских и восточно‑европейских покровителей.', '**Колоссальные потери** – По разным оценкам общие жертвы составили от\\u202f1,3\\u202fдо\\u202f4,2\\u202fмлн человек, включая\\u202f≈850\\u202f000 погибших и раненных северных и партизанских сил,\\u202f≈58\\u202f000 погибших американцев и десятки тысяч военнослужащих союзных стран, а также сотни тысяч гражданского населения.', '**Итоги и политический поворот** – Парижское мирное соглашение 1973\\u202fгода привело к выводу американских войск; в апреле\\u202f1975\\u202fгода падение Сайгона завершило войну, Республика\\u202fВьетнам исчезла, а Вьетнам объединился под руководством Коммунистической партии в виде Социалистической Республики Вьетнам.', '**Широкий международный масштаб** – В конфликт было втянуто более\\u202f40\\u202fстран (как в качестве прямых участников, так и в роли финансово‑политических спонсоров), что делает его одним из крупнейших локальных вооружённых конфликтов второй половины\\u202fXX\\u202fвека.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_War', 'key_points': ['**A protracted, Cold‑War “proxy” conflict (Nov\\u202f1955\\u202f–\\u202fApr\\u202f1975)** – The Vietnam War pitted communist‑backed North Vietnam (with Soviet, Chinese, and later North Korean support) against US‑backed South Vietnam and a coalition of anti‑communist allies, spreading into Laos and Cambodia and ending in a decisive North Vietnamese victory and national reunification.', '**Escalation and heavy American involvement** – After the 1964 Gulf of Tonkin incident, U.S. President\\u202fLyndon\\u202fB.\\u202fJohnson authorized massive troop deployments (peaking at\\u202f536\\u202f000 in 1969) and an extensive bombing campaign (“Rolling Thunder”), while the 1968 Tet Offensive shattered U.S. public confidence despite being a tactical defeat for the communists.', '**Massive human cost and lasting trauma** – Estimates of Vietnamese military and civilian deaths range from\\u202f0.9\\u202fto\\u202f3\\u202fmillion; U.S. losses exceed\\u202f58\\u202f000 killed and\\u202f300\\u202f000 wounded. The war caused widespread environmental damage (Agent\\u202fOrange), a refugee crisis, and deep psychological scars that fostered “Vietnam syndrome”—a lingering U.S. aversion to overseas military interventions.', '**Complex regional fallout** – The conflict dovetailed with the Laotian and Cambodian civil wars, contributed to the rise of the Khmer Rouge and the Cambodian genocide, and provoked later Sino‑Vietnamese clashes, illustrating how the war reshaped the whole Indochinese peninsula.', '**Enduring historical debates** – While the war is commonly called the “Vietnam War,” it is also referred to as the “Second Indochina War,” the “Vietnam Conflict,” or in Vietnam proper as the “Resistance War against America,” underscoring contested narratives about liberation, imperialism, and national identity.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Hungarian Revolution of 1956', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1956_Hungarian_Revolution'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1956_Hungarian_Revolution', 'name': 'Hungarian Revolution of 1956', 'years': '1956', 'short_description': \"The Hungarian Revolution of 1956 (23\\u202fOctober\\u202f–\\u202f4\\u202fNovember\\u202f1956) was a nationwide uprising in the Hungarian People's Republic against Soviet‑imposed communist rule. Sparked by student protests in Budapest, it quickly spread, establishing a new government that announced withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact. After 12 days of fighting, Soviet forces intervened, crushing the revolt and prompting thousands of deaths and a massive exodus.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph (first summary)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In October 1956, Hungarian citizens staged an uprising demanding reforms and Soviet withdrawal. After brief concessions, Soviet forces intervened, crushing the revolt by early November, causing thousands of deaths and exiles. The event became a pivotal Cold‑War flashpoint and enduring symbol of resistance.', 'bias_comment': 'English-language coverage often frames the 1956 uprising as a Cold‑War flashpoint and a symbol of resistance, emphasizing civilian agency and Soviet repression. Hungarian sources stress national sovereignty and martyrdom, while Russian sources may describe it as a counter‑revolutionary event. These linguistic differences affect emphasis on reform versus anti‑Soviet insurgency.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1956_Hungarian_Revolution', 'key_points': ['**Grassroots uprising:** A spontaneous, citizen‑led revolt erupted on 23\\u202fOctober\\u202f1956 in Budapest, driven by students, workers, and intellectuals demanding political reform, free elections, and withdrawal of Soviet troops.', '**Soviet crackdown:** After a brief period of limited concessions, the USSR intervened with massive military force—deploying tanks, troops, and the KGB—to crush the movement, re‑asserting control over Hungary by 10\\u202fNovember\\u202f1956.', '**Heavy human toll:** The Soviet‑backed repression left roughly\\u202f2,500\\u202fHungarians dead, thousands wounded, and about\\u202f200,000\\u202fpeople forced into exile, while many revolutionary leaders were executed or imprisoned.', '**Cold‑War flashpoint:** The revolt highlighted the deep fissures within the Eastern Bloc, inspiring anti‑Soviet sentiment across the region and underscoring the USSR’s willingness to use force to maintain its sphere of influence.', '**Enduring legacy:** Despite its defeat, the 1956 Revolution remains a powerful symbol of national resistance and a pivotal moment in the broader narrative of Cold‑War dissent that later influenced the liberalizing reforms of the 1980s.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Korean War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'name': 'Korean War', 'years': '1950-1953', 'short_description': 'The Korean War (25\\u202fJune\\u202f1950\\u202f–\\u202f27\\u202fJuly\\u202f1953) was a Cold‑War proxy conflict between North Korea, supported by China and the Soviet Union, and South Korea, backed by United Nations forces led by the United States, ending in an armistice that created the Korean Demilitarized Zone.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Korean War (1950‑1953) was a conflict between North Korean forces, supported by China and the Soviet Union, and South Korean forces backed by a United Nations coalition led by the United States. An armistice in 1953 established a heavily fortified Demilitarized Zone, leaving the peninsula divided.', 'bias_comment': \"Arabic sources stress Cold‑War proxy dynamics and highlight U.S./UN dominance, portraying South Korea as defending against aggression. English sources balance proxy framing with human cost and note competing names, reflecting Western and Korean perspectives. Chinese sources underline the war as a forgotten proxy conflict, emphasize multinational casualties, and frame the UN effort as collective security. These differences reveal national narratives, terminology choices, and emphasis on either ideological struggle or humanitarian impact, influencing readers' perception of responsibility and legacy.\", 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'key_points': ['**The Korean War is framed as a Cold‑War “proxy” that began as a Korean civil conflict** – the text stresses that the 25\\u202fJune\\u202f1950 North‑Korean invasion turned a fragile post‑World‑War division of the peninsula (the 38th parallel, set by the United\\u202fStates and the USSR) into an ideological showdown between communism and “the free world.”', '**U.S. and United Nations involvement is portrayed as decisive and overwhelmingly dominant** – the United\\u202fStates supplied the bulk of the ground, naval and air forces (≈50\\u202f% of the troops and >90\\u202f% of air power) and led a multinational UN coalition, while the South Korean government is repeatedly described as “defending” against North Korean aggression.', '**North Korean, Chinese and Soviet support is highlighted as extensive yet uneven** – the North relied on Soviet military advisors, limited Soviet arms, and massive Chinese “volunteer” forces; the narrative underscores China’s warning to the United\\u202fStates, its eventual entry in late\\u202f1950, and the limited Soviet logistical aid.', '**The war’s legacy is presented through its political terminology and outcomes** – multiple Korean names (e.g., “6·25 War,” “War of Liberation,” “Forgotten War”) reflect competing national narratives; the cease‑fire (1953) is emphasized as solidifying the division of Korea, creating the Demilitarized Zone and entrenching the Cold‑War split on the peninsula.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'key_points': ['**A Cold‑War proxy conflict:** The Korean War (1950‑1953) was one of the first major “proxy wars,” pitting U.S.–led UN forces (with South Korea) against a North Korean army backed by China and the Soviet Union, and it became a flashpoint in the wider East‑West standoff.', '**Inconclusive military outcome:** Fighting ended with an armistice, not a peace treaty, establishing the heavily fortified Korean Demilitarized Zone; both sides suffered massive losses, and the border essentially reverted to the pre‑war 38th‑parallel line.', '**Human cost and devastation:** Over 1‑3\\u202fmillion civilians died, more than a million soldiers were killed, and North Korea was bombed to the point of being “one of the most heavily bombed countries in history,” while major Korean cities were largely destroyed.', '**Competing narratives and names:** South Korea refers to the conflict as the “625 War,” the United States called it a “police action” and later “The Forgotten/Unknown War,” while North Korea frames it as the “Fatherland Liberation War,” reflecting deep ideological divides over its meaning.', '**Legacy of division:** The war’s unresolved status created a “frozen conflict” that still shapes regional security, U.S. policy in East Asia, and inter‑Korean relations, with periodic tensions and diplomatic efforts (e.g., the 2018 Panmunjom Declaration) failing to secure a lasting peace.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War proxy and “forgotten” conflict** – The Korean War is framed as the first major Cold‑War “proxy war,” pitting the U.S.–led UN forces against Chinese‑backed North Korean troops, yet it is often called the “Forgotten War” in the West because it is eclipsed by World War\\u202fII and the Vietnam War.', '**Massive multinational involvement and casualties** – Over 16 nations contributed troops, medical aid, or supplies; total military personnel deployed reached nearly a million on each side, with civilian deaths estimated at 2–3\\u202fmillion and total military deaths exceeding 1\\u202fmillion, making it one of the bloodiest 20th‑century conflicts.', '**Competing political narratives and naming** – Different sides label the war according to ideology: North Korea calls it the “祖国解放战争” (Liberation of the Motherland), South Korea refers to the “六二五事变” (June\\u202f25\\u202fincident), China calls it the “抗美援朝战争” (War to Resist U.S. Aggression and Aid Korea), while Western sources use “Korean War” or “Korean Conflict.”', '**UN as a collective security instrument** – The United Nations, with the United States as the de‑facto commander, organized the first large‑scale UN military operation, authorising a multinational force that turned the war into an internationally sanctioned effort to repel North Korean aggression.', '**Long‑lasting geopolitical legacy** – The armistice in 1953 created the Korean Demilitarized Zone, cemented the division of the peninsula, spurred U.S. anti‑communist policies (e.g., the Seventh Fleet in the Taiwan Strait), and set the stage for subsequent East‑Asian tensions that persist today.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'User:JustinTime55/sandbox/Space Race - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:JustinTime55/sandbox/Space_Race'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:JustinTime55/sandbox/Space_Race', 'name': 'Space Race', 'years': '1955-1991', 'short_description': 'A Cold War rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union to achieve superior spaceflight capability, featuring milestones such as Sputnik, the first human in space, and the US Apollo Moon landings, ending with cooperation on the International Space Station.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph describing the Space Race'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From 1955 to 1991, the United\\xa0States and the USSR competed in space, driven by Cold‑War rivalry. The USSR achieved early milestones like Sputnik and Gagarin; the US pursued a lunar landing, achieving Apollo\\xa011. Both invested heavily, leaving lasting technological legacies.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often emphasize the US lunar landing as the decisive victory, marginalising Soviet early achievements and framing the race as a security‑driven contest. Other language sources may foreground Soviet scientific breakthroughs or portray the competition as a broader human endeavour, shifting emphasis from ideology to technology.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:JustinTime55/sandbox/Space_Race', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War rivalry as the engine of the Space Race (1955‑1991).**', 'The competition was framed as a matter of national security and prestige, with the USSR and the United\\u202fStates vying to prove technological and ideological superiority in space.', '**Soviet early lead versus American “moon‑shot” turning point.**', 'The USSR seized the first milestones – Sputnik, Yuri\\u202fGagarin’s orbital flight, the first spacewalk and long‑duration missions – but the United\\u202fStates, after President Kennedy’s 1961 pledge to land a man on the Moon, overtook the Soviets with the development of the Saturn\\u202fV super‑heavy‑lift rocket and the Apollo program, culminating in the 1969 Moon landing that the article treats as the decisive U.S. victory.', '**Massive government investment and enduring technical legacies.**']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Cold War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_war'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_war', 'name': 'Cold War', 'years': '1947–1991', 'short_description': 'A global geopolitical rivalry between the United States and its allies versus the Soviet Union and its allies, marked by ideological conflict, arms and space races, and numerous proxy wars, but without direct warfare between the two superpowers.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph (intro summary)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Cold War (1947–1991) was a prolonged geopolitical rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union, marked by proxy wars, nuclear and conventional arms races, espionage, ideological competition, and diplomatic crises, ultimately ending with the Soviet Union’s dissolution in 1991.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language coverage often frames the conflict from a Western perspective, emphasizing US strategies and portraying Soviet actions as aggressive. Russian and other non‑Western language versions may stress defensive motives, highlight anti‑imperialist aspects, or downplay US interventions, leading to differing emphasis on events and moral judgments.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_war', 'key_points': ['**A global, “cold” rivalry:**\\u202fThe Cold War was a 44‑year contest between the United States and the Soviet Union that never turned into direct warfare but was fought through proxy wars, an escalating nuclear‑and‑conventional arms race, the Space Race, espionage, propaganda, sanctions and sports diplomacy.', '**Opposing strategies of containment vs. expansion:**\\u202fWashington responded to Soviet‑installed satellite regimes and the “Iron Curtain” with the Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan, NATO and a policy of containment/rollback; Moscow countered with the Warsaw Pact, Comecon, Cominform and heavy support for communist parties and left‑wing insurgencies worldwide.', '**Flashpoints that defined the tension:**\\u202fKey crises—Berlin Blockade/Airlift, Korean War, Cuban Missile Crisis, Vietnam War, the Soviet invasions of Hungary (1956) and Czechoslovakia (1968), the Sino‑Soviet split, and the Soviet‑Afghan War—illustrated how ideological conflict repeatedly threatened to erupt into open war.', '**Intelligence and propaganda as front‑line weapons:**\\u202fBoth blocs built massive espionage networks (KGB, CIA, Venona, atomic spies) and used radio, film, and covert operations to influence public opinion and undermine each other’s regimes, while “active measures” and disinformation became hallmarks of Soviet strategy.', '**From détente to dissolution:**\\u202fAfter a period of arms‑control talks and reduced tensions in the 1970s, renewed confrontations in the 1980s gave way to Gorbachev’s glasnost and perestroika, sparking the 1989 revolutions, the collapse of the Eastern Bloc, and the 1991 dissolution of the USSR, leaving the United\\u202fStates as the sole superpower.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Russian Empire–United States relations', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Empire%E2%80%93United_States_relations'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Empire%E2%80%93United_States_relations', 'name': 'Russian Empire–United States relations', 'years': '1803-1917', 'short_description': 'The Russian Empire officially recognized the United States in 1803 and established diplomatic relations in 1809, maintaining mostly cordial trade and political ties—including the 1867 Alaska purchase—until the empire’s collapse in 1917.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From early trade in the 1760s to the 1917 Russian revolutions, the Russian Empire and United States maintained generally cordial but limited relations, marked by neutral stances, trade treaties, immigration, occasional cooperation, and tensions over Russian anti‑Jewish violence.', 'bias_comment': 'The English summary stresses cooperation, trade, and humanitarian concerns, portraying relations as mostly friendly. The Russian text frames the relationship as a strategic security pillar, emphasizing cycles of tension, ideological rivalry, and modern sanctions. These divergent lenses reflect national narratives: US sources highlight mutual benefits, while Russian sources spotlight geopolitical competition.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_Empire%E2%80%93United_States_relations', 'key_points': ['**Long‑standing, mostly cordial ties:** From the 1760s Russian merchants traded with the Thirteen Colonies, and the two powers formally recognized each other (U.S. in\\u202f1803, diplomatic relations in\\u202f1809), maintaining generally friendly but low‑key relations through the 19th\\u202fcentury.', '**Strategic neutrality & occasional cooperation:** Russia stayed neutral during the American Revolution and the Civil War, while both nations cooperated on maritime law, trade, and joint diplomatic initiatives (e.g., Russia’s “Armed Neutrality” declaration, the 1824 Russo‑American treaty, and the 1867 Alaska purchase).', '**Underlying frictions and humanitarian concerns:** Repeated Russian pogroms (1890‑1914) and anti‑Jewish violence sparked U.S. public outcry and strained relations; the U.S. responded with relief efforts and tightened commercial ties.', '**Immigration and cultural exchange:** Massive migration from the Russian Empire—mainly Jews, Poles, Lithuanians, and Finns—fueled a growing Russian‑American community, reinforcing cultural links despite diplomatic coolness.', '**End of the imperial era:** The 1917 Russian revolutions and the collapse of the Russian Empire abruptly terminated the historic bilateral framework, paving the way for the Soviet‑U.S. relationship that followed.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A0%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE-%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F', 'key_points': ['**Strategic cornerstone:** Русско‑американские отношения рассматриваются как один из ключевых факторов глобальной безопасности\\u202f–\\u202fих состояние напрямую влияет на мировую стабильность.', '**Историческая динамика\\u202f–\\u202f«похолодания» и «потепления»:** более 200\\u202fлет дипломатических контактов (от 1698\\u202fг. и официальных отношений 1807\\u202fг.) включают периоды тесного сотрудничества (например, взаимная помощь в Американском голоде 1891‑92) и серьёзных конфликтов (русско‑японская война, Холодная война, советская интервенция в Афганистане).', '**Современный спад:** По данным МИД РФ (2019) и Госдепа США (2021) отношения находятся в «неблагоприятной»/«сниженной» фазе, характеризующейся ростом взаимных санкций, дипломатических закрытий и геополитической конкуренции (например, в Восточной Азии и на Ближнем Востоке).', '**Идеологическое и экономическое соперничество:** С XIX\\u202fв. и особенно в ХХ\\u202fв. соперничество усиливалось из‑за различий в политических режимах, борьбы за влияние в Азии, России‑японских конфликтов, гонки вооружений и конкуренции за ресурсы, что привело к многократным фазам холодных конфликтов и попыткам разрядки.', '**Переход к партнерству и окончание холодной войны:** Перестройка и дипломатические инициативы (договоры об ограничении ядерного вооружения, разрядка 1970‑х, встречи Рейган‑Горбачёв) позволили превратить противоборство в более сбалансированное взаимодействие, но после распада СССР глобальная конкуренция вновь усилилась.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Soviet Union–United States relations', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.-Soviet_relations'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.-Soviet_relations', 'name': 'Soviet Union–United States relations', 'years': '1933-1991', 'short_description': \"Bilateral relations between the Soviet Union and the United States, established in 1933, encompassed WWII alliance, Cold War rivalry, and concluded with the USSR's dissolution in 1991.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#History'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"From 1933 to 1991 the United States and Soviet Union moved from limited diplomatic recognition to wartime cooperation, then to a Cold War rivalry marked by arms races and crises, followed by periods of détente, and finally the Soviet collapse, shaping today's Russia‑U.S. relations.\", 'bias_comment': 'The English summary stresses U.S. views of the USSR as a hostile, ideological adversary and highlights American aid and Soviet propaganda, reflecting a Western historiography. The Russian summary foregrounds early U.S. economic interest, portrays diplomatic ties as strategically delayed, and emphasizes mutual cooperation and later de‑escalation, echoing Russian narratives. These language‑specific emphases illustrate how national perspectives shape event framing and what details are foregrounded or downplayed.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.-Soviet_relations', 'key_points': ['**Foundations of deep mistrust:** From the outset, U.S. officials portrayed the Soviet Union as a hostile, subversive force—citing its revolutionary ideology, militant atheism, and aggressive foreign policy—while the USSR was seen as a “pariah” threatening Western democratic values.', '**War‑time partnership of convenience:** The German invasion of 1941 forced a pragmatic alliance; massive U.S. Lend‑Lease aid (tens of billions of dollars in equipment, food, and fuel) helped the Red Army defeat the Axis, yet the cooperation remained limited and was later exploited by Soviet propaganda as evidence of an American plot.', '**Cold War rivalry and arms race:** After 1945 the two superpowers entered a protracted struggle for global influence—each leading opposing blocs, competing in nuclear and conventional weapon development, and engaging in pervasive espionage—punctuated by crises such as Berlin, the Cuban Missile Crisis, and the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan.', '**Détente as a fragile easing:** Beginning in the late 1960s, the Nixon‑Kissinger era produced high‑level summits, SALT treaties, and the Helsinki Accords, but underlying ideological conflict persisted, with Soviet interventions in Hungary, Czechoslovakia, and Afghanistan repeatedly undermining the thaw.', '**Legacy of a turbulent partnership:** The relationship, which started with tentative diplomatic recognition in 1933, oscillated between uneasy cooperation and open hostility, ultimately ending with the USSR’s collapse in 1991 and shaping the subsequent Russia‑U.S. dynamic.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE-%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F', 'key_points': ['**Отношения\\u202f—\\u202fчерез\\u202fпротиворечивый цикл конфликтов и экономического сотрудничества**: США сначала поддержали интервенцию против Советской России, но уже в начале 1920‑х годов американский бизнес заинтересовался советским рынком, открыв торговлю и инвестиционные проекты (форды, асбест, золотой резерв).', '**Дипломатическое признание\\u202f—\\u202fзапоздалый, но важный шаг**: официальные дипломатические контакты установились лишь в\\u202f1933\\u202fгоду, когда растущая угроза со стороны нацистской Германии и Японии вынудила обе державы искать совместные стратегии.', '**Вторая мировая война\\u202f—\\u202fвременное сближение, а затем «холодная война»**: после начала Второй мировой войны СССР и США стали союзниками (Ленд‑лиз, совместные операции), но уже в\\u202f1947\\u202fгоду расколок началась гонка вооружений, идеологическое противостояние и взаимные эмбарго.', '**Разрядка и дипломатические инициативы\\u202f—\\u202fуспехи 1950‑‑1970‑х годов**: встречи Хрущёва‑Эйзенхауэра (1955), «кухонные дебаты» (1959), многочисленные договоры о контроле над ядерным оружием и культурные обмены свидетельствовали о желании уменьшить риск конфликта.', '**Кульминация\\u202f—\\u202fот «империи зла» к окончанию холодной войны**: с приходом Горбачёва США перестали воспринимать СССР как враждебного противника; серии саммитов (Женева\\u202f1985, Рейкьявик\\u202f1986, Мальта\\u202f1989) и соглашения о СНВ привели к окончательной разрядке и распаду Советского Союза в\\u202f1991\\u202fгоду.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Category:Cold War conflicts - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Cold_War_conflicts'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Cold_War_conflicts', 'name': 'Category:Cold War conflicts - Wikipedia', 'years': '1947-1991', 'short_description': 'A Wikipedia category that compiles conflicts linked to or taking place within the broader Cold War era (approximately 1947–1991).', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Pages_in_category_\"Cold_War_conflicts\"'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia category “Cold War conflicts” lists wars, insurgencies, and confrontations linked to the Cold War. The English version contains 98 pages across 71 alphabetic sub‑categories, while the Chinese Wikipedia lacks this category, directing users to search or create a comparable entry.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry emphasizes the breadth of Cold War conflicts, reflecting extensive contributor activity and a focus on cataloguing. The Chinese version’s absence highlights gaps in coverage, possibly due to differing editorial priorities, language‑specific policies, or less community interest. This disparity can bias readers toward perceiving the topic as more documented in English than in Chinese.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Cold_War_conflicts', 'key_points': ['The **“Cold War conflicts”** category aggregates every war, insurgency, or confrontation that was connected to or took place within the broader Cold War context.', 'It is broken down into **71 alphabetically‑organized sub‑categories** (A, B, C…\\u202fY) to manage the sheer number of individual incidents.', '**98 distinct pages** (articles) are currently listed under this umbrella, reflecting the extensive and varied nature of Cold‑War‑related fighting.', 'Wikipedia notes that the list **may not be fully up‑to‑date**, as category pages are constantly edited and reorganized.', 'Overall, the categorization underscores just how **wide‑ranging and complex** the geopolitical struggles of the Cold War era were.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Cold_War_conflicts', 'key_points': ['The “Cold\\u202fWar\\u202fconflicts” category currently does not exist on Chinese\\u202fWikipedia, prompting users to verify whether a differently‑named category already covers the topic.', 'Editors are encouraged to either create the missing category (or edit it) or search existing categories and linked pages to locate the appropriate entry.', 'If the category was just created, the system may need a few minutes to update; users should purge the cache or wait before retrying.', 'Possible reasons for the error include case‑sensitivity, mixed‑script (traditional vs. simplified) issues, or that the category has been deleted—check the deletion log and Wikipedia’s deletion policies for details.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Strategic Defense Initiative', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Defense_Initiative'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Defense_Initiative', 'name': 'Strategic Defense Initiative', 'years': '1983-1993', 'short_description': 'The Strategic Defense Initiative was a U.S. program announced by President Ronald Reagan in 1983 to develop a space‑based missile defense system that would render ballistic nuclear missiles ineffective, aiming to make nuclear weapons obsolete; the program was discontinued in 1993.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Project_and_proposals'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Strategic Defense Initiative (SDI), announced by U.S. President Ronald Reagan in 1983, was a research program aiming to develop space‑ and ground‑based systems to intercept ballistic missiles. Funded heavily during the Cold War, it was cancelled in 1993, with some technologies influencing later missile‑defense efforts.', 'bias_comment': \"Arabic sources stress geopolitical framing and Soviet threat, English highlights scientific spin‑offs and policy controversy, French portrays SDI as a mythic, over‑ambitious political tool, while Russian emphasizes wasteful spending and strategic coercion. These variations reflect each language's national narratives, Cold‑War memories, and differing attitudes toward US military programs.\", 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'fr', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Defense_Initiative', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War “Star\\u202fWars”** – The Strategic Defense Initiative (SDI), dubbed “Star\\u202fWars,” was launched by President Ronald\\u202fReagan in March\\u202f1983 as a bold, space‑based shield against Soviet strategic nuclear missiles, signalling a shift from the old doctrine of Mutual Assured Destruction to a defensive posture.', '**Massive, secret‑ive spending** – Congress earmarked roughly **$26\\u202fbillion** for a five‑year program, wrapped in extreme secrecy; the project promised laser “death‑rays,” orbital interceptors and other high‑tech weapons that could “see” and destroy incoming warheads despite heat, smoke or fire.', '**Allied participation & geopolitical framing** – The initiative was presented as a collective Western effort, inviting NATO partners such as the United Kingdom, West\\u202fGermany, France and Israel to share the technology and the burden of countering the Soviet threat.', '**Controversial feasibility and legacy** – Critics repeatedly questioned the technical realism and cost‑effectiveness of SDI; the program’s grandiose claims and hidden budget fueled public and congressional skepticism, ultimately leading to its termination in 1993 after the Cold War’s end.', '**Enduring impact** – Although the original “Star\\u202fWars” system never materialised, SDI spurred lasting research in missile‑defence, satellite tracking and directed‑energy weapons that continues to shape U.S. and allied defense strategies today.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Defense_Initiative', 'key_points': ['**Ambitious but premature “Star\\u202fWars” vision** – Launched by Reagan in 1983 to make nuclear weapons obsolete, SDI called for space‑ and ground‑based lasers, particle beams, and autonomous interceptors that were, according to the American Physical Society and later assessments, still decades away from feasibility.', '**Political flashpoint and strategic controversy** – Critics such as Ted\\u202fKennedy and Joe\\u202fBiden denounced the program as reckless, arguing it would destabilize mutual‑assured destruction, trigger an expensive arms race, and threaten U.S. allies; the Soviet Union viewed it as a provocative attempt at a first‑strike advantage.', '**Significant scientific spin‑offs despite program setbacks** – Although most weapon concepts failed, SDI’s massive R&D effort seeded advances in basic physics, supercomputing, sensor technology, laser engineering, particle‑beam research, and materials (e.g., aerogels), many of which later fed into civilian and military systems (MIRACL, THOR, Clementine, rail‑gun experiments, etc.).', '**Evolution and partial revival** – After the Cold War the initiative was reshaped into the Ballistic Missile Defense Organization and later the Missile Defense Agency, with concepts like Brilliant\\u202fPebbles and theater‑scale interceptors surviving; elements resurfaced in the 2019 Space Development Agency’s architecture and have been championed by recent administrations as “SDI\\u202fII” or “Golden Dome” projects.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Defense_Initiative', 'key_points': ['**Politically‑driven “Star\\u202fWars” myth:**  The Strategic Defense Initiative (IDS) was marketed by Reagan’s administration as a futuristic “space shield” against Soviet ICBMs, borrowing the glamorous “Star\\u202fWars” nickname to sell a massive defense‑industrial program.', '**Technological over‑reach:**  Early studies (e.g., SDIO, 1984) warned that the proposed orbital‑laser and satellite interceptor systems were far beyond 1980s capabilities and would need a decade of research just to prove feasibility.', '**Cold‑War escalation tool:**  IDS was framed as a way to break the “balance of terror” doctrine, pressuring the USSR into an unsustainable arms race and, retrospectively, is portrayed as one of the factors that hastened the Soviet Union’s collapse.', '**Industrial and legal controversy:**  The program provoked criticism for violating existing ABM‑treaties, inflating the U.S. militaro‑industrial complex, and prompting legal battles (e.g., George\\u202fLucas vs. the “Star\\u202fWars” branding).', '**Legacy of partial success:**  Although the original IDS was cancelled in 1993, its research fed later missile‑defense systems (e.g., Patriot\\u202fPAC‑3, National Missile Defense) and continues to influence debates over space‑based weaponization.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_Defense_Initiative', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War political weapon, not just a technical project** – President Reagan announced SDI in 1983 as a “Star\\u202fWars” programme aimed at guaranteeing US strategic dominance by creating a space‑based anti‑missile shield, but its real‑world purpose (according to some analysts) was to force the Soviet economy into an unsustainable arms race.', '**Technological fantasy runaway** – The initiative fantasized about dozens of still‑immature “miracle” systems – nuclear‑pumped X‑ray lasers, orbital mirrors, kinetic “pebble” satellites, particle‑beam emitters, rail‑guns, chemical lasers, etc. – many of which never left the laboratory because of insurmountable physics, energy‑budget and reliability problems.', '**Massive, wasteful spending with little payoff** – By the early‑1990s the programme had consumed billions of dollars (thousands of contracts to U.S. firms and some NATO partners) yet produced no deployable defence; the high‑cost, low‑success rate fed the criticism that SDI was a political showpiece rather than a viable missile‑defence system.', '**Legacy of concepts and partial revival** – Although SDI was cancelled in 1993, several of its research threads survived (e.g., kinetic “Brilliant Pebbles”, THAAD, GMD, and today’s “Golden\\u202fDome”/Boost‑Phase Intercept concepts). The ambition to protect the continent from ballistic missiles lives on, but now shaped by more realistic, ground‑based or limited‑space architectures.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Berlin Crisis of 1961', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Crisis_of_1961'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Crisis_of_1961', 'name': 'Berlin Crisis of 1961', 'years': '1961', 'short_description': 'The Berlin Crisis of 1961 was the last major Cold War confrontation over Berlin, sparked by Soviet demands to withdraw Western forces and leading to the construction of the Berlin Wall and a tense stand‑off between American and Soviet tanks at Checkpoint Charlie.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Background'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The 1961 Berlin Crisis was a Cold‑War confrontation over Berlin's status, featuring Soviet ultimatums, heightened tensions, and a tank standoff at Checkpoint Charlie, culminating in the construction of the Berlin Wall and a diplomatic stalemate that left the city divided for decades.\", 'bias_comment': 'The English Wikipedia entry emphasizes diplomatic negotiations and Western perspectives, framing the Soviet actions as defensive against brain‑drain while portraying the Wall as a human‑rights violation. In German or Russian sources, the narrative may stress Soviet security concerns and portray the wall as a necessary protective measure, reflecting national historiographical biases.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Crisis_of_1961', 'key_points': ['**A Cold‑War flashpoint over Berlin’s status** –\\u202fAfter a series of Soviet ultimatums (1958‑1961) demanding Western withdrawal from the city and a separate peace with East Germany, the crisis exploded in June\\u202f1961 when Kennedy and Khrushchev met in Vienna and failed to reach any agreement.  The dispute was driven as much by Soviet fears of a “brain‑drain” from East to West as by the Western desire to preserve a foothold in the capital, making Berlin the symbolic battleground of the super‑power rivalry.', '**Escalation to brinkmanship** –\\u202fU‑2 espionage, the collapse of the Paris summit, and a series of high‑profile rallies (the Vienna summit, the Kennedy television address, and the August\\u202f1961 “garden‑party” order) heightened tension.  By October\\u202f1961 American M‑48 tanks and Soviet T‑55s faced each other at Checkpoint\\u202fCharlie, creating the only direct tank‑versus‑tank standoff of the Cold War; the episode ended only after back‑channel talks (via GRU spy\\u202fGeorgi\\u202fBolshakov) led both sides to pull back their armor.', '**Construction of the Berlin Wall as the “final, albeit tragic, solution”** –\\u202fEast German leader Walter\\u202fUlbricht, with Khrushchev’s blessing, covertly amassed barbed‑wire and construction materials and, on 12‑13\\u202fAugust\\u202f1961, sealed the border with a 156‑km barrier.  Western leaders (Adenauer, Kennedy) denounced the wall as a violation of human rights and the four‑power agreement, but the wall was presented by Soviet‑aligned officials as a necessary “peaceful” measure to stop the exodus and avoid a possible war.', '**Stalemate and lasting legacy** –\\u202fThe crisis ended in a diplomatic stalemate: the West kept its military presence in West Berlin, the Soviets withdrew their tanks, and the Berlin Wall solidified the city’s de‑facto partition for the next 28\\u202fyears.  The episode cemented the Berlin crisis as the last major European Cold‑War confrontation, illustrating how both superpowers were willing to risk armed clash yet ultimately chose a static, oppressive compromise over open warfare.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Berlin Crisis of 1958–1959', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Crisis_of_1958%E2%80%931959'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Crisis_of_1958%E2%80%931959', 'name': 'Berlin Crisis of 1958–1959', 'years': '1958-1959', 'short_description': \"A Cold War dispute over West Berlin's status, triggered by Soviet leader Khrushchev's opposition to U.S. nuclear missiles in West Germany; the crisis involved NATO, an ultimatum, and diplomatic negotiations but no military action, ending with a continuation of the status quo and setting the stage for the 1961 Berlin Wall.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#intro'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1958–59 Berlin Crisis was a Cold‑War standoff where Soviet Premier Khrushchev demanded Western withdrawal from West Berlin and removal of U.S. nuclear missiles, while the United States, NATO, and West Germany rejected the ultimatum, resulting in diplomatic de‑escalation without armed conflict.', 'bias_comment': 'English Wikipedia emphasizes a \"showdown\" and Western resolve, German articles stress local impact and diplomatic strategy, while Russian-language pages highlight Soviet security concerns and the desire to halt the East German brain‑drain. These national narratives can bias readers by emphasizing respective sides\\' motives and downplaying other perspectives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlin_Crisis_of_1958%E2%80%931959', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War showdown over Berlin:** Khrushchev’s 1958 ultimatum demanded Western withdrawal from West Berlin and the removal of U.S. nuclear missiles in West Germany, aiming to stop the massive “brain‑drain” of East German professionals and to reinforce Soviet prestige.', '**Western resolve and NATO mobilization:** Eisenhower, together with NATO and West‑German Chancellor Konrad\\u202fAdenauer, flatly rejected the Soviet demands, created the secret “Live\\u202fOak” planning group, and stressed that Berlin—though militarily insignificant—was a vital symbol of freedom against Communist expansion.', '**Diplomatic de‑escalation, not warfare:** The crisis never turned into armed conflict; instead, both sides moved toward a limited détente, preserving the status quo in Berlin while the fundamental division of Germany and the emigration problem remained unresolved.', '**Prelude to later escalation:** The standoff exposed Western reluctance to fully re‑arm West Germany (especially with nuclear weapons) and Soviet frustration with Berlin’s “loophole” border, foreshadowing the construction of the Berlin Wall in 1961.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Category:Violations of Soviet airspace - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Violations_of_Soviet_airspace'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Violations_of_Soviet_airspace', 'name': 'Category:Violations of Soviet airspace - Wikipedia', 'years': '1958-1987', 'short_description': \"A Wikipedia category that lists Cold War incidents in which foreign aircraft entered Soviet airspace, including the 1958 C‑130 shootdown, 1960 U‑2 incident, Korean Air Lines Flight 007 (1983), Mathias Rust's 1987 landing, and several U.S. reconnaissance projects.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Pages_in_category_\"Violations_of_Soviet_airspace\"'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia category lists twelve incidents from 1958‑1987 where aircraft entered Soviet airspace, including accidental collisions, civilian tragedies, and deliberate reconnaissance missions like the U‑2 flight, Korean Air Lines 007, and Mathias Rust’s 1987 flight.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language coverage stresses famous Cold‑War flashpoints and Western narratives of espionage, while Russian‑language articles tend to highlight sovereign defense and label incursions as violations. Other European languages may balance both, leading to variations in terminology (e.g., \"shootdown\" vs. \"interception\") and the perceived significance of each event.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Violations_of_Soviet_airspace', 'key_points': ['The category gathers **12 distinct incidents and programs** that breached Soviet airspace, ranging from accidental collisions to intentional reconnaissance missions.', 'Several high‑profile Cold‑War flashpoints dominate the list, notably **Korean Air Lines Flight\\u202f007**, **Flight\\u202f902**, the **1960 U‑2 spy‑plane incident**, and **Mathias Rust’s solo flight to Moscow**, underscoring the era’s tense air‑space disputes.', 'A mix of civilian tragedies (e.g., **Seaboard World Airlines Flight\\u202f253A**, **1958 C‑130 shootdown**) and covert projects (**Project Dark Gene, Genetrix, HOMERUN**) illustrates both accidental violations and systematic intelligence‑gathering efforts.', 'The assortment reflects the broader **Cold War dynamics** of aerial monitoring, diplomatic fallout, and the Soviet Union’s vigorous defense of its borders.', 'Placement within related categories (Aviation accidents, Soviet aviation, Cold‑War history, Soviet borders) emphasizes the incidents’ significance to both aviation safety and geopolitical history.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Defection of Viktor Belenko', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defection_of_Viktor_Belenko'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defection_of_Viktor_Belenko', 'name': 'Defection of Viktor Belenko', 'years': '1976', 'short_description': 'On 6 September 1976, Soviet Lieutenant Viktor Belenko defected by flying his MiG‑25P from near Vladivostok to Hakodate Airport in Japan, where the aircraft was examined by Japanese and US experts.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Defection_flight'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 6 September 1976 Soviet Lieutenant Viktor Belenko piloted a MiG‑25 from a base near Vladivostok to Hakodate Airport in Japan, seeking political asylum. The aircraft was examined by the United States, leading to intelligence gains, diplomatic tensions, and changes in Japanese and Soviet air‑defence policies.', 'bias_comment': \"The English article stresses Cold‑War intelligence, technical assessment of the MiG‑25, and the strategic impact on US/Japan defence, reflecting a Western security perspective. The Russian version highlights Belenko's ideological disillusionment, portrays the defection as a propaganda issue, and frames Soviet reactions as defensive, illustrating national narrative biases. Cross‑language differences arise from each side’s focus on either technical‑strategic outcomes or political‑ideological interpretation.\", 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defection_of_Viktor_Belenko', 'key_points': ['**A dramatic Cold‑War defection:** On 6\\u202fSeptember\\u202f1976 Lieutenant\\u202fViktor\\u202fBelenko flew his top‑secret MiG‑25 “Foxbat” from a Soviet base near Vladivostok to Hakodate Airport in Japan, sparking a diplomatic crisis and intense intelligence interest from both the United States and Japan.', '**Revealing the MiG‑25’s true performance:** After the aircraft was disassembled and examined at Hyakuri Air Base, U.S. analysts learned that, contrary to Western fears of a super‑fighter, the MiG‑25 excelled in speed but suffered from limited maneuverability, simple avionics, and reliance on outdated nuvistors—information that eased American anxiety and helped shape the F‑15 programme.', '**Japanese defence shortcomings exposed:** Japan’s radar and F‑4EJ fighters failed to track or intercept the high‑altitude intruder, prompting a swift overhaul of its air‑defence posture, acquisition of E‑2 Hawkeye AWACS and later F‑15 Eagles, and a re‑evaluation of air‑space security protocols.', '**Personal and political fallout:** Belenko was granted U.S. political asylum, became a CIA‑debriefed consultant, public speaker and businessman, while the Soviet Union demanded compensation, seized Japanese fishing vessels in retaliation, and subsequently improved conditions at his former Far‑East base; the incident also underscored broader Cold‑War pilot‑defection patterns.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%BE,_%D0%92%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87', 'key_points': ['**Талантливый летчик‑перебежчик**: Виктор\\u202fИванович Беленко, старший лейтенант ВВС СССР, в 1976\\u202fг. совершил дерзкий\\xa0полет на истребителе МиГ‑25П из Советского Дальнего Востока в Японию, где запросил политическое убежище и вскоре оказался в США.', '**Идеологическая разочарованность**: В интервью Беленко объяснял побег недоверием к советской пропаганде, ощущением «рабства» народа под номенклатурой и желанием увидеть реальность западных свобод; он подчёркивал, что даже без доступа к информации он пришёл к выводу, что система недостойна жизни.', '**Политический конфликт и раздвижка фактов**: Советское руководство пыталось представить происшествие как вынужденную посадку и похищение пилота, в то время как США и западные СМИ трактовали его как крупную разведывательную удачу. Последующее расследование СССР доказало, что вынужденной посадки не было, а Беленко сознательно изменил план полёта.', '**Последствия и наследие**: После получения американского гражданства Беленко стал консультантом разведки, а его МиГ‑25 использовался в качестве учебного пособия. Инцидент ускорил советско‑японские дипломатические переговоры, стал объектом пропагандистских «месяцев бегства» и оставил глубокий след в истории холодной войны как один из самых известных переломных случаев перебежчиков.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Portal:History of science/Article/14 - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:History_of_science/Article/14'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:History_of_science/Article/14', 'name': 'Portal:History of science/Article/14 - Wikipedia', 'years': '1957-1975', 'short_description': 'The Space Race was an informal competition between the United States and the Soviet Union, lasting roughly from 1957 to 1975, to achieve milestones in space exploration such as satellite launches, human spaceflight, and lunar landing.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From 1957 to 1975, the United States and the Soviet Union competed to achieve space milestones, beginning with Sputnik. Both pursued satellites, crewed missions, and a lunar landing, using achievements for national prestige and Cold‑War rivalry.', 'bias_comment': 'The English summary frames the contest as a \"race\" and highlights propaganda, reflecting a Western view. Russian versions often emphasize scientific progress and leadership, while French and Chinese texts may stress cooperation or peaceful exploration, illustrating how national historiographies shape perceived motives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal:History_of_science/Article/14', 'key_points': ['The Space Race was an informal yet intense rivalry between the United\\u202fStates and the Soviet Union from roughly 1957‑1975, driven by the Cold\\u202fWar’s cultural and technological competition.', 'It began in earnest after the USSR launched Sputnik\\u202f1 (4\\u202fOct\\u202f1957), marking the first artificial satellite and prompting the United\\u202fStates to accelerate its own space program.', 'Both nations pursued parallel goals: building artificial satellites, sending humans into space, and ultimately landing people on the Moon.', 'Space achievements were valued not only for their potential military uses but also for their morale‑boosting, propaganda‑worthy impact on national pride.', 'The term “Space Race” was coined as an analogy to the arms race, underscoring how space technology became a key arena of Cold\\u202fWar rivalry.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Angolan Civil War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_Civil_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_Civil_War', 'name': 'Angolan Civil War', 'years': '1975–2002', 'short_description': 'A 27‑year civil war in Angola fought between the Marxist MPLA government and the anti‑communist UNITA rebel group, with extensive involvement from Cuba, the Soviet Union, the United States and South Africa, ending with MPLA victory and the death of UNITA leader Jonas Savimbi in 2002.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph (introductory summary)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Angolan Civil War (1975‑2002) pitted the MPLA against UNITA, drawing Cold‑War support from the Soviet bloc and the United States and their allies, causing massive civilian casualties, displacement, and infrastructure damage before a 2002 peace settlement and political transition.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic entry stresses the war’s length, internal ethnic/ideological splits, and the post‑war political transformation, using emotive language (\"طويلة ومدمرة\"). The English entry foregrounds Cold‑War proxy dynamics, casualty numbers, and regional spill‑over, framing MPLA’s victory more prominently. These differences reflect linguistic and regional narrative priorities rather than factual contradictions.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_Civil_War', 'key_points': ['**طويلة ومدمرة** – استمرت الحرب الأهلية الأنغولية من 1975 حتى 2002 (\\u202f26 عاماً\\u202f)، أسفرت عن أكثر من نصف مليون مدني قتيل وملايين نازحين، مع تحطيم البنية التحتية والاقتصاد الوطني.', '**صراع أيديولوجي وجيو‑سياسي** – تحول النزاع إلى حرب بالوكالة خلال الحرب الباردة: حصلت الحركة الشعبية لتحرير أنغولا (MPLA) على دعم السوفييت، كوبا، وألمانيا الشرقية، بينما لُقّحت اليونيتا (UNITA) بدعم الولايات المتحدة، جنوب أفريقيا، وزائير (الآن جمهورية الكونغو).', '**تداخل داخلي وعقائدي** – رغم أن كل من MPLA واليونيتا كانتا تناضلان من أجل الاستقلال، إلا أن اختلافات عرقية (موبونجو‑أمبوندو‑باكونغو) واختلافات إيديولوجية (الاشتراكية ضد معاداة الشيوعية) أدت إلى انقسامات داخل الحركات وصراعات على السلطة (مثل انقؤام\\u202f“النيتيستاس” داخل MPLA).', '**نتيجة نهائية وتحوّل سياسي** – انتهت الحرب بانتصار MPLA في 2002، وسحب جميع القوات الأجنبية بحلول 1989، ما أدى إلى تأسيس نظام سياسي متعدد الأحزاب (1991‑92) وتضمين اليونيتا والجبهة الوطنية لتحرير أنغولا كقوى سياسية شرعية، إلا أن التوترات والتمرد في مقاطعة كابيندا استمرت.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_Civil_War', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War proxy war:** The Angolan Civil War was framed as a classic East‑vs‑West showdown—Cuba, the Soviet Union and other socialist allies backed the Marxist‑Leninist MPLA, while the United\\u202fStates, South\\u202fAfrica, Zaire and later private mercenaries supported the anti‑communist UNITA (and the short‑lived FNLA).', '**MPLA’s decisive victory:** With massive Cuban troop deployments (up to 380\\u202f000 soldiers, tanks, artillery and SAMs) and Soviet aid, the MPLA crushed the FNLA early, held Luanda, and ultimately forced UNITA into a prolonged insurgency that only ended after the 2002 death of Jonas\\u202fSavimbi.', '**Human and material devastation:** Between\\u202f500\\u202f000–800\\u202f000 Angolans were killed, 4\\u202fmillion displaced, and the country’s infrastructure—roads, rail, hospitals and schools—was wrecked; land‑mines still litter the countryside, causing ongoing civilian casualties.', '**Foreign withdrawal and political transition:** By 1989 all foreign combat forces had left, paving the way for a 1991‑92 shift toward a multiparty system, the integration of UNITA and FNLA as political parties, and the eventual end of the armed conflict in 2002.', '**Regional spill‑over:** The war intertwined with neighboring conflicts (the South\\u202fAfrican Border War, the Second Congo War, and the Cabinda separatist struggle), underscoring how Angola became a battleground for broader African and global power rivalries.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Angolan War of Independence', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_War_of_Independence'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_War_of_Independence', 'name': 'Angolan War of Independence', 'years': '1961–1974', 'short_description': \"A guerrilla war in Portuguese Angola between nationalist movements (MPLA, FNLA, UNITA) and Portugal, beginning with the Baixa de Cassanje uprising in 1961 and ending with Portugal's 1974 Carnation Revolution, leading to Angola's independence in 1975.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Lead paragraph (first paragraph after infobox)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Angarian War of Independence (1961–1974) was a guerrilla conflict between Portuguese forces and three nationalist movements—MPLA, FNLA, UNITA—backed by Cold‑War powers, resulting in Angola’s 1975 independence and subsequent civil war.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic article stresses Portuguese colonial oppression, forced cotton cultivation, and frames the war as anti‑colonial resistance, also noting Soviet, Cuban, South African involvement. The English article highlights Cold‑War geopolitics, foreign backers, Portuguese military professionalism, and casualty figures. Arabic tends to portray the colonizer more negatively and emphasizes local suffering, while English balances geopolitical analysis with military details; date ranges differ (Arabic 1961‑1975, English 1961‑1974).', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_War_of_Independence', 'key_points': ['**حرب الاستقلال الأنغولية (1961‑1975)**: اندلعت كحركة مقاومة ضد الاستعمار البرتغالي، بدءاً من انتفاضة 4\\u202fفبراير 1961 التي استهدفت نظام الزراعة القسرية للقطن، وتطورت إلى صراع مسلح شامل بين ثلاث جبهات قومية رئيسية – **الـ MPLA**، **الـ UNITA**، و**الـ FNLA** – إلى جانب تدخلات دولية (الاتحاد السوفيتي، كوبا، جنوب أفريقيا).', '**النتيجة النهائية**: توّجت الحرب باتفاقية ألڤور (نوفمبر\\u202f1975) التي أفضت إلى استقلال أنغولا، لكنها مهدت لاندلاع **الحرب الأهلية الأنغولية** بين الفصائل المتنازعة، ما أدى إلى صراعات مستمرة لعدة عقود.', '**الجانب الاستعماري**: تُظهر النصّ أن البرتغال سعت إلى إبقاء أنغولا كـ«مستعمرة عبر البحار»، مستفيدة من القوة العسكرية والاقتصادية، ما أسفر عن معاناة شديدة للسكان المحليين (قمع، استغلال، تشويه سكاني).', '**التحليل التاريخي**: يُؤكّد النصّ أن صراع أنغولا كان جزءاً من موجة أوسع من حروب الاستقلال البرتغالية (غينيا بيساو، موزمبيق) وأنه عُدّ مثالاً على **حروب العصابات** التي ارتكبت فيها كل من الطرفين جرائم، مع إشارة إلى أن الانتصار العسكري للبرتغالي كان مؤقتاً قبل سقوط النظام في عام\\u202f1974.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angolan_War_of_Independence', 'key_points': ['**A prolonged, Cold‑War‑shaped struggle:** The Angolan War of Independence (1961‑1974) is presented as a long‑running guerrilla conflict that formed part of the wider Portuguese Colonial War and the global decolonisation‑Cold‑War contest, ending only when Portugal’s 1974 Carnation Revolution forced a rapid withdrawal.', '**Multiple nationalist factions with competing foreign backers:** The article stresses the central role of three rival Angolan movements—MPLA (Soviet‑Cuban support, Ambundu‑based), FNLA (US‑European backing, Bakongo‑based) and UNITA (initially Chinese‑backed, later anti‑communist, Ovimbundu‑based)—and how their ethnic bases and external aid sowed the seeds for the post‑independence civil war.', '**Portuguese counter‑insurgency and its contradictions:** Portugal’s response is depicted as a massive, increasingly professional force (army, navy, air force, police, paramilitaries) expanding from ~6\\u202f500 to >65\\u202f000 troops, but marked by racial segregation, heavy‑handed tactics and alleged atrocities, illustrating the paradox of a “civilising” mission that relied on oppression.', '**Huge human cost and lasting legacy:** The conflict caused tens of thousands of combatant deaths and 30‑50\\u202f000 civilian casualties, culminating in an Angolan political victory, the 1975 independence and Alvor Agreement—yet immediately giving way to the devastating Angolan Civil War, underscoring the war’s irreversible impact on the nation’s trajectory.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Template talk:USSR conflicts - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:USSR_conflicts'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:USSR_conflicts', 'name': 'Template talk:USSR conflicts', 'years': 'N/A', 'short_description': 'Discussion page for the Wikipedia template that lists conflicts involving the Soviet Union, used for organizing related articles.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#bodyContent'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The talk page for Template:USSR conflicts lists its inclusion in WikiProject Soviet Union, Russia (history & military), China, and European History, marks it as Template‑class, notes extensive categorisation and a hidden maintenance tag, and calls for collaborative editing to improve coverage.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry stresses a broad multinational scope, while the Chinese version highlights ‘extremely high importance’ for Russia and ties the template to European history, reflecting differing editorial priorities. Such framing can bias perceived significance and may influence which aspects editors prioritize across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:USSR_conflicts', 'key_points': ['**Multinational WikiProject scope:** The talk page flags the article as belonging to **WikiProject\\u202fSoviet\\u202fUnion, WikiProject\\u202fRussia (history & military task forces), and WikiProject\\u202fChina**, urging editors to join the relevant project discussions and help improve coverage.', '**Template‑class status:** All three banners are marked **“Template‑class,”** meaning the page does not need a formal content‑assessment rating on Wikipedia’s quality scale.', '**Extensive categorisation:** The page is placed in a wide array of categories—template‑class, NA‑importance, and task‑force lists for Soviet, Russian, and Chinese military/history articles—underscoring its relevance to multiple thematic clusters.', '**Maintenance note:** It appears in the hidden category **“WikiProject banners without banner shells,”** signalling a minor formatting issue that should be fixed.', '**Collaborative tone:** Overall, the emphasis is on **community participation** to refine and expand the coverage of USSR‑related conflicts across the linked WikiProjects.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:USSR_conflicts', 'key_points': ['该模板同时隶属于 **欧洲历史** 与 **俄罗斯** 两大 WikiProject，旨在提升中文维基上关于这类历史内容的质量。', '按照项目的质量评级标准，模板被划为 **“模板级”**（无需进一步评级），但在俄罗斯专题中被评为 **“极高重要度”**，显示其在该领域的关键作用。', '页面鼓励有兴趣的编辑 **浏览项目主页、参与讨论、完成开放任务**，并提供了专门的讨论页链接以便发起关于“Template:苏联战争”的改进议题。', '页面所属的分类包括 **模板级（欧洲历史、俄罗斯）**、**极高重要度（俄罗斯）**，以及隐藏的 **自动评等级页面**，便于项目成员快速定位和管理。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Viktor Belenko', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Belenko'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Belenko', 'name': 'Viktor Belenko', 'years': '1976', 'short_description': \"Soviet fighter pilot who defected to the West in September 1976 by flying a MiG‑25 Foxbat to Hakodate, Japan, providing the United States and its allies first close access to the aircraft's capabilities.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Early_life_and_defection'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In September 1976 Soviet pilot Viktor Belenko defected by flying a MiG‑25 to Hakodate, Japan, seeking political asylum in the United States. The aircraft was examined by Western experts, returned to the USSR, and Belenko later lived in the U.S. until his death in 2023.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources highlight Western propaganda, reward, and the intelligence windfall, portraying Belenko as a heroic defector. French texts call the act \"spectacular\" and stress geopolitical and technical costs to the USSR, with less emphasis on personal motive. Russian accounts stress Belenko\\'s dissatisfaction, Soviet propaganda framing, and ideological context, while also citing independent verification of a planned defection. These variations reflect each language\\'s national narrative lenses and the selective emphasis of sources.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Belenko', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War coup‑d’état:** In September\\u202f1976 Lieutenant\\u202fViktor\\u202fBelenko flew a Soviet MiG‑25 “Foxbat” to Hakodate, Japan, delivering the West its first close‑up examination of the secretive interceptor—a “gold‑mine” of intelligence that exposed the aircraft’s true role and undermined Soviet air‑defence prestige.', '**Western reward and propaganda:** The United States granted Belenko asylum, a generous trust fund and eventual citizenship, portraying him as a heroic defectors‑‑a narrative amplified by books, media interviews and congressional praise, while the Soviet press smeared him as a traitor and even fabricated rumors of his death.', '**Strategic fallout for the USSR:** analysts judged the loss of the MiG‑25 a serious blow to Soviet morale and technology secrecy; the episode sparked costly diplomatic wrangling with Japan over the aircraft’s return and highlighted Soviet vulnerability to defections.', '**Post‑defection life:** Belenko settled in the American Midwest, worked as an aerospace consultant, published an autobiography, and largely avoided public appearances, citing personal safety concerns; his later years were marked by a low‑profile existence and occasional disputes over his family’s claims of abandonment.', '**Legacy and death:** Belenko died quietly in a nursing home in Rosebud, Illinois, on 24\\u202fSeptember\\u202f2023, with his passing only reported months later—leaving behind a complex legacy of Cold‑War intrigue, espionage triumph, and contested personal narratives.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Belenko', 'key_points': ['**Défection spectaculaire**\\u202f: Le 6\\u202fseptembre\\u202f1976, le capitaine Viktor\\u202fBelenko a survolé 990\\u202fkm sans être détecté et a atterri avec son MiG‑25 «\\u202fFoxbat\\u202f» à Hakodate, au Japon, offrant pour la première fois aux occidentaux un accès direct à cet avion secret soviétique.', '**Conséquences géopolitiques et techniques**\\u202f: Le vol a été qualifié de «\\u202fgros coup de renseignement\\u202f» par le président américain Gerald\\u202fFord, a coûté à l’URSS plusieurs milliards de roubles et a contraint le commandement soviétique à remplacer le système IFF national ainsi qu’à restreindre le remplissage des réservoirs dans l’Extrême‑Orient.', '**Réception et récompense occidentales**\\u202f: Belenko a obtenu l’asile politique aux États-Unis, un fonds spécial a assuré son confort, il a été interrogé pendant cinq mois puis employé comme consultant et conférencier sur les secrets du MiG‑25.', '**Vie post‑défection**\\u202f: Après avoir vécu aux États-Unis, il a exercé diverses professions (dentiste, conducteur de bus) avant de décéder le 24\\u202fseptembre\\u202f2023 à l’âge de 76\\u202fans, laissant une place notable dans l’histoire de la guerre froide.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%BE,_%D0%92%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87', 'key_points': ['**Беленко\\u202f— талантливый советский пилот‑испытатель, выросший в небогатой семье, служивший в ПВО и получивший звание старшего лейтенанта.**\\u202fОн стал «технически одарённым» лётчиком‑инструктором, но из‑за системных конфликтов с командованием, недополучения продвижения и растущей неудовлетворённости советским режимом (идеологические разочарования, ограничения личной свободы) задумался о побеге.', '**6\\u202fсентября\\u202f1976\\u202fг. он на МиГ‑25П совершил преднамерённый перелёт через советско‑японскую границу и посадился в аэропорту Хакодате, запросив политическое убежище в Японии, а затем переехал в США.**\\u202fСам самолёт был разобран, изучен американскими и японскими специалистами и позднее возвращён в СССР, где использовался лишь в учебных целях.', '**Советская пропаганда трактовала инцидент как «вынужденную посадку» и «похищение» пилота, в то время как США и независимые эксперты (ГНИКИ ВВС, САРПП) подтвердили плановый характер побега и отсутствие технической необходимости в принудительной посадке.**\\u202fЭто стало предметом острой дипломатической конфронтации: МИД СССР требовал немедленного возвращения как пилота, так и самолёта, а американские политики восхваляли действие как крупную разведывательную победу.', '**После получения американского гражданства Беленко сотрудничал с разведкой США, продолжил образование (стоматолог), работал водителем автобуса и жил в Иллинойсе.**\\u202fОн оставался публично критическим к советской системой, часто отмечая её «концентрационный» характер, но при этом подчёркивал, что решение бежать было продиктовано желанием свободы, а не материальными соображениями.', '**Беленко скончался 24\\u202fсентября\\u202f2023\\u202fгода в Розбаде, Иллинойс, в возрасте\\u202f76\\u202fлет.**\\u202fЕго история остаётся одним из самых знаковых примеров холодной войны, иллюстрирующим как технологический, так и идеологический конфликт между СССР и Западом.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Milton Schwartz', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Schwartz'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Schwartz', 'name': 'Milton Schwartz', 'years': '', 'short_description': 'A Wikipedia disambiguation page listing individuals named Milton Schwartz, including an American spy, a U.S. federal judge (1920–2005), and a fictional character.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Disambiguation page top'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia page \"Milton Schwartz\" is a disambiguation entry listing three distinct subjects—a U.S. spy, federal judge Milton Lewis Schwartz (1920‑2005), and a fictional character from *Marjorie Morningstar*—and directs readers to the appropriate article.', 'bias_comment': \"In English, the page distinguishes three entities, while many other language Wikipedias either lack a corresponding page or list fewer entries, reflecting an English‑centric bias in coverage and the prominence of certain individuals. This can affect non‑English users' access to the same information.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Schwartz', 'key_points': ['**Multiple notable bearers:**\\u202f“Milton\\u202fSchwartz” refers to three distinct entries—a U.S. spy, a federal judge (Milton\\u202fLewis\\u202fSchwartz,\\u202f1920‑2005), and a fictional character from the novel *Marjorie\\u202fMorningstar*.', '**Disambiguation purpose:**\\u202fThe page exists solely to direct readers to the correct article when an internal link lands on the ambiguous name.', '**Standard Wikipedia structure:**\\u202fIt includes the usual disambiguation notice, categories for human‑name disambiguation, and hidden maintenance categories (e.g., short‑description mismatch).']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Military history of the Soviet Union', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_history_of_the_Soviet_Union'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_history_of_the_Soviet_Union', 'name': 'Military history of the Soviet Union', 'years': '1917-1991', 'short_description': \"From the Red Army's creation after the 1917 Revolution, the Soviet Union fought the civil war, the Polish‑Soviet War, World War\\xa0II, the Cold War arms race, and numerous regional conflicts, culminating in its military decline and dissolution in 1991.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Overview'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From its 1917 creation, the Soviet Red Army evolved into a major land force, playing a key role in World War\\u202fII and later maintaining a sizable Cold‑War military, including nuclear capabilities, while its extensive arms focus strained the civilian economy and contributed to the USSR’s 1991 collapse.', 'bias_comment': 'English accounts often highlight Soviet military successes and doctrinal innovation, sometimes understating human costs and political repression. Russian sources may stress defensive necessity and patriotic achievement, whereas Western narratives can stress aggression and economic burden. These language differences shift emphasis on victories, casualties, and the military’s role in the state’s collapse.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_history_of_the_Soviet_Union', 'key_points': ['**From revolutionary militia to world‑class army** – The Red Army was created after the 1917 Revolution, survived early defeats (Polish‑Soviet War, Winter War) and, through massive industrialization and doctrinal innovation, became the most powerful land force on the planet by the end of WWII.', '**Decisive role in the Second World War** – Soviet troops fought and won the turning‑point battles of Kursk, Stalingrad and the final push on Berlin, bearing the heaviest casualties of any nation (≈8\\u202fmillion soldiers dead) while delivering the decisive defeat of Nazi Germany and occupying Eastern Europe.', '**Cold‑War juggernaut and political tool** – During the Cold War the USSR poured a quarter of its economy into a gigantic military‑industrial complex, achieved strategic nuclear parity with the United States, exported weapons (e.g., AK‑47, T‑55) and used its armed forces to enforce satellite regimes (Hungary\\u202f1956, Czechoslovakia\\u202f1968) and project power in Asia and the Middle East.', '**Militarisation as a double‑edged sword** – The relentless focus on arms production strained the civilian economy, contributed to the Soviet Union’s fiscal collapse, and left the military unable to preserve the state when political and economic crises erupted in the late‑1980s, culminating in the Soviet disintegration and the rapid downsizing of its armed forces.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Eugene Ivanov', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugene_Ivanov'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugene_Ivanov', 'name': 'Eugene Ivanov (spy)', 'years': '1926-1994', 'short_description': 'Soviet naval attaché and KGB spy active during the Cold War, noted for his relationship with British MP Stephen Ward.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first bullet – Yevgeny Ivanov (spy)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The English Wikipedia entry for “Eugene Ivanov” is a disambiguation page listing two individuals: Yevgeny Ivanov (1926‑1994), a Soviet naval attaché and alleged spy, and Eugene Ivanov (born 1966), a Russian‑Czech visual artist. The Russian page describes only the artist’s biography and work.', 'bias_comment': 'The English