ert recruitment at a Tokyo tennis club, exemplifies the complex, high‑stakes world of Cold‑War espionage.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=%D0%A0%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B3%D1%83%D0%B5%D0%B2', 'key_points': ['**Rastorguev (also Rastorgueva) is a Russian surname** that appears across a wide span of Russian history, from 18th‑century merchants and military officers to 20th‑century scientists, artists, and athletes.', '**Many bearers have achieved national prominence** in fields such as film directing (Alexander\\u202fEvgenevich\\u202fRastorguev), football (Andrey\\u202fRastorguev, Anton\\u202fValer’evich\\u202fRastorguev), philosophy and politics (Valeriy\\u202fNikolaevich\\u202fRastorguev), and the sciences (Nikolay\\u202fSergeevich\\u202fRastorguev, Yuri\\u202fLeonidovich\\u202fRastorguev).', '**The name is also linked to geographic landmarks**, most notably Rastorguev Island in the Kar\\u202fBay of Krasnoyarsk Krai, underscoring the family’s historical and cultural imprint.', '**Female members of the lineage have made scholarly contributions**, e.g., linguist and Iranist Vera\\u202fSergeevna\\u202fRastorgueva and test‑pilot Galina\\u202fViktorovna\\u202fRastorgueva, highlighting the surname’s cross‑gender legacy.', '**Overall, the Rastorguev surname functions as a “one‑surname” disambiguation page**, gathering a diverse set of notable individuals and a toponym, reflecting the breadth of Russian professional and regional history associated with the name.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of the Space Race', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Space_Race'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Space_Race', 'name': 'Timeline of the Space Race', 'years': '1955-1991', 'short_description': \"Chronological list of first achievements by the USSR and USA during the Cold War Space Race, from early satellite launches in 1955 through lunar landings and planetary missions, ending with the competition's conclusion in 1991.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Overview'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From 1957 to 1991, the US and USSR competed in space, achieving milestones such as the first satellite, human orbit, Moon landing, and planetary probes, before shifting toward joint missions like Apollo–Soyuz and collaborative stations, influencing modern space technology and cooperation.', 'bias_comment': 'English narratives often frame the race as a US‑Soviet rivalry, stressing \"firsts\" and competition. Russian sources may highlight Soviet scientific leadership and downplay US achievements, while other languages (e.g., Chinese, French) can emphasize geopolitical context or collaborative outcomes, leading to varied emphases on competition versus cooperation.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Space_Race', 'key_points': ['**Cold‑War rivalry drove the “first‑of‑its‑kind” breakthroughs:** From the USSR’s launch of the first ICBM (R‑7,\\u202f1957) and artificial satellite (Sputnik\\u202f1,\\u202f1957) to the United States’ first solar‑powered satellite (Vanguard\\u202f1,\\u202f1958), each side rushed to claim the next technical “first” that would capture global headlines.', '**Human spaceflight became the ultimate prestige contest:** The Soviet lead with Yuri\\u202fGagarin’s historic orbit (Vostok\\u202f1,\\u202f1961) was answered by the United States with Alan\\u202fShepard’s sub‑orbital flight (Freedom\\u202f7,\\u202f1961) and culminated in the U.S. Apollo\\u202f11 Moon landing (1969), the definitive triumph of the race.', '**Scientific and robotic milestones ran parallel to the manned race:** Both powers repeatedly out‑paced each other in planetary exploration—first Venus flyby (Venera\\u202f1,\\u202f1961), first Mars flyby (Mariner\\u202f2,\\u202f1962), first soft‑landings on the Moon (Luna\\u202f9,\\u202f1966) and Venus (Venera\\u202f7,\\u202f1970), and the first interplanetary probes to the outer planets (Pioneer\\u202f10/11,\\u202f1972‑79).', '**The competition gradually turned into cooperation:** After the landmark Apollo–Soyuz Test Project (July\\u202f1975), joint missions and shared scientific goals replaced the zero‑sum mindset, a transition cemented by the dissolution of the USSR in 1991 and the emergence of long‑duration, jointly staffed stations (Mir,\\u202f1990; International Space Station,\\u202f1998 onward).', '**Legacy of “firsts” reshaped space technology:** The race produced enduring innovations—geosynchronous and geostationary communications satellites (Syncom\\u202f2/3,\\u202f1963‑64), reusable spacecraft concepts (X‑15,\\u202f1963; Space Shuttle,\\u202f1981), and modular space‑station architecture (Mir Core Module,\\u202f1986)—all of which underpin today’s civilian and commercial space activities.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of conflicts in the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conflicts_in_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conflicts_in_the_United_States', 'name': 'List of conflicts in the United States', 'years': '1600s–present', 'short_description': 'A chronological compilation of wars, battles, uprisings, riots, and other armed conflicts that have taken place within the United States and its territories, from colonial times through the modern era.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#List_of_conflicts'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia article lists armed conflicts occurring on U.S. territory from the 1600s to the present, covering wars, rebellions, riots, strikes, and terrorist attacks, presented in chronological order.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry frames \"conflict\" broadly, emphasizing Native American wars and internal dissent, reflecting a US‑centric historiography. Other language versions (e.g., Spanish, French) may prioritize colonial or international wars and 