0%D1%86%D0%B8%D1%8F_%C2%AB%D0%A6%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%C2%BB'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9E%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%86%D0%B8%D1%8F_%C2%AB%D0%A6%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%C2%BB', 'name': 'Операция «Циклон»', 'years': '1979-1989', 'short_description': 'Кодовое название программы ЦРУ по поставкам оружия, денег и обучения афганским моджахедам, финансируемой США с целью противодействия советскому вторжению в Афганистан; ежегодный бюджет вырос с 20–30\\u202fмлн до 630\\u202fмлн\\u202fдолл. к 1987\\u202fгоду.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#%D0%9F%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BC%D0%B0'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Operation\\u202fCyclone (1979‑89) was a U.S. CIA program supplying Afghan mujahideen with weapons and funds via Pakistan. Operation\\u202fTyphoon (1989) was a Soviet offensive using air and artillery strikes to target northern Afghan forces before withdrawal. Both aimed at influencing the Afghan conflict’s outcome.', 'bias_comment': 'Russian‐language accounts of Typhoon tend to stress Soviet military success and minimize civilian impact, while English sources on Cyclone highlight U.S. strategic motives and long‑term destabilisation, often linking it to later terrorism. Cross‑language disparities reflect differing national narratives and the politicisation of Cold‑War legacies.', 'languages_used': ['ru', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9E%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%86%D0%B8%D1%8F_%C2%AB%D0%A6%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%C2%BB', 'key_points': ['**Operation\\u202fCyclone was the CIA’s longest‑running, most expensive Cold‑War covert program (1979‑89), pouring billions of dollars and advanced weapons (e.g., Stinger missiles) into Afghanistan to fund mujahideen against the Soviet invasion.**', '**U.S. policymakers—Carter, Brzezinski and later congressional backers such as Charlie\\u202fWilson—presented the effort as a “Vietnam‑style” strategy to trap the USSR and safeguard Middle‑East oil interests, routing aid through Pakistan’s ISI with support from Britain, Saudi Arabia, China and Israel.**', '**The operation has been heavily criticised for its unintended fallout: it armed and empowered warlords like Gulbuddin\\u202fHekmatyar, fostered connections with future terrorist figures (Osama\\u202fbin\\u202fLaden, al‑Qaida’s precursors), and helped create the militant “Frankenstein” that later evolved into the Taliban and broader global terrorism.**', '**While hailed by some as a decisive factor in the Soviet withdrawal, the legacy of Cyclone is debated as a short‑term geopolitical win that sowed long‑term destabilisation in Afghanistan and the wider region.**', '**Its story entered popular culture (e.g., the film *Charlie\\u202fWilson’s\\u202fWar*), reinforcing the narrative of a covert U.S. success that produced far‑reaching, often disastrous, consequences.**']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Typhoon_(1989)', 'key_points': ['**Last‑minute “show‑of‑force” before the Soviet pull‑out** – Operation\\u202fTyphoon was ordered by Moscow (Gorbachev) in the final days of the Soviet‑Afghan War to “stabilise” the north‑eastern zone and to crush Ahmad\\u202fShah\\u202fMassoud’s forces just before the troops left Afghanistan.', '**Massive, non‑contact firepower with minimal Soviet casualties** – The operation relied almost entirely on more than 1\\u202f000 air sorties, hundreds of artillery strikes and tactical missiles; Soviet losses were described as “very light,” while Soviet estimates claimed 600\\u202f+ enemy fighters killed.', '**Heavy civilian toll and alleged breach of a secret pact** – According to memoirs and Afghan accounts, the Soviets targeted known Massoud positions while “30 minutes before they crawl out… launch crazy artillery and air‑strikes,” destroying dozens of villages and killing over 1\\u202f000 civilians – a move portrayed as a violation of a promised