النووي وصواريخ المدى الطويل التي تُعَد تهديداً مباشراً للولايات المتحدة وكوريا الجنوبية.', 'الإدارة الأمريكية (من بوش إلى ترامب) تصف كوريا الشمالية جزءاً من «محور الشر» وتُطبق عقوبات اقتصادية صريحة، بينما تُظهر الحكومة الكورية الشمالية استمرارية تهديداتها العسكرية وتُؤكد موقفها ضد «العدو الإمبريالي».', 'رغم المشاعر السلبية السائدة في الرأي العام الأمريكي تجاه كوريا الشمالية (أقل من 10\\u202f% نظرة إيجابية)، شهدت السنوات الأخيرة محاولات دبلوماسية غير مسبوقة، بما فيها لقائات القمة بين ترامب وكيم جونغ‑أون (سنغافورة 2018، فيتنام 2019) ومحاولة إقامة حوار لإلغاء الأسلحة النووية.', 'النتائج العملية لهذه الجهود محدودة: لم تُسفر القمم عن اتفاقية نهائية لتجريد كوريا الشمالية من السلاح النووي، ولا يزال التوتر يتصاعد مع تجارب صواريخ جديدة وتدريبات عسكرية أمريكية في شبه الجزيرة الكورية.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korea%E2%80%93United_States_relations', 'key_points': ['**Fundamentally antagonistic relationship:** No formal diplomatic ties exist—\\u200bthe Swedish Embassy in Pyongyang serves as the U.S. protecting power, while North Korea’s UN mission in New\\u202fYork functions as its de‑facto embassy.', '**Core points of contention:** The U.S. focuses on DPRK’s nuclear‑missile program, sanctions, and the heavy U.S.‑South\\u202fKorea military presence; North Korea frames these as direct threats to its security.', '**Limited back‑channel engagement:** Periodic talks (Six‑Party talks, 2008‑2018 summits) aim to de‑escalate crises and pursue denuclearisation, yet substantive breakthroughs have been minimal.', '**Public‑opinion and stance divergence:** American sentiment has grown more supportive of a strong U.S. presence in South\\u202fKorea and skeptical of diplomatic overtures toward the North, while North Korean rhetoric consistently portrays the United\\u202fStates as the primary aggressor.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'George Koval', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Koval'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Koval', 'name': 'George Koval', 'years': '1940-1948', 'short_description': 'George Koval, a Soviet GRU agent, infiltrated the U.S. Manhattan Project during World\\u202fII, serving as a health physics officer at Oak Ridge and later at the Dayton Project, and transmitted detailed information on polonium, uranium and plutonium production to the Soviet Union, helping accelerate its atomic bomb development.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Atomic_secrets'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'George Koval (1913‑2006), an American‑born chemical engineer, worked for Soviet GRU and infiltrated the Manhattan Project, passing technical data on nuclear materials; his espionage was revealed decades later, and Russia posthumously honored him, while scholars debate the extent of his impact.', 'bias_comment': 'Arabic sources portray Koval as a celebrated Soviet hero, emphasizing his “greatest success” and national honors, reflecting a patriotic narrative. English coverage balances Russian claims with academic skepticism, highlighting contested impact. French texts stress his decisive role in the Soviet bomb and valorize Soviet intelligence. These variations show how language and national perspectives shape the portrayal of Cold‑War espionage.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Koval', 'key_points': ['**عميل سوفيتي بارع**: عمل جورج كوفال (المستعار «دلمار») كجاسوس داخل مشروع مانهاتن الأمريكي، حيث نجح في سرقة معلومات حساسة عن الأسلحة النووية خلال الأربعينات.', '**دور حاسم في السباق النووي**: ساهمت تقاريره الدقيقة في تسريع برنامج القنابل الذرية السوفيتي، مما مكن الاتحاد السوفيتي من اختبار أول قنبلة نووية عام\\u202f1949 قبل الولايات المتحدة.', '**أعلى نجاح إستخباراتي**: يُعتبر كوفال “أكثر عميل نجاحاً” في تاريخ الجاسوسية السوفيتية، وقد حظي بتكريمات رفيعة مثل وسام “بطل الاتحاد الروسي” وشهادة “بطلة الحرب الوطنية”.', '**عودة إلى العلم بعد التجسس**: بعد عودته إلى موسكو، استمر كوفال كأستاذ وباحث في معهد التكنولوجيا الكيميائية، نشر أكثر من مئة دراسة علمية، وظل سرّاً عن ماضيه الجاسوسي حتى وفاته عام\\u202f2006.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Koval', 'key_points': ['**Key espionage role:**\\u202fGeorge\\u202fKoval, an American‑born engineer recruited by the GRU (code‑name\\u202fDelmar), infiltrated the Manhattan Project as a health‑physics officer at Oak\\u202fRidge and later in Dayton, relaying detailed data on polonium, plutonium and uranium production that Soviet officials say hastened their atomic‑bomb programme.', '**Background & recruitment:**\\u202fBorn to Russian‑Jewish immigrants in Iowa, Koval moved with his family to the Soviet Jewish Autonomous Region in 1932, was trained by Soviet military intelligence, and returned to the United States in 1940, eventually gaining top‑secret clearance while serving in the U.S. Army’s Special Engineer Detachment.', '**Late discovery & Russian honor:**\\u202fHis spying remained hidden for decades; it emerged after the 2002 publication *The GRU and the Atomic Bomb* and was publicly acknowledged when President Vladimir\\u202fPutin posthumously awarded him the title **Hero of the Russian Federation** in 2007.', '**Contested impact:**\\u202fRussian accounts claim Koval’s information “considerably shortened” the Soviet bomb development timeline, whereas scholars such as John\\u202fEarl\\u202fHaynes argue the USSR would have built a bomb by the early‑1950s regardless and that Koval’s contribution has been overstated for nationalist propaganda.', '**Post‑war life:**\\u202fKoval stayed in the USSR after 1948, worked as a chemistry lecturer at the Mendeleev Institute, unsuccessfully applied for U.S. Social‑Security benefits, and died in Moscow in 2006.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Koval', 'key_points': ['**George\\u202fKoval (1913‑2006) – un Américain d’origine juive devenu espion\\u202fsoviétique** : né à Sioux\\u202fCity (Iowa), il émigre avec ses parents en URSS en 1932, se forme en chimie et rejoint le GRU.', '**Infiltration du projet Manhattan** : sous le nom de code «\\u202fDelmar\\u202f», il travaille dans les laboratoires d’Oak\\u202fRidge (Tennessee) puis de Dayton (Ohio) pendant la Seconde Guerre mondiale, transmettant à Moscou des informations cruciales sur les recherches nucléaires américaines, notamment des données sur le neutron qui accélèrent le programme atomique soviétique.', '**Contribution décisive à la bombe atomique russe** : les renseignements fournis par Koval ont aidé les scientifiques dirigés par Igor\\u202fKourtchatov à résoudre les problèmes techniques, permettant le premier essai de la bombe RDS‑1 en août\\u202f1949.', '**Reconnaissance posthume par la Russie** : après sa mort, il est décoré Héros de la Fédération de Russie (2007) et reçoit la Médaille pour la victoire et l’Ordre de la Guerre patriotique, soulignant le rôle valorisé du renseignement soviétique dans la victoire nucléaire.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Soviet espionage in the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_espionage_in_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_espionage_in_the_United_States', 'name': 'Soviet espionage in the United States', 'years': '1920s‑1990s', 'short_description': 'From the 1920s to the 1990s, Soviet intelligence agencies (GRU, NKVD, KGB) recruited resident spies and American communists to conduct espionage and active‑measure operations in the United States, forming spy rings that transmitted military, industrial and atomic secrets, especially during the 1940s.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#First_efforts'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From the 1920s to the 1990s, Soviet intelligence agencies operated extensive spy networks in the United States, recruiting American communists and foreigners to obtain military, industrial, and scientific secrets, while also conducting propaganda and sabotage. U.S. counter‑intelligence responded with heightened security measures.', 'bias_comment': 'English Wikipedia emphasizes extensive Soviet spy networks, infiltration of government/industry, and active measures, often reflecting a US-centric perspective. Non‑English editions may downplay the breadth of infiltration, emphasize ideological motivations, or include different incidents, reflecting national historiographies and political narratives. These linguistic variations can bias readers toward a particular Cold‑War framing.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_espionage_in_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**Pervasive, state‑directed espionage:** From the 1920s through the Cold\\u202fWar, Soviet agencies (GRU, NKVD, KGB) ran extensive spy networks in the United\\u202fStates, exploiting both American communists and recruited foreigners to steal military, industrial and scientific secrets—including critical atomic‑weapon information.', '**Deep infiltration of government and industry:** High‑level “fifth‑column” groups such as the Browder, Golos, Silvermaster, and Ware rings penetrated the State Department, Treasury, Treasury‑related wartime agencies, and key defense contractors, providing the USSR with classified data on everything from tank designs to proximity fuzes.', '**Active measures and disinformation:** Beyond straight intelligence‑gathering, Soviet operatives conducted propaganda, forged documents, and elaborate sabotage campaigns (e.g., the “The Thing” bug, AIDS‑as‑bioweapon myths) aimed at destabilising U.S. institutions, sowing anti‑American sentiment, and weakening NATO alliances.', '**Motivations and recruitment tactics:** Agents were motivated by a mix of ideology, money, ego and revenge (the “MIRE” model), with the KGB using both genuine left‑wing sympathizers and false‑flag contacts to build its networks; even prominent figures like Harry\\u202fDexter\\u202fWhite and the Rosenbergs were later shown to have cooperated.', '**U.S. counter‑response and legacy:** Revelations from the Venona decrypts, the Soblen and Walker trials, and later archival research spurred tighter security measures (e.g., Truman’s Executive Order\\u202f9835) and a lasting perception of Soviet espionage as a major, sustained threat to American national security.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Middle Eastern Cold War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_East_Cold_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Eastern_Cold_War', 'name': 'Cold War in the Middle East', 'years': '1947-1991', 'short_description': \"The Cold War in the Middle East was the regional dimension of the global Cold War, where the United States and the Soviet Union vied for influence over Middle Eastern states from 1947 until the Soviet Union's collapse in 1991.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Bullet entry for Cold War in the Middle East (regional aspect of the global Cold War, 1947-1991)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The \"Middle Eastern Cold War\" label covers multiple rivalries: the 1952‑1991 Arab Cold War, current Iran‑Saudi and Iran‑Israel proxy conflicts, Cold‑War era US‑Soviet involvement, and newer disputes like Qatar‑Saudi tensions, all using “cold war” rhetoric.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language sources often foreground US‑Soviet Cold War frameworks, potentially over‑emphasizing global superpower influence and downplaying local agency. The term \"Cold War\" can be politically charged, framing intra‑regional rivalries as binary confrontations, which may reflect contemporary geopolitical narratives rather than historical nuances. Cross‑language coverage (e.g., Arabic sources) may prioritize different rivalries or use alternative terminology, affecting perception of the same events.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Eastern_Cold_War', 'key_points': ['**“Middle Eastern Cold War” is a 