ons, keeping surveillance vessels at a safe distance, using standard international signals, and prohibiting simulated attacks or hazardous maneuvers near each other’s vessels and aircraft.', '**Transparency & Communication** – Both powers pledged to give 3‑5\\u202fday advance notice of potentially dangerous naval or aerial activities, route all incident information through naval attachés, and convene annual reviews to monitor compliance.', '**High‑Level Endorsement** – Signed at the 1972 Moscow Summit by senior naval leaders (US Secretary of the Navy John Warner and Soviet Fleet Admiral Sergey Gorshkov), underscoring the political weight both sides placed on preventing Cold‑War maritime flashpoints.', '**Legacy & Relevance** – The agreement set a precedent for later codes of conduct (e.g., the Code for Unplanned Encounters at Sea) and remains a cited reference in Cold‑War naval history and maritime‑security studies.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Judith Coplon', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Coplon'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Coplon', 'name': 'Judith Coplon espionage case', 'years': '1949–1967', 'short_description': 'Judith Coplon, a U.S. Department of Justice analyst, was recruited by the Soviet NKVD, arrested on March\\u202f4\\u202f1949 for passing classified documents, convicted of espionage and conspiracy, but both convictions were overturned; the government finally dismissed the case in 1967.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Detection'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Judith Coplon, a U.S. Justice Department employee, was arrested in 1949 for passing classified documents to Soviet intelligence. Convicted of espionage and conspiracy, her convictions were later overturned due to illegal FBI surveillance and procedural issues. The case influenced Cold‑War espionage law and public debate.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry foregrounds Coplon’s Jewish background, media fascination, and the legal precedent, reflecting a U.S. narrative that intertwines identity with Cold‑War hysteria. The French version emphasizes the Soviet espionage angle and procedural violations, downplaying personal details and media hype, showing national differences in framing espionage and gender.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Coplon', 'key_points': ['**Soviet\\u202fspy in the Justice Department** – Coplon, a Barnard‑educated Jewish woman, was recruited in 1945 to pass high‑level counter‑intelligence files to NKVD/KGB operatives under the codename “SIMA.”', '**Landmark legal saga** – Her 1949 arrest (the first Venona‑related trial) led to convictions for espionage and conspiracy, but both were overturned on appeal because the FBI used illegal warrantless bugging and false testimony, setting a lasting precedent for constitutional limits on espionage prosecutions.', '**National media frenzy** – The case captured public attention, with the press portraying her as a glamorous “government girl” turned traitor and sparking broader debates about motive, loyalty, and the Red‑Scare climate.', '**Aftermath and obscurity** – After the convictions were vacated, Coplon married her attorney, earned a master’s degree, wrote bilingual books, taught creative writing to inmates, and withdrew from the public eye until her 2011 death.', '**Historical impact** – Her trials, alongside the Hiss‑Chambers and Smith Act cases, helped shape Cold‑War espionage policy and legal standards for evidence and due process in national‑security cases.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Coplon', 'key_points': ['**Espionne soviétique au cœur du gouvernement américain** – Judith\\u202fCoplon, diplômée de Barnard et engagée dans la Ligue des jeunes communistes, a été recrutée en 1945 pour transmettre à l’URSS des dossiers sensibles du ministère de la Justice, devenant l’une des informatrices les plus appréciées du NKVD.', '**Procès emblématiques et jurisprudence** – Son arrestation en 1949, grâce à un piège du FBI et au projet Venona, a donné lieu à deux condamnations (espionnage\\u202f/\\u202fassociation de malfaiteurs) qui furent annulées en appel à cause de violations de procédure (écoute illégale du téléphone, absence de mandat). Ces décisions ont profondément influencé la procédure judiciaire en matière d’espionnage pendant la Guerre froide.', '**Médiatisation et débat public** – L’affaire a suscité un grand intérêt médiatique, les journaux comme le *New\\u202fYork\\u202fTimes* interrogeant les motivations des «\\u202ftraîtres\\u202f» et soulignant le profil atypique de Coplon (jeune diplômée, employée modèle, image «\\u202fNew\\u202fLook\\u202f»).', '**Vie post‑procès et réhabilitation** – Après le retrait des poursuites en 1967, Coplon a repris une vie discrète : mariage avec son avocat, quatre enfants, un master en éducation, la publication de livres bilingues, l’enseignement aux détenues et la gestion de deux restaurants mexicains à Manhattan.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Soviet space program', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_space_program'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_space_program', 'name': 'Soviet space program', 'years': '1951-1991', 'short_description': \"The Soviet space program was the state space program of the USSR, operating from 1951 until the Union's dissolution in 1991, achieving numerous firsts such as the first satellite, human spaceflight, spacewalk, and lunar probes.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph under the article lead'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The Soviet space program (1951‑1991) pioneered many firsts in human and robotic spaceflight, operated through multiple competing design bureaus, combined military and scientific goals, faced secrecy and later revealed failures, and its legacy continues in Russia's current space activities.\", 'bias_comment': 'Arabic sources stress the program as evidence of Soviet technical superiority and highlight German‑technology roots and post‑Soviet continuity, framing achievements within a broader geopolitical narrative. English sources focus more on organizational fragmentation, military‑propaganda motivations, and internal challenges, offering a more critical view of inefficiencies and setbacks.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_space_program', 'key_points': ['**Pioneer of human spaceflight and lunar exploration** – The Soviet program achieved a string of historic firsts (R‑7 ICBM, Sputnik\\u202f1, Laika on Sputnik\\u202f2, Yuri\\u202fGagarin’s orbital flight, Valentina\\u202fTereshkova’s flight, the first spacewalk, the first Moon impact (Luna\\u202f2) and soft‑land (Luna\\u202f9), the first lunar rover, and the first space station, Salyut\\u202f1). These milestones are repeatedly highlighted as proof of the USSR’s technical superiority during the Cold‑War space race.', '**A highly fragmented, competition‑driven bureaucracy** – Unlike the United States, which funneled its effort through a single agency (NASA), the Soviet effort was split among numerous rival design bureaus (Korolev, Yangel, Glushko, Chelomei, etc.). The article stresses that this internal competition spurred rapid innovation but also created duplication and coordination problems.', '**Secrecy, propaganda, and later revelation of failures** – The program’s achievements were publicized as propaganda victories, while setbacks (e.g., the fatal Soyuz\\u202f1 crash, the N‑1 rocket failures, the deaths of Korolev and other key figures) were concealed for decades. The narrative stresses that glasnost in the 1980s finally exposed these hidden costs, showing a more balanced picture of triumphs and tragedies.', '**German‑technology legacy and post‑Soviet continuity** – Early Soviet rockets were heavily influenced by captured German V‑2 expertise and later benefited from German engineers, a point the article foregrounds to underline the program’s multinational roots. After the USSR’s dissolution, its space heritage was inherited chiefly by Russia (Roscosmos) and, to a lesser extent, Ukraine.', '**Broad scientific scope beyond crewed flight** – While crewed milestones dominate the story, the text also stresses the Soviet Union’s pioneering work on interplanetary probes (Venera\\u202f1,\\u202fMars\\u202f1, successful Venus and Mars landings) and scientific instrumentation, presenting the program as a comprehensive scientific enterprise, not merely a political showcase.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soviet_space_program', 'key_points': ['**Fragmented organization gave the Soviet program both a competitive edge and chronic inefficiency** – unlike NASA’s single‑agency structure, space work was split among rival design bureaus (Korolev’s OKB‑1, Yangel, Glushko, Chelomei, etc.), which spurred rapid breakthroughs but also duplicated effort, internal politics, and costly dead‑ends.', '**A parade of historic “firsts” cemented its super‑power reputation** – the USSR launched the world’s first artificial satellite (Sputnik\\u202f1), first animal (Laika), first human (Yuri\\u202fGagarin), first woman (Valentina\\u202fTereshkova), first spacewalk, first lunar soft‑landing, first rover (Lunokhod), first planetary sample‑return (Luna\\u202f16), and the first long‑duration space stations (Salyut\\u202f1, Mir).', '**Military and propaganda imperatives, not pure science, drove funding and priorities** – the program was tied to the Strategic Rocket Forces and ICBM development; Soviet leadership used successes as political propaganda while mission planning was often ad‑hoc, with occasional overt political choices (e.g., the rushed Vostok\\u202f3/4 race with the United States).', '**Leadership loss and technical missteps marked the latter half** – Korolev’s death in 1966 led to a power vacuum, the disastrous Soyuz\\u202f1 flight, repeated N‑1 launch failures, and the eventual cancellation of the crewed lunar landing effort, although unmanned planetary probes continued to achieve notable milestones.', '**Its legacy endures in today’s Russian space activity** – the Baikonur Cosmodrome (now leased to Russia), design heritage of rockets and spacecraft, and the experience gained from early achievements underpin the modern Russian space program and its continued participation in international missions.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Forgotten war', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgotten_war'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgotten_war', 'name': 'Korean War', 'years': '1950-1953', 'short_description': 'The Korean War (1950–1953) was a conflict between North Korea, supported by China and the Soviet Union, and South Korea, backed by United Nations forces led by the United States, fought on the Korean Peninsula.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': \"first bullet under 'most often refers to' (Korean War)\"}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The term “Forgotten War” primarily denotes three topics: the 2013 Australian book on frontier conflicts, the Korean War—especially in U.S. discourse—and the Ifni War in Spanish contexts; broader usage loosely applies to many overlooked conflicts worldwide.', 'bias_comment': 'Labeling varies by national perspective: U.S. sources emphasize the Korean War as forgotten, Spanish sources highlight the Ifni War, and Australian discourse references colonial frontier wars. The English‑language focus may underrepresent non‑Western narratives, and the broad, loose application can obscure distinct historical contexts.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forgotten_war', 'key_points': ['**Primary usage of “Forgotten War” (capitalized)** – most commonly points to three specific topics: the 2013 book *Forgotten War* about Australian frontier conflic