d by a British signals‑intelligence unit, hinting at covert surveillance motives.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of Olympic Games boycotts', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Olympic_Games_boycotts'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Olympic_Games_boycotts', 'name': 'List of Olympic Games boycotts', 'years': '1920, 1964, 1968, 1972, 1980, 1984, 2020, 2022', 'short_description': 'Nation‑state and diplomatic boycotts have affected the Olympic Games across decades, notably full boycotts of the 1920 Antwerp Games, the 1964 and 1968 Games (South Africa), the 1972 Munich Games (Rhodesia), the 1980 Moscow and 1984 Los\\u202fAngeles Games (Cold‑War protests), and diplomatic boycotts of the 2022 Beijing Winter Olympics over human‑rights concerns, with other diplomatic non‑attendance in 2020 (North Korea) and 2021‑2022.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '## List of full boycotts of an Olympic Games or full non-attendance'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since 1920, several nations have boycotted or diplomatically boycotted Olympic Games—often to protest wars, apartheid, or human‑rights issues—resulting in full withdrawals (e.g., 1980 Moscow) or limited diplomatic protests while athletes still compete (e.g., 2022 Beijing).', 'bias_comment': 'The English Wikipedia entry emphasizes geopolitical and human‑rights motives, framing boycotts as strategic political tools. Versions in other languages (e.g., Russian, Chinese) may stress national security or sovereignty concerns, downplay human‑rights narratives, or highlight domestic athlete perspectives, reflecting differing editorial priorities and state narratives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Olympic_Games_boycotts', 'key_points': ['**Olympic participation is repeatedly weaponized for political protest** – countries have been excluded or have voluntarily withdrawn from the Games in response to wars (e.g., the 1920 Antwerp Games), apartheid (South\\u202fAfrica, Rhodesia), and Cold‑War tensions (the 1980 Moscow and 1984 Los\\u202fAngeles boycotts).', '**Full boycotts are rare, but diplomatic boycotts have become a common compromise** – governments refuse to send official delegations while still allowing athletes to compete, as seen in the 2022 Beijing Winter Games where a dozen nations (U.S., U.K., Australia, Canada, etc.) protested China’s human‑rights record.', '**Human‑rights concerns now dominate the justification for Olympic boycotts** – recent actions cite China’s treatment of Uyghurs, the selection of a PLA officer as a torch‑bearer, and Russia’s invasion of Crimea, highlighting a shift from purely geopolitical disputes to moral/ethical arguments.', '**The pattern shows that Olympic boycotts are a strategic, high‑visibility tool for the international community** – by leveraging the global spotlight of the Games, states signal condemnation, pressure policy changes, and rally allied nations, even when the boycott is limited to diplomatic representation rather than athlete participation.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '1981 in Afghanistan', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1981_in_Afghanistan'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1981_in_Afghanistan', 'name': '1981 in Afghanistan', 'years': '1981', 'short_description': \"In 1981, the Soviet‑Afghan conflict persisted with intensified Mujahideen resistance across Afghanistan; Babrak Karmal's government rejected negotiations, while Soviet forces maintained an 85,000‑strong presence. A 1981‑85 trade agreement exchanged Afghan food, gas and raw materials for Soviet machinery and industrial goods.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Opening overview paragraph before sections'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'During 1981, the Soviet‑backed Afghan government under Babrak Karmal resisted negotiations, while Soviet forces remained, leading to intensified mujahideen resistance and harsh reprisals. Over 2\\u202fmillion Afghans fled, a trade pact deepened Soviet economic ties, and political reshuffles and failed UN talks occurred.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often stress Soviet aggression and civilian displacement, Russian-language accounts may downplay external pressure and highlight internal PDPA dynamics, while Pashto or Dari narratives could focus more on mujahideen resilience and humanitarian crises. These linguistic lenses shape emphasis on actors, blame, and the scale of suffering.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1981_in_Afghanistan', 'key_points': ['**Stalemate deepens:** Throughout 1981 the Soviet‑backed PDPA government under Babrak\\u202fKarmal refused any negotiations that did not suit its own terms, while the USSR showed no sign of withdrawing its 85,000‑strong force, cementing an impasse in the Afghan crisis.', '**Escalating resistance and repression:** Mujahideen guerrilla activity spread from Kandahar to the Soviet border in Badakhshan, prompting brutal Soviet reprisals—helicopter gunships and artillery devastated villages and inflicted heavy, though unquantified, Soviet casualties.', '**Humanitarian and economic fallout:** UN statistics recorded 1.7\\u202fmillion Afghans fleeing to Pakistan and 400\\u202f000 to Iran; at the same time a 1981‑85 trade agreement further bound Afghanistan’s economy to the Soviet bloc, exchanging food, gas and raw materials for Soviet machinery and industrial goods.', '**Political reshuffle:** In June Karmal gave up the premiership, handing it to fellow Parcham‑faction figure Sultan\\u202fAli\\u202fKeshtmand, who also assumed direct control of the newly created National Patriotic Front.', '**Failed diplomatic overtures:** Late‑year Karmal proposed UN‑mediated tripartite talks with Pakistan and Iran, but Iran rejected the offer and Pakistan dismissed it as de‑facto recognition of the regime; subsequent UN mediation by Javier Pérez de Cuéllar also failed to produce a breakthrough.']}]}}], 'source': 'USA_USSR_discovered.json', 'embedding_model': 'sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2', 'clusters': 110, 'start_year': 1900, 'end_year': 2005, 'countries': ['USA', 'USSR'], 'max_events': 200, 'search_engine': 'exa'}\n",
      "{'llm': 'openai/gpt-oss-120b', 'langs': ['en', 'fr', 'ru', 'zh', 'ar', 'he'], 'datetime': '2025-08-12T14:07:06.072544', 'data': [{'seed': {'name': 'Sino-British Joint Liaison Group', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-British_Joint_Liaison_Group'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-British_Joint_Liaison_Group', 'name': 'Sino-British Joint Liaison Group', 'years': '1985-1999', 'short_description': 'The Sino‑British Joint Liaison Group was an intergovernmental body created in 1985 by the UK and China to implement the 1984 Sino‑British Joint Declaration and oversee the smooth transfer of Hong Kong’s sovereignty in 1997; it operated until it was dissolved on 31\\u202fDecember\\u202f1999.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Sino‑British Joint Liaison Group (1985‑1999) was an intergovernmental body created after the 1984 Joint Declaration to coordinate the treaty’s implementation and the 1997 Hong\\u202fKong hand‑over, covering customs, WTO participation, international rights, and transition procedures; its minutes remain classified.', 'bias_comment': 'English Wikipedia emphasizes procedural and legal aspects, reflecting UK transparency expectations, while Chinese-language sources often stress sovereignty and the Group’s role in guaranteeing a smooth transition. The lack of released minutes fosters speculation, and differing national narratives can lead to bias in how the Group’s effectiveness and secrecy are portrayed across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-British_Joint_Liaison_Group', 'key_points': ['**Purpose‑driven intergovernmental body** – The Sino‑British Joint Liaison Group (JLG) was set up in 1985, after the Sino‑British Joint Declaration, to coordinate and consult on the implementation of the treaty and to ensure a smooth hand‑over of Hong\\u202fKong’s government in 1997