na’s assertion of internal sovereignty and Western expectations of treaty‑based autonomy, shaping international responses (sanctions, foreign‑policy legislation) and the future of Hong\\u202fKong’s “special administrative region” status.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-British_Joint_Declaration', 'key_points': ['**Legal status is contested** – The United Kingdom maintains that the Sino‑British Joint Declaration remains a fully‑binding international treaty, while the Chinese government treats it as a historical document whose obligations were “fully performed” with the 1997 hand‑over and thus has no continuing legal effect.', '**“One Country, Two Systems” promised stability** – The declaration pledged that Hong\\u202fKong’s capitalist system, way of life and fundamental freedoms would remain unchanged for 50\\u202fyears after 1997, a commitment repeatedly cited by the Hong\\u202fKong administration and Western governments as the cornerstone of the “one‑country‑two‑systems” framework.', '**Perceived violations and diplomatic disputes** – Since the 2000s, Britain (and later the United\\u202fStates and the EU) have accused Beijing of breaching the declaration—citing the 2020 National Security Law, the disqualification of pro‑democracy legislators, and alleged unlawful removals of Hong\\u202fKong residents—while China rebuffs these claims, calling them interference in its internal affairs.', '**Hong\\u202fKong’s public sentiment and the “no‑three‑legged‑stool” narrative** – Early polls showed a strong desire among Hong\\u202fKongers to preserve the status quo; Chinese negotiators repeatedly emphasized that Hong\\u202fKong was not a party to the talks (“no three‑legged stool”), underscoring the narrative that sovereignty decisions were a bilateral matter between Beijing and London.', '**Hidden contingency planning** – Declassified UK documents reveal that, anticipating a possible collapse of the declaration’s guarantees, Britain drafted a