sing against foreign aggression; today it is taught in every school textbook and commemorated with monuments, shrines and a “Bunker\\u202fHill‑the‑Alamo”‑type legacy.', '**Trigger: alleged British misconduct** – Chinese accounts claim a British patrol raped a villager’s wife, sparking an angry crowd of up to 10\\u202f000 locals who armed themselves with makeshift weapons and attacked the troops; British sources acknowledge looting but downplay the incident.', '**Asymmetric fighting and limited British losses** – The villagers managed to kill four Indian soldiers and wound many, while British forces, hampered by rain‑soaked flintlocks, eventually withdrew after a brief standoff, reinforcing the narrative of a “miraculous” Chinese triumph despite overwhelming odds.', '**Qing officials condemned as traitors** – Governor Yu\\u202fBaochun’s threat to raze Canton and his plea for the crowd to disperse earned him public vilification and a demoted career, underscoring the episode’s portrayal of loyal locals versus cowardly imperial officials.', '**Enduring symbolic weight** – Historians like Frederic\\u202fWakeman note that the episode allows China to extract a popular moral victory from the humiliating Opium War defeats, turning a minor military episode into a cornerstone of nationalist historiography.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%85%83%E9%87%8C%E6%8A%97%E8%8B%B1%E4%BA%8B%E4%BB%B6', 'key_points': ['**本次冲突被描绘为一次全民抗英的“义举”**：在英军抢劫、强奸当地百姓后，三元里村民在短短三天内自发组织千余乡勇，用刀、锄头等简易武器围堵并击退了英属印度陆军的30人小分队，取得了局部的“胜利”。', '**英军失利被归因于后勤失策与自然因素**：英军在雨季进入潮湿稻田，火绳枪受潮失效，加之指挥官受伤、补给困难，导致部队被迫撤回四方炮台，最终被迫接受停战条款。', '**文章同步呈现“协助英军”的汉奸现象**：除了大规模的反英行动，史料亦记载约1200名当地居民向英军提供弹药、情报，甚至直接参与作战，强调事件的复杂性和社会分裂。', '**事件在中华民族记忆中被高度赞颂**：当地流传民谣、张维屏的长诗《三元里》以及后来的纪念设施均将此役塑造成爱国斗争的象征，常被用作反侵略的宣传素材。', '**史料对双方伤亡的记载模糊**：英军官方只报告约5人阵亡、23人负伤，而中国方面缺乏明确统计；这种信息不对称反映出史学研究中对“胜负”评价的争议。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Fatshan Creek', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fatshan_Creek'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fatshan_Creek', 'name': 'Battle of Fatshan Creek', 'years': '1857', 'short_description': 'A naval engagement on 1\\u202fJune\\u202f1857 during the Second Opium War, where the British Royal Navy under Rear‑Admiral Sir Michael Seymour defeated the Qing Chinese fleet at Fatshan Creek near Canton, securing British control of the waterway.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 1\\xa0June\\xa01857, British forces under Rear‑Admiral Sir Michael Seymour attacked Chinese defensive positions at Fatshan Creek, capturing the fort, seizing artillery and vessels. British casualties were 13 dead and 44 wounded; Chinese losses were not comprehensively recorded.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often emphasize British naval superiority and low casualties, presenting the battle as a decisive triumph, while Mandarin accounts highlight foreign aggression, civilian suffering, and may report higher Chinese casualties, shaping divergent narratives across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fatshan_Creek', 'key_points': ['**Decisive British naval triumph** – On\\u202f1\\u202fJune\\u202f1857 Rear‑Admiral Sir\\u202fMichael Seymour’s squadron smashed the Qing Cantonese fleet, capturing the fort, seizing 25 guns and taking up to 80 war‑junks, while British losses were limited to 13 dead, 44 wounded and minimal material damage.', '**Overwhelming force disparity** – The Royal Navy deployed >1,900 marines and sailors, a screw sloop, a paddle steamer and seven gunboats (plus numerous auxiliary boats), against a modest Chinese defense of one fortified position, 25 guns and roughly 100 war‑junks.', '**Strategic foothold for the Second Opium War** – The victory cleared the way for the British advance on Canton (modern‑day Foshan), cementing their control of the Pearl River delta and 