ives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Whampoa', 'key_points': ['**Decisive British amphibious win** – On\\u202f2\\u202fMarch\\u202f1841 a small British squadron (HMS\\u202fWellesley,\\u202fSulphur and a bomb‑ketch) seized the concealed Chinese battery on Whampū\\u202fIsland, forcing the 250‑strong Manchu/Tartar garrison to flee and blowing up the forts.', '**Light British losses vs. heavier Chinese casualties** – Only one British seaman was killed (mortally wounded by grapeshot), while British reports claim 15–20 Chinese troops were killed.', '**Key commanders and aftermath** – The operation was ordered by Commodore\\u202fJames\\u202fBremer and carried out by Capt.\\u202fEdward\\u202fBelcher; after the capture Bremer handed land‑force command to Maj‑Gen.\\u202fHugh\\u202fGough, who joined the fleet aboard HMS\\u202fCruizer.', '**Emphasis on primary‑source narrative** – The article draws almost entirely on 19th‑century eyewitness accounts (Bulletins of State Intelligence, Belcher’s narrative, Hall &\\u202fBernard, etc.) and carries a Wikipedia maintenance tag warning of an excess of primary sources.', '**Chinese viewpoint briefly noted** – Lin\\u202fZexu’s diary entry for the day records his awareness of the “English rebel ships” forcing the fort, providing a rare contemporary Chinese perspective on the engagement.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Human rights in China', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights_in_China'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights_in_China', 'name': 'Human rights in China', 'years': '1949-present', 'short_description': 'The PRC’s human rights record is characterized by widespread restrictions on civil, political, religious and cultural freedoms, including censorship, mass surveillance, arbitrary detention, forced labor, and persecution of minorities such as Uyghurs and Tibetans.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Legal_system'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since 1949, China’s record on human rights has been marked by periodic state repression, including restrictions on speech, religion, assembly, and treatment of minorities, alongside legal mechanisms that align judiciary with party priorities and government claims of development and stability.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry foregrounds documented abuses and international criticism, using terms like ‘genocide’ and ‘crimes against humanity’, reflecting a rights‑focused, external perspective. The Chinese entry places abuses within a broader historic‑political narrative, stresses developmental and stability goals, and frames criticism as politicized, illustrating a state‑centric bias.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights_in_China', 'key_points': ['**Severe and widely documented rights violations** – International treaty bodies, the UN Human‑Rights Council, and NGOs such as Amnesty International and Human Rights Watch rank China among the world’s worst performers, citing systematic abuse of freedom of speech, press, assembly, religion, movement and the right to a fair trial.', '**Party‑over‑law legal framework** – The Chinese Constitution elevates the Communist Party’s “Four Cardinal Principles” and the doctrine of the “Three Supremes,” ensuring that the judiciary and law‑enforcement serve party directives; vague crimes like “subversion of state power” and “picking quarrels” are routinely used to imprison critics and lawyers.', '**Targeted repression of minorities and dissenters** – State‑directed campaigns punish Tibetans, Uyghurs, Christians, Falun Gong practitioners and migrant workers through mass internment camps, forced “re‑education,” strict religious controls, the discriminatory hukou residency system, denial of independent unions, and alleged organ‑harvesting and forced‑labour practices.', '**China’s defensive narrative vs. external findings** – Beijing frames its policies as culturally‑relative, “security‑driven” or necessary for development, while UN assessments, foreign governments and independent researchers label them crimes against humanity, ethnic cleansing, or genocide, highlighting the stark gap between official claims and documented realities.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B8%AD%E5%8D%8E%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%91%E5%85%B1%E5%92%8C%E5%9B%BD%E4%BA%BA%E6%9D%83', 'key_points': ['The article traces a long‑standing pattern of human‑rights abuses in the People’s Republic of China—from early Communist campaigns (e.g., “Three‑Anti Five‑Anti”, Cultural Revolution) to the intensified repression under Xi\\u202fJinping, including mass “re‑education” camps in Xinjiang, harsh crack‑down in Tibet, and pervasive censorship.', 'It stresses that, although the Reform‑and‑Opening era brought modest improvements, the post‑2012 “Xi era” has reversed many gains, eroding legal protections, silencing dissent, and expanding state‑controlled surveillance and “social‑credit” systems.', 'International organisations, NGOs and foreign governments repeatedly condemn these policies as violations of fundamental freedoms—labeling them as crimes against humanity, genocide, forced organ harvesting, and calling for sanctions and UN‑level investigations.', 'The Chinese government counters the criticism by invoking constitutional guarantees, emphasizing “developmental” and “survival” rights, and portraying its actions as necessary for stability, anti‑terrorism, and national unity, while dismissing external criticism as politically motivated.', 'Systemic issues highlighted include the lack of independent media, restrictions on NGOs, forced abortions, secretive death‑penalty practices, and the use of household‑registration and social‑credit mechanisms to control the population.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'One country, two systems', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_country,_two_systems'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_country,_two_systems', 'name': 'One country, two systems', 'years': 'early 1980s', 'short_description': 'A constitutional principle devised by Deng Xiaoping in the early 1980s, allowing Hong\\u202fKong and Macau to retain their capitalist systems under Chinese sovereignty for 50\\u202fyears.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Early 1980s formulation of the principle'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': '‘One country, two systems’ is a constitutional framework granting Hong\\u202fKong and Macau high autonomy for 50\\u202fyears after their handovers, while keeping them under Chinese sovereignty; its application, future, and extension to Taiwan or Tibet are contested and evolving.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic entry stresses the principle and uncertain post‑2047 future, presenting a relatively neutral tone. The English version foregrounds erosion of promised freedoms, protests and Western criticism, reflecting a liberal democratic bias. The Chinese article frames the policy as a core national strategy, emphasizing legal foundations and downplaying dissent, aligning with state narratives. These differences illustrate how language communities foreground distinct political concerns and source selection, leading to varied emphases on autonomy, control, and legitimacy.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_country,_two_systems', 'key_points': ['**Core principle:** “One\\u202fCountry, Two\\u202fSystems” is a constitutional framework that keeps China’s sovereign unity while granting Hong\\u202fKong, Macau (and theoretically Taiwan and Tibet) distinct political, economic, and legal systems.', '**Implementation in Hong\\u202fKong & Macau:** Both regions were promised a **high degree of autonomy for at least 50\\u202fyears** after their 1997 and 1999 handovers, preserving their own legal systems, civil liberties, and capitalist economies despite China’s communist regime.', '**Uncertain future:** The 50‑year guarantees end in **2047 for Hong\\u202fKong** and **2049 for Macau**, yet Beijing has never clarified what will replace the “two‑systems” arrangements thereafter.', '**Tibet’s exclusion:** Although the Dalai Lama suggested extending the model to Tibet, Chinese authorities **reject the notion**, insisting the policy was designed solely for Hong\\u202fKong and Macau, underscoring the regime’s reluctance to grant genuine autonomy to other restive regions.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_country,_two_systems', 'key_points': ['**“One country, two systems” is a constitutional framework devised by Deng\\u202fXiaoping to let Hong\\u202fKong, Macau\\u202f(and theoretically Taiwan) keep their own capitalist, legal and administrative arrangements while remaining under PRC sovereignty.**', '**In practice, Beijing has repeatedly tightened control—citing “destructive forces” and “national security”—leading to the 2020 national‑security law, the 2019‑2020 anti‑extradition protests, and the erosion of promised universal‑suffrage and civil liberties in Hong\\u202fKong.**', '**Macau has experienced far less dissent, largely because its population is more pro‑Beijing; the region’s autonomy has been portrayed as a “model” of the policy’s success.**', '**Taiwan has consistently rejected the formula, with polls showing overwhelming opposition; the PRC’s recent “Two‑Systems Taiwan Plan” remains vague and is viewed by many as a re‑packaging of a failed Hong\\u202fKong experiment.**', '**Overall, the principle is marketed as a long‑term “high‑degree of autonomy” promise, but mounting protests, legal crackdowns, and Beijing’s statements about extending the model beyond 2047 reveal a growing gap between the original “two‑systems” ideal and the reality of increasing central‑government dominance.**']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B8%80%E5%9B%BD%E4%B8%A4%E5%88%B6', 'key_points': ['**中國官方的「一國兩制」政策**被定位為實現「和平統一」臺灣的根本國策，同時也用於香港、澳門的回歸與特殊行政區治理，強調在「一個中國」前提下保留資本主義制度不變。', '**實踐效果與國內外爭議**：在香港與澳門的「五十年不變」承諾被指未能兌現，臺灣各 major 政黨（民進黨、國民黨、民眾黨）明確表態拒絕「一國兩制」方案；香港則經歷雨傘運動、港區國安法等政治回合，引發國際譴責與本地民主倒退的指控。', '**制度設計與法律框架**：通過《中英聯合聲明》與《基本法》賦予香港、澳門高度自治、獨立司法與經濟體系；但中央政府保留外交、國防與「解釋權」等核心權力，並於2021年修改選舉制度，加強「愛國者治港」的政治控制。', '**民意與批評聲音**：多項民調顯示香港與臺灣民眾對「一國兩制」的支持率持續下降，且有學者與本土運動提出「一國良制」或聯邦制等替代方案，指該政策是「單方面的政治想像」與「權宜之計」。', '**黨章與未來走向**：自中共二十大以來，「全面貫徹一國兩制」已寫入黨章，並被視為中國特色社會主義與民族復興的核心戰略；同時，各界仍在論辯其是否仍具可行性或已成「一廂情願」的過時概念。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Japanese occupation of Hong Kong', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_occupation_of_Hong_Kong'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_occupation_of_Hong_Kong', 'name': 'Japanese occupation of Hong Kong', 'years': '1941–1945', 'short_description': \"The Imperial Japanese forces occupied Hong Kong after the British surrender on 25\\u202fDecember\\u202f1941, maintaining military control for three years and eight months until Japan's surrender in August\\u202f1945.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Lead paragraph describing the surrender and occupation period'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"From December 1941 to September 1945, Japan occupied Hong\\u202fKong under military rule, imposing Japanese language, currency, and harsh economic controls. The period saw widespread atrocities, severe deprivation, and resistance by local guerrillas. British forces re‑established control after Japan's surrender, leaving lasting social and economic impacts.\", 'bias_comment': 'English Wikipedia presents a balanced overview but may under‑represent Japanese wartime propaganda and Chinese nationalist narratives. Chinese sources often emphasize civilian suffering and resistance, while Japanese sources may downplay atrocities and emphasize administrative aspects. Cross‑language variations affect casualty figures, language‑policy descriptions, and moral framing.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_occupation_of_Hong_Kong', 'key_points': ['**A brutal military rule that reshaped Hong\\u202fKong (Dec\\u202f1941\\u202f–\\u202fSep\\u202f1945).** After a 18‑day defence, British Governor Mark Young surrendered; the territory was governed by a Japanese military administration under governors such as Takashi Sakai and Rensuke Isogai, with martial law, Japanese‑imposed language, street‑name changes and a new legal order.', '**Severe economic deprivation and social control.** The occupiers outlawed the Hong\\u202fKong dollar, imposed a devalued Japanese military yen, forced rationing of rice and other staples, and commandeered schools, hospitals and factories for military use, leaving the civilian population impoverished, malnourished and displaced (population fell from 1.6\\u202fmillion to ~600\\u202f000).', '**Widespread atrocities and war crimes.** Japanese forces executed an estimated 10\\u202f000 civilians, carried out mass killings during censuses, forced labour on Hainan, sank refugee ships, and committed notorious massacres such as the Silver Mine Bay massacre and the killing of POWs aboard the Lisbon\\u202fMaru.', '**Active resistance and guerrilla effort.** Local Chinese guerrilla groups—most prominently the East River Column and the Hong\\u202fKong‑Kowloon Brigade—conducted sabotage, rescued Allied airmen and POWs, and sustained a covert network that kept the anti‑Japanese struggle alive throughout the occupation.', '**Liberation and lasting legacy.** Following Japan’s surrender (15\\u202fAug\\u202f1945), British forces re‑established control on 30\\u202fAug, marking “Liberation Day”. The occupation left deep scars—population loss, infrastructure damage, and collective memory of hardship—but also accelerated post‑war recovery and set the stage for Hong\\u202fKong’s later economic resurgence.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'User:Blackmane/Boxer Origins - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Blackmane/Boxer_Origins'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Blackmane/Boxer_Origins', 'name': 'Boxer Origins', 'years': '1899-1901', 'short_description': 'The Society of Righteous and Harmonious Fists, known as the Boxers, arose in Shandong as a secret society of dispossessed peasants opposing foreign influence, leading to the Boxer Rebellion (1899‑1901).', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Origins_of_the_Boxers'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Boxer movement emerged in Shandong (1899‑1901) among peasants facing land loss, economic hardship, and foreign influence; it aligned with the Qing, incorporated martial and spiritual practices, merged with earlier secret societies, and reacted against missionary activity and foreign presence.', 'bias_comment': \"English-language accounts often stress the Boxers' anti‑foreign violence and mystical practices, while Chinese sources may highlight anti‑imperialist resistance and socioeconomic grievances. Translations can downplay Qing support or over‑emphasize foreign victimhood, influencing perceived motives across languages.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Blackmane/Boxer_Origins', 'key_points': ['**Reaction to Imperialism & Dispossession** – The Boxers (Society of Righteous and Harmonious Fists) arose in Shandong among peasants and laborers who had lost land and jobs to foreign encroachment, natural disasters, opium addiction, and religious conflict.', '**Pro‑Qing Alignment** – Manchu official Yuxian renamed the group “Yihequan” to stress its loyalty to the Qing dynasty; the slogan later became “support the Qing, destroy the foreign” (扶清灭洋), reflecting a political alliance that Cixi exploited to push out missionaries and foreign influence.', '**Mystical Martial Culture** – Western observers called them “Boxers” for their rigorous martial‑arts training and calisthenics. Central to their identity was spirit‑possession rituals promising invulnerability to guns, bullets and even supernatural “spirit soldiers” that would aid them in purging foreign elements.', '**Intersection with Other Secret Societies** – Earlier groups such as the Big Swords Society and other regional militias fought banditry and clashed with Catholic converts, creating a volatile milieu of secret societies that fed into the Boxer movement.', '**Religious & Racial Intolerance as a Catalyst** – The protection of Christian missionaries and converts under extraterritoriality, combined with growing European immigration, fueled anti‑Christian and anti‑foreigner anger that the Boxers turned into violent rebellion.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '1990s in Hong Kong', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990s_in_Hong_Kong'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990s_in_Hong_Kong', 'name': '1990s in Hong Kong', 'years': '1990-1999', 'short_description': \"The 1990s were Hong Kong's final decade under British colonial rule, marked by political transition, the 1997 handover to China, social anxiety, emigration, economic shifts, and major infrastructure projects.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1990s in Hong\\u202fKong were shaped by the Joint Declaration and 1997 handover, prompting democratic reforms, political tension, significant emigration, a shift from manufacturing to finance, major infrastructure projects, and a flourishing entertainment industry.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language Wikipedia emphasizes political uncertainty, pro‑democracy reforms, and the brain‑drain narrative, reflecting Western media focus. Chinese‑language sources often frame the handover as reunification, stress economic stability, and portray migration as temporary, shifting emphasis between political unrest and national cohesion.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990s_in_Hong_Kong', 'key_points': ['**Political transition dominated the decade** – The 1990s were framed by the 1984 Sino‑British Joint Declaration and the 1997 handover, with Governor\\u202fChris\\u202fPatten’s democratic reforms, anxieties after Tiananmen, and the clash between pro‑Beijing and pro‑democracy camps over the Basic Law and the legitimacy of the provisional Legislative Council.', '**Mass out‑migration reflected deep uncertainty** – Fear of a loss of autonomy prompted a sizeable “brain‑drain”: about 60\\u202f000 people left annually in the early ’90s, sending billions of dollars abroad and prompting emigration schemes for Hong\\u202fKong’s elite and middle class.', '**Economic turbulence and structural shift** – Although the Asian Financial Crisis hit Hong\\u202fKong hard, the territory’s strong banking system and government market‑intervention cushioned the fallout. At the same time, manufacturing’s share of GDP fell dramatically, while finance, real‑estate development, and massive infrastructure projects (new airport, Tsing\\u202fMa Bridge, extensive highway/MTR network) reshaped the economy.', '**Cultural renaissance amid change** – Despite political unrest, Hong\\u202fKong’s music, film, and television scenes flourished: the rise of the “Four Heavenly Kings,” internationally acclaimed directors like Wong\\u202fKar‑wai, and the spread of Japanese and Taiwanese pop culture cemented a vibrant, globally‑connected entertainment industry that became a hallmark of 1990s Hong\\u202fKong identity.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Ty-ho Bay', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kuhlan'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kuhlan', 'name': 'Battle of Ty-ho Bay', 'years': '1855', 'short_description': 'A joint British‑American naval action on 4\\u202fAugust\\u202f1855 off Tai O, Hong Kong, where steamers Eaglet and the sloop‑of‑war Rattler, aided by USS Powhatan, attacked a fleet of Chinese pirate junks, destroying many vessels, killing around 500 pirates, liberating captured merchant ships, and securing an Anglo‑American victory.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Battle'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1855, a joint British‑American naval force attacked Chinese pirate junks at Ty‑ho Bay, sinking many vessels, killing hundreds of pirates, and rescuing captured merchant ships; the Allies incurred minimal casualties.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language accounts stress a decisive Anglo‑American victory and anti‑piracy triumph, framing the pirates as lawless aggressors. Chinese sources often portray the junks as local resistance to foreign intrusion, emphasizing civilian losses and portraying the battle as foreign aggression. Other language versions (e.g., Japanese, French) may downplay the event’s broader significance, reflecting national historiographic priorities.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kuhlan', 'key_points': ['**Decisive Anglo‑American triumph:** A small force of a British steamer, sloop and a U.S. screw‑frigate, supported by armed boats, crushed a fleet of 36 Chinese war‑junks, sinking 20 and forcing the remainder to flee.', '**One of the last great anti‑piracy actions in Asia:** The battle marked the end of the era when large pirate armadas could operate openly in the South‑China Sea, a region where piracy persisted long after it had been eradicated in the Atlantic.', '**First substantial joint operation:** British Commander William Fellowes and U.S. Commodore William\\u202fMcCluney coordinated the attack, highlighting early U.S.–British naval cooperation far from home waters.', '**Heavy pirate losses vs. light Western casualties:** Roughly 500 pirates were killed or wounded and about 1\\u202f000 captured, while the Allies suffered only nine dead and six wounded, underscoring the technological and tactical superiority of the steam‑powered Western ships.', '**Legacy largely forgotten despite a memorial:** The victory liberated seven captured merchant vessels and prompted a monument (now in Hong\\u202fKong Cemetery), yet the battle is rarely mentioned in popular histories of 19th‑century naval warfare.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Dogger Bank incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dogger_Bank_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dogger_Bank_incident', 'name': 'Dogger Bank incident', 'years': '1904', 'short_description': 'On the night of 21–22 October 1904, the Russian Baltic Fleet, fearing Japanese torpedo boats, mistakenly opened fire on British fishing trawlers at the Dogger Bank, sinking one vessel, injuring several fishermen, and causing two deaths, while also firing on its own ships; the incident nearly provoked war between Britain and Russia.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Incident'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In October 1904 the Russian Baltic Fleet misidentified British trawlers near Dogger Bank as Japanese torpedo boats, opened fire, killing two British fishermen and wounding several. The incident sparked a diplomatic crisis, leading to international arbitration and Russian reparations of £66,000.', 'bias_comment': 'English accounts emphasize Russian panic and British victimhood, while Russian-language versions stress miscommunication and downplay aggression. German sources often focus on diplomatic tension and naval implications. These variations show how national narratives shape blame, responsibility, and strategic interpretation.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dogger_Bank_incident', 'key_points': ['**A panic‑driven misidentification:**  In October\\u202f1904 the Russian Baltic Fleet, terrified by false reports of Japanese torpedo boats, mistook ordinary British trawlers off the Dogger Bank for enemy vessels and opened fire, even turning on each other in the ensuing chaos.', '**Human cost and friendly fire:**  Two British fishermen were killed (one later died of shock) and several others wounded; one trawler was sunk.  The Russian cruisers\\u202f*Aurora* and\\u202f*Dmitrii Donskoi* were also hit by friendly fire, killing a chaplain and a sailor.', '**Near‑war diplomatic crisis:**  The attack sparked a furious British press outcry (“pirates”) and forced the Royal Navy to ready 28 battleships for conflict; the incident threatened to break the Anglo‑Japanese Alliance and almost led to war between Britain and Russia.', '**International arbitration and reparations:**  Under pressure, Russia agreed to an international commission (Hague‑based) which ultimately cleared Admiral Rozhestvensky of personal blame, and Russia paid £66,000 in compensation and a memorial was erected in Hull.', '**Strategic aftermath:**  The fleet’s morale and cohesion suffered, contributing to its later defeat at the Battle of Tsushima, while the Dogger Bank affair became a notable case study in war‑induced paranoia and the importance of diplomatic dispute settlement.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Gaselee Expedition', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasalee_Expedition'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasalee_Expedition', 'name': 'Gaselee Expedition', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'The Gaselee Expedition was the multinational second relief force that marched from Tianjin to Beijing in August 1900, defeating Boxer and Qing forces and lifting the siege of the foreign legations.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#The_expedition'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In August 1900, a multinational force of about 55,000 troops from Japan, Britain, the United States, France, Russia, Germany, Italy, and Austria‑Hungary marched from Tianjin to relieve the besieged Beijing legions. The campaign faced extreme heat, disease, logistical problems, command disputes, and incidents of violence among allies and against civilians.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article highlights