iplined occupation, while Chinese-language accounts highlight oppression and local guerrilla activity. Cross‑language differences affect perceived scale and moral evaluation of the garrison.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hong_Kong_Defence_Force_(Imperial_Japanese_Army)', 'key_points': ['The Hong\\u202fKong Defence Force was the principal Imperial Japanese Army garrison in occupied Hong\\u202fKong from January\\u202f1942 until Japan’s surrender in September\\u202f1945.', 'Organized like an Independent Mixed Brigade, it consisted of three infantry battalions (67th‑69th), a modest artillery complement (AA guns, trench‑mortars, field batteries) and a hospital, totaling just 106 officers and 3,028 enlisted men – a force later bolstered by a 250‑man detachment from the Twenty‑Third Army.', 'Its units were distributed across Hong\\u202fKong Island, Kowloon and the New\\u202fTerritories, policing urban areas, the Kowloon‑Canton Railway and key strategic posts; however, the force proved too small to secure the colony without additional reinforcements.', 'In the war’s later stages the Japanese built extensive defensive works—including concrete pillboxes and hillside tunnels, especially around Fei\\u202fNgo\\u202fShan and Tate’s\\u202fCairn—to fortify the occupied territory.', 'The Hong\\u202fKong Defence Force capitulated together with all Japanese forces in Hong\\u202fKong on 16\\u202fSeptember\\u202f1945, ending its brief but intensive role in the colony’s World\\u202fWar\\u202fII history.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Shanhaiguan (1900)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Shanhaiguan_(1900)'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Shanhaiguan_(1900)', 'name': 'Battle of Shanhaiguan (1900)', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': \"In 1900, during the Boxer Rebellion, Qing imperial troops and Boxer forces fought British forces at Shanhaiguan, China. The battle 