hinese losses are recorded only as the destruction of the “old dragon head,” reflecting the asymmetrical reporting of the battle’s toll.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Kivu frontier incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kivu_frontier_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kivu_frontier_incident', 'name': 'Kivu frontier incident', 'years': '1909-1910', 'short_description': 'A 1909–1910 standoff in the Lake Kivu region where Belgian, British and German forces contested colonial borders; diplomatic negotiations averted war and led to the division of the area among the Belgian Congo, British Uganda Protectorate and German East Africa.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Background'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1909‑1910 Belgium, Britain and Germany almost clashed over the Lake Kivu border, each claiming the Ufumbiro plain; diplomatic talks in 1910 led to the Uganda‑Congo Boundary Convention, establishing the current borders.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article stresses a three‑power standoff and British overreach, the French version highlights Belgian defensive actions and downplays British moves, while the German version frames the incident as a German strategic safeguard, portraying Belgium as reactive. These national viewpoints shape perceived responsibilities and the colonial narrative.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kivu_frontier_incident', 'key_points': ['**A three‑way colonial showdown** – In 1909‑1910 Belgian, British and German troops almost went to war over the poorly‑mapped Lake\\u202fKivu frontier, each claiming the strategic Ufumbiro plain and surrounding mountain passes.', '**British overreach, Belgian resistance** – British officer John\\u202fMethuen\\u202fCoote marched into the disputed zone, setting up fortified posts and asserting a new boundary, while Belgian commander Frederik‑Valdemar\\u202fOlsen warned that any further advance would be met with fire and demanded diplomatic resolution.', '**German maneuvering added tension** – Germany, seeking to protect its East\\u202fAfrican interests, occupied nearby volcanic heights and threatened the Belgians, turning the incident into a three‑power standoff that forced all sides to pull back troops.', '**Diplomacy prevailed, but colonial interests won** – A series of conferences (Feb\\u202f1910\\u202f–\\u202fAug\\u202f1910) produced the Uganda‑Congo Boundary Convention and an Anglo‑German‑Belgian commission; the final borders favored the stronger colonial powers (especially Britain), ignored local peoples, and kept the region under European control.', '**Legacy of a “peaceful” crisis** – Although outright war was averted for a few years, the episode highlighted the arbitrary nature of African borders drawn from European rivalries and set the stage for later tensions in the Great Lakes region.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Tang Man Sit v Capacious Investments Ltd', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tang_Man_Sit_v_Capacious_Investments_Ltd'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tang_Man_Sit_v_Capacious_Investments_Ltd', 'name': 'Tang Man Sit v Capacious Investments Ltd', 'years': '1995', 'short_description': 'A 1995 English trusts law case before the Privy Council concerning breach of trust and dishonest assistance, where the court held that a beneficiary could recover damages for loss of market rental value and deterioration of property despite the availability of alternative restitution remedies.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Judgment'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In Tang Man Sit v Capacious Investments Ltd (1995), the Privy Council held that a beneficiary may claim both restitution (accounting of profits) and damages for breach of trust, allowing recovery of lost market rent and property deterioration without needing to elect a remedy beforehand.', 'bias_comment': 'The English Wikipedia entry emphasizes the doctrinal significance for trust law and later cases, reflecting a common law perspective. Absence of versions in other languages means potential cultural or jurisdictional nuances—such as local views on equitable remedies—are not captured, limiting a fuller cross‑jurisdictional understanding.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tang_Man_Sit_v_Capacious_Investments_Ltd', 'key_points': ['**Core issue:**\\u202fThe Privy Council examined whether a beneficiary could claim both restitution (accounting of profits) and damages for breach of trust involving the unauthorized leasing of a house that the settlor had assigned to Capacious Investments Ltd.', '**Fact pattern:**\\u202fMr\\u202fTang signed a deed assigning property to Capacious, then secretly let the house to a third party. Capacious sought to have the property reconveyed free of encumbrances, to recover the lease profits, and to be compensated for loss of market rental value and deterioration caused by the unlawful occupation.', '**Key holding (Lord\\u202fNicholls):**\\u202fCapacious may recover damages for loss of market rent and for deterioration *in addition to* an accounting of profits. The claimant does not have to make an “election” of remedy at the time the court orders the accounting; it can first obtain a damages assessment and then choose the appropriate recovery.', '**Broader significance:**\\u202fThe decision clarifies that restitution and damages are alternative, not mutually exclusive, remedies in breach‑of‑trust claims, influencing later dishonest‑assistance and trust‑law cases (e.g., *Twinsectra v Yardley*, *Dubai Aluminium v Salaam*).']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Nile Expedition', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile_Expedition'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile_Expedition', 'name': 'Nile Expedition', 'years': '1884-1885', 'short_description': 'The Nile Expedition (1884–1885), also called the Gordon Relief Expedition, was a British‑led mission to relieve General Charles George Gordon at Khartoum during the Mahdist uprising; a Canadian contingent of voyageurs helped navigate the Nile, but the force arrived two days after Khartoum fell.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#lead'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1884‑85 Nile Expedition aimed to relieve General Gordon in Khartoum but arrived two days after the city fell, resulting in the deaths of Gordon, the garrison, and civilians. It involved civilian Canadian voyageurs, sparked blame disputes among British officials, and influenced later Sudan campaigns.', 'bias_comment': 'English accounts often stress British command failures and Canadian contributions, while French or Arabic versions may highlight imperial aggression or Sudanese suffering. National narratives can shift blame onto different figures (Wolseley, Gladstone, Gordon) and under‑represent local perspectives, illustrating how language shapes perceived responsibility and heroism.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile_Expedition', 'key_points': ['**A failed rescue:** The 1884‑85 Nile (Gordon Relief) Expedition set out to save General Charles\\u202fGordon in Khartoum but reached the city two days after its fall, resulting in the slaughter of the garrison, Gordon himself, and thousands of civilians.', '**Contested blame:** Senior officers and the British public exchanged accusations—Wolseley condemned Wilson for “losing his nerve,” while many historians shift responsibility to Wolseley, Prime Minister Gladstone, or even Gordon for refusing to evacuate when he still could.', '**Canada’s first overseas deployment:** Although the 386 “Nile Voyageurs” were civilian contractors (including 86 First Nations men), they played a pivotal role navigating the Nile’s cataracts, earning official thanks and later memorialisation despite not wearing military uniforms.', '**Enduring legacy:** The disaster prompted Britain’s short‑term withdrawal from Sudan (later reversed under Kitchener), left a lasting imprint on Canadian military history through monuments, published accounts, and cultural references, and remains a cautionary example of mis‑managed imperial expeditions.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Shakee Massacre', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shakee_Massacre'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shakee_Massacre', 'name': 'Shakee Massacre', 'years': '1925', 'short_description': 'The Shakee Massacre occurred on 23 June 1925 in Shaji, Guangzhou, when British, French and Portuguese troops opened fire on a protest of over 100,000 Chinese demonstrators, killing over 50 and injuring more than 170.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph of the article'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 23 June 1925, colonial troops from Britain, France, and Portugal fired on a large anti‑imperialist protest in Shakee, Guangzhou, killing over 50 and wounding more than 170. The event heightened Chinese anti‑foreign sentiment and influenced subsequent nationalist movements.', 'bias_comment': 'Chinese-language accounts often stress national humiliation and portray the incident as a symbol of foreign aggression, sometimes using higher casualty figures. English-language sources may adopt a more detached tone, focusing on the colonial military response and contextualizing the event within broader imperial history, which can understate Chinese emotional framing.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shakee_Massacre', 'key_points': ['**A brutal crackdown by colonial troops:** On 23\\u202fJune\\u202f1925, British, French and Portuguese soldiers opened fire on a massive anti‑imperialist protest in Shakee (Guangzhou), killing\\u202fover\\u202f50 people (including 20 civilians and 23 cadets) and wounding more than 170.', '**Part of the wider anti‑foreign wave:** The demonstration was organized in response to the May\\u202f30 Movement and aimed to overturn the “unequal treaties” and expel foreign powers from China; the massacre underscored the violent resistance of colonial forces to Chinese nationalist demands.', '**Chinese authorities’ pleas ignored:** Canton’s provisional government demanded a formal apology, punishment of the officers, removal of warships, return of Shamian Island and reparations; the British and French consulates flatly rejected these requests, fueling further anti‑colonial sentiment.', '**Long‑lasting memorialisation:** The tragedy was commemorated with a public memorial ceremony in July\\u202f1925, a dedicated monument, and the naming of “Six‑Two‑Three Road” (623\\u202f路) after the date of the massacre, cementing it as a symbol of national humiliation and resistance.', '**Legacy of resistance:** The massacre became a rallying point for Chinese labor and student movements, inspiring subsequent strikes (e.g., Hong\\u202fKong’s October\\u202f29 counter‑strike) and remaining a poignant reminder of foreign aggression in modern Chinese historiography.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': '1994 Hong Kong electoral reform', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1994_Hong_Kong_electoral_reform'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1994_Hong_Kong_electoral_reform', 'name': '1994 Hong Kong electoral reform', 'years': '1994', 'short_description': 'A set of constitutional changes introduced by Governor Chris Patten to broaden the electorate for District Board, Municipal and Legislative Council elections before the 1997 handover, passed in 1994 and sparking a diplomatic row with China.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Legislative_voting (section detailing passage of the reform bills)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"In 1994 Governor Chris Patten introduced electoral reforms expanding the franchise and altering legislative composition. The British administration supported them, while