sizing communist subversion, while Chinese‑language histories stress anti‑colonial popular resistance and highlight bureaucratic miscommunication. These linguistic lenses can amplify different actors and motives, affecting how the event’s agency and spontaneity are interpreted.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1954_National_Service_Riots', 'key_points': ['**A British‑imposed National Service Ordinance sparked student defiance:**\\u202fIn December\\u202f1953 Singapore’s colonial government ordered all male British subjects (18‑20\\u202fyrs) to register for part‑time service; Chinese middle‑school students refused to comply, leading to a petition‑meeting with Chief Secretary William\\u202fGoode on\\u202f13\\u202fMay\\u202f1954 that erupted into a clash, injuring dozens and resulting in 48 arrests.', '**The incident is framed through three competing lenses:**', '1. **Communist subversion** – scholars cite MCP’s “united‑front” strategy, arguing the protests were deliberately mobilised by communists to recruit and train future cadres.', '2. **Anti‑colonial popular movement** – historians emphasise broader Chinese resentment toward British policies (English‑only education, emergency repression) and portray the riots as a spontaneous, grassroots challenge to colonial rule.', '3. **Misperception and bureaucratic failure** – recent work stresses translation errors (“National Service” misread as *minzhong fuwu*), poor communication and forced registration as the immediate trigger, casting the unrest as an unintended fallout rather than a pre‑planned agitation.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Mackay Treaty', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mackay_Treaty'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mackay_Treaty', 'name': 'Mackay Treaty', 'years': '1902', 'short_description': 'A sixteen‑article treaty signed on 5\\u202fSeptember\\u202f1902 between the United Kingdom and the Qing dynasty, abolishing the likin tax and initiating steps to end extraterritorial rights for foreign nationals in China.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The 1902 Mackay Treaty, signed between Britain and Qing China, ended the likin tax, raised import and export duties, and included a clause allowing the gradual removal of extraterritorial rights if Chinese law met Western standards. It was the Qing's final treaty before the 1911 revolution.\", 'bias_comment': 'English sources often stress the treaty as a Chinese‑led reform, while Chinese-language accounts may emphasize concessions made under pressure. Western narratives can downplay imperialist constraints, whereas Chinese narratives highlight sovereignty gains. Cross‑language differences affect how agency, fairness, and impact are portrayed.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mackay_Treaty', 'key_points': ['**A rare “Chinese‑led” negotiation:**\\u202fUnlike earlier unequal treaties (Nanking, Tientsin), the Mackay Treaty was drafted after vigorous Chinese involvement, with Qing officials actively pushing for concessions—especially the abolition of the unpopular *likin* tax.', '**Fiscal reshuffle in China’s favour:**\\u202fThe treaty eliminated the *likin* system (a major revenue source for provincial authorities) and compensated the loss by raising import duties to 12.5\\u202f% ad\\u202fvalorem and export duties to 7.5\\u202f%, while scrapping internal transit duties on foreign goods.', '**First step toward ending extraterritoriality:**\\u202fArticle\\u202fXII signaled Britain’s willingness to relinquish its extraterritorial rights once China’s legal system met “Western standards,” marking a symbolic yet limited acknowledgement of Chinese sovereignty.', '**Mixed reception and limited enforcement:**\\u202fAlthough the China Association narrowly endorsed the treaty, British merchants soon complained that 