aval blockade of Germany and the Central Powers from 1914–1919, restricting maritime supply of goods, especially food, causing civilian deaths and contributing to the Allied victory in World War I.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph summarizing the blockade'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The British‑led naval blockade (1914‑1919) restricted Germany’s imports of food, raw materials and oil, weakening its war economy. It caused civilian shortages, including the Turnip Winter, and contributed to civilian deaths. The blockade persisted after the armistice, ending with the 1919 Treaty of Versailles.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources stress strategic success, German accounts highlight civilian suffering, French works focus on neutral‑trade disputes, and Dutch narratives examine economic impacts on neutral shipping. These language‑based emphases can bias interpretations of effectiveness versus humanitarian cost, so cross‑language balance is essential.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blockade_of_Germany_(1914%E2%80%931919)', 'key_points': ['**A decisive weapon of the Allies:**\\u202fThe British‑led “distant” naval blockade (1914‑1919) effectively cut off the Central Powers from overseas supplies of food, raw materials and oil, strangling Germany’s war‑economy and helping secure an Allied victory.', '**Strategic versus humanitarian impact:**\\u202fWhile the blockade crippled German industry—forcing the recycling of metal, substitution of chemicals and even iron‑tyred trucks—it also caused acute food shortages, the infamous “Turnip Winter,” and mass civilian distress that fed Allied propaganda and post‑war political pressure.', '**Death‑toll controversy:**\\u202fGerman officials claimed up to\\u202f~760\\u202f000 civilian deaths from starvation and disease; scholarly estimates range from\\u202f~300\\u202f000 to\\u202f~424\\u202f000, with many historians emphasizing the 