hai Volunteer Corps was a multinational, volunteer militia under the Shanghai Municipal Council, serving the International Settlement from 12\\u202fApril\\u202f1853 until its disbandment on 27\\u202fFebruary\\u202f1942; it took part in actions such as the Battle of the Muddy Flat (1854), the Boxer Rebellion, and the defence of Shanghai in World War\\xa0II.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#History – describes its formation in 1853 and disbandment in 1942'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Shanghai Volunteer Corps (1853‑1942) was a multinational militia organized by the Shanghai Municipal Council, comprising volunteers from many nationalities to defend the International Settlement during several conflicts, including the Boxer Rebellion, World\\u202fI, the 1932 and 1937 Shanghai incidents, and was dissolved after the Japanese occupation in 1942.', 'bias_comment': \"The English article emphasizes the Corps' multiculturalism and volunteer spirit, which can downplay the colonial context of foreign control over Shanghai. Chinese-language accounts often stress the militia as an instrument of foreign imperialism, while Japanese wartime narratives portray it as a hostile opponent. These divergent framings illustrate how national perspectives shape the interpretation of the same historical entity.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghai_Volunteer_Corps', 'key_points': ['**A unique, multinational militia** – The Shanghai Volunteer Corps (1853‑1942) was an auxiliary force under the Shanghai Municipal Council, drawing volunteers from dozens of nationalities—including British, American, Chinese, German, Japanese, Jewish, Portuguese, and White Russian communities—to protect the International Settlement.', '**Key combat roles in Shanghai’s crises** – The Corps first saw combat in the 1854 “Battle of the Muddy Flat” against Qing troops, later mobilised for the Boxer Rebellion, World War\\u