son (1895‑1977)** – prominent British diplomat who served in key wartime and post‑war posts.', '**Leadership of intelligence:** headed the United Kingdom’s Joint Intelligence Committee (1936‑1939), shaping pre‑WWII policy.', '**High‑profile ambassadorships:** British envoy to Yugoslavia (1943‑1946), Ambassador to China (1946‑1948), and Ambassador to Egypt (1950‑1955).', '**Honours:** awarded the Knight Grand Cross of the Order of St\\u202fMichael and St\\u202fGeorge, underscoring his distinguished service.', '**Personal background:** born 16\\u202fMay\\u202f1895, married Helen Boreel (1921‑1944), had one son; died 23\\u202fJune\\u202f1977 at age\\u202f82.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Angus I. Ward', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angus_I._Ward'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angus_I._Ward', 'name': 'Angus I. Ward', 'years': '1952-1956', 'short_description': 'Angus Ivan Ward (1893–1969) was an American career Foreign Service Officer who served as Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary to Afghanistan from 1952 to 1956.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'opening paragraph (lead section)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Angus I. Ward (1893‑1969) was a U.S. Foreign Service officer who served as ambassador to Afghanistan (1952‑56). Earlier, as consul in Mukden during the Chinese Civil War, he refused to surrender the consulate’s radio, was placed under house arrest, and left China after the Communist takeover.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article frames Ward as a resolute diplomat opposing Communist demands, reflecting a U.S.-centric Cold‑War narrative. Chinese‑language sources often describe the incident as a diplomatic dispute or espionage accusation, emphasizing sovereignty rather than personal heroism. Reliance on a single language may thus skew perception toward Western viewpoints.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angus_I._Ward', 'key_points': ['