mage** – attacks disrupt essential services (e.g., Colonial Pipeline, Australian government sites, health‑care systems), expose sensitive personal data of millions of individuals, and fuel geopolitical tension, prompting governments worldwide to treat cyber‑incidents as a core component of national security strategy.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Nagasaki incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagasaki_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagasaki_incident', 'name': 'Nagasaki incident', 'years': '1886', 'short_description': \"A violent clash in August 1886 between about 500 Qing Chinese sailors from the Beiyang Fleet and Japanese police in Nagasaki, Japan, triggered by the sailors' disorderly behavior ashore; it caused dozens of deaths, injuries, and heightened anti‑Qing sentiment in Japan.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Outline'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In August 1886, about 500 Qing Beiyang Fleet sailors, reportedly intoxicated, clashed with Japanese locals in Nagasaki, causing 57 deaths and injuries. The incident inflamed Japanese nationalist sentiment, led to reparations, and aided Japanese code‑breaking, though Wikipedia’s account lacks citations.', 'bias_comment': 'Japanese narratives often highlight the incident as evidence of Qing lawlessness, supporting nationalist agendas, while Chinese accounts may stress humiliation and aggression. The English article’s uncited claims risk overstating strategic impacts like code‑breaking. Cross‑language versions differ: Japanese sources stress anti‑Qing sentiment, Chinese sources emphasize diplomatic grievances, and Western sources may focus on the diplomatic resolution.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagasaki_incident', 'key_points': ['**A drunken, disorderly landing of roughly\\u202f500 Beiyang‑Fleet sailors sparked violent clashes in Nagasaki (13‑15\\u202fAugust\\u202f1886), leaving 5