rted by destroyers **HMS\\u202fIcarus**, **HMS\\u202fTartar**, and armed trawlers; **all arrived safely** despite the harsh Arctic conditions.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Convoy QP 13', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_QP_13'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_QP_13', 'name': 'Convoy QP 13', 'years': '1942', 'short_description': 'Convoy QP 13 was a WWII Allied Arctic convoy that sailed westbound from Arkhangelsk on 26\\u202fJune\\u202f1942, encountered fog and struck a British minefield on 5\\u202fJuly, resulting in the loss of several merchant ships and heavy casualties before reaching Reykjavík on 7\\u202fJuly.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Voyage'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Convoy QP\\u202f13 left Arkhangelsk on 26\\u202fJune\\u202f1942, heading west. On 5\\u202fJuly, seven ships entered a British minefield, causing losses and casualties. The convoy arrived in Reykjavík on 7\\u202fJuly, with most vessels completing the journey.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article highlights Allied logistical challenges and the tragic friendly‑fire mine incident, offering limited Soviet or German perspectives. Russian sources may emphasize Soviet escort contributions and navigation expertise, while German texts could focus on U‑boat strategy and why they ignored the convoy, leading to differing narratives of responsibility and significance.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convoy_QP_13', 'key_points': ['**Westbound Arctic convoy under difficult conditions** – Convoy\\u202fQP\\u202f13 left Arkhangelsk on\\u202f26\\u202fJune\\u202f1942, sailing back to the UK/Iceland while the heavily‑escorted eastbound convoy\\u202fPQ\\u202f17 set out simultaneously, highlighting the Allies’ “dual‑track” effort to keep the supply line open despite the extreme Arctic weather and enemy threat.', '**Large, mixed‑nationality escort and merchant force** – The convoy c