ent': 'The English article highlights heroic bravery, elite education, and family legacy, reflecting a traditional British military narrative that can glorify imperial campaigns and downplay colonial impacts. Other language versions might reduce emphasis on aristocratic background or critique the imperial context, showing cross‑cultural variation.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Borton', 'key_points': ['**Highly decorated war hero** – Lieutenant‑Colonel Arthur Drummond Borton (1883‑1933) earned the Victoria Cross for conspicuous bravery during the 1917 Palestine campaign, where he repeatedly exposed himself to heavy fire to lead assaults and capture enemy guns.', '**A career of relentless service** – Despite being declared medically unfit after a severe crash as a Royal Flying Corps observer, Borton re‑joined the fight, serving in the Army, Royal Naval Volunteer Reserve (including Gallipoli armoured‑car duties), and the London Regiment, fighting in the Second Boer War, WWI and the Russian Civil War.', '**Elite upbringing and family legacy** – Educated at Eton and Sandhurst, he came from a distinguished military family (grandfather General Sir Arthur Borton, brother Air Vice‑Marshal Amyas Borton) and continued that tradition with numerous high‑level honours (VC, CMG, DSO, Order of the Nile, Order of St\\u202fVladimir).', '**Enduring remembrance** – Borton died at 49 in Southwold; his Victoria Cross is displayed at the Queen’s Royal Surrey Regiment Museum, and his grave lies in Hunton, Kent, marking a lasting tribute to his gallantry and leadership.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Lincoln Battalion', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Battalion'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Battalion', 'name': 'Lincoln Battalion', 'years': '1936–1938', 'short_description': 'The Lincoln Battalion was a U.S. volunteer unit of the International Brigades that fought for the Spanish Republic during the Spanish Civil War, seeing action from 1936 until its disbandment in 1938.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#History'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Lincoln Battalion was a unit of roughly 3,000 American volunteers who fought for the Republican side in the Spanish Civil War (1936‑1938), suffering heavy casualties, notably at Jarama, and later faced U.S. government scrutiny during the Red Scare.', 'bias_comment': 'English articles stress anti‑fascist zeal and later U.S. anti‑communist persecution, portraying the battalion as heroic yet vilified. Spanish pages foreground international solidarity and the broader Spanish‑civil‑war context, downplaying U.S. repression. French sources highlight Communist International organization and ideological framing. Chinese entries are brief, focusing on the anti‑fascist aspect without detailed U.S. political fallout. These variations reflect each language’s national historiographical priorities and political sensitivities.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Battalion', 'key_points': ['**A politically‑driven, anti‑fascist volunteer force:** The Lincoln Battalion was recruited and organized by the Communist International as part of the International Brigades, deliberately invoking Abraham Lincoln’s name to present the American volunteers as defenders of progressive democracy against Franco’s Nazi‑backed Nationalists.', '**Heavy combat losses and integrated composition:** Roughly 3,000 U.S. volunteers (including a notable number of Black soldiers) fought in Spain, suffering a 22‑½\\u202f% casualty rate—most famously at the Battle of Jarama, where two‑thirds of the unit were wiped out.', '**Post‑war persecution and Red‑Scare legacy:** After WWII, former members were labeled subversive; the U.S. government barred them from military promotion, placed the veterans on the Attorney General’s List of Subversive Organ