gal status, working‑class composition, and post‑war neglect, reflecting a narrative of marginalisation. French versions tend to stress heroic anti‑fascist sacrifice, while Spanish accounts embed the unit within broader international solidarity. These variations stem from national memory politics and ideological framing, potentially shaping readers’ perception of legitimacy and moral valence.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mackenzie%E2%80%93Papineau_Battalion', 'key_points': ['**A brave but outlawed force** – The Mackenzie‑Papineau Battalion was a Canadian‑led unit of the International Brigades that fought for the Spanish Republic (1937‑38); volunteers had to travel secretly after the Canadian Foreign Enlistment Act made foreign combat illegal.', '**Predominantly working‑class, left‑leaning volunteers** – Most members were labourers and Communists (many of immigrant origin), distinguishing them from the more student‑intellectual contingents from Britain and the U.S.; about half the battalion were Canadians, the rest Americans.', '**Heavy combat and high cost** – The battalion saw action in major battles (Jarama, Brunete, Teruel, Aragon, the Ebro) and suffered severe losses – 721 dead out of 1,546 Canadians who served. Survivors faced RCMP scrutiny, denial of veterans’ benefits, and difficulty returning home.', '**Long‑standing official neglect** – Because the Canadian government never recognised the volunteers, they are absent from national war memorials, the Books of Remembrance, and Remembrance Day ceremonies, and receive no state pensions.', '**Gradual rehabilitation through grassroots memorials** – Since the 1990s, veterans’ groups have secured three monuments (Toronto\\u202f1995, Victoria\\u202f2000, Ottawa\\u202f2001) and official historic‑event status, acknowledging the battalion’s contribution and inscribing the names of all 1,546 volunteers.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Yankee Squadron', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yankee_Squadron'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yankey_Squadron', 'name': 'Yankee Squadron', 'years': '1936–1937', 'short_description': 'A group of American mercenary aviators who fought for the Spanish Republican Air Force during the Spanish Civil War.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph (introductory summary)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In late 1936 the Spanish Republic contracted six U.S. aviators, known as the Yankee Squadron, to fly for its air force. They were equipped with outdated aircraft, faced training shortfalls, and left Spain by early 1937 after disputes over pay and conditions.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources often stress the 'mercenary' label and the squadron's hardships, potentially under‑representing Spanish administrative challenges or broader foreign‑volunteer contexts. Spanish‑language accounts may highlight different motivations and political nuances, while other national narratives could downplay the negative aspects of the pilots' experience.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yankee_Squadron', 'key_points': ['**A small group of American “mercenary” pilots** – six U.S. aviators were hired in late\\u202f1936 by the Spanish Republic to fight for the Loyalist air force, famously arriving on the ocean liner\\u202fSS\\u202fNormandie and being paid $1,500 a month plus bonuses for enemy aircraft destroyed.', '**They were thrust into combat with inadequate, unsafe equipment** – unlike the better‑trained British and French volunteers, the Americans received dilapidated sports planes (often Soviet‑built Polikarpov I‑15/I‑16) and were expected to fly immediately; one test flight even saw a wing break off, underscoring the danger.', '**Harsh treatment and broken promises spurred their quick departure** – the pilots protested the lack of training, the low‑quality aircraft, and the failure t