mmercial and Industrial Bank continued to exist despite the Bolshevik takeover, rejecting the view that nationalisation automatically extinguished the corporation.', '**Branch‑manager’s authority remained effective** – The Court ruled that the London branch manager’s power of attorney was not invalidated by the Soviet decree; the authority persisted until the bank was officially recognised as dissolved, which never happened.', '**Estoppel bars the French bank’s defence** – Because the French bank had already accepted payments and acted on the branch’s instructions, it was estopped from denying the bank’s authority or claiming a lack of title to the bonds.', '**The appeal overturned the Court of Appeal’s majority** – The earlier decision that the French bank could keep the bonds was reversed; the House of Lords granted judgment for the Russian bank, affirming its right to recover the securities.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'List of Anglo-Indian wars', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Anglo-Indian_wars'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Anglo-Indian_wars', 'name': 'List of Anglo-Indian wars', 'years': '1686–1946', 'short_description': 'The Anglo‑Indian wars were a series of conflicts from 1686 to 1946 between the British East India Company (and later the British Crown) and various Indian states, including the Mughal Empire, Mysore, Maratha Confederacy, Sikh Empire and others, culminating in the establishment of British colonial rule over the Indian subcontinent.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#List_of_wars'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From 1686 to 1946, a series of conflicts—starting with the Anglo‑Mughal War and ending with the 1946 Royal Indian Navy Mutiny—saw the British East India Company and later the Crown repeatedly clash with major Indian states, shaping colonial control over the subcontinent.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language sources often frame the wars as coordinated strategic campaigns, emphasizing British objectives and milestones, while Hindi, Bengali, and Tamil accounts tend to highlight indigenous resistance, economic exploitation, and nationalist narratives. Omission of smaller sieges reflects editorial choices that can downplay localized impacts, and different language editions may vary in the emphasis placed on civilian casualties and political motives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Anglo-Indian_wars', 'key_points': ['**Centuries‑long campaign:** From the Anglo‑Mughal War (1686‑90) through the Royal Indian Navy Mutiny (1946), a succession of wars—over 60\\u202fyears of fighting—pitted the British East India Company (later the Crown) against almost every major Indian polity, from the Mughal Empire and Maratha Confederacy to the Sikh and Mysore kingdoms.', '**Strategic purpose:** These conflicts were not isolated skirmishes but a coordinated effort to dismantle indigenous power structures and secure commercial, territorial, and political dominance, ultimately paving the way for full British colonial rule across the subcontinent.', '**Comprehensive but selective list:** The Wikipedia entry catalogs wars (e.g., Carnatic, Anglo‑Mysore, Anglo‑Afghan, and Anglo‑Burmese campaigns) while deliberately omitting individual sieges and battles, underscoring the focus on broader, decisive confrontations.', '**Milestones & legacy:** Notable turning points include the Battle of Plassey (1757), the Indian Rebellion of 1857, and the First World War‑era Burma campaign (1944), illustrating how military victories translated into administrative control; a footnote even highlights the first use of tanks in South Asia during the 1935 Mohmand campaign.', '**End of the era:** The final Anglo‑Indian engagements—World War\\u202fII’s Burma operations and the 1946 naval mutiny—marked the closing chapter of British military involvement just before India’s independence, signaling the collapse of the colonial war machine.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Alfred Knox', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Knox'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Knox', 'name': 'Alfred Knox', 'years': '1870-1964', 'short_description': 'British Army major‑general and Conservative MP who served as military attaché in Russia during WWI, headed the British mission in Siberia during the Russian Civil War, and represented Wycombe in Parliament from 1924 to 1945.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Political_career'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Alfred William Fortescue Knox (1870‑1964) was a British Indian Army officer who reached lieutenant‑general, served in World\\xa0I, and later represented Wycombe as a Conservative MP from 1924 to 1945. He wrote memoirs about his wartime experiences and was knighted.', 'bias_comment': 'The Arabic article focuses on his parliamentary service and honors in a neutral tone, while the English article adds emphasis on his anti‑communist activities, imperialist statements, and literary mentions, showing differing editorial priorities across languages.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Knox', 'key_points': ['**British Conservative MP (1924‑1945)** – Alfred William\\u202fFortescue\\u202fKnox served continuously as the MP for the Wicome constituency from 1924 through the 1935 election, holding the seat through four parliamentary terms.', '**Military background & WW\\u202fI veteran** – Before entering politics, Knox rose to the rank of **Lieutenant‑General in the British Indian Army**, fighting in the First World War and earning the prestigious **St\\u202fMichael and St\\u202fGeorge Order** (1919).', '**Long‑lived public figure** – Born in **30\\u202fOctober\\u202f1870** in Ulster (then part of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland) and dying on **9\\u202fMarch\\u202f1964** at age\\u202f93, he witnessed the transition from the imperial era to post‑war Britain.', '**Conservative Party stalwart** – Throughout his parliamentary career he was a loyal member of the **Conservative (Presbyterian) Party**, consistently supporting its policies and representing the party’s interests in the House of Commons.', '**Recognised by multiple authority databases** – His identity is catalogued in international authority files (ISNI, VIAF, GND, LCNAF, etc.), underscoring his historical significance as a notable British politician and military officer.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Knox', 'key_points': ['**Hard‑line anti‑communist** – Knox spent the World War\\u202fI and Russian Civil War years as a Russian‑language liaison to the Imperial army, witnessed the Bolshevik seizure of the Winter Palace, and later headed the British mission in Siberia; his post‑war political work was dominated by warnings about Soviet threats and support for anti‑communist causes (e.g., lobbying for aid to Finland in 1939).', '**British‑Indian imperialist** – As a Conservative MP (1924‑1945) he publicly rejected Indian self‑government, arguing that a racially and culturally “diverse” India “is not ready for democracy,” reflecting the typical colonial‑ist stance of the era.', '**Military‑political career** – Knox rose from a Sandhurst‑trained officer to Major‑General, serving in the Indian Army, the Russian front, and the British Mission to Admiral Kolchak; despite his rank, he was largely sidelined by Kolchak and never exercised significant command.', '**Public‑author and cultural figure** – He published memoirs “With the Russian Army: 1914‑1917,” contributed first‑hand accounts of the 1917 Bolshevik coup, and later appears as a “troublesome attache” in Solzhenitsyn’s novel, cementing his role as a primary source on early‑20th‑century Anglo‑Russian relations.', '**Long‑lived establishment figure** – Served as MP for Wycombe for 21\\u202fyears, earning a knighthood, and remained a steadfast Conservative, championing British re‑armament, opposing communism, and upholding the imperialist status‑quo until his death at 93.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Jarama', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Jarama'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Jarama', 'name': 'Battle of Jarama', 'years': '1937', 'short_description': 'The Battle of Jarama (6–27 February 1937) was a Nationalist offensive during the Spanish Civil War aimed at breaking Republican defenses along the Jarama River east of Madrid and cutting the Madrid–Valencia road. Nationalist forces, including Army of Africa troops, German Condor Legion and Italian units, temporarily crossed the river but failed to achieve a breakthrough; the fighting was indecisive and resulted in heavy casualties on both sides.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In February 1937, Nationalist forces launched an offensive near the Jarama River aiming to cut the Madrid‑Valencia road. Intense fighting produced heavy casualties on both sides, with the front lines stabilising into trench warfare and no decisive territorial gain.', 'bias_comment': \"English‑language accounts often highlight the International Brigades and foreign aid, framing the battle as a proxy clash, which can underplay Spanish nationalist narratives that stress internal strategy and Franco's leadership. Spanish sources may stress Nationalist heroism, while Russian or Soviet‑era texts amplify Soviet support, creating divergent emphases across languages.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Jarama', 'key_points': ['**A bloody stalemate:** \\u202fThe February\\u202f1937 Jarama offensive failed to achieve Franco’s strategic aim of cutting the Madrid‑Valencia road; both sides ended up entrenched in a trench‑war pattern, each suffering 6\\u202f000–25\\u202f000 casualties and gaining no decisive breakthrough.', '**Nationalist firepower vs. Republican tenacity:** \\u202fFranco’s forces, bolstered by the Army of Africa, German‑Condor Legion artillery and tanks, seized the western bank and crossed the river, but ferocious Republican resistance—especially from veteran International Brigade units (British, American, Irish, Balkan battalions) and seasoned commanders such as Líster and “El\\u202fCampesino”—prevented any further exploitation.', '**International volunteers paid the price:** \\u202fThe battle became infamous for its “Suicide Hill” and “Suicide Day” assaults, where the Lincoln Brigade lost 66\\u202f% of its men and the British Battalion lost most of its officers, underscoring the lethal nature of the fighting and the high human cost borne by foreign volunteers.', '**Foreign aid on both sides:** \\u202fWhile German aircraft, 88\\u202fmm guns and Italian SM.81 bombers amplified Nationalist firepower, the Republicans initially held air supremacy with Soviet‑supplied planes before being challenged by the arriving Axis air units, illustrating the broader proxy dimension of the conflict.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Гражданская война в России', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%A0%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B8'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%A0%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B8', 'name': 'Гражданская война в России', 'years': '1917‑1922', 'short_description': 'Многосторонний вооружённый конфликт в России после Октябрьской революции 1917\\u202fг., в котором Красная армия (большевики) сражалась против Белого движения, национальных и интервентских сил; закончился победой большевиков и образованием СССР.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Russian Civil War (1917‑1922) was a multi‑factional conflict involving Bolshevik Red forces, anti‑Bolshevik Whites, nationalist groups, and foreign interventions, resulting in Bolshevik victory, the creation of the USSR, and massive human and territorial losses.', 'bias_comment': \"Russian sources tend to emphasize internal class struggle and Soviet legitimacy, downplaying foreign intervention, while English sources highlight external involvement and Bolshevik repression. Both narratives reflect their historiographical traditions, affecting the portrayal of casualty figures, the war's duration, and the characterization of opposing factions.\", 'languages_used': ['ru', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%A0%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B8', 'key_points': ['**A multi‑layered conflict:** The Russian Civil War is portrayed as a sprawling, “civil” struggle that combined the Bolsheiks’ fight to keep Soviet power, the White movement’s reactionary “restoration” effort, numerous nationalist and separatist uprisings, and a massive foreign‑intervention campaign by the Allied and Entente powers.', '**Bolshevik triumph and Soviet birth:** Despite the chaotic front‑lines and the involvement of dozens of armies, the Red forces ultimately defeated the Whites and the interveners, leading to the establishment of the USSR (1922) and the incorporation of the former Russian Empire’s territories under Soviet rule.', '**Human cost and social upheaval:** The war caused staggering casualties—estimates range from 10\\u202fto 17\\u202fmillion dead, with military losses accounting for only a fraction—while also triggering land redistribution, the dissolution of the old aristocracy, and the emergence of new national states (Finland, Poland, the Baltic states, etc.).', '**Contested chronology and interpretation:** Historiography still debates the war’s