vy deliberately “bumped” the ships to force them back to international waters.', '**Physical clash but limited damage:** Soviet frigates\\u202fBezzavetny\\u202fand\\u202fSKR‑6\\u202fmade contact with the U.S. vessels—Yorktown suffered minor hull and Harpoon‑missile‑canister damage, Caron only a superficial paint scrape, and the Soviet frigate lost its starboard anchor. No casualties occurred.', '**Legal dispute and propaganda:** The United States argued that international law (the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea) does not permit a coastal state to limit war‑ship passage to designated sea lanes, whereas the USSR claimed its 1983 “Rules of Navigation” allowed only restricted lanes. Soviet officials blamed U.S. ships for dangerous manoeuvres, while the U.S. condemned the Soviet use of force.', '**Diplomatic resolution and lasting impact:** The incident precipitated the **U.S./U.S.S.R. Joint Statement on Uniform Acceptance of Rules Governing Innocent Passage**, effectively normalising the right of war‑ships to transit Soviet territorial waters and easing a flashpoint in U.S.–Soviet naval relations.', '**Strategic message:** The bumping episode underscored both powers’ willingness to test the limits of maritime law during the late‑Cold‑War period, reinforcing the United States’ “freedom‑of‑navigation” policy while forcing the Soviet Union to clarify—and eventually moderate—its restrictive maritime stance.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Voskhod 2', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voskhod_2'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voskhod_2', 'name': 'Voskhod 2', 'years': '1965', 'short_description': 'Voskhod 2 was a Soviet two‑person crewed mission launched on 18\\u202fMarch\\u202f1965; cosmonauts Pavel Belyayev and Alexei Leonov performed the first human spacewalk, a 12‑minute EVA, before landing on 19\\u202fMarch\\u202f1965.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Voskhod_2'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Voskhod\\u202f2, launched 18\\u202fMarch\\u202f1965, performed the first human spacewalk by Alexei\\u202fLeonov, who spent 12\\u202fminutes outside using an inflatable airlock and the Berkut suit; the mission faced severe suit and spacecraft malfunctions, a mis‑directed landing, and ended with the crew’s cold‑weather rescue.', 'bias_comment': 'The English source foregrounds technical achievement and lasting legacy, portraying Soviet ingenuity, while the French article stresses Cold‑War propaganda, the pressure for spectacular firsts, and the program’s eventual cancellation. These emphases reflect national narratives that can over‑emphasize pride or criticism, potentially down‑playing comparable aspects present in the other language.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voskhod_2', 'key_points': ['**Historic milestone:** Voskhod\\u202f2 achieved the world’s first human spacewalk when Alexei\\u202fLeonov ventured outside the capsule for a 12‑minute EVA, showcasing Soviet ingenuity with the inflatable “Volga” airlock and the Berkut spacesuit.', '**Technical perils:** The EVA exposed severe suit‑ballooning, forcing Leonov to dangerously lower suit pressure to re‑enter, while the mission suffered a cascade of malfunctions—including hatch‑sealing issues, a failed automatic landing system, and a mis‑firing orbital module that jeopardized crew safety.', '**Harsh recovery:** After a chaotic re‑entry, the capsule landed 386\\u202fkm off‑target in the dense Perm forest, leaving the cosmonauts to endure sub‑zero temperatures, limited supplies, and a makeshift rescue that required overnight shelter and a survival pistol before helicopter extraction.', '**Enduring legacy:** Despite its troubles, Voskhod\\u202f2 cemented its place in space history; the spacecraft is displayed at the RKK\\u202fEnergiya museum, and the mission has been commemorated in stamps, films, television, music, and even inspired an inflatable airlock in the “Kerbal Space Program” game.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voskhod_2', 'key_points': ['\\u202f**Premier «\\u202fmarché\\u202f» de l’homme dans l’espace** – le 18\\u202fmars\\u202f1965, le cosmonaute Alexeï\\u202fLeonov réalise la toute première sortie extravéhiculaire, une prouesse de 12\\u202fminutes qui devient le symbole phare du programme\\u202fVoskhod.', '\\u202f**Course à l’espace et propagande soviétique** – la mission est lancée sous la pression de la Guerre froide\\u202f; le gouvernement pousse les cosmonautes à des «\\u202fpremières\\u202f» spectaculaires pour afficher la supériorité du système soviétique, tout en cachant les défaillances et les risques.', '\\u202f**Technologie improvisée et nombreux incidents** – le vaisseau est équipé d’un sas gonflable Volga et de la combinaison Berkut, mais Leonov lutte pour réintégrer le module, la cabine perd de l’air, et la rentrée atmosphérique nécessite un redémarrage manuel du système d’orientation, entraînant des écarts de trajectoire et une décélération brutale.', '\\u202f**Retour chaotique et survie en taïga** – l’atterrissage se fait dans une forêt enneigée des Monts Oural, à plus de 300\\u202fkm du site prévu; l’équipage doit attendre plusieurs heures, puis passer une nuit supplémentaire avant d’être secouru, soulignant les conditions extrêmes auxquelles ils ont été exposés.', '\\u202f**Fin du programme\\u202fVoskhod et transition vers Soyouz** – malgré le succès historique, les limites du\\u202fVoskhod et les nombreux problèmes techniques conduisent à l’abandon du programme l’année suivante, ouvrant la voie au programme\\u202fSoyouz qui prendra le relais des vols habités soviétiques.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Talk:United Nations Security Council Resolution 502 - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_502'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:United_Nations_Security_Council_Resolution_502', 'name': 'United Na