ment influence on Eastern‑European scholarship and wartime policy** – Chaired the council of the School of Slavonic and East European Studies (1943‑45) and acted as a Foreign Office censorship adviser (1944‑45).', '**Accidental death after a fall** – In October\\u202f1956, a trip over a curb in Chelsea led to a fractured femur, pneumonia, and his death nine days later.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'David Kelly (diplomat)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Victor_Kelly'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Victor_Kelly', 'name': 'David Kelly (diplomat)', 'years': '1949–1951', 'short_description': \"Sir David Victor Kelly was the United Kingdom's Ambassador to the Soviet Union, serving in Moscow from 1949 until 1951.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Career'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Sir David Victor Kelly (1891–1959) was a British diplomat who served as Minister to Switzerland (1940‑42) and ambassador to Argentina, Turkey, and the Soviet Union (1942‑51), retiring in 1951 after a career spanning three decades.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article highlights his military award, knighthood, family background, literary works, and film portrayal, presenting a more celebratory narrative. The Russian article focuses on his education, diplomatic postings, and retirement, offering a concise factual tone with less emphasis on personal honors and cultural legacy, reflecting differing national editorial priorities.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Victor_Kelly', 'key_points': ['**Distinguished diplomatic career:** Sir\\u202fDavid Victor\\u202fKelly (1891‑1959) served as Britain’s Minister to Switzerland (1940‑42) and as Ambassador to Argentina, Turkey and the Soviet Union (1942‑51), playing a key diplomatic role during and after World\\u202fII.', '**Highly honoured and decorated:** He earned the Military Cross for frontline service in WWI, 