', '**Член Тайного совета Великобритании** с 1939\\u202fгода, получивший высшие ордена (Кавалер, Командор и затем Рыцарь Великого Креста ордена Святого Михаила и Георгия).', '**Скептичен к советским чисткам**\\u202f— находясь в Москве во время репрессий 1937\\u202fгода, докладывал в Форин‑офис, что признания Радека и Пятакова получены под давлением и являются недостоверными.', '**Отставка и личная жизнь**\\u202f— после 1939\\u202fгода ушёл в отставку, провёл остаток жизни в семейном имении в Мейдстоне, был женат на Эми Дженнингс (с 1903) и имел двух сыновей, один из которых умер в 1940\\u202fгоду.', '**Наследие**\\u202f— известен как один из последних британских послов в СССР до начала Второй мировой войны, а также как представитель традиционного британского дипломатического корпуса, оставивший заметный след в истории англо‑российских отношений.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Beira Patrol', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beira_Patrol'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beira_Patrol', 'name': 'Beira Patrol', 'years': '1 March 1966 – 25 June 1975', 'short_description': \"A Royal Navy maritime blockade (1\\u202fMar\\u202f1966–25\\u202fJun\\u202f1975) that intercepted oil tankers bound for Beira, Mozambique, to enforce UN sanctions against Rhodesia's illegal oil imports.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Blockade'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The Beira Patrol (1966–1975) was a British‑led naval operation enforcing UN oil sanctions on Rhodesia after its UDI. Limited engagement rules and territorial restrictions limited effectiveness, though later UN resolutions expanded the navy's authority to use force.\", 'bias_comment': 'The English entry frames the patrol as a legal, British‑led effort to enforce UN sanctions, emphasizing procedural limitations and modest effectiveness. Non‑English versions (e.g., Portuguese, African languages) may emphasize colonial aggression, Portuguese sovereignty, or anti‑colonial resistance, highlighting different political motivations and local impacts, which can affect perceived legitimacy.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beira_Patrol', 'key_points': ['**A British‑led naval blockade aimed at enforcing UN oil sanctions on Rhodesia (1966‑1975).**', 'The Royal Navy’s “Beira Patrol” was created after Rhodesia’s Unilateral Declaration of Independence and UN Security Council Resolution\\u202f217, to stop tankers from delivering oil via the port of Beira in Portuguese‑controlled Mozambique.', '**Limited authority and restrictive rules of engagement hampered enforcement.**', 'Initially warships could only shadow and question vessels, needing the flag state’s permission before forcing a diversion; even then they could only fire warning shots and could not enter Portuguese territorial waters. Subsequent UN Resolution\\u202f221 granted the Royal Navy exclusive use of force at Beira, but still left many loopholes that allowed oil shipments to reach Rhodesia through other ports or by rerouting.', '**Operational scale was sizable but effectiveness was modest.**']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Evgenevka incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evgenevka_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evgenevka_incident', 'name': 'Evgenevka incident', 'years': '1919', 'short_description': 'An armed standoff in August 1919 at Evgenevka, Siberia, between the U.S. 27th Infantry Regiment and Japanese forces during the Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War, triggered by a confrontation over a Japanese soldier entering a guarded area.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Incident'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In August 1919, U.S. troops guarding a Trans‑Siberian Railway station clashed with a larger Japanese force after a sentry and a Japanese soldier fought, prompting a brief standoff that left one Japanese soldier wounded and no American casualties.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry frames the incident as a tense Allied standoff, highlighting U.S. resolve and Japanese aggression. The Russian entry, describing a different 1919 uprising, stresses anti‑Soviet rebellion and Soviet repression. These differing national narratives affect terminology, focus, and perceived responsibility.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'ru'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evgenevka_incident', 'key_points': ['**A tiny but tense flashpoint in the chaotic Allied intervention:**\\u202fThe Evgonevka incident was a brief, localized standoff between a 250‑man U.S. 27th Infantry detachment and a far larger Japanese force (≈4,500 troops) on the Trans‑Siberian Railway in August\\u202f1919.', '**Rooted in a simple misunderstanding that escalated:**\\u202fA U.S. sentry (Priv. “Little”\\u202fSmith) blocked a Japanese soldier from entering the American‑guarded section of the station; the Japanese man struck the sentry, prompting the sentry to bayonet‑wound him after his rifle jammed.', '**U.S. forces showed resolve while Japanese aggression was highlighted:**\\u202fWhen the Japanese tried to seize the wounded American, Major\\u202fFitzhugh\\u202fLee\\u202fAllderdice marched his entire regiment to the station, confronting a Japanese captain and demanding the release of Smith—ultimately securing his freedom after the Americans agreed to salute the Japanese officers.', '**Minimal blood‑shed, but the episode underscored broader frictions:**\\u202fOnly one Japanese soldier was wounded; no American casualties were recorded. The incident illustrated the volatile mix of competing Allied powers in Siberia and foreshadowed later near‑clashes (e.g., the “October\\u202f1” episode) as the Russian Civil War drew foreign forces into a precarious, multi‑national power struggle.']}, {'language': 'ru', 'url': 'https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8C%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%B2%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5', 'key_points': ['**Major anti‑Soviet outbreak** – The Grigoriev uprising (7‑31\\u202fMay\\u202f1919) is presented as the largest peasant‑led revolt against Soviet power in Ukraine, sparked by the “red terror,” forced grain requisitions (prodrazvyorstka) and other harsh war‑communist measures.', '**Rapid spread and brutal violence** – Under the charismatic “ataman” N.\\u202fGrigoriev, formerly a Red‑Army commander, thousands of soldiers and local militias seized dozens of towns (Kremenchug, Kyiv, Kherson, etc.). The rebellion was accompanied by widespread pogroms, executions of Bolsheviks and mass looting, highlighting its chaotic, quasi‑bandit character.', '**Soviet counter‑offensive and repression** – In response, the RSFSR dispatched ~30\\u202f000 troops under Voroshilov and Parchomenko, crushing the revolt by the end of May. The Soviet authorities then intensified terror, executed rebel leaders, purged the Ukrainian Soviet government, and reorganised the army to eliminate “ataman”‑based forces.', '**Political destabilisation of the Ukrainian SSR** – The uprising exposed the fragility of Bolshevik control, contributing to a broader crisis on the Southern Front of the Civil War, undermining plans for intervention in Romania and Hungary, and a shift in Soviet policy (inclusion of Borotbists, removal of key commanders).', '**Critical view of Bolshevik policies** – The article emphasizes the role of Soviet exploitation—grain monopolies, corruption, anti‑Semitic propaganda, and “commissar‑state” governance—in fueling popular rage, portraying the revolt as a reaction to an illegitimate, oppressive regime rather than a purely nationalist or anarchist movement.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'SS Charlotte Cords', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Nikolay_Bauman'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Nikolay_Bauman', 'name': 'SS Charlotte Cords', 'years': '1923-1964', 'short_description': 'Charlotte Cords was a German 