go of radioactive material, making it a notable example of Soviet-era disposal of nuclear waste at sea.', '**Legacy reflects the complex post‑war maritime legacy:** The vessel’s multiple renamings, international incidents, and final use for hazardous waste disposal underscore how merchant ships were repurposed amid shifting political, economic, and environmental priorities after World\\u202fII.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Category:Ships sunk by Soviet submarines - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Ships_sunk_by_Soviet_submarines'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Ships_sunk_by_Soviet_submarines', 'name': 'Ships sunk by Soviet submarines', 'years': '1917-1945', 'short_description': 'Soviet submarines sank a variety of enemy and neutral vessels, including merchant ships and warships, from World War I through the end of World War II (1917–1945).', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Category overview page listing ships'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Wikipedia category lists 15 ships and a sub‑category of four German U‑boats sunk by Soviet submarines from 1917‑1945, including merchant, passenger, and military vessels, some with high civilian casualties, and notes possible incompleteness.', 'bias_comment': 'The English list emphasizes high‑profile civilian disasters, possibly reflecting Western interest in humanitarian loss, while lesser‑known vessels receive brief mentions. Other language wikis may include additional regional ships, use different naming conventions, or downplay Soviet actions, illustrating how national narratives and source availability shape coverage.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Ships_sunk_by_Soviet_submarines', 'key_points': ['**A small, focused category:**\\u202fOnly\\u202f15 individual ship pages are currently listed under “Ships sunk by Soviet submarines,” plus a single sub‑category devoted to 