, illegally copied classified documents and attempted to pass them to the Soviet KGB in 1984. Convicted under the Official Secrets Act, he was sentenced to 23 years, served 14, and died in 2018.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources stress political motives and security lapses, while French and German entries highlight personal desperation and ideological ambiguity. Cold‑War narratives and national security perspectives can colour portrayals, leading to differing emphasis on Bettaney's fascist sympathies, his personal crises, and the institutional failures that enabled his espionage.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Bettany', 'key_points': ['**MI5 officer turned Soviet spy:** Michael\\u202fJohn\\u202fBettaney (also known as Michael\\u202fMalkin) worked in MI5’s counter‑espionage and Soviet‑focus units before illegally copying classified papers and attempting to hand them to the KGB’s London rezident, resulting in his 1984 conviction under the Official Secrets Act.', '**Questionable background and motivation:**  Sources claim Bettaney harboured extremist fascist sympathies at university, and later cited “political motives” for his treason; however, investigators and scholars argue his betrayal was more likely driven by personal desperation—impending dismissal after a string of minor offences and a fear of exposure.', '**Major security lapses at MI5:**  A Security Commission report sharply criticised MI5’s handling of Bettaney’s career, noting repeated managerial failures that allowed him unsupervised access to sensitive material and ultimately enabled his espionage.', '**Consequences and later life:**  Sentenced to 23\\u202fyears, he served 14 before release on licence in 1998; while incarcerated he learned Russian, married a pro‑socialist woman, and maintained a conflicted Roman‑Catholic/Marxist outlook.  He died of alcohol poisoning in 2018.', '**Legacy:**  Bettaney’s case remains a caution