ations, fueled anti‑Western sentiment in China and Serbia, and left a lingering “toxic legacy” that shaped subsequent diplomatic interactions and public memory of the Kosovo War.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of the Twin Tunnels', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Twin_Tunnels'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Twin_Tunnels', 'name': 'Battle of the Twin Tunnels', 'years': '1951', 'short_description': 'The Battle of the Twin Tunnels, fought on 1 February 1951 near Jipye‑ri in South Korea, saw U.S. 23rd Infantry and French Battalion assault Chinese‑PVA forces occupying the Twin Tunnels. After intense close‑in fighting with artillery and over eighty air strikes, UN forces forced the PVA to withdraw, counting 1,300 enemy bodies (estimated 3,600 casualties) while sustaining 45 killed and 207 wounded, resulting in a UN victory.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Battle'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Battle of the Twin Tunnels (20–29 February 1951) was a Korean War engagement near Chipyong‑ni where UN forces, mainly the US 23rd Infantry Regiment with French support, defeated successive Chinese attacks, sustaining 45 killed and 207 wounded while inflicting about 3,600 enemy casualties.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources stress a decisive UN victory, turning‑point status, and US‑French cooperation, often highlighting command decisions. Korean‑language accounts give greater weight to Korean troops and civilian hardships. Chinese‑language versions portray the battle as a heroic defensive effort, emphasizing PVA sacrifice. French coverage highlights the French battalion’s role. These differing emphases reflect national narratives and source availability.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Twin_Tunnels', 'key_points': ['**Decisive UN victory:**  The UN’s 23rd\\u202fInfantry Regiment (supported by a French battalion and 