was to “relieve the besieged foreign legations and protect Chinese Christians,” historians note that the campaign quickly turned into a **punitive colonial expedition**, marked by widespread looting, execution of suspected Boxers, rape, and the systematic destruction of priceless cultural heritage (e.g., the Yongle Dadian encyclopedia, palace treasures, and the Summer Palace).', '**A brutal, unequal‑power response to a popular anti‑foreign movement** – The Boxers, a largely peasant uprising opposed to foreign imperialism, were brutally suppressed; the alliance’s troops inflicted disproportionate casualties (e.g., Japanese forces suffered half of the allied losses despite comprising only a quarter of the combatants) and imposed heavy indemnities on China after the 1901 Boxer Protocol.', '**Long‑term geopolitical gains for the victors** – The invasion secured extraterritorial concessions, expanded spheres of influence (notably Russia’s control over Manchuria and Italy’s Tianjin concession), and reinforced the “scramble for China” that cemented the participating powers’ colonial footholds in the region.', '**Legacy of resentment** – The Eight‑Nation Alliance is remembered in China as a symbol of foreign aggression and cultural devastation, shaping anti‑imperial sentiment and influencing modern Chinese nationalism.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%85%AB%E5%9C%8B%E8%81%AF%E8%BB%8D', 'key_points': ['**跨國聯軍的組成與目的**：1900 年以保護在華外國僑民、宣教士及基督徒、鎮壓義和團為名，英、法、德、美、俄、日、義、奧八國共同出兵，兵力最初約 3 萬人，最終擴增至約 5 萬人，指揮權先後由英國海軍中將愛德華·霍巴特·西摩爾和德國陸軍元帥阿爾弗雷德·馮·瓦德西交接。', '**主要戰事與北京陷落**：聯軍先在天津登陸、擊退義和團與清軍，隨後於 1900 年 8 月發動北京之，經過激烈街戰與城門突圍，最終占領紫禁城，迫使慈禧太后與光緒帝逃往西安，清廷被迫與列強談判。', '**《辛丑條約》與巨額賠款**：條約要求中國支付 4.5 億兩白銀（實際以海關稅與鹽稅作為償付來源），並允許列強在北京及沿鐵路設駐軍與永久要塞，削弱清政府主權，促使地方官員自行宣布中立，加速清朝衰亡與中國近代化的被迫進程。', '**歷史評價與資料問題**：條目指出現有內容過度依賴單一來源、缺乏可靠引用，需補充多元史料；同時學術界對八國聯軍的「正義之師」與「侵略行為」仍有爭議，反映該事件在中外史觀中的高度政治敏感性與解釋分歧。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Bunker Hill (1952)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bunker_Hill_(1952)'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bunker_Hill_(1952)', 'name': 'Battle of Bunker Hill (1952)', 'years': '1952', 'short_description': \"A series of fierce engagements from 9\\u202fAugust to 30\\u202fSeptember\\u202f1952 between United Nations forces, primarily the U.S. 1st Marine Division, and Chinese People's Volunteer Army over the Bunker Hill outpost on the Jamestown Line in the Korean War, resulting in a UN victory with heavy casualties on both sides.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Battle'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In July 1952, UN forces, chiefly the U.S. 1st Marine Division, defended Hill\\u202f122 (Bunker Hill) on the Jamestown Line, repelling repeated Chinese attacks with artillery, air support, and tank fire, sustaining casualties but retaining the position until the armistice.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes UN strategic success, heroic individual actions, and high Chinese casualty figures, reflecting a Western military perspective. Chinese-language sources often portray the battle as a costly stalemate with lower enemy loss figures and focus on political motives. Korean sources highlight civilian displacement and suffering, shifting emphasis from military outcomes to humanitarian impact.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bunker_Hill_(1952)', 'key_points': ['**Strategic “linchpin”** – Bunker Hill (Hill\\u202f122) sat on the Jamestown Line’s main defensive ridge; holding it let the UN forces dominate the surrounding outposts and keep Chinese artillery from zeroing‑in on the Marine‑held sector.', '**UN forces prevailed through relentless firepower** – Repeated Marine assaults, close‑air support (napalm‑‑bomb strikes), and massive artillery and tank barrages repelled seven major Chinese attacks, ultimately keeping the hill in UN hands despite relentless PVA artillery bombardments.', '**High cost, but decisive victory** – The 1st\\u202fMarine Division suffered 48 killed and 313 seriously wounded, while Chinese casualties topped 400 dead and likely exceeded 3,200 total, underscoring the ferocity of the fighting and the UN’s ability to absorb losses while still winning.', '**Heroic individual actions shaped the outcome** – Numerous Marines earned Medals of Honor for self‑sacrifice on nearby outposts (e.g., Simanek, McLaughlin, Garcia, Benfold), illustrating the personal bravery that helped sustain the defense of Bunker Hill and its adjoining positions.', '**Long‑term impact** – Bunker Hill remained a UN‑held strongpoint until the armistice, symbolizing the broader UN resolve to hold the line along the 38th\\u202fParallel despite intense Chinese offensives and harsh weather conditions.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Export restriction', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_restriction'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_restriction', 'name': 'Export restriction', 'years': '2007-2008', 'short_description': 'During the 2007–2008 food crises, more than thirty countries imposed export restrictions—like quantitative limits, prohibitions, taxes, and price controls—to protect domestic food supply, which further elevated global food prices.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '## Impact of food crises 2008 on export restriction measures'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Export restrictions are government‑imposed limits on goods exported to protect domestic interests, security, or foreign‑policy goals. During 2007‑2008, many nations used them amid food crises, impacting prices and trade. Views differ on their economic effects and legal frameworks.', 'bias_comment': 'Arabic version emphasizes governmental motives, security concerns, and specific U.S. regulatory bodies, reflecting a policy‑centric view, while English version emphasizes economic impacts, welfare effects, and WTO/academic critique, showing a more economic‑policy perspective. These differences may reflect regional priorities and source selection bias.', 'languages_used': ['ar', 'en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'ar', 'url': 'https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_restriction', 'key_points': ['\\u202fقيود التصدير تُفرض من قبل الحكومات لتقليل نقص السلع في السوق المحلي، وحماية الصناعات الوطنية من المنافسة الخارجية، وضمان استقرار الأسعار.', '\\u202fتُستَخدم القيود كأداةٍ في السياسة الخارجية، فغالبًا ما تُدرج ضمن العقوبات التجارية لتقييد علاقات الدول المستهدفة أو لفرض ضغط دبلوماسي.', '\\u202fأحد أهم دوافعها هو منع انتشار الأسلحة والمواد ذات الاستخدام المزدوج (نقطة نفاذ للغالي الكلم) التي يمكن أن تُستَخدم في أسلحة نووية، كيميائية أو بيولوجية، أو في أنشطة إرهابية.', '\\u202fفي الولايات المتحدة تُحدَّد القواعد الإدارية للتصدير بواسطة مكتب الصناعة والأمان (BIS)، بينما تُراقب وزارة الخارجية المواد الدفاعية وفقًا لقانون التجارة الدولية في الأسلحة (ITAR).', '\\u202fالقيود تُطبق أيضًا على الدول التي يُفرض عليها حظر تجاري كامل، ما يضع إطارًا قانونيًا لمنع أي تعامل تجاري معها.']}, {'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_restriction', 'key_points': ['**Definition & aims** – Export restrictions are government‑imposed limits on the quantity or value of goods shipped abroad, used to protect food security, the environment, domestic industries, or to advance foreign‑policy goals such as sanctions or arms control.', '**Economic impact** – They often raise export prices, giving domestic firms a price advantage, but typically generate net welfare losses: consumers face higher world prices and producers lose export volume, with only large exporters able to capture higher global prices.', '**Food‑crisis example** – During the 2007‑2008 food crises, more than 30 nations imposed quantitative bans, taxes and price controls to safeguard domestic supplies; these measures instead pushed up global food prices and exacerbated the crisis.', '**Market distortion & trade retaliation** – Restrictions on natural resources, minerals or wood can create market failures, distort welfare distribution and provoke trade‑diversion or retaliation, undermining the original policy objective.', '**WTO/academic stance** – The WTO and scholars view export restrictions as “regulatory deficiencies” that distort trade and recommend limiting them to genuine security, health or environmental cases, rather than as routine economic tools.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Battle of Old Baldy', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Old_Baldy'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Old_Baldy', 'name': 'Battle of Old Baldy', 'years': '1952-1953', 'short_description': 'A series of five engagements for Hill 266 in west‑central Korea during the Korean War, fought from June\\u202f26\\u202f1952 to March\\u202f26\\u202f1953, where UN forces (US, South Korea, Colombia) fought Chinese forces for control of Old Baldy, resulting in a Korean victory in 1952 and a Chinese victory in 1953.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Battle'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'From August 1952 to March 1953, Hill\\u202f266 (Old Baldy) changed hands five times between UN forces—including U.S., South Korean, and Colombian troops—and Chinese forces, resulting in heavy casualties on both sides. The hill’s tactical position made it a focal point, but its capture did not alter the overall front line.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes the Colombian battalion’s role, framing it as a source of national pride, while Korean sources tend to focus on the broader UN‑Chinese contest and Korean casualties. Chinese narratives portray the battle as a defensive victory, minimizing UN losses. Language differences can shift attention to distinct national contributions, casualty figures, or strategic interpretation, affecting perceived significance.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Old_Baldy', 'key_points': ['**A grueling, back‑and‑forth struggle** – “Old Baldy” (Hill\\u202f266) changed hands repeatedly in five fierce engagements over ten\\u202fmonths, with both UN (U.S., South Korean, Colombian) and Chinese forces launching massive artillery barrages, hand‑to‑hand assaults, and countless counter‑attacks.', '**Heavy toll on both sides** – U.S. forces suffered\\u202f≈\\u202f307 killed and\\u202f≈\\u202f1,952 casualties; Chinese estimates run above\\u202f1,100 dead; the Colombian Battalion alone lost\\u202f≈\\u202f95 KIA,\\u202f97\\u202fWIA and\\u202f30\\u202fMIA, reflecting the battle’s high human cost.', '**Strategic and symbolic importance** – The hill dominated the surrounding terrain in three directions, so holding it was seen as crucial to protect the main line of resistance and prevent enemy penetration toward Seoul; commanders repeatedly praised the defenders’ “commendable” performance.', '**Colombian contribution highlighted** – The article devotes extensive detail to the lone Latin‑American unit’s heroic but costly defense, noting its prior decorations and the fierce fighting it endured, underscoring a national‑pride narrative.', '**Outcome: a stalemate mirroring the wider war** – Despite temporary UN victories in 1952, the Chinese recaptured the hill in early\\u202f1953 and the front line ultimately reverted to its pre‑battle positions, illustrating the broader dead‑lock of the Korean War.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of Japan–United States relations', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Japan%E2%80%93United_States_relations'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Japan%E2%80%93United_States_relations', 'name': 'Timeline of Japan–United States relations', 'years': '1791-2023', 'short_description': 'Chronological list of key diplomatic, military, and cultural events in Japan–U.S. relations from the first recorded encounter in 1791 to the present day, including treaties, wars, diplomatic missions, and notable incidents.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Introductory paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Japan‑U.S. relationship evolved from early 19th‑century forced opening, through alliance in World\\u202fI, wartime conflict in the 1940s, Cold‑War security partnership, to contemporary cooperation with disputes over bases, trade, and immigration, forming a complex but largely collaborative alliance.', 'bias_comment': 'The English version foregrounds diplomatic and security milestones, which may underplay Japanese domestic reactions, cultural exchanges, and anti‑U.S. sentiments. Other language versions may emphasize different events (e.g., Meiji modernization, anti‑base protests) and frame the partnership more critically or positively, reflecting national historiographic biases.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Japan%E2%80%93United_States_relations', 'key_points': ['**From tentative opening to formal partnership** – Early U.S. attempts (Lady\\u202fWashington, Perry’s “Black Ships”) forced Japan out of its isolationist *sakoku* policy, leading to the 1854 Convention of Kanagawa and the 1858 Harris Treaty that established regular trade and diplomatic channels.', '**20th‑century swing between alliance and antagonism** – Japan and the United States fought side‑by‑side in World\\u202fI, but the 1930s‑40s saw escalating friction (Manchurian invasion, Pearl Harbor) that erupted into full‑scale war, ending with Japan’s unconditional surrender, U.S. occupation, and the 1951‑52 San\\u202fFrancisco and Security Treaties that reshaped the relationship.', '**Cold‑War era cemented a durable strategic bond** – With the U.S. stationed in Japan (e.g., Okinawa) and mutual defense pacts, the two nations became pivotal allies against Soviet expansion, while growing economic interdependence turned Japan into a key U.S. trading partner.', '**Modern cooperation tempered by lingering frictions** – Joint actions such as the Iraq reconstruction mission, the 1985 Plaza Accord, and coordinated disaster relief (e.g., 2011 Tōhoku earthquake) underscore collaboration, yet issues like the U.S. military footprint in Okinawa, trade disputes, and immigration restrictions periodically strain the partnership.', '**Overall stance: a complex, largely positive alliance** – Despite historic wars and periodic tensions, the narrative emphasizes a long‑term, mutually beneficial relationship in security, economics, and diplomatic coordination that defines U.S.–Japan ties today.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Second Phase Offensive', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Phase_Offensive'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Phase_Offensive', 'name': 'Second Phase Offensive', 'years': '1950', 'short_description': \"The Second Phase Offensive (25\\u202fNov\\u202f–\\u202f24\\u202fDec\\u202f1950) was a major Chinese People’s Volunteer Army attack during the Korean War, striking UN forces in North Korea. It included the battles of the Ch'ongch'on River and Chosin Reservoir, forced the UN’s Eighth Army and X Corps into a retreat, and resulted in a Chinese victory that reclaimed most of North Korea.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'During November–December 1950, the Chinese People’s Volunteer Army launched a large, concealed attack that halted UN advances in North Korea, causing a forced retreat and heavy casualties, aggravated by extreme cold, and leading to a strategic stalemate that reshaped the war’s trajectory.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article highlights the surprise and scale of the Chinese offensive, emphasizing US intelligence failures, the harsh cold, and strategic stalemate. Chinese-language sources often frame the event as a decisive anti‑imperialist victory, while Korean-language accounts stress Korean casualties and the impact on Korean civilians. These linguistic emphases can shape readers’ perception of who bore the cost and who achieved strategic success.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Phase_Offensive', 'key_points': ['**Surprise Chinese offensive shattered UN confidence** – The People’s Volunteer Army launched a massive, well‑concealed attack on 25\\u202fNov\\u202f1950, catching the Eighth Army and X Corps off‑guard despite U.S. intelligence wildly under‑estimating Chinese strength (70\\u202f000 vs. the actual 300‑+\\u202f000 troops).', '**“Home‑by‑Christmas” crushed and UN forces forced into retreat** – The dual thrust at the Ch’ongch’on River and the Chosin Reservoir turned the UN’s offensive into a defensive withdrawal, ending the drive to conquer North Korea and leaving China in control of almost the entire peninsula by “big bugout” late‑December.', '**Extreme cold inflicted casualties as severe as combat** – Temperatures plummeted to –30\\u202f°C (‑22\\u202f°F); frostbite and non‑combat attrition accounted for roughly half of the Chinese losses (≈80\\u202f000 total) and a comparable share of UN casualties (≈30\\u202f000), underscoring how weather became a decisive weapon.', '**Chinese tactics relied on mobility, night attacks and deception, not “human waves”** – Compact combat groups (50‑100 men) infiltrated via the Yalu, feigned withdrawal, released false POW reports, and struck at vulnerable ROK flanks, exploiting the UN’s reliance on roads, air support and daylight operations.', '**Strategic fallout reshaped the war’s course** – The defeat jolted U.S. leadership (MacArthur’s “entirely new war” warning), curbed ambitions of a unified Korea, and cemented a stalemate that would persist for years, while China’s success fostered a belief it could defeat American forces—until later offensives were repelled.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Imperial decree of declaration of war against foreign powers', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_decree_of_declaration_of_war_against_foreign_powers'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_decree_of_declaration_of_war_against_foreign_powers', 'name': 'Imperial decree of declaration of war against foreign powers', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'In June 1900, the Qing dynasty issued a simultaneous declaration of war against eleven foreign powers, signed in the name of the Guangxu Emperor but dictated by Empress Dowager Cixi. The decree triggered the Boxer Rebellion and prompted the formation of the Eight‑Nation Alliance.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph (intro section)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"In June 1900, the Qing court, led by Empress Dowager Cixi, issued a war decree against eleven Western powers, officially supporting the Boxer movement. The proclamation, seen as a response to internal reformist threats, contributed to foreign intervention and the Qing dynasty's decline.\", 'bias_comment': \"English sources often frame the decree within a 'century of humiliation' narrative, emphasizing foreign aggression and Qing decline, whereas Chinese-language accounts may emphasize national resistance and Cixi’s decisive leadership. Western and Japanese sources sometimes downplay imperial agency, presenting the decree as a bluff. These linguistic and cultural perspectives shape interpretation of the event's significance and causality.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_decree_of_declaration_of_war_against_foreign_powers', 'key_points': ['**A desperate, top‑down declaration** – In June\\u202f1900 the Qing court, under Empress\\u202fDowager\\u202fCixi’s firm control (the Guang‑xu Emperor being under house arrest), issued a simultaneous war edict against eleven Western powers, turning the imperial seal into a symbol of last‑ditch resistance.', '**Political intrigue as the catalyst** – The decree was a reaction to the failed Hundred‑Days’ Reform plot, where reformers Kang\\u202fYouwei and Liang\\u202fQichao tried to use Yuan\\u202fShikai to overthrow the conservative Manchu establishment; Cixi’s crackdown and fear of foreign‑backed rebellion prompted the war proclamation.', '**A bluff more than a genuine war effort** – Historians note the wording of the edict was deliberately vague, functioning as a “persona non‑grata” notice meant to deter foreign powers rather than signal a realistic military campaign.', '**Direct trigger of the Boxer Rebellion and foreign intervention** – The declaration gave the anti‑foreign Boxer movement official backing, which in turn provoked the formation of the Eight‑Nation Alliance and the subsequent punitive Boxer Protocol.', '**Legacy of humiliation** – The war decree is remembered as a moment when Qing authority collapsed, accelerating the dynasty’s decline and cementing the narrative of China’s “century of humiliation” at the hands of foreign imperialism.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Betrayal from the East', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betrayal_from_the_East'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betrayal_from_the_East', 'name': 'Betrayal from the East', 'years': '1945', 'short_description': \"Betrayal from the East is a 1945 American spy drama film directed by William A. Berke, starring Lee Tracy and Nancy Kelly. Based on Alan Hynd's book about Japanese espionage in the United States, it follows an American recruited by a Japanese spy ring before the U.S. entered World War\\u202fII.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'infobox'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Betrayal from the East is a 1945 American film directed by William A. Berke, depicting a Japanese spy network recruiting an American before WWII; starring Lee Tracy, Nancy Kelly, and Richard Loo. The article is a stub with citation gaps.', 'bias_comment': \"The English entry emphasizes the film's anti‑Japanese propaganda role, while the French version focuses on wartime paranoia and propaganda. Both reflect a U.S‑centric wartime view, lacking nuanced perspectives on Japanese characters and suffering from limited scholarly sources.\", 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betrayal_from_the_East', 'key_points': ['**Wartime spy drama:** *Betrayal from the East* (1945) is presented as a classic American World\\u202fII‑era spy film that dramatizes a Japanese espionage network recruiting an American before the U.S. entered the war.', '**Propaganda over nuance:** The plot and casting—highlighting Japanese villains such as Lt.\\u202fCommander Toshio\\u202fMiyazaki—reflect the period’s anti‑Japanese sentiment and serve more as wartime propaganda than a balanced thriller.', '**Production pedigree:** Directed by William\\u202fA.\\u202fBerke and produced by RKO Radio Pictures, the movie features a notable ensemble (Lee\\u202fTracy, Nancy\\u202fKelly, Richard\\u202fLoo, Philip\\u202fAhn, Victor\\u202fSen\\u202fYung) and is based on Alan\\u202fHynd’s non‑fiction book on Japanese spies.', '**Citation gaps:** The article itself flags a lack of reliable sources, noting that it “needs additional citations for verification,” which underscores the limited scholarly documentation of the film’s historical impact.', '**Legacy as a stub:** Classified as a 1940s drama‑film stub, the entry signals that the film remains a minor, under‑researched work within the broader canon of Pacific‑War and World\\u202fWar\\u202fII cinema.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betrayal_from_the_East', 'key_points': ['**1945 American espionage thriller** – *Trahison japonaise* (“Betrayal from the East”) was directed by William A.\\u202fBerke for RKO Radio Pictures, runs 82\\u202fminutes, and was released in the United States in February/April\\u202f1945 (France in 1949).', '**Plot centered on wartime paranoia** – The story follows an ordinary American who is recruited by a Japanese spy network operating on U.S. soil, tasked with stealing information about the strategically vital Panama Canal.', '**Notable cast and crew** – Stars Lee Tracy (Eddie Carter) and Nancy\\u202fKelly (Peggy Harrison), with Richard\\u202fLoo as the Japanese lieutenant commander. The screenplay by Aubrey\\u202fWisberg adapts a novel by Alan\\u202fHynd; cinematography by Russell\\u202fMetty and music by Roy\\u202fWebb.', '**Historical context and legacy** – Produced during the final months of World\\u202fII, the film reflects contemporary US fears of Japanese infiltration and serves as an early example of Hollywood’s wartime propaganda; the Wikipedia entry remains a stub, signalling limited scholarly attention.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Timeline of the Qing dynasty', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Qing_dynasty'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Qing_dynasty', 'name': 'Timeline of the Qing dynasty', 'years': '1583-1915', 'short_description': \"Chronological listing of key events from the Jurchen rise (1583) through the Qing dynasty's rule (1644–1912) to its final collapse, covering wars, reforms, and territorial changes.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The Qing dynasty timeline (1583‑1915) traces its rise from Nurhaci's tribal consolidation, conquest of China, territorial expansion, and eventual decline marked by internal strain, limited reforms, and foreign pressures, culminating in its fall in the early 20th century.\", 'bias_comment': 'English summaries often stress military conquests and administrative reforms, while Chinese versions may emphasize ethnic integration and legitimacy narratives, and Japanese or Korean accounts might highlight regional conflicts. These linguistic perspectives can shape perceived importance of events.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Qing_dynasty', 'key_points': ['**From tribal chieftain to imperial power (1583‑1636):** Nurhaci’s consolidation of Jurchen tribes, creation of the Banner Army, and successive victories over Ming, Mongol, and Korean forces laid the military and political foundations for the Later Jin, which Hong\\u202fTaiji renamed the Qing dynasty in 1636.', '**Conquest of China and integration of peoples (1644‑1683):** After Wu\\u202fSangui opened the Great Wall, Qing forces captured Beijing and swiftly subdued the Southern Ming, imposed the Manchu queue, and extended control to Taiwan, Shandong, and Inner\\u202fMongolia, while reshaping the Eight Banners to incorporate Han, Mongol, and later “Han‑jun” units.', '**Territorial expansion and frontier wars (1650‑1750):** The Qing fought protracted campaigns against the Dzungar Khanate, Russian forts on the Amur, and rebel uprisings in Taiwan, Yunnan, and among the Miao, securing the empire’s western borders (Treaty of Nerchinsk\\u202f1689, Treaty of Kyakhta\\u202f1727) and annexing Tibet (1720).', '**Internal strain, reform attempts, and decline (1820‑1900):** Chronic poverty among the Eight Banners, costly military campaigns, and repeated rebellions (Lin\\u202fShuangwen, Miao) weakened the state; limited modernization (first steam engine\\u202f1841, Hundred‑Days’ Reform\\u202f1898) and foreign pressure (Treaty of Nanking\\u202f1842, Boxer Rebellion\\u202f1900) signaled the Qing’s gradual loss of authority.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Military history of China', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_History_of_China'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_History_of_China', 'name': 'Military history of China', 'years': '2200\\u202fBC–present', 'short_description': \"China's recorded military history spans from about 2200\\u202fBC to the present day, covering pre‑modern and modern periods, including the PLA and ROC Army.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"China's military history spans from circa 2200\\u202fBC to the present, covering ancient warfare, technological innovations, imperial campaigns, modern revolutions, and contemporary disputes, illustrating a continuous evolution of strategy, organization, and geopolitical influence.\", 'bias_comment': 'Chinese-language articles often stress a heroic, unbroken martial tradition and national pride, sometimes downplaying internal strife or foreign aggression; English-language versions tend to highlight imperial expansion, internal instability, and modern criticism, reflecting differing historiographical and political lenses across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_History_of_China', 'key_points': ['**A millennia‑long martial tradition:**\\u202fChina’s recorded military history stretches from\\u202f≈\\u202f2200\\u202fBC to today, underscoring a continuous thread of warfare that predates the 1912 republican revolution.', '**From philosophy to firepower:**\\u202fAlthough Confucian doctrine prized civil governance over brute force, Chinese armies repeatedly pioneered decisive technologies—crossbows, standardized metallurgy, early gunpowders (cannons, fire‑lances) and sophisticated logistics—while also absorbing nomadic cavalry tactics and later Western weaponry.', '**Modern metamorphosis:**\\u202fThe 20th‑century shift from a peasant guerrilla force (the nascent People’s Liberation Army, 1927) to the world’s largest standing army illustrates China’s dramatic post‑imperial military transformation, heavily influenced by Sun\\u202fTzu’s strategic legacy.', '**Republic of China’s evolving role:**\\u202fFounded as the National Revolutionary Army (1924), the ROC Army moved from attempts to reconquer the mainland to a defensive posture protecting Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen, and Matsu—now emphasizing joint C2, air‑defence, and expeditionary capabilities rather than large‑scale infantry.', '**A legacy of perpetual conflict:**\\u202fFrom ancient internal rebellions and border wars to contemporary disputes with India, Vietnam, and Taiwan, China’s military history is marked by a relentless series of wars and skirmishes that shape its current strategic outlook and geopolitical ambitions.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Export of cryptography from the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_of_cryptography_from_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_of_cryptography_from_the_United_States', 'name': 'Export of cryptography from the United States', 'years': '1954-2021', 'short_description': 'U.S. export controls on cryptography began with its inclusion in the Munitions List in 1954 and evolved through the 1990s, culminating in major relaxations in the mid‑1990s and subsequent updates through 2021.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Early restriction period (1954-1996)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The United States classified cryptographic technology as munitions from the 1950s, restricting exports. Legal challenges and commercial demand led to policy shifts, moving most encryption to the Commerce Control List in 1996. Today, civilian encryption is largely unrestricted, with stricter controls on high‑strength and military‑grade items.', 'bias_comment': 'Only the English Wikipedia entry is used, so the summary reflects the U.S.-centric perspective and terminology of that source. Translations could shift emphasis—for example, non‑English articles may stress civil liberties or highlight different legal cases—potentially altering the perceived balance between security and freedom.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Export_of_cryptography_from_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**Export‑control history:** From the 1950s Cold‑War era, U.S. cryptography was classified as “munitions” and placed on the United States Munitions List, meaning any RSA source‑code – even printed on a T‑shirt – was treated as a prohibited export weapon.', '**Free‑speech protest:** The “Munitions T‑shirt” (RSA code on fabric) was deliberately marketed as a **symbol of resistance** to the government’s encryption bans, framing source‑code as protected speech and challenging the notion that code could be treated like arms.', '**Shift to commercial‑driven policy:** Growing commercial demand (banking, e‑commerce, Netscape’s SSL) and high‑profile legal battles (e.g., *Bernstein v. United States*, Junger v. Daley) forced the U.S. to relax restrictions – first by carving out non‑military uses in the early 1990s, then by moving most encryption from the State‑Department’s Munitions List to the Commerce Control List with Executive Order\\u202f13026 in 1996.', '**Current regime:** Today, non‑military cryptography is overseen by the Department of Commerce’s Bureau of Industry & Security under the Export Administration Regulations (EAR). While most “mass‑market” encryption can be shipped freely, tighter controls still apply to high‑strength keys, military‑grade systems, and exports to “rogue” or terrorist‑supporting countries, reflecting a **still‑complex but far‑more permissive** framework.', '**Underlying tension:** The narrative repeatedly pits **national‑security concerns** against **civil liberties and economic innovation**, showing how the “crypto wars” have gradually tipped the balance toward openness while leaving legacy restrictions and geopolitical caveats in place.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Korean War order of battle', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War_order_of_battle'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War_order_of_battle', 'name': 'Korean War order of battle', 'years': '1950-1953', 'short_description': 'A comprehensive list of United Nations, Republic of Korea, and Communist forces and their subordinate units during the Korean War, 1950‑1953.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The article lists military units and support elements that fought in the Korean War, dividing forces into United Nations‑led (South Korean, US, and allied) and communist (North Korean, Chinese, and limited Soviet) sides, with details on composition, timelines, and humanitarian aid.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes UN‑led forces, listing South Korean and US units in greater detail, while the communist side receives a more aggregated treatment. Other language versions (e.g., Korean, Chinese, Russian) often highlight their own national contributions, ideological framing, or casualty figures, leading to divergent narratives about scale and significance.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War_order_of_battle', 'key_points': ['The article presents a **comprehensive, though admittedly incomplete, catalogue of every military unit and support element that fought in the Korean War**, split between the United Nations‑led “Pro‑Southern” side (South Korea, the U.S. and a broad coalition of Commonwealth, Asian, African and Latin‑American forces) and the “Pro‑Northern” side (North Korean divisions, Chinese People’s Volunteer Army groups and limited Soviet air units).', '**South Korean forces are detailed first**, listing pre‑war ROK infantry divisions and their component regiments, followed by the massive UN ground, naval and air contributions—U.S. divisions, multinational brigades, the U.S. Seventh Fleet, and air‑force commands—highlighting the war’s truly international character.', '**The North Korean and Chinese entries emphasize the scale and organization of the communist side**, naming each NKPA infantry division, Chinese army group, and the constituent divisions and artillery brigades, while noting Soviet aviation assistance only in passing.', 'A separate section underscores the **wide‑ranging medical and humanitarian aid** provided by neutral nations (e.g., Swedish Red Cross, Indian field ambulance, Danish hospital ship, Italian Red Cross, etc.), reflecting the war’s global humanitarian footprint beyond combat troops.', 'Throughout the page, **the structure stresses the layered command hierarchy and the fluidity of unit deployments over time**, with dates indicating when forces entered the conflict and brief notes on their roles, reinforcing the complexity of the Korean War’s order of battle.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Third Battle of Seoul', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Battle_of_Seoul'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Battle_of_Seoul', 'name': 'Third Battle of Seoul', 'years': '1950-1951', 'short_description': 'The Third Battle of Seoul (31\\u202fDec\\u202f1950\\u202f–\\u202f7\\u202fJan\\u202f1951) was a Chinese–North Korean offensive that broke UN defenses on the 38th Parallel, forced the evacuation of Seoul, and briefly placed the city under Communist control before UN forces later retook it.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'introductory paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Third Battle of Seoul (Dec\\u202f1950–Jan\\u202f1951) saw Chinese‑North Korean forces break the UN line, prompting a UN evacuation of Seoul on Jan\\u202f3\\u202f1951. The offensive, a Chinese “Third Phase” campaign, resulted in higher Chinese/North Korean casualties and set the stage for subsequent UN counter‑offensives.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize the UN retreat and strategic outcomes, often highlighting UN casualty figures and the subsequent UN counter‑offensive, reflecting a Western/UN perspective. Korean-language accounts may stress Korean civilian hardship and view the evacuation as a national tragedy. Chinese sources typically highlight the Chinese offensive as a strategic victory, possibly downplaying UN losses. These variations illustrate how national narratives shape the portrayal of the battle.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Battle_of_Seoul', 'key_points': ['**Chinese‑North Korean offensive forced a UN retreat** – After the PVA’s 13th Army broke through the 38th\\u202fParallel on Dec\\u202f31\\u202f1950, UN defenses collapsed and Seoul was evacuated on Jan\\u202f3\\u202f1951, marking a clear Chinese/DPRK victory.', '**Strategic gamble by Mao and Ridgway** – Mao ordered the “Third Phase Campaign” to pressure the UN into abandoning Korea, while Ridgway, newly in command of the Eighth Army, pre‑emptively prepared a “Bridgehead Line” and ultimately chose evacuation over a costly defense.', '**Rough terrain and exhausted troops tipped the balance** – Harsh winter froze the Imjin and Hantan rivers, eliminating natural obstacles, and both Chinese and Korean forces were severely depleted, but Chinese artillery and night attacks still overran poorly equipped ROK divisions.', '**Political fallout reinforced UN resolve** – Although the Chinese captured Seoul, the operation galvanized UN political support for South Korea and led to the abandonment of any evacuation plan, setting the stage for the subsequent UN counter‑offensive (Operation\\u202fRipper).', '**Casualties and losses** – UN forces suffered relatively light casualties (≈\\u202f800 total for U.S., U.K., Australia) compared with Chinese (≈\\u202f5,800) and North Korean (≈\\u202f2,700) losses, underscoring the high price of the Chinese advance despite its temporary success.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Taiyuan massacre', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiyuan_Massacre'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiyuan_Massacre', 'name': 'Taiyuan massacre', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'On 9 July 1900, during the Boxer Rebellion, Governor Yuxian ordered the execution of 44–45 foreign missionaries and Chinese Christians in Taiyuan, Shanxi, resulting in a massacre of Christians.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#The-massacre'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 9\\u202fJuly\\u202f1900, Qing governor Yuxian ordered the execution of 44 foreign missionaries and dozens of Chinese Christians in Taiyuan, Shanxi, during the Boxer Rebellion; the killings are recalled as a major tragedy in Chinese anti‑missionary history.', 'bias_comment': 'The English source frames the Taiyuan massacre as a religious tragedy, emphasizing missionary martyrdom and Western evangelical loss, reflecting a Eurocentric view. The Chinese source, mistakenly about the 1896 Yun‑lin massacre, stresses Japanese colonial brutality and anti‑imperial sentiment, showing how national narratives shape portrayals across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiyuan_Massacre', 'key_points': ['**A brutal episode of the Boxer Rebellion** – On\\u202f9\\u202fJuly\\u202f1900, Qing governor\\u202fYuxian of Shanxi ordered the execution of 44 foreign missionaries (men, women and children) and dozens of Chinese Christians in Taiyuan; total Christian deaths are usually given as 45.', '**A watershed tragedy for Christian missions** – Historians such as Nat\\u202fBrandt describe it as “the greatest single tragedy in the history of Christian evangelicalism”; the massacre included the murder of two Italian bishops (Gregory\\u202fGrassi and Francis\\u202fFogolla), who were later canonised by Pope\\u202fJohn\\u202fPaul\\u202fII as part of the 120\\u202fMartyrs of China.', '**Contested responsibility** – While many contemporary accounts blame Yuxian’s anti‑foreign policy, scholar Roger\\u202fThompson argues that mob violence, not the governor, was the immediate cause; other researchers contend that the surviving reports converge on a core narrative, leaving the event a potent symbol of Chinese anti‑missionary sentiment.', '**Enduring legacy** – The indemnity imposed on Shanxi funded the founding of Shansi Imperial University (1901) with missionary Timothy\\u202fRichard as chancellor; the slain Catholics were canonised in 2000, and the massacre remains a focal point in studies of missionary martyrdom and Sino‑Western relations.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%9B%B2%E6%9E%97%E5%A4%A7%E5%B1%A0%E6%AE%BA', 'key_points': ['**日軍對雲林的殘酷鎮壓** – 1896 年日本軍在掃蕩雲林抗日行動時，先斬殺俘虜，隨後以六百餘名義軍襲擊斗六街，導致日軍傷亡慘重，隨即展開報復性的大規模屠殺與焚燒。', '**死亡人數與規模爭議** – 官方統計約 6\\u202f000 人遇害；地方官今村平藏的《蠻煙瘴雨日誌》記載逾 1 萬人；近代學者劉枝萬則估計超過 3 萬人，顯示事件規模在史料間仍有巨大分歧。', '**國際譴責與日本政府壓力** – 事件在香港、英國等外國報紙上被廣泛報導，譴責日軍「野蠻且苛酷」的行為；國際輿論迫使日本政府向台灣總督府施壓，最終撤職松村雄之進支廳長並要求繳回勳章。', '**長遠影響與歷史記憶** – 雲林大屠殺成為台灣抗日史的重要血腥象徵，促成日治時期對地方官制與保甲制度的調整，並在後續歷史敘事中被視為日本殖民暴行的典型案例，持續影響台灣社會對日治遺留問題的認知與討論。']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Yinhe incident', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yinhe_incident'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yinhe_incident', 'name': 'Yinhe incident', 'years': '1993', 'short_description': 'In 1993 the United States accused the Chinese container ship Yinhe of carrying chemical‑weapon precursors to Iran, forcing it to remain under surveillance in the Indian Ocean for 24 days; a joint Saudi‑U.S. inspection later found no prohibited cargo.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Timeline'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In July 1993, the US intercepted the Chinese vessel Yinhe, claiming it carried chemical‑weapon precursors for Iran. After a 24‑day stand‑off, inspection found none. The incident sparked Chinese anti‑US sentiment, demands for apology, and influenced later diplomatic and navigational policies.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes U.S. intelligence motives and later speculation about Chinese disinformation, while Chinese sources highlight U.S. aggression and nationalism. This framing reflects national narratives, influencing perceived culpability and the event’s significance in each country’s discourse.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yinhe_incident', 'key_points': ['**U.S. intelligence‑driven interdiction:**\\u202fIn July\\u202f1993 the United States, citing CIA‑derived ship‑manifest data, claimed the Chinese container ship\\u202fYinhe was transporting chemical‑weapon precursors to Iran and forced the vessel into a 24‑day stand‑off in the Indian Ocean, even jamming its GPS to curb its navigation.', '**Outcome of the inspection:**\\u202fA joint Saudi‑U.S. inspection after the ship finally docked in Dammam found no prohibited chemicals on board; the U.S. maintained it acted “in good faith” on multiple sources, while some Clinton officials later speculated China might have seeded false intelligence to embarrass Washington.', '**Chinese nationalist backlash:**\\u202fBeijing demanded a public apology and compensation, and the episode fueled a surge of anti‑American sentiment and Chinese nationalism throughout the 1990s, being portrayed domestically as an illustration of U.S. hegemonic bullying and a catalyst for the development of China’s independent BeiDou navigation system.', '**Long‑term diplomatic impact:**\\u202fAlthough the incident initially strained Sino‑U.S. ties, President Jiang\\u202fZemin’s “sixteen‑character formula” of confidence‑building and non‑confrontation helped restore relations, while the Yinhe case remains cited in Chinese discourse as a touchstone of perceived U.S. overreach.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Cyberwarfare and China', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyberwarfare_and_China'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyberwarfare_and_China', 'name': 'Development of Chinese state-sponsored cyberwarfare capabilities', 'years': '1992–present', 'short_description': 'China has built extensive cyber warfare capabilities since the early 1990s, beginning with PLA warnings in 1992, formal approval of cyber coercive programs in 2000, establishment of cyber units in 2011, and the creation of the Strategic Support Force in 2015, later reorganized into the PLA Cyberspace Force in 2024.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Development_history'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since the early 1990s the PLA has built a large, multi‑layered cyber apparatus, integrating military, security, and private actors, producing numerous APT groups that have been linked to espionage and sabotage across many countries, prompting widespread international criticism.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often portray China’s cyber program as a strategic threat, emphasizing espionage and global risk, while Chinese-language sources typically frame it as defensive information warfare and national security. This discrepancy can lead to over‑emphasis on hostile intent in Western narratives and under‑emphasis of defensive motivations in Chinese narratives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyberwarfare_and_China', 'key_points': ['**Strategic priority:**\\u202fSince the early‑1990s the People’s Liberation Army (PLA) has framed cyber‑warfare as a core pillar of China’s “information warfare” doctrine, creating dedicated cyber units, a Strategic Support Force (later the PLA Cyberspace Force), and a nation‑wide recruitment pipeline that now fields an estimated 50\\u202f000‑100\\u202f000 “hacker‑army” personnel.', '**Organised ecosystem:**\\u202fChina’s cyber capability is split among three main layers—military‑run “specialised network warfare forces,” Ministry of State Security/Public Security‑affiliated units, and semi‑governmental/private “non‑governmental” hackers—producing dozens of state‑backed Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) groups (e.g., APT1/61398, APT10, APT40, Volt/Charcoal/Salmon Typhoon, etc.).', '**Global espionage & sabotage record:**\\u202fAt least a dozen countries (U.S., U.K., Australia, EU members, African Union, ASEAN, Brazil, India, Taiwan, etc.) have publicly attributed high‑profile cyber‑espionage, data‑theft, and infrastructure‑targeting campaigns to Chinese state‑linked actors, with notable operations such as Volt\\u202fTyphoon, APT40, and the 2020 COVID‑19 research attacks.', '**International backlash:**\\u202fThe United States, its Five‑Eyes allies, and many European nations have repeatedly labeled China the “single greatest risk” to critical‑technology security, issuing indictments, sanctions, joint advisories, and public reports that link Chinese APT activity to theft of intellectual property, election‑interference attempts, and pre‑positioning for potential sabotage.', '**Debate over “cyberwarfare”:**\\u202fExperts dispute whether these activities constitute true warfare or advanced espionage; nevertheless, the consensus in the article emphasizes that China’s state‑directed cyber operations have strategic, possibly kinetic, implications and are now an integral part of its broader military competition.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'US Mexico Trade Dispute - Stainless Steel Sheets and Coils dumping', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Mexico_Trade_Dispute_-_Stainless_Steel_Sheets_and_Coils_dumping'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Mexico_Trade_Dispute_-_Stainless_Steel_Sheets_and_Coils_dumping', 'name': 'US Mexico Trade Dispute - Stainless Steel Sheets and Coils dumping', 'years': '2006-2013', 'short_description': 'A WTO trade dispute between the United States and Mexico over alleged dumping of stainless steel sheets and coils, with consultations starting on May 26, 2006, a panel established in December 2006, and a mutually satisfactory solution reached in May 2013.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Opening paragraph (intro)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"Mexico contested U.S. anti‑dumping rulings on stainless‑steel sheets, alleging the U.S. 'zeroing' method breached WTO rules. The WTO panel found the method inconsistent, and the Appellate Body declared it a breach. The U.S. complied, leading to a settlement in 2013.\", 'bias_comment