na’s military history stretches back millennia**, beginning with legendary mythic clashes (e.g., Yellow Emperor’s victories) and evolving into recorded dynastic wars that shaped the nation’s early state formation.', '**Dynastic succession and territorial ambition dominate the narrative**: almost every era— from the Zhou and Warring‑States period through Qin’s unification, the Han’s Xiongnu campaigns, and the Three Kingdoms’ brutal power struggles—features wars aimed at consolidating or expanding imperial authority.', '**External conquest and frontier defense are recurring themes**, illustrated by repeated campaigns against nomadic peoples (Xiongnu, Xianbei, Xiangnu), neighboring kingdoms (Gojoseon, Nanyue, Goguryeo), and far‑flung regions such as Central Asia and Vietnam, underscoring China’s long‑standing expansionist drive.', '**Internal rebellions and civil wars are portrayed as equally pivotal**, with countless uprisings (e.g., Red Eyebrows, Rebellion of the Seven States, War of the Eight Princes) highlighting the fragile stability of imperial rule and the frequent fragmentation of central power.', '**The list emphasizes the cyclical pattern of fragmentation and reunification**, culminating in the Jin dynasty’s eventual unification of the Three Kingdoms and later periods of disunity, reinforcing the view that China’s history is defined by continual cycles of war, division, and restoration.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'User:Blackmane/Boxer Origins - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Blackmane/Boxer_Origins'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Blackmane/Boxer_Origins', 'name': 'Origins of the Boxers', 'years': '1890s-1900', 'short_description': 'The Society of Righteous and Harmonious Fists, later known as the Boxers, formed in Shandong in the late 1890s among disenfranchised peasants opposing foreign influence, leading to the Boxer Rebellion of 1900.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Origins_of_the_Boxers'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Boxer movement in northern China (1890s‑1900) arose from economic distress, anti‑foreign sentiment, secret societies, and Qing political manipulation, combining martial training and religious rituals; it led to violent attacks on missionaries and foreign legations, prompting an international military response and a punitive treaty.', 'bias_comment': 'The English source stresses economic hardship, foreign intrusion, and Qing‑engineered mythology, highlighting internal dissent and secret‑society roots, while the Chinese source foregrounds anti‑foreign/anti‑Christian motives, the movement’s loose structure, Qing ambivalence, and the foreign punitive response. These differences reflect national historiographical biases: English accounts lean toward a socio‑political analysis of Qing manipulation, whereas Chinese narratives emphasize nationalist anti‑imperialist resistance and contest the movement’s character.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:Blackmane/Boxer_Origins', 'key_points': ['**Root causes\\u202f–\\u202feconomic desperation and foreign intrusion** – The Boxers originated in Shandong among people whose livelihoods were shattered by imperialist land grabs, natural disasters, opium addiction, and the protection foreign missionaries received under extraterritorial law.', '**Qing‑aligned mythology** – Although the group began as a secret anti‑foreign society (Yihetuan), the Manchu official Yuxian renamed it Yihequan to cement its “support the Qing, destroy the foreign” (扶清灭洋) slogan, turning the movement into a tool of the imperial court.', '**Spiritual martialism** – Boxers combined intense physical training, Taoist‑Buddhist spirit‑possession rituals, and the belief that chanting and diet could render them invulnerable to bullets, even summoning “spirit soldiers” from heaven to purge foreign influence.', '**Pre‑Boxer secret societies and anti‑Christian violence** – Earlier groups such as the Big‑Swords Society had already fought bandits‑turned‑Catholics; their clashes with Christian converts and the punitive response of local officials helped fuel the later Boxer surge.', '**Political manipulation and internal dissent** – While Empress\\u202fDowager\\u202fCixi employed the Boxers to weaken foreign powers, some leaders (e.g., Zhu Hongdeng) initially “overthrow the Qing, restore the Ming,” illustrating the movement’s fractured aims before the slogan was forced into outright pro‑Qing, anti‑foreign rhetoric.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B9%89%E5%92%8C%E5%9B%A2%E8%BF%90%E5%8A%A8', 'key_points': ['**Anti‑foreign, anti‑Christian uprising** – The 1900\\u202fBoxer Rebellion was a massive grassroots revolt in northern China sparked by resentment toward Western missionaries, foreign concessions, natural disasters, and the Qing court’s political turmoil.', '**Loose, heterogeneous organization** – “义和拳/义和团” were loosely linked martial‑arts and secret‑society groups (often linked to the White\\u202fLotus and Dadao), lacking a unified command; they carried out widespread attacks on missionaries, Chinese Christians, and foreign legations.', '**Qing ambivalence and opportunism** – The Qing government, especially Empress‑dowager Cixi, vacillated between suppressing the Boxers and courting them as a tool against foreign powers, issuing contradictory edicts that both tolerated and later ordered the “eradication” of the movement.', '**Foreign military intervention and punitive settlement** – The violence prompted the Eight‑Nation Alliance to invade, defeat the Boxers, and force China to sign the harsh Boxer Protocol (1901), imposing massive indemnities and cementing further foreign dominance.', '**Contested historical interpretation** – Historians stress that while foreign exploitation and missionary expansion were key catalysts, the rebellion’s motives were chaotic and contradictory, leaving its legacy viewed both as a nationalist anti‑imperialist protest and as a reactionary, xenophobic outbreak.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Oberlin Band (China)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberlin_Band_(China)'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberlin_Band_(China)', 'name': 'Oberlin Band (China)', 'years': '1882-1900', 'short_description': 'The Oberlin Band was a group of American Christian missionaries from Oberlin College who operated in Shanxi province, China, from 1882 until the Boxer Rebellion in 1900, when fifteen members were killed.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#The_Oberlin_Band_in_Shanxi'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Oberlin Band, a group of missionaries from Oberlin College, worked in Shanxi, China (1882‑1900). Facing language barriers, disease, and limited resources, they converted few locals. In 1900, all members were killed during the Boxer Rebellion, prompting memorials and later educational aid.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize missionary zeal and moral legacy, while Chinese accounts stress imperialist intrusion and local suffering. Spanish and French articles often highlight the tragedy and humanitarian response. These variations reflect national narratives: Western texts frame it as martyrdom, whereas Chinese portray it as foreign aggression.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberlin_Band_(China)', 'key_points': ['**Missionary zeal rooted in Oberlin College’s reformist, abolitionist legacy** – the Oberlin Band was sent to Shanxi (1882‑1900) as part of a broader 19th‑century belief that “true civilization” could only be achieved through Christian conversion.', '**Chronically under‑prepared and under‑resourced** – missionaries received no Chinese language training, faced unsanitary, disease‑riddled conditions, and suffered high mortality (10 of 30 adult missionaries and 12 of 25 children died).', '**Limited evangelical success amid cultural misunderstanding** – after two decades the Band had converted only 76 Chinese, despite operating schools, a hospital, and an opium‑rehab program; internal doctrinal splits (e.g., Tenney’s Unitarian leanings vs. Stimson’s “bottom‑up” approach) further hampered effectiveness.', '**Violent backlash culminating in the Boxer Rebellion massacres** – rumors of “well‑poisoning” and anti‑foreign sentiment led to the 1900 Taiyuan, Taigu, and Fenzhou massacres, in which all 15 Oberlin missionaries (including children) were killed by Boxers and provincial troops.', '**Post‑tragedy memorialization and legacy** – the events spurred an international military response, a commemorative arch at Oberlin College, and the establishment of the Oberlin Shansi Memorial Association, which later helped rebuild educational work in Shanxi (e.g., Ming Hsien school, now Shanxi Agricultural University).']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Boxer movement', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxer_movement'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxer_movement', 'name': 'Boxer movement', 'years': '1899–1901', 'short_description': 'The Boxer movement, a secret Chinese society of peasants and artisans in northern China, opposed foreign influence and the Qing government, launching violent attacks on missionaries and foreigners; it culminated in the Boxer Rebellion of 1899‑1901.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Lead paragraph describing the Boxers and their role'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Boxer movement (1899–1901) was a largely peasant‑based uprising in northern China that combined anti‑foreign, anti‑Christian, and anti‑imperial sentiments, briefly aligned with conservative Qing factions, and escalated into attacks on missionaries, railways, and foreign legations before being suppressed by an eight‑nation coalition.', 'bias_comment': \"English narratives often stress the Boxers' xenophobia and disorder, reflecting Western imperial viewpoints, whereas Chinese sources highlight grassroots grievances, millenarian beliefs, and portray the Boxers as patriotic defenders. These contrasting emphases shape differing narratives on legitimacy and the Qing court's role.\", 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxer_movement', 'key_points': ['The Boxers (Society of Righteous and Harmonious Fists) were a secret, largely peasant‑based nationalist movement in northern China that combined anti‑foreign, anti‑Christian, and anti‑imperial sentiment with a call to “Uphold the Qing, destroy foreigners.”', 'Although initially opposed to the Qing government, the group soon allied with conservative court factions—most famously Prince Duan and Empress Dowager Cixi—to help the dynasty resist the Eight‑Nation Alliance’s invasion during the 1900 Boxer Rebellion.', 'Their violent campaign, which included the destruction of railways, telegraph lines, and the murder of missionaries and Chinese Christians, peaked in the siege of the International Legations in Beijing before being crushed by foreign forces and a subsequent Qing crackdown.', 'Estimates of the Boxers’ strength vary widely, but the movement likely mobilized tens of thousands of fighters (up to 100\\u202f000) across loosely organized village militias, making its size and impact difficult to pin down.', 'The legacy of the Boxers remains contested: Chinese historiography often frames them as patriotic defenders of Chinese culture, while Western accounts emphasize their xenophobia and the chaos they unleashed, a duality reflected in the many names and translations used for the group.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E4%B9%89%E5%92%8C%E5%9B%A2%E8%BF%90%E5%8A%A8', 'key_points': ['**Grassroots anti‑foreign uprising:** The Boxer movement erupted in 1899‑1900 as a spontaneous, largely un‑coordinated popular revolt targeting foreign missionaries, churches, and “foreign devils,” fueled by drought, famine, and resentment toward the growing presence of Western powers in China.', '**Qing court’s ambivalent role:** While some officials (e.g., Empress\\u202fCixi) initially tolerated and even encouraged the Boxers to “assist the court” against foreigners, the central government’s policies swung back and forth—issuing vague edicts that treated the societies as “law‑abiding citizens” while later ordering harsh crack‑downs under pressure from foreign diplomats.', '**Foreign powers’ collective retaliation:** The indiscriminate attacks on legations, missionaries, and Chinese Christians prompted the eight‑nation alliance (Britain, France, Germany, Japan, Russia, the United\\u202fStates, Italy, and Austria‑Hungary) to launch a joint military expedition, culminating in the defeat of the Boxers and the signing of the “Boxer Protocol” (辛丑條約).', '**Complex motivations and legacy:** Historians stress that the rebellion was not a monolithic, ideologically driven movement; it blended millenarian “神拳” beliefs, local grievances, and opportunistic banditry, resulting in chaotic violence that victimised both foreigners and ordinary Chinese civilians alike.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Chinese Labour Corps', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Labour_Corps'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Labour_Corps', 'name': 'Chinese Labour Corps', 'years': '1916-1920', 'short_description': 'The Chinese Labour Corps was a force of about 140,000 Chinese workers recruited by Britain and France during World War I (1916‑1920) to perform non‑combatant labour on the Western Front, freeing soldiers for front‑line duties.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Origins'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Chinese Labour Corps, consisting of about 140,000 Chinese workers recruited mainly from Shandong, provided non‑combat support for the British and French on the Western Front (1916‑1920). They faced harsh conditions, high illness, and casualties, with estimates of deaths ranging from 2,000 to 20,000.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article stresses recruitment motives, links to Chinese intellectual movements, and the later recognition of the Corps, reflecting a broader international perspective. The French version highlights the formal 1917 agreement, specific front‑line tasks, and memorialization, using terms like “célestes” and focusing on French‑centric commemoration. Both note casualty ranges, but the French text foregrounds the Spanish flu and the Commonwealth graves data, while the English text mentions poverty‑driven enlistment and racial attitudes, indicating differing national narratives and source emphasis.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Labour_Corps', 'key_points': ['\\u202fThe Chinese Labour Corps (CLC) mobilised roughly\\u202f140\\u202f000 Chinese men—mainly from Shandong—to provide vital non‑combat support (unloading ships, building roads, loading shells, digging trenches, etc.) for the British and French armies on the Western Front during WWI.', '\\u202fWorking under harsh, low‑pay conditions and strict restrictions, the labourers suffered high illness rates, occasional mutinies, and significant casualties (officially\\u202f≈\\u202f2\\u202f000 deaths, with some estimates up to\\u202f20\\u202f000).', '\\u202fThe recruitment was driven by Allied manpower shortages and Chinese poverty, involving British, French and Canadian authorities, and provoked controversy from trade‑union pressure and prevailing racial attitudes.', '\\u202fAfter the armistice most CLC members were repatriated; their contribution remained largely unrecognised for decades until recent memorials, medals and cemeteries (e.g., Noyelles‑sur‑Mer) began to honour them.', '\\u202fThe CLC experience influenced Chinese intellectuals of the New Culture Movement and helped seed early Chinese diaspora communities in France.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Labour_Corps', 'key_points': ['**Création et rôle officiel** – Mis en place par le Royaume‑Uni en 1917 à la suite d’un accord sino‑britannique (octobre\\u202f1916), le Chinese Labour Corps était une force de volontaires chinois engagés pour trois ans afin d’appuyer l’effort de guerre\\u202f: travaux de construction, logistique, entretien des lignes, blanchisserie, soins et récupération des morts, mais aucune participation directe au combat.', '**Travail dangereux et essentiel au front** – Les corps de travailleurs étaient déployés surtout dans le Nord‑Pas‑de‑Calais et la Somme, près du front, où ils creusaient des tranchées, déminaient, réparaient des voies ferrées, démantaient des chars et récupéraient les corps des soldats, démontrant que le soutien logistique était crucial pour la guerre des tranchées.', '**Pertes humaines et reconnaissance** – Environ 2\\u202f000 corps chinois sont morts, majoritairement de la grippe espagnole en 1918‑1919, mais certains historiens chinois estiment le chiffre à 20\\u202f000. Le Commonwealth War Graves Commission recense 2\\u202f067 noms et a installé des tombes et mémoriaux dans plus de 90 cimetières (notamment le gigantesque cimetière chinois de Nolette avec 838 sépultures).', '**Mémoire et visibilité culturelle** – Malgré leur contribution décisive, les travailleurs restent largement méconnus; ils sont aujourd’hui honorés par des monuments, des études universitaires, des documentaires (CCTV‑F) et des œuvres littéraires, rappelant le rôle souvent oublié des «\\u202fcelestes\\u202f» dans la Première Guerre mondiale.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Seymour Expedition', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Expedition'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Expedition', 'name': 'Seymour Expedition', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'The Seymour Expedition was a multinational force that marched from Tianjin to Beijing in June 1900 to relieve the besieged legations, but was defeated by Qing troops and Boxers, forcing a retreat to Tianjin.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Opening paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1900, Vice‑Admiral Edward Seymour led a multinational force of over 2,000 sailors and marines from eight nations to rescue besieged legations in Beijing. The expedition faced strong Qing and Boxer resistance, suffered logistical and command failures, and withdrew with significant casualties.', 'bias_comment': \"English-language sources tend to highlight the expedition's rapid assembly, logistical failures, and the resulting humiliation for the Western powers, while Chinese-language narratives emphasize the legitimacy of Chinese resistance, the role of the Kansu Muslim Braves, and frame the event as anti‑imperialist defense. These linguistic emphases shape the narrative emphasis.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Expedition', 'key_points': ['**A hastily‑assembled multinational force** – In just 24\\u202fhours Vice‑Admiral Edward\\u202fSeymour marshaled over 2\\u202f000 sailors and marines from eight powers (Britain, Germany, Russia, France, the U.S., Japan, Italy and Austria‑Hungary) and set out from Tianjin to rescue the besieged legations in Beijing.', '**Catastrophic under‑estimation of Chinese resistance** – The expedition ran head‑long into a coordinated defence by Qing regulars (including the Kansu Muslim Braves) and tens of thousands of Boxers; the Battle of Langfang exposed how badly the Allies had misjudged both the scale and the fighting capability of their foes.', '**Logistical and command blunders sealed the defeat** – Reliance on rail, the loss of telegraph lines, insufficient supplies and ammunition, and poor coordination between the field column and Tianjin command left the column exposed, forcing a chaotic river retreat that cost 62 dead and 232 wounded.', '**Widely viewed as a humiliating failure** – Contemporary and later historians label the Seymour Expedition a “serious failure” and a “humiliation,” prompting the much larger, better‑equipped Gaselee Expedition later that summer to finally relieve the legations.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'The Hundred-Year Marathon', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hundred-Year_Marathon'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hundred-Year_Marathon', 'name': 'The Hundred-Year Marathon', 'years': '2015', 'short_description': 'A 2015 non‑fiction book by Michael Pillsbury asserting that China follows a long‑term \"hundred‑year marathon\" strategy to supplant the United States as the global superpower by 2049, drawing on historical Chinese strategic thought and declassified documents.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first introductory paragraph describing the book'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Hundred-Year Marathon, 2015, by Michael Pillsbury, posits that China pursues a long‑term strategy to surpass U.S. global influence by 2049, citing historical and economic tactics; the book achieved bestseller status and sparked debate, praised by some U.S. outlets and criticized for alarmist tone.', 'bias_comment': 'English coverage often frames the work as a warning, reflecting U.S. strategic anxiety, while Chinese‑language sources label it propaganda, showing national bias. Limited attention in other languages can reinforce a Western‑centric narrative, and the author’s defense‑policy background may predispose a securitized interpretation.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hundred-Year_Marathon', 'key_points': ['**Core Thesis:**\\u202fMichael\\u202fPillsbury argues that China is executing a covert, century‑long “marathon” to supplant the United States as the world’s dominant power by 2049, using historic strategic thinking (Sun\\u202fTzu, Warring‑States era), economic leverage, espionage, and manipulation of international norms.', '**Author’s Credibility:**\\u202fA former senior defense‑policy official under Reagan and Bush\\u202fSr., Pillsbury leverages his China‑analyst background, declassified documents, and defectors’ testimonies to claim insider insight into Beijing’s long‑term agenda.', '**Reception & Influence:**\\u202fThe book topped bestseller lists and has been lauded by many U.S.‑media and policy outlets (Wall\\u202fStreet\\u202fJournal, Washington\\u202fPost, Fox\\u202fNews, Foreign\\u202fAffairs, former CIA director James\\u202fWoolsey) as a “wake‑up call” and a key text for understanding Sino‑American rivalry.', '**Criticism & Controversy:**\\u202fSome reviewers (PBS\\u202fNewsHour, Rasmussen Reports) contend Pillsbury overstates China’s capabilities and relies heavily on hawkish Chinese voices, arguing the work can amplify a securitized, alarmist narrative about China.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'International Traffic in Arms Regulations', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Traffic_in_Arms_Regulations'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Traffic_in_Arms_Regulations', 'name': 'International Traffic in Arms Regulations', 'years': '1976', 'short_description': 'The International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) are U.S. State Department rules that control the export of defense articles and technical data, implementing the Arms Export Control Act to protect national security and foreign policy.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) is a U.S. State Department framework controlling export of defense articles, services, and technical data on the US Munitions List, requiring licenses and imposing civil and criminal penalties, with notable impacts on industry, academia, and foreign partners.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize national‑security justification and note industry/academic concerns, while French sources highlight protectionist and sovereignty impacts, portraying ITAR as a geopolitical tool limiting allies’ autonomy. English text may under‑state trade‑restriction bias; French text emphasizes economic coercion, reflecting national perspectives.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Traffic_in_Arms_Regulations', 'key_points': ['**Purpose & Scope:**\\u202fITAR is a U.S. State‑Department regime that treats virtually all defense‑related articles, services and technical data on the United States Munitions List (USML) as “arms” and restricts their export or even “deemed export” to foreign persons unless a State‑Dept license is obtained – a tool the government says protects national security and foreign‑policy goals.', '**Enforcement & Penalties:**\\u202fThe Directorate of Defense Trade Controls (DDTC) administers licenses, while Customs‑ICE, the Department of Defense, and the Commerce Bureau’s BIS vet compliance. Violations are prosecuted as strict‑liability offences; fines can reach millions of dollars, companies may be forced to appoint compliance officers, and individuals can face criminal imprisonment.', '**Economic & Academic Impact:**\\u202fIndustry and universities argue that ITAR’s sweeping reach creates a major trade barrier – shrinking U.S. market share in satellites, forcing “ITAR‑free” foreign alternatives, and complicating research collaborations, foreign‑student involvement, and dual‑national employment. Critics claim the regulation harms U.S. competitiveness more than it enhances security.', '**Evolving Rules & Controversy:**\\u202fSince its Cold‑War origins, the USML has been repeatedly amended (e.g., removal of most satellite tech in 2014, partial exemptions for allies like the U.K. and Australia in 2024). While the State Department maintains the controls are essential, lawmakers and industry groups have repeatedly pushed for roll‑backs, citing lost revenue, slowed innovation, and diplomatic friction over re‑transfer restrictions.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Traffic_in_Arms_Regulations', 'key_points': ['**ITAR\\u202f: un instrument de contrôle américain** – Le «\\u202fInternational Traffic in Arms Regulations\\u202f» impose des autorisations d’import/export sur tout matériel et service figurant sur la United\\u202fMunitions\\u202fList, afin de protéger la sécurité nationale et d’avancer les objectifs de la politique étrangère des États‑Unis.', '**Effet de dépendance et de souveraineté affaiblie** – De nombreux alliés (France, Europe, Égypte, etc.) sont contraints de déposer des centaines de dossiers de licences chaque année ou de développer des solutions «\\u202fITAR‑free\\u202f», ce qui augmente les coûts, retarde les programmes et limite leur autonomie industrielle.', '**Sanctions sévères et pressions commerciales** – Des violations (ex.\\u202fBoeing\\u202f–\\u202fQRS‑11, Thales\\u202fAlenia\\u202fSpace) entraînent des amendes lourdes et des annulations de contrats, montrant que le non‑respect d’ITAR peut bloquer des ventes de satellites, de missiles ou d’avions civils.', '**Instrument de protectionnisme économique** – Les États‑Unis utilisent ITAR pour bloquer des exportations stratégiques (ex.\\u202fRafale\\u202fvers\\u202fl’Égypte) ou pour contraindre les lancements de satellites chinois, illustrant une dimension géopolitique et commerciale du règlement.', '**Tendance à un durcissement** – Depuis les années\\u202f2010, les exigences ITAR se sont renforcées, rendant la mise en œuvre de technologies «\\u202fITAR‑free\\u202f» de plus en plus difficile et poussant les industries étrangères à repenser leurs chaînes d’approvisionnement.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Military history of the United States', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_history_of_the_United_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_history_of_the_United_States', 'name': 'Military history of the United States', 'years': '1607–2024', 'short_description': \"The United States' military history spans from colonial beginnings in 1607, through the Revolutionary War, territorial expansion, major wars, and its rise as a world superpower after World\\u202fII, continuing to the present.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The United States’ military history (1607–2024) chronicles the evolution from colonial militia to a global, multi‑branch force, detailing involvement in over two dozen wars, territorial expansion, technological innovation, and controversies surrounding warfare, imperialism, and civilian impacts.', 'bias_comment': \"The English entry emphasizes the United States' rise as a global super‑power and technological innovation, which can reflect a Western, state‑centric viewpoint. Non‑English versions (e.g., Spanish, Chinese, Arabic) often highlight imperialist actions, civilian casualties, and the experiences of colonized peoples, revealing differing national narratives and emphasis on moral critique.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_history_of_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**From frontier militia to global super‑power:**  Over four centuries the United\\u202fStates transformed its ad‑hoc colonial militias into a permanent, multi‑branch armed forces (Army, Marine Corps, Navy, Air Force, Space Force, Coast Guard) under the Department of Defense, enabling the nation to project power worldwide after World\\u202fII.', '**A history of almost constant warfare:**  From early colonial‑Indian conflicts, the Revolutionary War, the War of\\u202f1812, the Mexican‑American War, the Civil War, the Spanish‑American and Philippine‑American wars, the World Wars, Korea, Vietnam, the Gulf conflicts and the post‑9/11 campaigns, the United\\u202fStates has been engaged in more than two dozen major wars and countless smaller interventions.', '**Expansionist and often imperialist policies:**  Manifest Destiny, the annexation of Texas, the conquest of Mexican territories, the overthrow of Hawaii, the “Banana Wars” in Latin America, and the acquisition of overseas colonies (Puerto\\u202fRico, Guam, Philippines) illustrate a pattern of using military force to secure economic and strategic interests beyond the continent.', '**Human cost and controversial conduct:**  Battles such as Gettysburg (≈50,000 casualties) and the Civil War’s total death toll remain the deadliest in U.S. history; later conflicts like the Indian Wars, the Philippine‑American War, and the Wounded\\u202fKnee Massacre involved brutal tactics, civilian deaths and lasting moral criticism.', '**Technological and doctrinal evolution:**  The U.S. military repeatedly adopted new technologies and doctrines—from Prussian‑style training in the 1770s, ironclads and railroads in the Civil War, a modern steel navy in the 1890s, the Great White Fleet’s power‑projection, to air power and nuclear capabilities—helping it become a decisive force in 20th‑century global conflicts.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Purple Storm', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple_Storm'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Purple_Storm', 'name': 'Purple Storm', 'years': '1989', 'short_description': 'Operation Purple Storm was a series of United States military exercises conducted in Panama in 1989, involving joint training of Army, Navy, Air Force, and Marine units.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first bullet (Operation Purple Storm) on disambiguation page'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Operation Purple Storm, the U.S. invasion of Panama launched on 20\\u202fDecember\\u202f1989 with about 27,000 troops and 300 aircraft, quickly defeated the Panama Defense Forces, installed opposition leader Guillermo\\u202fEndara, and led to the capture of dictator Manuel\\u202fNoriega in January\\u202f1990.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often frame the operation as a swift, decisive action to restore democracy, while Spanish-language accounts emphasize civilian casualties and legal controversies. This reflects differing national perspectives on U.S. intervention, with potential bias toward either legitimizing the invasion or highlighting humanitarian costs.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Purple_Storm', 'key_points': ['**Operation Just\\u202fCause – a rapid, high‑profile U.S. assault:**', 'Launched on\\u202f20\\u202fDecember\\u202f1989 with ~27,000 troops, 300 aircraft and a “four‑objective” justification (protect U.S. citizens, restore democracy, combat drug trafficking, safeguard the Panama Canal), the invasion quickly overwhelmed the 16,000‑strong Panama Defense Forces, installed opposition leader Guillermo\\u202fEndara and captured dictator Manuel\\u202fNoriega in January\\u202f1990.', '**Human‑cost controversy:**', 'Official Pentagon figures list 516 Panamanian deaths (314 soldiers,\\u202f202 civilians), but independent estimates range from 300 to\\u202f3,000 civilian casualties. Human‑rights groups argue the force used was disproportionate, violating the principle of proportionality and causing an “inordinate” number of non‑combatant deaths.', '**Legal legitimacy heavily disputed:**']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Chinese spring offensive', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_spring_offensive'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_spring_offensive', 'name': 'Chinese spring offensive', 'years': '1951', 'short_description': 'The Chinese spring offensive, also called the Fifth Phase Offensive, was a large‑scale PVA attack during the Korean War launched on 22\\u202fApril\\u202f1951, involving three field armies totalling about 700,000 troops, aimed at recapturing Seoul but halted by UN forces by late May.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'opening summary paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In spring 1951 the People’s Volunteer Army launched a large-scale offensive on the Korean Peninsula, aiming to capture Seoul. UN forces, after initial setbacks, halted the advance by early May and counterattacked, forcing Chinese and Korean forces to retreat with heavy casualties on both sides.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often emphasize UN tactical success and Chinese casualties, while Chinese-language accounts may stress strategic intent and downplay losses. Cross-language differences can affect casualty figures, narrative focus, and attribution of agency, highlighting the need for diverse sources.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_spring_offensive', 'key_points': ['**Massive yet ultimately failed Chinese push:**\\u202fIn spring\\u202f1951 the People’s Volunteer Army mobilized\\u202f≈\\u202f700\\u202f000 troops in their largest offensive since late\\u202f1950, aiming to seize Seoul as a “May\\u202fDay” gift and drive UN forces off the peninsula.', '**UN forces held the line and turned the tide:**\\u202fDespite initial breakthroughs—most notably the ROK\\u202f6th Division’s collapse and heavy assaults on the 24th\\u202fInfantry and Marine units—UN troops halted the offensive at the “No‑Name”/Kansas lines by early‑May, then launched a decisive counter‑offensive that forced the exhausted Chinese‑Korean forces to retreat and kept Seoul out of Chinese hands.', '**Heavy casualties on both sides but especially for China:**\\u202fUN reports cite about\\u202f15\\u202f770 casualties (including\\u202f≈\\u202f1\\u202f200 US killed), while Chinese estimates range from\\u202f≈\\u202f83\\u202f000\\u202f–\\u202f90\\u202f000\\u202fcasualties, with UN estimates placing total Chinese/Korean losses at\\u202f110\\u202f000‑160\\u202f000.', '**Article’s reliability note:**\\u202fThe entry is flagged for relying almost entirely on a single source, highlighting the need for additional citations to balance the narrative.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'George C. Remey', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_C._Remey'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_C._Remey', 'name': 'George C. Remey', 'years': '1841–1928', 'short_description': 'George Collier Remey (1841–1928) was a United States Navy rear admiral who served in the Civil War and the Spanish–American War, with a career spanning 1859‑1903, including command of the Asiatic Squadron and key naval operations.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'first paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'George C. Remey (1841–1928) was a U.S. Navy officer who graduated in 1859, served 44 years, fought in the Civil War, commanded the Asiatic Squadron, led the Portsmouth Navy Yard during the Spanish‑American War, chaired the Lighthouse Board, and is buried at Arlington.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources emphasize Remey's valor and leadership, while non‑English versions may downplay wartime actions or focus on his later administrative roles. Differences may arise from national narratives about the Civil and Spanish‑American Wars, potentially affecting the portrayal of his combat bravery versus bureaucratic contributions.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_C._Remey', 'key_points': ['**Long‑service naval officer:** Graduated from the U.S. Naval Academy in 1859 and served 44\\u202fyears, rising to rear admiral and commanding key ships (Marblehead, Enterprise, Charleston) and the Asiatic Squadron.', '**Combat bravery and sacrifice:** Fought in the Civil War—most notably leading a daring boat assault on Fort Sumter, was captured, endured 13\\u202fmonths of imprisonment, and returned to active duty after exchange.', '**Strategic wartime leadership:** As commandant of Portsmouth Navy Yard at the start of the Spanish‑American War, he oversaw the Key\\u202fWest naval base, directing supply and repair operations that sustained U.S. forces in Cuban waters.', '**Enduring legacy:** Retired in 1903, later chaired the Lighthouse Board, and was commemorated by the destroyer\\u202fUSS\\u202fRemey (DD‑688); he is buried at Arlington National Cemetery.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Oscar J. Upham', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscar_J._Upham'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscar_J._Upham', 'name': 'Oscar J. Upham', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'Private Oscar J. Upham earned the Medal of Honor for assisting in the erection of barricades under heavy enemy fire in Peking, China, from 21 July to 17 August 1900 during the Boxer Rebellion.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Medal_of_Honor_citation'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Oscar J. Upham, a U.S. Marine (1896‑1901), served in the Spanish‑American War and the Boxer Rebellion. He earned the Medal of Honor for defending the International Legations in Peking. He later worked for the postal service in Oklahoma and is buried in Guthrie.', 'bias_comment': 'Only English sources were used, so the narrative emphasizes U.S. military achievements and regional ties, potentially overlooking non‑American perspectives (e.g., Chinese or Spanish viewpoints) and broader geopolitical context. Cross‑language sources could reveal differing national narratives or criticism not captured here.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscar_J._Upham', 'key_points': ['**Valor in the Boxer Rebellion** – As a Marine private, Upham helped erect barricades under heavy fire during the 1900 siege of the International Legations in Peking, actions for which he earned the Medal of Honor (one of only 33 Marines so honored).', '**Pioneer of the Spanish‑American War** – While serving as a “powder monkey” aboard USS\\u202fOregon, Upham is credited with firing the very first shot that opened hostilities on July\\u202f3\\u202f1898, marking him as a notable figure at the war’s outset.', '**Dual‑theater veteran** – Upham’s five‑year Marine career (1896‑1901) spanned two major conflicts (the Spanish‑American War and the Boxer Rebellion), illustrating his broad experience in America’s turn‑of‑the‑century overseas engagements.', '**Documented eyewitness** – He kept a detailed diary during the Peking siege, portions of which have been published repeatedly, providing a rare first‑hand perspective on the harsh conditions and morale of the defenders.', '**Post‑service life and legacy** – After his parents moved to Oklahoma during the 1889 Land Run, Upham settled there, worked for the U.S. Postal Service, and was buried in Guthrie, Oklahoma, where his family remains, underscoring his lasting regional ties.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'China Girl (1942 film)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Girl_(1942_film)'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Girl_(1942_film)', 'name': 'China Girl (1942 film)', 'years': '1942', 'short_description': 'China Girl is a 1942 American drama film about an American newsreel photographer in China and Burma during World War\\u202fII, starring Gene Tierney, George Montgomery, Lynn Bari and Victor McLaglen, directed by Henry Hathaway.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'China Girl (1942) is an American black‑and‑white drama set during the Burma Road campaign. It follows newsreel cameraman Johnny Williams as he becomes entangled in espionage, romance, and combat against Japanese forces in China and Burma. The film was directed by Henry Hathaway and released by 20th\\u202fCentury\\u202fFox.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article frames the film as wartime propaganda, emphasizing American heroism and Japanese antagonism. Non‑English versions may downplay the propaganda angle or highlight Chinese or Japanese perspectives, altering the tone of heroism, cultural representation, and moral judgments. Such variations reflect differing national memories of WWII.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Girl_(1942_film)', 'key_points': ['**Wartime adventure‑propaganda:** *China Girl* (1942) is a black‑and‑white American drama set against the Burma Road campaign, positioning an American newsreel cameraman as the reluctant hero who ultimately battles Japanese forces in China and Burma.', '**Star‑driven studio picture:** Directed by Henry Hathaway and produced by 20th\\u202fCentury\\u202fFox, the film features Gene\\u202fTierney, George\\u202fMontgomery, Lynn\\u202fBari and Victor\\u202fMcLaglen, with a screenplay by Ben\\u202fHecht and Darryl\\u202fF.\\u202fZanuck. It was released on December\\u202f9\\u202f,\\u202f1942 under the alternate titles *A Yank in China*, *Burma Road* and *Over the Burma Road*.', '**Plot hinges on romance and espionage:** The protagonist, Johnny Williams, is drawn into a tangled web of Japanese agents, a fake‑girlfriend (Captain\\u202fFifi), and a sincere Eurasian love interest (Haoli Young). After a series of betrayals and the death of Haoli, he storms a Japanese‑controlled building and shoots down a fighter, finally taking an active combat role.', '**Commercial impact and legacy:** The film earned about $1.4\\u202fmillion in US rentals—a solid box‑office showing for a wartime release—and is listed among the “Pacific War” and “World War\\u202fII films made in wartime” categories, reflecting its role as both entertainment and morale‑boosting propaganda.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'China (1943 film)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_(1943_film)'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_(1943_film)', 'name': 'China (1943 film)', 'years': '1943', 'short_description': 'A 1943 American war drama directed by John Farrow, starring Loretta Young, Alan Ladd and William Bendix, about an American oil salesman who aids Chinese guerrillas fighting the Japanese invasion of China.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'opening summary paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1943 film *China* is a World‑II drama about an American oilman who joins Chinese guerrillas against Japan, featuring a notable Asian supporting cast, production conflicts, mixed critical reception, and commercial success.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources highlight the film's propaganda tone and production issues, while Chinese accounts stress its patriotic portrayal of Chinese resistance and praise the inclusive casting. Japanese references downplay the film, noting only its wartime context. These differences reflect national narratives affecting emphasis on ideology, representation, and historical framing.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_(1943_film)', 'key_points': ['**Propaganda‑flavoured WWII drama** –\\u202fMade during the war, *China* portrays an American oil‑man who ends up fighting the Japanese alongside Chinese guerrillas, serving as a patriotic rally‑cry for American support of China.', '**High‑profile cast with unusual Asian representation** –\\u202fStars Loretta\\u202fYoung, Alan\\u202fLadd and William\\u202fBendix, while the entire supporting ensemble (Philip\\u202fAhn, Richard\\u202fLoo, Sen\\u202fYung, etc.) is of Chinese/East‑Asian descent, a rarity for Hollywood at the time.', '**Troubled production and on‑set clashes** –\\u202fLoretta\\u202fYoung fought against a suicide‑scene she deemed immoral, threatened to quit, and complained about Ladd’s temperament; Ladd himself was injured during the battle sequences.', '**Box‑office hit but critically divided** –\\u202fThe film earned $2.5\\u202fmillion in U.S. rentals and spurred a follow‑up pairing of Young and Ladd, yet reviewers were split: *The New York Times* called it “Hollywood trumpery,” while *Variety* praised its authenticity and Farrow’s direction.', '**Cultural legacy** –\\u202fLadd’s rugged, fedora‑wearing hero inspired the later Indiana\\u202fJones archetype, and the film’s spectacular avalanche scene set a record for on‑screen Japanese casualties in a Hollywood war picture.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Template:Boxer Rebellion - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Boxer_Rebellion'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Boxer_Rebellion', 'name': 'Boxer Rebellion', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'The Boxer Rebellion (1900) was a Chinese anti-foreign, anti‑Christian uprising; the Eight‑Nation Alliance intervened to relieve the foreign legations in Beijing.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Infobox showing forces of the Eight-Nation Alliance'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Eight‑Nation Alliance, comprising Japan, Britain, Russia, Germany, France, United States, Italy, and Austria‑Hungary, intervened to lift the siege of foreign legations in Beijing, deploying roughly 51,800 soldiers, 5,000 marines, and 54 warships, with Japan providing the largest ground force and Britain the largest marine contingent.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources often stress the multinational relief effort and military scale, potentially downplaying Chinese civilian impact and anti‑imperial sentiment. Chinese-language accounts may frame the alliance as an invasive force, highlighting local resistance, while other languages (e.g., Russian, Japanese) might stress protection of nationals, leading to differing emphases across linguistic contexts.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Boxer_Rebellion', 'key_points': ['The Eight‑Nation Alliance was assembled as a multinational force specifically to **relieve the besieged foreign legations in Beijing** during the Boxer Rebellion.', '**Japan contributed the largest ground force** (≈20,300 soldiers) and the most warships (18), making it the dominant military player among the allies.', '**Britain supplied the biggest marine contingent** (2,020 marines) despite a relatively modest army size (10,000) and only eight warships, highlighting its emphasis on expeditionary troops.', '**Russia, Germany, France, the United States, Italy, and Austria‑Hungary** each added smaller but still significant naval and land elements; Russia fielded the second‑largest army (12,400) and a notable marine force (750).', '**Overall, the coalition deployed 54 warships, 4,971 marines, and 51,755 soldiers**, illustrating the scale and coordination of foreign intervention against the Boxer uprising.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Gaselee Expedition', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasalee_Expedition'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasalee_Expedition', 'name': 'Gaselee Expedition', 'years': '1900', 'short_description': 'A multinational allied force marched from Tianjin to Beijing in August 1900, relieving the besieged foreign legations and defeating Boxer and Qing forces during the Boxer Rebellion.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#The_expedition'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"In August 1900, a multinational force of about 55\\u202f000 troops from eight nations marched from Tianjin to relieve Beijing's besieged legations, ending the Boxer Rebellion's Battle of Peking. The campaign suffered extreme heat, disease, and inter‑Allied friction, with casualties on both sides.\", 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes the multinational nature and logistical hardships, while highlighting Western prejudice against a non‑white commander, reflecting a Eurocentric lens. Non‑English sources (e.g., Chinese, Japanese) may stress national contributions, portray Japanese leadership more positively, and frame the conflict in terms of anti‑imperial resistance.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasalee_Expedition', 'key_points': ['**A massive, multinational force** (≈55\\u202f000 troops from eight powers) marched from Tianjin in August\\u202f1900 and succeeded in relieving the besieged legations in Beijing, ending the Boxer Rebellion’s “Battle of Peking.”', '**Command and prejudice** – Although Japanese General\\u202fYamaguchi Motomi was the senior officer, the Allies appointed British Lt‑Gen\\u202fAlfred\\u202fGaselee as interim commander because of “prejudices” against a non‑white leader.', '**Harsh logistics and heavy losses** – Soldiers suffered extreme heat (up to 108\\u202f°F), disease, supply shortages, and “moderate” Allied casualties versus “heavy” Chinese losses, with many troops dying from heat exhaustion and illness.', '**Inter‑Allied friction** – Episodes of friendly fire and mutual accusations (e.g., British troops killing Germans, French firing on allies) underscored the strained coordination among the coalition.', '**Atrocities on both sides** – Contemporary reports described brutal acts, including beheading of Chinese civilians by Allied forces and savage retaliation by Chinese fighters, highlighting the war’s brutal, reciprocal violence.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Republic of China declaration of war on Germany', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China_declaration_of_war_on_Germany'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China_declaration_of_war_on_Germany', 'name': 'Republic of China declaration of war on Germany', 'years': '1917', 'short_description': \"In August 1917, the Beiyang government's National Assembly, led by Premier Duan Qirui, declared war on the German Empire and Austria‑Hungary, denouncing all treaties and supporting the Entente Powers amid internal power struggles.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Paragraph describing the 14 August 1917 declaration'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1917, after intense debate between hawkish Premier Duan Qirui and a dovish opposition, the Republic of China declared war on Germany and Austria‑Hungary on August 14, aligning with the Entente and reclaiming concessions amid political turmoil.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize parliamentary debate and internal power struggles, while Chinese-language sources often emphasize nationalist motivations and the quest for international recognition. These differing emphases can bias the portrayal toward internal politics in English and a more patriotic narrative in Chinese.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China_declaration_of_war_on_Germany', 'key_points': ['**Intense parliamentary debate (Apr‑May\\u202f1917)** – The first National Assembly of the Republic of China fiercely contested whether to formally join World\\u202fWar\\u202fI by declaring war on Germany.', '**Hawkish vs. dovish split** – The “hawk” camp, led by Premier\\u202fDuan\\u202fQirui, pushed for war and was later exposed for secretly borrowing Japanese funds (the Nishihara Loans scandal), which stalled the declaration.', '**Political turmoil tipped the balance** – A failed Manchu restoration attempt and President\\u202fLi\\u202fYuan‑hong’s resignation weakened the dovish faction, paving the way for Duan’s second cabinet to act.', '**War declaration (14\\u202fAug\\u202f1917)** – The Duan government finally declared war on the German and Austro‑Hungarian Empires, annulled existing treaties, reclaimed concessions in Tianjin and Hankou, and aligned China with the Entente—an outcome that underscored the underlying power struggle between the president and the premier.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Intelsat 708', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelsat_708'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelsat_708', 'name': 'Intelsat 708', 'years': '1996', 'short_description': 'Intelsat 708 was a communications satellite destroyed on 15\\u202fFebruary\\u202f1996 when its Long\\u202fMarch\\u202f3B launch vehicle failed shortly after liftoff from Xichang, China, crashing into a nearby village and killing at least six people.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Launch_failure'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'On 15\\u202fFebruary\\u202f1996, a Long\\u202fMarch\\u202f3B launch carrying the American‑built Intelsat\\u202f708 failed, crashing into a village and destroying the satellite. The accident prompted divergent investigations, U.S. export‑control reforms, and lasting restrictions on satellite component sales to China.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasise U.S. policy impact and technical disputes, while French sources highlight higher casualty estimates and Chinese official narratives, reflecting differing national perspectives on responsibility, human cost, and technology transfer concerns.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelsat_708', 'key_points': ['**Catastrophic launch failure:** On\\u202f15\\u202fFebruary\\u202f1996 a Long\\u202fMarch\\u202f3B rocket carrying the American‑built Intelsat\\u202f708 veered off‑course seconds after liftoff, crashing into a nearby village, destroying the satellite and killing\\u202fat\\u202fleast six people (official Chinese figures), while independent observers questioned the true casualty count.', '**Technical cause & disputed investigations:** The Chinese inquiry blamed a faulty inertial‑measurement unit, but an Independent Review Committee (including Loral, Hughes and other U.S. firms) identified a different guidance‑system failure; the Chinese report was later revised to match the committee’s findings, highlighting competing narratives.', '**U.S. national‑security fallout:** U.S. authorities concluded that the joint investigation gave China “significant benefit” for its missile‑guidance technology, prompting Congress to re‑classify satellite technology as a munition in 1998, tighten ITAR controls, and levy a $20\\u202fmillion settlement on Loral for export‑control violations.', '**Long‑term ramifications:** No U.S. export licences for satellite components have been issued to China since 1996, and the incident remains a reference point for the strict U.S. export regime that still bars any U.S.‑origin content from reaching China, underscoring how a single launch disaster reshaped commercial‑space policy and Sino‑U.S. technology relations.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelsat_708', 'key_points': ['**Intelsat\\u202f708\\u202f–** satellite de télécommunications construit par Space Systems/Loral pour Intelsat, destiné à une orbite géostationnaire\\u202f; il a été perdu lors du premier vol du lanceur chinois Longue\\u202fMarche\\u202f3B depuis Xichang le\\u202f15\\u202ffévrier\\u202f1996.', '**Catastrophe au lancement :** deux secondes après le décollage, le lanceur a dévié, s’est écrasé horizontalement et a explosé, détruisant un village voisin. La commission d’enquête chinoise a imputé l’accident à une défaillance du système d’inertie du lanceur.', '**Polémique sur le bilan humain :** les autorités chinoises ont annoncé 6 morts et 57 blessés, tandis que des estimations occidentales évoquent entre 200\\u202fet\\u202f500 victimes, les témoignages citant d’importantes foules et des ambulances sur les lieux.', '**Débats sur le transfert de technologie :** la présence d’un module de chiffrement et d’autres technologies sensibles à bord du satellite a alimenté les soupçons de récupération illégale par la Chine, entraînant une enquête du Congrès américain et le durcissement des contrôles à l’exportation.', '**Conséquences juridiques :** en 2002, les entreprises américaines Space Systems/Loral et Boeing Satellite Systems ont été condamnées à d’importantes amendes (20\\u202fM$ et 32\\u202fM$) pour violation des lois de contrôle des exportations liées aux lancements de satellites en Chine.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'George Seward', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seward'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seward', 'name': 'George Seward', 'years': '1840–1910, 1851–1904', 'short_description': 'George Seward may refer to two American figures: a diplomat to China (1840–1910) and a baseball player (1851–1904).', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Disambiguation page'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Two American individuals named George\\xa0Seward are listed: George\\xa0Seward (1840–1910), a U.S. diplomat who served in China, and George\\xa0Seward (1851–1904), a baseball player from the late 1800s. The page follows the standard name‑disambiguation format.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article presents a straightforward, factual list without cultural or regional context; in other language versions, the emphasis might differ—e.g., Chinese or Spanish pages could highlight one individual over another based on regional relevance or available sources, potentially affecting prominence and detail.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Seward', 'key_points': ['**Two distinct individuals** share the name\\u202fGeorge\\u202fSeward, prompting a dedicated disambiguation page.', '**George\\u202fSeward (1840‑1910)** was an American diplomat who served in China during the late‑19th\\u202fcentury.', '**George\\u202fSeward (1851‑1904)** was an American baseball player active in the late 1800s.', 'The page follows Wikipedia’s standard format for human‑name disambiguation, listing each person’s primary occupation and lifespan.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'George Seward (diplomat)', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_F._Seward'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_F._Seward', 'name': 'George Seward (diplomat)', 'years': '1876-1880', 'short_description': 'George Frederick Seward (1840–1910) was a United States diplomat who served as U.S. Minister to China from 1876 to 1880 after earlier consular posts in Shanghai.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Diplomatic_career_in_China'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'George F. Seward served as U.S. Consul in Shanghai, Consul‑General, and Minister to China (1861‑1880). Allegations of land speculation and embezzlement led to investigations and his 1880 removal. He later became a corporate executive and received several honors, leaving a mixed legacy.', 'bias_comment': 'English‑language sources, especially U.S. historiography, may emphasize nepotism and scandal while downplaying contemporary Chinese perspectives. Cross‑language coverage (e.g., Chinese, Danish) could highlight diplomatic impact differently, reflecting national interests and varying access to archival evidence.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_F._Seward', 'key_points': ['**Prominent diplomatic career (1861‑1880)** – Nepotistically appointed by his uncle, Secretary of State\\u202fWilliam\\u202fH.\\u202fSeward, George\\u202fF.\\u202fSeward rose from U.S. Consul in Shanghai to Consul‑General and ultimately U.S. Minister to China, playing a notable part in early negotiations that foreshadowed the Chinese Exclusion Act.', '**Controversial conduct and accusations** – While in Shanghai he and his vice‑counsel were alleged to have engaged in private land and capital speculation that violated the Burlingame Treaty; subsequent reports by successors and the Hong\\u202fKong consul John\\u202fS.\\u202fMosby described a wider network of embezzlement and shady dealings involving other American officials in East Asia.', '**Political fallout and removal** – The scandals prompted investigations in Washington, a threatened impeachment, and a “clean‑up” by Secretary of State\\u202fWilliam\\u202fM.\\u202fEvarts, culminating in Seward’s replacement as Minister to China in the summer of\\u202f1880.', '**Successful business and civic life after diplomacy** – Transitioning to the private sector, Seward became vice‑president (1887) and then president (1893) of the Fidelity and Casualty Company, while also holding directorships in several firms and active memberships in numerous professional and civic societies.', '**Recognition and legacy** – He received foreign honors (Denmark’s Order of the Dannebrog, the Order of the Dragon of Annam) and an honorary LL.D. from Union College, but his reputation remains mixed, reflecting both his high‑profile diplomatic service and the corruption allegations that marred his tenure.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Fishing industry in China', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing_industry_in_China'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing_industry_in_China', 'name': 'Fishing industry in China', 'years': '2005‑present', 'short_description': \"China's fishing industry supplies roughly one‑third of the world's fish and two‑thirds of aquaculture output, employs about 7.9\\u202fmillion people, and in 2005 recorded a total harvest of 49.5\\u202fmillion\\u202ftonnes (32.4\\u202fmillion\\u202ftonnes from aquaculture) with a fisheries GDP of US$45.9\\u202fbillion.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Statistics'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Since 2005, China’s fishing sector has become the world’s largest producer of capture and farmed fish, employing millions, and receiving substantial government subsidies, while facing concerns over overfishing, illegal activities, and environmental impacts.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry stresses economic scale, state subsidies, and regulatory challenges, presenting a largely industry‑centric view. The Chinese article foregrounds ecological degradation, pollution, and global sustainability, offering a more critical environmental narrative. These differing emphases reflect language‑specific audience framing and potential national bias in source selection.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing_industry_in_China', 'key_points': ['**China is the world‑leader in both capture fishing and aquaculture**, producing roughly one‑third of global fish catch and two‑thirds of global farmed fish (≈\\u202f49\\u202fMt total in 2005) and employing almost 8\\u202fmillion people.', '**The industry is heavily state‑subsidised**, especially for fuel (the single largest “harmful” subsidy worldwide at $5.9\\u202fbn in 2018), which makes distant‑water fleets economically viable and fuels a massive, often illegal, expansion of the fishing fleet.', '**IUU fishing and over‑reporting are chronic problems** – Chinese vessels are responsible for the most illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing globally; concerns have been raised that official catch statistics vastly overstate actual wild‑catch trends.', '**Environmental pressures and management measures clash** – despite “zero‑growth” and moratorium policies, over‑capacity, habitat degradation, and abundant subsidies keep over‑fishing rates high, while the government’s recent actions (fleet reductions, marine‑reserve bans, and a “minus‑growth” target) have had limited success.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E6%8D%95%E6%92%88%E5%B0%8D%E7%92%B0%E5%A2%83%E7%9A%84%E5%BD%B1%E9%9F%BF', 'key_points': ['**Over‑exploitation is reshaping marine food webs.** Intensive harvesting of high‑trophic species (e.g., tuna, shark) and destructive methods such as bottom‑trawling, cyanide fishing and blast fishing have driven a “fishing‑down” shift toward low‑trophic organisms (e.g., jellyfish), triggering trophic cascades and ecosystem regime changes.', '**Fishing is a major source of marine pollution.** Abandoned or lost gear—especially plastic‑based ghost nets and drift nets—accounts for the bulk of large‑size marine litter, entangles and kills marine wildlife, and releases stored seabed carbon, compounding climate impacts.', '**The scale of extraction is unsustainable.** Global capture volumes keep rising (1.726\\u202fbillion\\u202ft in 2017, a 4\\u202f% increase over 2016) while roughly one‑third of fish stocks were already classified as overfished; modelling suggests wild capture could be exhausted by 2048 without drastic change.', '**Mitigation must be ecosystem‑focused.** International bodies and many nations are moving toward ecosystem‑based fisheries management, marine protected areas, quota reforms, and reduced subsidies, while promoting responsible aquaculture—though aquaculture itself can bring side‑effects (disease spill‑over, nutrient runoff, plastic infrastructure).', '**Public awareness and policy action are essential.** The article emphasizes that fishing‑related impacts affect not only marine biodiversity but also global food security, climate regulation, and human well‑being, urging coordinated scientific, regulatory, and consumer‑level responses.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Root–Takahira Agreement', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-Takahira_agreement'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-Takahira_agreement', 'name': 'Root–Takahira Agreement', 'years': '1908', 'short_description': \"The 1908 Root–Takahara Agreement was a US‑Japan accord that recognized the territorial status quo, affirmed China's independence, affirmed free trade, and acknowledged U.S. control of Hawaii and the Philippines and Japanese control of Korea and Manchuria, while limiting Japanese immigration to California.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The 1908 Root‑Takahira Agreement was a diplomatic pact in which the United\\u202fStates and Japan acknowledged each other's existing overseas holdings, pledged to uphold China’s territorial integrity and the Open‑Door policy, and affirmed free trade and equal commercial opportunities in the region.\", 'bias_comment': 'English narratives often highlight the agreement’s role in managing US‑Japan tensions and immigration limits, while Japanese accounts stress mutual recognition and balance of power. Chinese-language perspectives may stress the Open‑Door pledge and concerns over sovereignty, revealing national biases in interpreting the pact’s significance.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-Takahira_agreement', 'key_points': ['The 1908 Root‑Takahira Agreement was a diplomatic “status‑quo” pact in which the United\\u202fStates and Japan formally recognized each other’s existing overseas possessions—U.S. control of Hawaii and the Philippines, and Japanese domination of Korea and Manchuria.', 'Both powers pledged to uphold China’s territorial integrity and the Open‑Door principle, while also guaranteeing free trade and equal commercial opportunities in the region.', 'The accord tacitly accepted U.S. limits on Japanese immigration to California and Japanese acquiescence to the U.S. annexation of Hawaii, softening anti‑Japanese sentiment on the West Coast.', 'Though intended to defuse rising tensions, the agreement ultimately cemented Japan’s foothold in East Asia; Japan’s subsequent rapprochement with Russia and investment in Manchuria reduced American influence and foreshadowed greater Japanese control over China.', 'As a non‑treaty, the agreement required no Senate ratification, underscoring its nature as a pragmatic, power‑balancing statement rather than a legally binding treaty.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Kosmos 2261', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos_2261'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos_2261', 'name': 'Kosmos 2261', 'years': '1993-2012', 'short_description': \"Kosmos\\u202f2261 was a Russian US‑K early‑warning satellite launched on 10\\u202fAugust\\u202f1993 into a Molniya orbit to detect missile launches; it operated for about four years, was believed non‑functional by 1998, and reentered the Earth's atmosphere on 31\\u202fDecember\\u202f2012.\", 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Kosmos\\u202f2261 was a Russian Oko early‑warning satellite launched on 10\\u202fAugust\\u202f1993 into a Molniya orbit. Designed for a four‑year mission, it ceased operation around March\\u202f1998 and re‑entered uncontrolled on 31\\u202fDecember\\u202f2012, burning up in the atmosphere.', 'bias_comment': \"English articles emphasize the satellite's role in missile‑early‑warning and its early loss of function, whereas Russian sources stress its contribution to national security and may downplay the early failure. Western media may frame the uncontrolled re‑entry as a potential hazard, showing how language and regional context shape portrayal.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos_2261', 'key_points': ['**Purpose & design** –\\u202fKosmos\\u202f2261 was a Russian US‑K “Oko” early‑warning satellite, built to spot ballistic‑missile launches using onboard optical telescopes and infrared sensors.', '**Launch & orbit** –\\u202fIt was launched on\\u202f10\\u202fAugust\\u202f1993 from Plesetsk on a Molniya‑M/2BL rocket, placing the 1\\u202f900\\u202fkg spacecraft into a highly elliptical\\u202fMolniya orbit (≈\\u202f625\\u202fkm\\u202f×\\u202f39\\u202f700\\u202fkm, 62.9° inclination, 12‑hour period).', '**Operational lifespan** –\\u202fDesigned for a four‑year mission, the satellite is believed to have ceased functioning around\\u202fMarch\\u202f1998, well before its expected end‑of‑life.', '**End of life & re‑entry** –\\u202fAfter orbit‑decay, Kosmos\\u202f2261 made an uncontrolled, destructive re‑entry on\\u202f31\\u202fDecember\\u202f2012 (early\\u202f1\\u202fJanuary\\u202f2013 in some time zones).', '*Emphasis*: the article stresses Kosmos\\u202f2261’s role in the Soviet/Russian missile‑early‑warning network, its relatively short functional life, and its eventual uncontrolled atmospheric burn‑up.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Kosmos 2251', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos-2251'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos-2251', 'name': 'Kosmos 2251', 'years': '2009', 'short_description': 'On 10\\u202fFebruary\\u202f2009, the defunct Russian communications satellite Kosmos‑2251 collided with the active Iridium‑33 satellite, resulting in the first major satellite‑to‑satellite collision in orbit, destroying both spacecraft and generating a large debris field.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Destruction'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 2009, the defunct Russian satellite Kosmos‑2251 collided with the operational Iridium‑33, creating extensive debris. The event highlighted risks of space debris, spurred calls for better end‑of‑life policies, and prompted avoidance maneuvers by the International Space Station.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry emphasizes legal compliance and criticism of Russia, mentions Kessler syndrome and regulatory needs, while the French version emphasizes technical details (mass, altitude) and the impact on the International Space Station, reflecting differing national focus and source selection.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos-2251', 'key_points': ['**Kosmos‑2251 was a Russian military communications satellite launched in 1993 and declared non‑functional by 1995, leaving it as dead space debris in a congested low‑Earth orbit.**', '**The satellite’s lack of propulsion meant it could not be de‑orbited, a fact Russia cited as compliance with international law, while critics (e.g., *The Space Review*) condemned the decision as irresponsible stewardship of the space environment.**', '**On\\u202f10\\u202fFeb\\u202f2009, Kosmos‑2251 collided with the operational Iridium\\u202f33 satellite, producing a massive debris cloud and marking the first major in‑orbit collision between two satellites.**', '**The incident is frequently cited as a cautionary example of the Kessler syndrome‑risk and the need for stronger norms or regulations governing the disposal of defunct spacecraft.**']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos-2251', 'key_points': ['**Satélite russe de télécommunications Kosmos‑2251**\\u202f: lancé le 16\\u202fjuin\\u202f1993 (Strela‑2M, 900\\u202fkg) en orbite basse (~800\\u202fkm, inclinaison\\u202f74°).', '**Collision historique**\\u202f: le 10\\u202ffévrier\\u202f2009, il percuta le satellite commercial Iridium\\u202f33, constituant le premier choc enregistré entre deux engins actifs en orbite terrestre. Les deux satellites furent détruits.', '**Conséquences majeures**\\u202f: la détonation a généré une grande quantité de débris spatiaux qui ont depuis menacé d’autres missions, obligeant la Station spatiale internationale à deux manœuvres d’évitement (mars\\u202f2012\\u202f; octobre\\u202f2014).', '**Impact sur la politique spatiale**\\u202f: l’événement a mis en lumière le problème croissant des débris orbitaux et a renforcé les appels à une meilleure gestion du trafic et à des normes de fin de vie pour les satellites.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Kosmos 2312', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos_2312'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosmos_2312', 'name': 'Kosmos 2312', 'years': '1995', 'short_description': 'Kosmos 2312 is a Russian US‑K early‑warning missile‑detection satellite launched on 24\\u202fMay\\u202f1995 from Plesetsk, designed to detect missile launches using optical telescopes and infrared sensors as part of the Oko programme.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Launch'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Kosmos 2312, launched on 24 May 1995 by a Molniya‑M/2BL from Plesetsk, was a Russian Oko early‑warning satellite in a Molniya orbit. Designed for a four‑year mission, it used optical and infrared sensors to detect missile launches, and was cataloged as 1995‑026A (SATCAT\\u202f23584).', 'bias_comment': \"E