, 2003 after WTO ruled them illegal.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#intro (first paragraph)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 2002, the United States imposed 8–30% tariffs on steel imports, citing national security and domestic industry concerns. The measures faced WTO challenges, were ruled illegal in 2003, and were rescinded. Analyses reported mixed economic impacts and partisan political reactions.', 'bias_comment': \"English sources emphasize political motives and domestic industry benefits, while non‑English versions (e.g., Spanish, French) may stress WTO legality and international trade norms. This can lead to differing portrayals of the tariff's justification and impact, reflecting each country's trade policy perspective.\", 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2002_United_States_steel_tariff', 'key_points': ['**Politically‑driven protectionism:** The Bush administration imposed 8‑30% steel tariffs in 2002 largely to aid swing‑state steel‑producing regions, despite the president’s prior free‑trade record; critics argued the move was a calculated political play rather than an economic necessity.', '**Economic costs outweighed benefits:** Numerous studies—including a 2005 review and ITC analysis—found that the tariffs produced a net welfare loss (up to a $110\\u202fmillion loss, ≈0.001\\u202f% of GDP) and resulted in more job losses in steel‑consuming sectors than gains in steel‑making, indicating the measures harmed overall GDP and employment.', '**International backlash & WTO ruling:** The EU, Japan, Korea, China, and others challenged the tariffs at the WTO, which declared them illegal in November\\u202f2003 and threatened $2\\u202fbillion in sanctions; the United States withdrew the tariffs shortly thereafter to avoid a broader trade war.', '**Mixed political reaction:** While some Democrats and free‑trade advocates condemned the tariffs for hurting consumers and steel‑using states, certain conservative allies and steel‑producer supporters praised them as necessary safeguards—highlighting the deep partisan split over protectionism versus free trade.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'China Venture', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Venture'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Venture', 'name': 'China Venture', 'years': '1953', 'short_description': 'China Venture is a 1953 American adventure war film directed by Don Siegel, depicting a 1945 mission in South China during World War\\u202fII where an American team tries to rescue an injured Japanese admiral with crucial intelligence.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Plot'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'China Venture (1953) is a Columbia war adventure directed by Don Siegel, starring Edmond O’Brien, Barry Sullivan and Jocelyn Brando, about an American patrol in WWII‑occupied China rescuing a Japanese admiral; the film is a brief 1950s American war‑film stub.', 'bias_comment': 'The English entry emphasizes Cold‑War symbolism, scholarly analysis, and gender dynamics, reflecting a more interpretative, Western academic perspective. The French entry provides a concise factual account focused on production details, cast, and article status, likely due to translation gaps and less emphasis on thematic interpretation. This illustrates how language‑specific editorial practices can bias the portrayal of the same film across Wikipedia languages.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Venture', 'key_points': ['**Classic WWII adventure‑war picture** – *China Venture* (1953) is a Columbia Pictures production directed by Don\\u202fSiegel, starring Edmond\\u202fO’Brien, Barry\\u202fSullivan and Jocelyn\\u202fBrando, that follows an American Marine/Navy patrol sent into jungle‑ridden South China to capture a wounded Japanese admiral for interrogation.', '**Plot twists into Cold‑War symbolism** – The mission is beset by Japanese occupiers and Chinese guerrilla forces; after the admiral’s rescue and interrogation, the film closes with real footage of a mushroom cloud, explicitly linking the war’s resolution to the looming atomic bomb.', '**Atypical for Siegel’s oeuvre** – Film scholar Judith\\u202fM.\\u202fKass notes the movie deviates from Siegel’s usual preoccupations: there are no anti‑heroic struggles, femme\\u202ffatales, or damaged heroes; the American protagonists are straightforwardly dutiful, and the lone female character (Brando) is a competent, non‑seductive rescuer.', '**Genre and production context** – Though marketed as an adventure/war film, the article flags it as a stub in the World‑War‑II film category, underscoring its relatively minor status in both Siegel’s prolific career and the broader canon of 1950s American war cinema.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Venture', 'key_points': ['**“China Venture” (1953)**\\u202f‑\\u202fune courte production américaine de 82\\u202fminutes, réalisée par **Don\\u202fSiegel**, qui situe son intrigue pendant la Seconde\\u202fGuerre\\u202fmondiale\\u202f: des soldats américains sont envoyés en Chine occupée par le Japon pour libérer un prisonnier de guerre.', '**Distribution principale**\\u202f: **Edmond\\u202fO’Brien**, **Barry\\u202fSullivan** et **Jocelyn\\u202fBrando**, soutenus par des acteurs tels que **Lee\\u202fStrasberg**, **Leo\\u202fGordon**, **Richard\\u202fLoo** et d’autres rôles non crédités.', '**Genre et technique**\\u202f: film de guerre tourné en format 35\\u202fmm, inscrit dans la catégorie «\\u202ffilm de guerre américain\\u202f».', '**État de l’article Wikipédia**\\u202f: classé comme **ébauche** (stub) et contenant un appel à la traduction\\u202f/\\u202fdéveloppement, indiquant que le contenu reste très limité.', '**Références externes**\\u202f: plusieurs bases de données cinématographiques (AllMovie, AFI, IMDb, The\\u202fMovie\\u202fDatabase, etc.) sont listées, et le film est rattaché à de multiples catégories (films de 1953, films de Don\\u202fSiegel, films se déroulant en Chine, etc.).']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'American China Development Company', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_China_Development_Company'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_China_Development_Company', 'name': 'American China Development Company – Canton–Hankow Railway concession', 'years': '1898-1904', 'short_description': 'The American China Development Company secured a concession in 1898 to build the Guangzhou‑Wuchang (Canton‑Hankow) railway, operating it and related coal mines. The line was built between 1902 and 1904 but the concession was revoked by the Chinese government in December 1904, ending the project.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Canton–Hankow_Railway'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The American China Development Company, founded in 1895, aimed for U.S. railway and mineral concessions in China. It secured a partial Canton‑Hankow railway concession, faced European competition, lost control to Belgian investors, and saw its concession revoked in 1904.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article frames the venture as a U.S. economic and diplomatic push, while Chinese-language accounts may stress Chinese sovereignty and resistance to foreign control, and European-language sources often stress financial competition. Absence of non‑English sources can skew the narrative toward a Western corporate perspective.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_China_Development_Company', 'key_points': ['**U.S.‑backed venture to secure Chinese railway and mineral concessions** – The American China Development Company, founded in 1895 under ex‑senator Calvin\\u202fBrice and financed by leading U.S. financiers (J.P.\\u202fMorgan, Carnegie Steel, National City Bank, Chase), was a flagship of American economic ambition in China and a tool of the Open Door Policy.', '**Early diplomatic push and rival‑state competition** – Agent A.W.\\u202fBash, armed with State Department letters, negotiated a north‑south railway (Marco\\u202fPolo Bridge to Hankou) in 1896, but Chinese officials, urged by U.S. officials, granted the concession to a Belgian‑backed consortium, illustrating how U.S. pressure could not out‑maneuver European rivals.', '**Canton–Hankow line as the sole American‑realised project** – By 1898 the company secured a loan to build the Guangzhou‑Wuchang line, including coal‑mine rights, but the financing covered only half the estimated cost; a 1900 supplemental bond agreement attempted to bridge the shortfall, while the firm was forced to share the concession with the British‑Chinese Corporation.', '**Belgian takeover and loss of American control** – A 1901 re‑organization installed Belgian directors and a Brussels bank, leading to Belgian majority control by 1903‑04; Chinese officials, fearing foreign‑political domination, pressed for cancellation, and despite U.S. State Department lobbying, the concession was revoked in December\\u202f1904.', '**Final dissolution and modest settlement** – After the revocation, American investors (notably J.P.\\u202fMorgan) repurchased stock in early\\u202f1905, and a June\\u202f1905 settlement paid China $675\\u202fk for the forfeited assets—marking the end of the most prominent U.S. railway concession in China and underscoring the limits of American expansionism amid intense international rivalry.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Chinese-American service in World War II', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese-American_service_in_World_War_II'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese-American_service_in_World_War_II', 'name': 'Chinese-American service in World War II', 'years': '1939-1945', 'short_description': 'Around 12,000‑15,000 Chinese Americans enlisted in the U.S. military or worked in defense industries during World War\\u202fII, comprising up to 20\\u202f% of the Chinese‑American male population and helping to shift public attitudes and immigration policy.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Service'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Approximately 12‑15\\u202f000 Chinese‑American men, about 20\\u202f% of the community’s male population, served in World\\u202fII, mainly in integrated units and the Army Air Forces. They formed majority‑Chinese units, women served as pilots, and their service later supported civil‑rights advances and earned honors.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article highlights patriotic motives, large numbers, and post‑war civil‑rights gains, reflecting a US‑centric view. Chinese‑language sources may stress loyalty to China and contributions in the China‑Burma‑India theater, while other language editions could downplay numbers or gender roles, showing how national narratives shape portrayal.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese-American_service_in_World_War_II', 'key_points': ['**Massive participation:**\\u202fAn estimated\\u202f12‑15\\u202fthousand Chinese‑American men—about\\u202f20\\u202fpercent of the community’s male population—served in World\\u202fWar\\u202fII, most in integrated units, with a notable concentration in the Army Air Forces and, after 1942, broader Navy ratings.', '**Patriotic motivation and early draft:**\\u202fDriven by loyalty to both the United States and China, the desire for social acceptance, and the fact that many had no dependents, Chinese Americans volunteered or were among the first drafted following Pearl Harbor.', '**Diverse service roles:**\\u202fBeyond combat, Chinese Americans formed ten majority‑Chinese units (nine in the 14th Air Service Group, one in the 987th Signal Company) in the CBI theater; women served as WASP pilots, in the Women’s Air Corps, and as defense‑industry workers in shipyards and factories.', '**Social impact and later recognition:**\\u202fTheir contributions helped pressure the repeal of the Chinese Exclusion Act, accelerated naturalization and family reunification, expanded post‑war educational and professional opportunities, and culminated in official honors—including a 2018 Congressional Gold Medal for WWII veterans and individual awards such as the Medal of Honor for Capt.\\u202fFrancis\\u202fB.\\u202fWai.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'United States in World War I', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_in_World_War_I'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_in_World_War_I', 'name': 'United States in World War I', 'years': '1917–1918', 'short_description': 'The United States entered World War\\u202fI on 6\\u202fApril\\u202f1917, declaring war on Germany and later Austria‑Hungary. Over five million Americans were mobilized, with more than two million serving overseas on the Western Front. The U.S. supplied vital material, financial aid, and troops, contributing to the Allied victory culminating in the armistice of 11\\u202fNovember\\u202f1918.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Lead paragraph (introduction before sections)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'In 1917 the U.S., after German submarine attacks and the Zimmermann Telegram, entered WWI. Over 5\\u202fmillion served; industry mobilized, war bonds raised funds. Domestic debate pitted preparedness advocates against isolationists, while women and labor saw expanded roles.', 'bias_comment': 'The English article stresses progressive reform and domestic mobilization, whereas the Spanish page highlights labor and immigrant opposition, the German version downplays U.S. aggression, the French article stresses alliance contributions, and Chinese sources often minimize American involvement. These variations reflect national historiographical priorities and political narratives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_in_World_War_I', 'key_points': ['**From neutrality to a “war to end all wars”** – After three years of staying out of the conflict, President\\u202fWoodrow\\u202fWilson finally asked Congress to declare war (April\\u202f6\\u202f1917) once German unrestricted submarine attacks and the Zimmermann Telegram made the German Empire appear a direct threat to American security and values.', '**Mobilization of manpower and industry** – Over 5\\u202fmillion Americans were drafted or enlisted; more than 2\\u202fmillion fought in Europe under General\\u202fJohn\\u202fJ.\\u202fPershing. At home the federal government created a host of agencies (Food Administration, War Industries Board, Liberty‑bond drives, etc.) that reorganized the economy, raised taxes, sold $21\\u202fbillion in war bonds and turned the United\\u202fStates into the Allies’ chief supplier of food, raw materials and financing.', '**Domestic debate and the Preparedness movement** – Prior to 1917 a powerful “Preparedness” coalition of Republicans, manufacturers and navalists pressed for a larger standing army and navy, while many progressives, labor unions, women’s groups and ethnic communities (Irish, German, Scandinavian) championed isolationism and feared a militarist, corporate‑driven war. The clash over conscription, the National Guard, and wartime censorship highlighted deep political and cultural divisions.', '**Societal changes on the home front** – The war spurred unprecedented participation of women in industrial and volunteer work, broadened labor‑management cooperation through the National War Labor Board, and generated a massive propaganda effort that linked American democracy to “German efficiency” and framed the conflict as a fight for liberty and democratic ideals.', '**Legacy of the Progressive Era** – Wilson’s entry into the war was presented as the climax of the Progressive Era, aiming to export American reform and democracy and to shape the post‑war peace (League of Nations, Fourteen Points), even as the war accelerated the growth of federal power and set the stage for the United\\u202fStates’ emergence as a global super‑power.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'United States in World War II', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_in_World_War_II'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_in_World_War_II', 'name': 'United States in World War II', 'years': '1941-1945', 'short_description': 'The United States participated in World War\\u202fII in many different ways, covering both home‑front activities and military operations.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': \"The United States' World War\\u202fII page is a set‑index article linking the home‑front experience, military operations, and pre‑war non‑interventionism, serving as a navigational hub for related topics rather than a detailed narrative.\", 'bias_comment': 'Non‑English versions may stress distinct aspects: e.g., the Japanese article emphasizes Pacific battles and Japanese-American internment; the German article highlights the European theater and US–German relations. Such variations reflect national historiographies and source availability, potentially shifting emphasis away from the home‑front or non‑interventionism details noted in the English set‑index.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_in_World_War_II', 'key_points': ['The United\\u202fStates’ role in World\\u202fWar\\u202fII is presented as multifaceted, encompassing both the home‑front experience and the nation’s military operations.', 'Key sub‑articles highlighted are **“United States home front during World War\\u202fII”** (civilian mobilization, industry, society) and **“Military history of the United States during World War\\u202fII”** (combat actions, strategy, overseas deployments).', 'A “See also” link points to **“