led city of Tianjin, confronting about 12,000 Qing, Boxer, and auxiliary forces. After intense fighting and heavy casualties on both sides, the Allies breached the city gates and forced Chinese defenders to withdraw.', 'bias_comment': 'English accounts emphasize the multinational alliance’s objectives and casualty figures, often portraying Chinese resistance as formidable yet ultimately defeated. Other language versions may stress imperialist aggression, nationalist resistance, or vary casualty numbers, reflecting differing historiographical and political perspectives.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tientsin', 'key_points': ['**A multinational “Eight‑Nation Alliance” forced its way into the walled city of Tianjin on 13–14\\u202fJuly\\u202f1900**, breaking the siege of the foreign concessions and securing a foothold for the subsequent march on Beijing.', '**The Allies fielded about 6,900 troops (Russia, Japan, Britain, the United\\u202fStates, France, Germany, Italy and Austria‑Hungary) against roughly 12,000 well‑armed Chinese Imperial soldiers, Boxers and auxiliary forces**, who defended the city with heavy artillery, machine‑guns and rifle fire.', '**Lack of a single commanding officer and poor coordination made the initial assault costly**, with the Allies pinned down in mud under relentless fire; notable losses included the death of U.S. Colonel Emerson\\u202fLiscum and heavy casualties among British, American and Japanese troops.', '**Despite the fierce Chinese resistance—highlighted by accurate artillery fire and the tenacious defense of the South and East Gates—the Japanese finally breached the South Gate and the Russians the East Gate**, forcing the Qing forces to withdraw.', '**The battle proved the bloodiest engagement of the Boxer Rebellion for the foreign powers (≈250 allied killed, 500 wounded) and demonstrated that Chinese troops could mount formidable defense**, leading t