ited_States'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Chinese_spy_cases_in_the_United_States', 'name': 'List of Chinese spy cases in the United States', 'years': '2013-2024', 'short_description': 'A compiled list of individuals accused by the U.S. government of espionage or related crimes for Chinese intelligence, spanning cases dated before 2013 and a surge from 2019 through 2024.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Accused_of_espionage'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Between 2013 and 2024, U.S. authorities charged dozens of individuals—including citizens, military members, scientists, and engineers—alleged to spy for Chinese intelligence, covering technology theft, cyber intrusions, illegal exports, and recruitment of officials.', 'bias_comment': 'English-language sources stress a national‑security threat and legal outcomes; Chinese-language coverage may downplay scope, question evidence, or frame cases as politically motivated, leading to divergent framing across languages.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Chinese_spy_cases_in_the_United_States', 'key_points': ['**Widespread, ongoing espionage campaign:**  Since the early 2010s the U.S. government has repeatedly identified and charged dozens of individuals—including naturalized U.S. citizens, military personnel, scientists, engineers, and academics—with acting on behalf of Chinese intelligence agencies, showing a sustained, nation‑state‑directed effort to steal U.S. technology, military data, and trade‑secret information.', '**Broad spectrum of targets and methods:**  The alleged offenses span theft of proprietary code from a locomotive manufacturer, hacking of vaccine‑development networks, large‑scale cyber‑intrusions (e.g., APT‑41, APT‑27), illegal export of defense‑related technologies, and recruitment of U.S. officials and service members to provide classified defense and scientific data.', '*