ed sharply and have hovered around 5% ever since.', '**Limited economic payoff of protectionism:** Historians such as Douglas\\u202fIrwin argue that the myth of high 19th‑century tariffs “making America great” is overstated—industrial growth was driven more by abundant resources and openness to ideas than by tariffs, which cost roughly 0.5\\u202f% of GDP and only marginally accelerated a few sectors.', '**Policy driven by politics, not economics:** Throughout the 19th and early‑20th centuries, tariff levels reflected partisan battles—Republicans championed high protective duties to reward northern manufacturers, while Democrats pushed low‑tariff “revenue‑only” policies for consumers and the agrarian South—resulting in frequent swings but a generally stable institutional bias once a regime took hold.', '**Modern swing back toward protectionism:** After decades of liberalization (GATT/WTO, NAFTA, low average tariffs), the United States saw a resurgence of protectionist measures after the 2016 election and sporadic sector‑specific duties (e.g., steel, “Chicken tax”), underscoring that tariff policy remains a potent political tool despite the long‑term trend toward free trade.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'File:\"China-First to Fight - NARA - 513567.tif - Wikipedia', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:%22China-First_to_Fight_-_NARA_-_513567.tif'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:%22China-First_to_Fight_-_NARA_-_513567.tif', 'name': '\"China--First to Fight\" World War II propaganda poster', 'years': '1941-1945', 'short_description': 'A United States World War II propaganda poster produced by the Office of War Information, urging support for China and emphasizing its role as the first nation to fight the Axis powers, dated between 1941 and 1945.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#metadata'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The 1941‑45 U.S. Office of War Information poster “China – First to Fight” is a public‑domain WWII prop