ort equipment, illustrating the conflict's multinational nature and the era's rapid technological evolution.\", 'bias_comment': 'The English article emphasizes the breadth and technological diversity of equipment, reflecting a Western, technology‑focused perspective. Non‑English versions (e.g., Korean, Chinese) may highlight different aspects such as domestic production, ideological significance, or casualty impact, leading to variance in emphasis and detail.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_military_equipment_used_in_the_Korean_War', 'key_points': ['The Korean\\u202fWar saw an unusually **wide mix of equipment**: everything from light armored cars and half‑tracks to heavy tanks, self‑propelled artillery, and amphibious vehicles.', 'Both **Western (U.S., British, Australian, etc.) and Eastern (Soviet, Chinese, North Korean) forces** fielded a massive variety of weapons—rifles, sub‑machine guns, machine guns, mortars, recoilless rifles, and rockets—showing the conflict’s multinational nature.', 'Air power was a **major factor**, with dozens of combat and transport aircraft ranging from prop‑driven bombers (B‑29, B‑17) to first‑generation jets (MiG‑15, F‑86 Sabre) and a host of support planes and helicopters.', 'The list also includes **support gear** such as radios (AN/PRC‑6/10), helmets, field coats, and specialized fire‑control equipment, underscoring how logistics and communications were integral to the war effort.', 'Overall, the inventory highlights the **technological diversity and rapid evolution** of mid‑20th‑century military hardware that were pressed into service during the Korean conflict.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Second Kuomintang-Communist Civil War', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Kuomintang-Communist_Civil_War'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Kuomintang-Communist_Civil_War', 'name': 'Second Kuomintang-Communist Civil War', 'years': '