e, laser, anti‑satellite and super‑gun system for China within a decade—an outlook later judged unrealistic.', '**Technical and political bottlenecks stalled progress:** Limited indigenous expertise, the turmoil of the Cultural Revolution, and competing priorities (e.g., the DF‑5 ICBM) caused chronic delays, launch failures, and ultimately the abandonment of most missile and super‑gun projects.', '**No operational system emerged:** Despite years of testing (HQ‑81/FJ‑1, radar prototypes, 120\\u202fmm laser) the programme was formally terminated in 1982 without delivering a deployable defence architecture.', '**Spin‑off benefits persisted:** Research on high‑g gyroscopes, radar (7010 early‑warning, 110 tracking), and underground‑entry physics fed later Chinese weapons and space programmes, and laser/anti‑satellite work was later revived under the 863 Program.', '**Legacy of a pioneering but failed effort:** Project\\u202f640 illustrates early Chinese attempts to match super‑power missile‑defence ambitions, highlighting the gap between strategic intent and actual capability in the 1960s‑70s.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Executive Order 12711', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_12711'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_12711', 'name': 'Executive Order 12711', 'years': '1990', 'short_description': 'Executive Order 12711, issued by President George H. W. Bush on 11\\u202fApril\\u202f1990, deferred deportation of Chinese nationals and dependents present in the U.S. between 5\\u202fJune\\u202f1989 and 11\\u202fApril\\u202f1990, waived the two‑year home residency requirement, and granted employment authorization through 1\\u202fJanuary\\u202f1994.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'lead paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Executive Order 12711, signed by President George H. W. Bush on April 11, 1990, temporarily protected Chinese nationals who arrived in the U.S. between June 5, 1989, and April 11, 1990, from deportation and granted employment authorization, later codified by the 1992 Chinese Student Protection Act.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize the order as a humanitarian response and a tool of U.S. foreign‑policy pressure, portraying the U.S. positively. Chinese‑language versions tend to downplay U.S. involvement, focusing on the impact on Chinese students and framing the measure as a response to domestic unrest, which can shift perceived agency and moral judgment. These linguistic differences reveal national narratives that influence the interpretation of the order’s intent and effects.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_12711', 'key_points': ['**Humanitarian response to Tiananmen:** \\u202fIssued by President George\\u202fH.\\u202fW.\\u202fBush on\\u202f11\\u202fApril\\u202f1990, EO\\u202f12711 was a direct U.S. reaction to the PRC’s violent suppression of the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests, signalling solidarity with Chinese dissidents.', '**De‑ferral and status relief:** \\u202fThe order temporarily halted deportation of Chinese nationals (and their immediate dependents) who entered the United States between\\u202f5\\u202fJune\\u202f1989\\u202fand\\u202f11\\u202fApril\\u202f1990, waived the usual two‑year home‑country residency requirement, and granted them employment authorization through\\u202f1\\u202fJanuary\\u202f1994.', '**Legislative cementing:** \\u202fThe protections initially set by the executive order were made permanent two years later when Congress passed the **Chinese Student Protection Act of\\u202f1992**, embedding the humanitarian measures into law.', '**Broader diplomatic impact:** \\u202fEO\\u202f12711 highlighted a period of heightened U.S.–China tension in the early 1990s, reinforcing America’s willingness to use immigration policy as a tool of foreign‑policy pressure and to support democratic values abroad.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'China Relief Expedition', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Relief_Expedition'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Relief_Expedition', 'name': 'China Relief Expedition', 'years': '1900-1901', 'short_description': 'The China Relief Expedition (1900–1901) was a United States military expedition as part of the Eight‑Nation Alliance to rescue foreign nationals and suppress the Boxer Rebellion in China, culminating in the capture of Beijing in August 1900 and subsequent occupation until 1901.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'intro paragraph'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The China Relief Expedition (1900‑01) was a multinational military effort, including U.S. forces, to lift the siege of foreign legations in Beijing during the Boxer Rebellion. American troops fought alongside allies, suffered casualties, and later received campaign medals.', 'bias_comment': 'English articles often stress U.S. humanitarian motives and the expedition’s role in American power emergence, while Chinese-language versions tend to highlight imperialist aggression and the unequal treaty consequences. These differences reflect national narratives about foreign intervention and the legacy of the Boxer Rebellion.', 'languages_used': ['en'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Relief_Expedition', 'key_points': ['**U.S. intervention was driven by a mix of humanitarian rescue and protection of growing commercial interests in China**, especially after the Philippines became a U.S. overseas base; the expedition formed part of the multinational Eight‑Nation Alliance that moved to lift the siege of foreign legations in Beijing.', '**The campaign quickly shifted from “relief” to active combat**, as American troops joined a 19\\u202f000‑strong coalition that stormed Tianjin, fought at Beicang and Yangcun, and finally broke into Beijing’s outer and inner cities, suffering about 200 casualties while the bulk of the fighting was carried out by other Allied forces.', '**The operation marked the United States’ first large‑scale joint overseas expedition since the Revolutionary War**, signalling a departure from strict isolationism and foreshadowing America’s emergence as a global power; it also led to the creation of the China Campaign and China Relief Expedition medals.', '**Politically, the Allies forced a peace settlement that imposed a massive indemnity on China**, of which the United States claimed only a fraction and later returned much of its portion to fund Chinese education, portraying the U.S. as a relatively magnanimous participant.', '**Historically the expedition is presented as a turning point in U.S. foreign‑policy doctrine**, illustrating the growing willingness to use military force abroad to safeguard national interests and to cooperate with other imperial powers despite domestic isolationist sentiment.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Xihu Trough', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xihu_Trough'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xihu_Trough', 'name': 'Xihu Trough', 'years': '2003-2004', 'short_description': 'In August 2003, a joint venture between Sinopec, CNOOC, Royal Dutch/Shell, and Unocal was announced to develop the Xihu Trough gas fields, targeting production by 2005. In September 2004, Royal Dutch/Shell and Unocal withdrew, citing doubts over commercial viability and territorial disputes.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': 'Joint venture announcement (2003) and withdrawal (2004)'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'The Xihu Trough, a strategic offshore basin in the East China Sea, saw a joint venture between Sinopec, CNOOC, Shell and Unocal in 2003 aiming for production by 2005, but Shell and Unocal withdrew in 2004 citing commercial and jurisdictional concerns.', 'bias_comment': 'The English Wikipedia entry emphasizes geopolitical tension and commercial doubts, potentially reflecting Western media focus on foreign partner