ichment and operational losses. These variations reflect national narratives: U.S. focus on security breach, Chinese emphasis on patriotism, and French attention to corruption and procedural details.', 'languages_used': ['en', 'fr', 'zh'], 'perspectives': [{'language': 'en', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larry_Wu-tai_Chin', 'key_points': ['**Deep‑cover mole for decades** – Larry\\u202fWu‑tai\\u202fChin infiltrated the U.S. Army and the CIA from 1944‑1985, passing classified policy papers, intelligence reports and the identities of covert agents to the Chinese Communist Party’s intelligence service.', '**Strategic damage to U.S. diplomacy** – In 1970 he supplied Mao\\u202fZedong and Zhou\\u202fEnlai with President\\u202fNixon’s secret plans to open relations with China, allowing Beijing to pre‑empt and manipulate the negotiations to extract maximal concessions.', '**Devastating operational losses** – Chin’s access enabled China to identify, imprison or execute dozens of CIA operatives in China and to mislead U.S. forces during the Korean War, undermining American tactical decisions.', '**Exposure, conviction and suicide** – After a 1985 defection exposed his espionage, Chin was arrested, faced two life‑sentence counts, and committed suicide in his jail cell, reportedly after a Chinese consul promised financial support for his family.', '**One of the Cold‑War’s most valuable Chinese assets** – Historians and intelligence analysts rank Chin among the most consequential foreign spies of the era, citing his role in reshaping U.S.–China relations and crippling U.S. intelligence operations in the PRC.']}, {'language': 'fr', 'url': 'https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larry_Wu-tai_Chin', 'key_points': ['**Traducteur à la CIA puis espion pour la Chine** : Larry\\u202fWu‑tai\\u202fChin a travaillé comme traducteur pour le FBIS de la CIA, mais a simultanément transmis des documents classifiés à la République populaire de Chine de 1952 à 1985, y compris des renseignements sur les prisonniers de guerre pendant la guerre de Corée.', '**Impact opérationnel majeur** : Ses falsifications de traductions et la remise d’informations aux services chinois ont entraîné des pertes de vies américaines, des retards dans les négociations d’armistice et la persécution de prisonniers chinois qui tentaient de défectionner.', '**Enrichissement et style de vie ostentatoire** : Chin a accumulé environ un million de dollars grâce à ses activités d’espionnage, qu’il a blanchis en biens immobiliers et en dépenses de jeu, suscitant des soupçons au sein de la CIA malgré l’absence de surveillance officielle.', '**Condamnation et mort** : Arrêté en novembre\\u202f1985, il a été reconnu coupable d’espionnage et d’évasion fiscale en 1986. Le jour même de son jugement, il a été retrouvé mort dans sa cellule, la mort étant attribuée à un suicide par suffocation avec un sac plastique.']}, {'language': 'zh', 'url': 'https://zh.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=%E9%87%91%E6%97%A0%E6%80%A0&oldid=79339392', 'key_points': ['**A “super‑spy” who allegedly reshaped Cold‑War geopolitics** – 金无怠 (Larry\\u202fWu‑Tai\\u202fChin) is portrayed as one of the most influential Chinese agents ever, feeding Beijing secret CIA intelligence that helped steer the Korean War, the Vietnam War and the historic 1972 U.S.–China rapprochement.', '**Deep‑cover CIA insider** – From the 1950s to the early 1980s he worked as a translator and analyst for the CIA’s Foreign Broadcast Intelligence Service, gaining access to highly classified material while secretly passing it to China’s intelligence services.', '**High‑profile arrest, conviction and a “mysterious” suicide** – After a prolonged FBI investigation he was charged with 17 counts (spy‑related, fraud and tax offenses), found guilty on all, and died in prison by self‑inflicted asphyxiation—an ending that his widow and later commentators have repeatedly called suspicious and inadequately investigated.', '**Political fallout and media narrative** – 