tain commercial value and validity, leading to inefficiencies and dead‑weight loss.', '**Litigation is rare and opaque:** only a tiny fraction of patents ever reach trial, so most disputes are settled privately, limiting public insight into patent scope and preventing broader invalidation of weak patents.', '**Macroeconomic and micro‑economic impacts are mixed:** patents can both promote diffusion of knowledge (when disclosed) and hinder competition, sometimes inflating prices, discouraging R&D, or generating “patent thickets” that block follow‑on innovation.', '**Valuation is context‑dependent:** a patent’s worth hinges on market demand, enforceability, and strategic use (e.g., licensing, cross‑licensing, or defensive blocking), meaning patents are not intrinsically valuable but derive value only from the economic opportunities they enable.']}]}}, {'seed': {'name': 'Hermann Speck von Sternburg', 'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Freiherr_Speck_von_Sternburg'}, 'extracted': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Freiherr_Speck_von_Sternburg', 'name': 'Hermann Speck von Sternburg', 'years': '1852–1908', 'short_description': 'German diplomat (1852–1908) who served as ambassador to the United States (1903–1908) after earlier posts in Beijing, Belgrade, Washington and as high commissioner on the Samoan Commission.', 'paragraph_anchor_or_comment': '#Career'}, 'debiased': {'neutral_description_50_words_max': 'Hermann Freiherr Speck von Sternburg (1852‑1908) was a German baron, military officer, and diplomat who served in the Franco‑Prussian War, held posts in Beijing, Belgrade, and Washington, D.C., married American Lillian Langham, collected art, and died of lupus in 1908.', 'bias_comment': 'English sources emphasize his aristocratic lineage, military background, and a diplomatic scandal with the U.S., potentially framing him as a privileged elite. Non‑English versions may focus more on his diplomatic achievements or cultural patronage, reflecting nat